  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="Basal Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer that can return to its original area or metastasize to lymph nodes or distant parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal cell skin cancer, also known as basal cell carcinoma (BCC), is the most common type of skin cancer, and it occurs when basal cells become abnormal and grow uncontrollably.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="United States">
      <data key="d0">United States</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The United States is the country where approximately 3 million cases of basal cell skin cancer are diagnosed each year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UV Radiation Exposure">
      <data key="d0">UV Radiation Exposure</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">UV radiation exposure is a significant risk factor for developing basal cell skin cancer, as it can damage the DNA in skin cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Cancer Recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Skin Cancer Recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin cancer recurrence refers to the return of basal cell skin cancer after treatment, which may happen in the original area or spread to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) is the most common type of skin cancer, characterized by abnormal growth of basal cells in the epidermis. It is often caused by UV exposure and is usually curable if caught early.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal cell carcinoma is a less aggressive type of skin cancer originating in basal cells, the ones responsible for producing new skin cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin">
      <data key="d0">Skin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin is an organ from which samples can be taken via biopsy to analyze any genetic predispositions to leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin is the largest organ of the body, consisting of three layers: the epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis, and plays a crucial role in protecting the body and regulating temperature.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin is the largest organ of the body, providing protection, temperature regulation, and sensation as well as being a site for cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epidermis">
      <data key="d0">Epidermis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The epidermis is the top layer of skin, where basal cell skin cancer primarily occurs, and it contains basal cells that can become cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dermis">
      <data key="d0">Dermis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The dermis is the middle layer of skin that contains connective tissue, blood vessels, nerves, and glands, and supports the epidermis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypodermis">
      <data key="d0">Hypodermis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The hypodermis, or subcutaneous layer, is the deepest layer of skin made up of fat and connective tissue and helps insulate the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Lesions">
      <data key="d0">Skin Lesions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin lesions are abnormal changes in the skin, which can include various formations and are indicators of conditions such as basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Factors">
      <data key="d0">Risk Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk factors are characteristics or conditions that increase the likelihood of developing basal cell skin cancer, such as UV exposure, fair skin, and age.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors are conditions or behaviors that increase the likelihood of developing cervical cancer, including HPV infection, smoking, and a history of multiple births.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors are conditions or behaviors that increase the likelihood of developing diseases, including sun exposure for skin cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors are specific behaviors, environmental exposures, or genetic predispositions that can increase the likelihood of developing liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors for developing primary CNS lymphoma include having a weakened immune system and certain neurological conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors for lung cancer include genetic predispositions, lifestyle choices such as smoking, and environmental factors that increase the likelihood of developing the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors for prostate cancer include age, family history, race, and genetic mutations that may increase an individual's likelihood of developing the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors for thyroid cancer include radiation exposure and family history, which contribute to increased likelihood of developing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Options">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Different medical procedures, medications, and therapies available for patients diagnosed with primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment options for AML can include chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and supportive care approaches aimed at managing symptoms and improving outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment options for basal cell skin cancer include surgical removal, radiation therapy, and systemic therapies, depending on the case severity.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment options for prostate cancer include a range of therapies like hormone therapy and chemotherapy tailored to individual needs.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment options refer to various available methods for treating cancer, including clinical trials and standard therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Open Sores">
      <data key="d0">Open Sores</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Open sores are a potential symptom of basal cell skin cancer, indicating advanced lesions that may bleed or fail to heal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early Detection">
      <data key="d0">Early Detection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early detection of basal cell skin cancer is critical for effective treatment and better patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer offers information and support for patients with various types of cancer, including mouth cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Patient Guides provide resources and guidance for cancer patients, helping them understand treatment options and advocate for themselves.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) Patient Guides for Cancer provide information and resources for cancer patients, including details on fertility preservation and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer is an app that offers guidance on cancer screening and treatment for patients, particularly in colorectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer offer resources and guidelines for patients dealing with various cancers, including basal cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer provide comprehensive resources and guidelines to help patients understand their cancer treatment options and care.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer provide resources and information to support cancer patients in treatment and management options.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer provide valuable information and guidelines for patients with cancer, including treatment options and resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Glands">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The adrenal glands are small, triangle-shaped glands located on top of each kidney that produce hormones affecting various bodily functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal tumors are growths that develop in the adrenal glands, which can be benign or malignant and may affect hormone production.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenal tumors form in the adrenal glands, can be benign or malignant, and are categorized based on their characteristics and functionality.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenal tumors form in the adrenal glands, can be either benign or malignant, and are categorized based on their characteristics and functionality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenocortical Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adrenocortical Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenocortical carcinoma (ACC) is a type of adrenal cancer originating from the adrenal cortex, which can be functioning or nonfunctioning and is most likely to affect children ages 5 and under, and adults in their 40s and 50s.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenocortical carcinoma (ACC) is a type of adrenal cancer originating from the adrenal cortex, which can be functioning or nonfunctioning and is most likely to affect children and adults in their 40s and 50s.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenocortical carcinoma is the most common type of adrenal gland cancer, considered very rare and affecting about 1 to 2 people per million.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cushing Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Cushing Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cushing syndrome is a condition caused by excessive cortisol, typically resulting from an adenoma affecting hormone production in the adrenal glands.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroblastoma">
      <data key="d0">Neuroblastoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuroblastoma is a rare and cancerous type of adrenal tumor that primarily affects children ages 5 and under, often found in infants, and can spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Neuroblastoma is a rare type of cancerous adrenal tumor primarily affecting children, particularly infants, and can spread to other body parts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment encompasses various therapeutic approaches that may affect fertility and overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Treatment includes various medical interventions such as surgery, chemotherapy, and radiation therapy aimed at eliminating cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses a range of therapies such as surgery, radiation, systemic therapy, and others specific to various types of cancer, including bone cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various approaches used to manage and eliminate cancer, including surgery, radiation, and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various medical interventions designed to combat cancer, including surgeries, chemotherapy, and radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various medical interventions used to treat cancer, including surgery, chemotherapy, and radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various medical procedures and therapies aimed at combating cancer and managing patient health.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various medical procedures and therapies aimed at eradicating cancerous cells and managing the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various medical procedures and therapies aimed at treating cancer and managing its symptoms, including chemotherapy and radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various procedures and therapies aimed at managing and eradicating cancer, including surgery, chemotherapy, and lifestyle modifications.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment involves various approaches tailored to the specific type of cancer, with nasopharyngeal cancer requiring specialized therapies based on tumor location.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment involves various approaches that are determined based on tumor location and cancer stage, emphasizing tailored strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment involves various medical interventions aimed at eradicating cancer cells and managing symptoms, including chemotherapy and radiotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment refers to the various medical strategies employed to combat cancer, which may include surgery, chemotherapy, and targeted therapies depending on the type and stage of the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Scan">
      <data key="d0">MRI Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan is a medical imaging technique that uses radio waves and powerful magnets to generate detailed images of internal structures without using x-rays.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI Scan is a medical imaging technique that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to take detailed pictures of internal body structures without using radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a diagnostic imaging technique that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed images of organs and tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a diagnostic imaging technique that uses powerful magnets and a computer to create detailed images of the organs and tissues inside the body.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a diagnostic procedure that uses radio waves and powerful magnets to take pictures of the inside of the body without radiation exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a diagnostic tool used to visualize the brain and detect swelling due to leukemia, such as leukemic meningitis.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a medical imaging technique that employs strong magnetic fields to create detailed images of organs and tissues in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a medical imaging technique that uses powerful magnets and radio waves to create detailed images of the body's internal structures.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a medical imaging technique that utilizes radio waves and magnets to create images of internal body structures without the use of x-rays.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a medical imaging technique used to visualize internal structures of the body, particularly the brain in the context of diagnosing lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses powerful magnets and radio waves to produce detailed images of organs and tissues in the body, particularly useful for diagnosing prostate conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a type of imaging test that uses magnetic waves to produce detailed images of the inside of the body, useful in assessing prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan uses radio waves and magnets to produce images of the body's internal structures without radiation exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan utilizes magnetic fields and radio waves to generate images of organs and structures within the body, without using radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI scan is a medical imaging procedure used for detailed visualization of the prostate and surrounding tissues, aiding in cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormones">
      <data key="d0">Hormones</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormones are chemical substances produced by the endocrine glands that regulate various functions in the body, including metabolism and stress response.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormones are chemicals released by glands that regulate various body functions, and their manipulation is crucial in breast cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Gland Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Gland Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal gland cancer is a rare form of cancer that affects the adrenal glands, with an incidence of approximately 1 to 2 people out of every 1 million.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pheochromocytoma">
      <data key="d0">Pheochromocytoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pheochromocytoma is a tumor that begins in the adrenal medulla and may cause symptoms like high blood pressure and rapid heartbeat; most are benign but can be cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenoma is a type of non-cancerous growth (polyp) that forms on the inner lining of the colon and rectum, with the potential to become invasive rectal cancer over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenoma is a type of non-cancerous polyp found in the colon and rectum that has the potential to evolve into invasive rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenoma is the most common type of colon polyp that can eventually lead to invasive colon cancer if left untreated.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenomas are benign tumors that can form in glands and may progress to colorectal cancer if not monitored or removed.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenal adenoma is the most common type of tumor in the adrenal gland, usually noncancerous, and can either be functioning or nonfunctioning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is a hereditary condition that can increase the risk of developing adrenal tumors among other cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carney Complex">
      <data key="d0">Carney Complex</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carney complex is a genetic disorder that predisposes individuals to various tumors including adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP)">
      <data key="d0">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FAP is a rare genetic condition that leads to the development of numerous adenomas in the colon and rectum, significantly heightening the risk of colorectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;FAP is a rare inherited syndrome that causes numerous polyps to develop in the colon and rectum, which can eventually lead to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Familial adenomatous polyposis is a hereditary condition that increases the likelihood of developing several types of cancers, including adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Paraganglioma-Pheochromocytoma Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Paraganglioma-Pheochromocytoma Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This syndrome is a hereditary condition that raises the risk for developing adrenal tumors, particularly pheochromocytomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Li-Fraumeni Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Li-Fraumeni Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a hereditary condition that significantly increases the risk for developing various cancers, including adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a rare genetic condition that significantly increases the risk of various cancers, including breast, brain, and pancreatic cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lynch Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Lynch Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lynch Syndrome is a genetic condition that increases the risk of colorectal cancer and other cancers in individuals.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch Syndrome is an inherited condition that increases the risk for several types of cancer, including colorectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch Syndrome is an inherited condition that increases the risk of developing several types of cancer, including colorectal cancer, due to defective MMR genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition associated with an increased risk of developing certain cancers, potentially including adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of various cancers, including bladder cancer, often necessitating genetic counseling.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition that increases the risk of colorectal and other cancers due to mutations in mismatch repair genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited genetic condition with mutations in mismatch repair genes that significantly increases the risk of colorectal and other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome, also known as hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC), is a genetic disorder that increases the risk of several types of cancer, including pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1 (MEN 1)">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1 (MEN 1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Men 1 is a hereditary syndrome that affects hormone-producing glands, which may also include an increased risk for adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2 (MEN 2)">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2 (MEN 2)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MEN 2 is related to a group of rare hereditary disorders that can lead to tumors in endocrine glands and may increase the risk of adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (NF1)">
      <data key="d0">Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (NF1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neurofibromatosis type 1 is a genetic disorder that can increase susceptibility to various tumors, including adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome (VHL)">
      <data key="d0">Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome (VHL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">VHL syndrome is a hereditary condition associated with an increased risk for various tumors, including adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® are comprehensive health care guidelines specifically designed for managing adrenal tumors, providing patients with essential information about treatment and care options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET Scan is a diagnostic imaging test that employs radioactive tracers to visualize metabolic activity in tissues, commonly used to detect cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET Scan is a diagnostic imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to visualize metabolic processes in tissues, often used to detect cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is a diagnostic imaging technique that uses radioactive tracers to visualize active cancer cells in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is a diagnostic imaging technique that uses radioactive tracers to visualize active cancer cells within the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is a type of imaging test that employs a radioactive tracer to help visualize cancerous cells and assess treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is a type of imaging test that helps doctors see how tissues and organs are functioning by detecting radioactive tracers in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is a type of medical imaging test that uses radioactive substances to visualize the functioning of organs and tissues, primarily to detect cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is an imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to observe metabolic activity in tissues and can indicate cancer presence.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is an imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to observe metabolic activity in tissues, helping to identify cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is an imaging test that utilizes a radioactive substance to visualize cells in the body, particularly in assessing cancerous activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan uses a radioactive tracer to identify cancer cells in the body and monitor their growth.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan uses a radioactive tracer to visualize metabolic processes in the body and is particularly useful in oncology.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan uses radioactive drugs to identify the presence of cancer cells in the body, appearing as bright spots on the images.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan uses radioactive tracers to detect cancer cells in the body by highlighting areas of increased metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan utilizes a radioactive tracer injected into a vein to produce images of cellular activity and is particularly effective in detecting cancer cells based on their metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan utilizes a radioactive tracer to visualize metabolic processes in the body, often used in cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A positron emission tomography (PET) scan utilizes a radioactive drug to visualize cancer cells in the body, often in combination with CT or MRI for enhanced accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;PET scan combines radioactive glucose detection to identify cancerous cells in the body during the diagnostic process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family Health History">
      <data key="d0">Family Health History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family health history encompasses the health issues experienced by blood relatives, providing essential information that may influence an individual's health risks and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history involves gathering information about health issues that family members have experienced, which can provide crucial insights into genetic risks and inform medical decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history is a record of health conditions and diseases in a family, which can influence individual cancer risk assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history is the record of diseases and health issues faced by relatives, providing context for an individual's cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history refers to the health information about an individual’s family, important in assessing risks for inherited diseases like rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history refers to the record of diseases and health conditions in a person's family, important for assessing hereditary cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history refers to the record of diseases and health issues in family members, which can influence individual health assessments and cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Binder">
      <data key="d0">Medical Binder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Medical Binder is an organizational tool that helps individuals keep important health-related documents, results, and information in one accessible location.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical binder is a personal organizational tool used by patients to keep records of medical documents, treatment plans, and test results.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical binder is an organizational tool for patients to keep their medical records, test results, and treatment information in one place.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical binder is an organizational tool used by patients to compile and manage their health records, test results, and information about their medical history and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical binder is an organizational tool used to keep various medical records, test results, and essential health information in one accessible place for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contact Information">
      <data key="d0">Contact Information</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contact Information refers to the details of individuals on a patient’s care team, crucial for communication regarding healthcare needs and emergencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Physician">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Physician</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Primary Care Physician is a healthcare provider who manages general health and can guide patients in their cancer screening and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Primary Care Physician is a medical professional responsible for overseeing a patient's general health and coordinating care, particularly in emergencies.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician (PCP) plays a significant role in managing a patient’s health and coordinating care during diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Records">
      <data key="d0">Medical Records</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical Records are documents that contain a patient’s health history, treatments, test results, and other information important for ongoing care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health History">
      <data key="d0">Health History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health History is a comprehensive record detailing a patient’s past medical illnesses, surgeries, medications, and treatments, essential for guiding current clinical decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Health history includes a patient's past and current medical conditions, medications, and lifestyle factors that may influence treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Health history involves the collection of a patient’s medical background, which can include past illnesses, treatments, surgeries, and family health issues, and is essential for diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Health history is a record of health information of a patient and their family, crucial for understanding disease risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Health history refers to documented medical background of an individual, which plays a crucial role in assessing cancer risks and predispositions.&lt;SEP&gt;Health history refers to the medical background of a patient, crucial for determining appropriate treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms are physical or mental features that indicate a condition or disease, such as headaches or sensitivity to light in the context of leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms are physical or mental indicators that can signify the presence of a disease, such as those experienced by cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of ovarian cancer often include bloating, abdominal pain, changes in bowel habits, and urinary issues, which can be mistaken for other conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of primary CNS lymphoma may include weakness, changes in coordination, memory loss, personality changes, and loss of bladder control.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms refer to the physical or mental indicators of a condition, such as lumps or pain, which are crucial for guiding diagnostic processes in healthcare.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms refer to the physical or mental indicators of a medical condition, which are important for diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms refer to the signs or indications of health issues, including those related to prostate conditions such as difficulty urinating or sexual dysfunction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family History">
      <data key="d0">Family History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family History compiles health issues and conditions that affect family members, helping identify genetic risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history encompasses information about health issues in a patient's relatives, which can influence cancer risk assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history in the context of prostate cancer relates to the record of health issues within a person's family that can affect their risk of developing the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history involves documenting health issues of relatives to assess inherited cancer risks, crucial in evaluating bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history involves documenting the health issues of relatives, which can influence cancer risk assessments by healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history involves understanding the health conditions of blood relatives, which can be significant in assessing risk factors for diseases like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history is relevant as it can highlight hereditary risks for certain cancers, including liver cancer, aiding in patient evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history pertains to the health background of blood relatives, significant in assessing genetic risks for diseases like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history records health issues in relatives, which can play a crucial role in assessing genetic predisposition to cancers such as stomach cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to health issues and diseases of blood relatives which may affect an individual's health risks and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to information about health issues in close relatives that may influence the understanding and diagnosis of cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the health background of an individual’s relatives, which can influence disease risks, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the health background of one's relatives, which can influence the likelihood of developing bladder cancer, highlighting genetic risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the health conditions present among family members, which can increase the risk of developing prostate cancer if close relatives have the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the medical history of diseases among family members, which can affect individual cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the record of health conditions in family members, which can affect individual risk for diseases like prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the record of health issues among blood relatives, which can provide insights into genetic predispositions to diseases like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Materials">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Materials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast Materials are substances used in imaging tests to improve the clarity of the images produced, aiding in accurate diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Cells">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably and can be detected using various imaging techniques like PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably and can invade nearby tissues and organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably and can invade nearby tissues, leading to cancerous tumors and other health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably and can invade other tissues in the body, leading to cancerous growths.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably and can lead to tumors; they are targeted in treatments like chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably in the body, leading to cancerous tumors and symptoms associated with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably, representing various grades indicating their aggressiveness and growth rate.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that grow uncontrollably and can spread to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET/MRI Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET/MRI Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PET/MRI scans are medical imaging techniques used to locate cancer cells in the body, providing more accurate results than using either scan alone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG-PET scans utilize radioactive sugar as a tracer to detect active cancer cells based on their higher glucose consumption.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Somatostatin Receptor (SSTR) Antagonist">
      <data key="d0">Somatostatin Receptor (SSTR) Antagonist</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SSTR antagonists are proteins found on cells that bind to somatostatin, which is involved in controlling hormones in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biochemical Tests">
      <data key="d0">Biochemical Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biochemical tests measure various substances, often hormones, in bodily fluids to assess the presence of adrenal tumors or syndromes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal squamous cells. It is treated based on various criteria including tumor grade and type.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal squamous cells. Its diagnosis and treatment depend on various factors including tumor grade and type.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that can be categorized based on its risk level, either as local, regional, or metastatic.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that is primarily treated with surgery and may involve risk assessments.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer (CSCC) is a common form of skin cancer originating from squamous cells in the skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a common form of skin cancer that is highly treatable and often arises from the skin's squamous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer that can develop from precancerous lesions and is characterized by the uncontrolled growth of skin cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer that develops from squamous cells, typically found in the outer layer of the skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is identified as the second most common type of skin cancer, arising primarily from sunburns or prolonged sun exposure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melanoma">
      <data key="d0">Melanoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Melanoma is a less common but more aggressive type of skin cancer, originating from melanocytes, the cells that produce skin pigment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sun Exposure">
      <data key="d0">Sun Exposure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sun exposure is a significant risk factor for developing squamous cell skin cancer, particularly for individuals with fair skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indoor Tanning">
      <data key="d0">Indoor Tanning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Indoor tanning is associated with an increased risk of skin cancer, as it exposes the skin to harmful UV radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Toxins">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Toxins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Environmental toxins include various pollutants such as tobacco smoke, vehicle exhaust, and arsenic, which contribute to skin damage and increase the risk of squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental toxins, such as tobacco smoke and heavy metals, can damage skin and lead to skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroendocrine Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Neuroendocrine Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuroendocrine tumors are abnormal growths that originate from neuroendocrine cells, which can be influenced by hormone activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SSTR-PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">SSTR-PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SSTR-PET scans use tracers that bind to somatostatin receptors on tumors, aiding in the detection of neuroendocrine tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scaly Patches">
      <data key="d0">Scaly Patches</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Scaly patches are early signs of skin cancer, particularly squamous cell carcinoma, indicating abnormal skin cell changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ulcers">
      <data key="d0">Ulcers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ulcers are sore areas that can indicate chronic wounds, and they are linked to higher risks for developing cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tanning Machines">
      <data key="d0">Tanning Machines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tanning machines are devices used for indoor tanning that expose users to ultraviolet (UV) radiation, increasing skin cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cells">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cells are thin, flat cells found in the skin's outer layer, which can become cancerous in conditions like squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cells">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal cells produce new skin cells and are located in the base of the epidermis; they can develop into basal cell carcinomas under certain conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melanocytes">
      <data key="d0">Melanocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Melanocytes are cells responsible for producing melanin, the pigment in skin, and can become cancerous in melanoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin cancer encompasses different types of cancer that affect the skin, the most common being basal cell carcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and melanoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the skin cells, with squamous cell carcinoma being one of its most common forms, often resulting from UV exposure or other damaging agents.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin cancer refers to the uncontrolled growth of skin cells, often as a result of excessive sun exposure or other environmental factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actinic Keratosis">
      <data key="d0">Actinic Keratosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Actinic keratosis is a precancerous skin condition marked by scaly or rough patches, often a result of prolonged sun exposure, indicating an elevated risk of developing squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinic keratosis is a skin condition characterized by rough patches caused by excessive sun exposure, which increases the risk of developing squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinic keratosis is a skin condition characterized by rough, scaly patches caused by prolonged exposure to ultraviolet (UV) light, often leading to increased risk of skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN">
      <data key="d0">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) provides resources and guidelines for cancer treatment and survivorship, particularly for patients and healthcare professionals.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN is an organization dedicated to providing comprehensive guidelines and resources for cancer patients, including information on non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) and small cell lung cancer (SCLC) through their website and patient guides.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN is an organization that provides guidelines and resources for cancer patients, including information regarding non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) and small cell lung cancer (SCLC).&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN is the National Comprehensive Cancer Network, an organization providing information and resources for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) provides guidelines and resources for cancer care, including patient information and distress management.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) provides guidelines and resources for patients regarding cancer care, including Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an alliance of cancer centers that aims to improve the quality and effectiveness of cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an alliance of leading cancer centers that provides guidelines and recommendations for cancer treatment, including clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization dedicated to improving the quality, effectiveness, and efficiency of oncology care through guidelines and education.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides guidelines and resources for cancer treatment, including publications on non-small cell lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides guidelines for cancer treatment, including fertility preservation options and patient support resources.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines and recommendations, including referrals and counseling for patients at risk of skin cancer due to genetic syndromes.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines and resources aimed at improving the quality of cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines and resources for cancer patients, emphasizing patient education and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines and resources for cancer treatment, including information on throat cancer and related conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Xeroderma Pigmentosum">
      <data key="d0">Xeroderma Pigmentosum</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Xeroderma pigmentosum is a rare genetic condition that increases susceptibility to skin cancer due to the body’s inability to repair DNA damage caused by UV light.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recessive Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa">
      <data key="d0">Recessive Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa (RDEB) is a rare inherited condition that causes fragile skin, increasing the likelihood of skin cancer if trauma occurs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune System">
      <data key="d0">Immune System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The immune system defends the body against infections but can become weakened due to various health conditions, increasing the risk of squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The immune system is a complex network of cells and proteins that defends the body against infections and diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;The immune system is a complex network of cells and tissues that help defend the body against infections and diseases, including cancers like follicular lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The immune system is a complex network of cells and tissues, including the lymphatic system, that protects the body from infection and disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Skin Biopsy is a medical procedure where a small sample of skin is removed for testing to diagnose potential skin cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;A skin biopsy is a medical procedure to remove a small sample of skin tissue for examination under a microscope to determine if cancer cells are present.&lt;SEP&gt;A skin biopsy is a medical procedure used to diagnose skin conditions, including squamous cell carcinoma, by removing a sample for laboratory analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin biopsy is a medical procedure that involves removing a sample of skin for diagnosis of skin conditions, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scars and Chronic Wounds">
      <data key="d0">Scars and Chronic Wounds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Scars and chronic wounds can lead to squamous cell carcinoma, particularly when the cancer forms in damaged skin, referred to as Marjolin’s ulcer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of blood cancer that can increase the risk of developing anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is a type of cancer affecting white blood cells, which may weaken the immune system and increase susceptibility to skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)">
      <data key="d0">Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV can weaken the immune system, increasing susceptibility to HPV infection and the development of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system, making individuals more vulnerable to infections and various types of cancer, including CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system, potentially leading to an increased risk of developing squamous cell carcinoma due to immunosuppression.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is a virus that can affect health and treatment but should not impact the options described for cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is a virus that weakens the immune system, putting individuals at higher risk for various infections and cancers, including CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Lesion">
      <data key="d0">Skin Lesion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A skin lesion is an abnormal area of skin which may indicate conditions like skin cancer and requires examination and possibly a biopsy for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Biopsy Types">
      <data key="d0">Skin Biopsy Types</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">There are several types of skin biopsy, including shave, punch, and excisional biopsies, each utilized to diagnose skin conditions such as squamous cell carcinoma based on the lesion's characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunosuppressants">
      <data key="d0">Immunosuppressants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunosuppressants are medications taken to prevent organ rejection after transplants but can reduce the immune system's ability to counteract diseases, thereby increasing cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunosuppressants are medications that weaken the immune system, increasing the risk of infection and cancer, including anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic counseling provides support and information for individuals concerned about inherited conditions, including predisposition to cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic counseling provides support and information to individuals with genetic syndromes that may elevate their risk of skin cancer, enabling informed decisions about health monitoring and interventions.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic counseling provides support and information to patients regarding genetic testing, results interpretation, and implications for family health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) is a common type of skin cancer that typically affects lighter-skinned individuals, mainly found on sun-exposed areas like the head and neck, and is usually highly treatable if detected early.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Punch Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Punch Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A skin punch biopsy is a medical procedure where a small piece of skin is removed to test for genetic predisposition to conditions, including leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A skin punch biopsy is a procedure to remove a small piece of skin and tissue, often employed to diagnose skin conditions including various skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excision Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Excision Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An excision biopsy involves the surgical removal of all or part of a lesion for diagnostic purposes, used to assess skin cancers like CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Dye">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Dye</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast dye is a substance injected during imaging procedures like CT scans to enhance the visibility of internal structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Portals">
      <data key="d0">Patient Portals</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient portals are online platforms that allow patients to access their medical records and communicate with healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient portals are online systems that allow patients to access their health information, including test results, and communicate with healthcare teams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nodal Disease">
      <data key="d0">Nodal Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nodal disease refers to the condition where cancer spreads to lymph nodes, which can severely impact treatment options and outlook.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head-to-Toe Skin Exam">
      <data key="d0">Head-to-Toe Skin Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A head-to-toe skin exam is a comprehensive examination performed by a healthcare provider to identify lesions or skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetics">
      <data key="d0">Genetics</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetics refers to the study of heredity and the variation of inherited characteristics which can influence the risk of developing certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marjolin’s Ulcer">
      <data key="d0">Marjolin’s Ulcer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Marjolin’s ulcer is a form of squamous cell skin cancer that arises in chronic skin wounds, burns, or scars, often making it more challenging to treat.&lt;SEP&gt;Marjolin’s ulcer is a specific variant of squamous cell skin cancer that can arise from chronic skin wounds, burns, or scars and is often more difficult to treat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthcare Team">
      <data key="d0">Healthcare Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Healthcare Team consists of medical professionals who collaborate to manage and provide care for patients with AML and other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;The healthcare team for cancer care includes various specialists such as oncologists, dermatologists, and nurses, who work together to manage patient treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The healthcare team includes medical professionals involved in the diagnosis and treatment of breast cancer, guiding patients through the care process.&lt;SEP&gt;The healthcare team refers to the group of professionals involved in a patient's care, including doctors, nurses, and various specialists.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dermatologists">
      <data key="d0">Dermatologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dermatologists are medical doctors specializing in diagnosing and treating skin conditions, including skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Dermatologists are specialists in diagnosing and treating skin diseases, including skin cancers, and perform skin exams to monitor changes in skin health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Mohs Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohs surgeons are dermatologists specialized in the Mohs surgery technique, which involves removing cancerous skin tissue layer by layer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs surgeons are specialized dermatologists trained to perform Mohs micrographic surgery, which ensures complete removal of cancerous skin while preserving surrounding healthy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical oncologists are doctors trained to diagnose and surgically treat cancerous tumors that may have spread beyond the primary site.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical oncologists are surgeons with specialized training in the treatment of cancer, performing operations to remove tumors or cancerous cells from the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Medical Oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical oncologists are specialists in treating cancer with chemotherapy, targeted therapy, or immunotherapy, and they oversee the overall management of a patient's cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical oncologists specialize in prescribing cancer treatment drugs and managing chemotherapy for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologists">
      <data key="d0">Pathologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologists are doctors who examine tissue samples to determine the presence and stage of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologists are medical doctors who analyze tissue samples from biopsies or surgeries to diagnose the presence and type of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plastic Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Plastic Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Plastic surgeons are specialists who reconstruct and repair body parts after the removal of cancerous tissue and are involved in post-surgical recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic surgeons specialize in reconstructive surgeries to restore appearance after tumor removal and can work with other specialists during the procedure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation oncologists are medical doctors specializing in treating cancer with radiation therapy to destroy cancer cells and reduce tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation oncologists are trained to use radiation therapy as a treatment for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharmacists">
      <data key="d0">Pharmacists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Pharmacists are healthcare professionals responsible for dispensing medications and advising patients on their proper use and potential side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Pharmacists prepare and dispense medications and educate patients about their proper use and potential side effects, especially in cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geneticists">
      <data key="d0">Geneticists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Geneticists focus on studying genes and heredity, providing insights related to genetic predispositions to cancer and testing options for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Geneticists study hereditary traits and genetic disorders, offering counseling and treatment options for cancer predisposition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Nurses">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Nurses</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncology nurses are nurses specializing in providing care for cancer patients, administering chemotherapy, monitoring side effects, and offering emotional support.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncology nurses provide direct patient care, administer treatments, manage side effects, and support patients throughout their cancer treatment journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncology nurses provide direct patient care, support during treatment, and help manage side effects of cancer therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncology nurses provide hands-on patient care and manage treatment-related side effects during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Scans">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging scans are medical procedures that create pictures of the inside of the body to aid in the diagnosis and monitoring of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging scans, such as MRI or CT scans, are diagnostic tools used to visualize the extent of cancer and assess whether it has spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging scans, such as MRIs, are diagnostic tools used to visualize the internal structures of the body, helping to assess tumors and staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A lymph node biopsy involves the surgical removal of lymph node tissue to evaluate for cancerous changes, particularly in the diagnosis of lymphoid malignancies.&lt;SEP&gt;A lymph node biopsy is a procedure that involves removing lymph nodes to examine them for the presence of cancer cells, especially when cancer spread is suspected.&lt;SEP&gt;A lymph node biopsy is a specific type of biopsy that involves removing lymph node tissue for diagnostic testing, particularly in cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Types">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Types</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment types for squamous cell skin cancer vary and can include surgery, radiation therapy, systemic therapy, and participation in clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An Oncologist is a physician specialized in the treatment and management of cancer patients, collaborating closely with the care team.&lt;SEP&gt;An Oncologist is a specialized physician focusing on diagnosing and treating patients with cancer, often working with imaging and test results.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a doctor specialized in the treatment of cancer, providing care and treatment plans for patients with conditions like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical doctor focused on diagnosing and treating cancer, including providing treatment plans for patients diagnosed with CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical doctor specialized in diagnosing and treating cancer, responsible for recommending treatment options and managing patient care.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical doctor specializing in diagnosing and treating cancer, responsible for conducting follow-up visits and exams for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical doctor specializing in the treatment of cancer, responsible for reviewing lab results and planning treatment for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical professional specialized in diagnosing and treating cancers such as CLL, offering guidance on treatment options and patient care.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical professional who diagnoses and treats cancer, consulting with patients about their cancer diagnosis and treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck surgeons are specialized surgeons who treat cancers of the head and neck regions and may perform reconstructive surgeries post-cancer removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Superficial Treatments">
      <data key="d0">Superficial Treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Superficial treatments refer to therapies that target only the top layer of the skin for treating early stages of squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cryotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Cryotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Cryotherapy is a procedure that freezes and destroys cancer cells, used for conditions like actinic keratosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryotherapy is a treatment method that uses extreme cold to freeze and destroy abnormal skin cells, commonly used for actinic keratosis and skin cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryotherapy is a treatment option that involves freezing abnormal tissues, often used for actinic keratoses and other skin conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryotherapy is a treatment that involves freezing cancerous tissues using extreme cold to destroy abnormal cells, commonly utilizing liquid nitrogen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Photodynamic Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Photodynamic Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Photodynamic therapy is a medical treatment that uses light-sensitive drugs and light to destroy cancer cells, particularly in skin cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Photodynamic therapy is a treatment that uses a light-sensitive drug and a specific light source to kill cancer cells, often applied in the treatment of skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-Fluorouracil">
      <data key="d0">5-Fluorouracil</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) is a chemotherapy medication applied directly to the skin for the treatment of actinic keratosis, contrasting with systemic chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Calcipotriene">
      <data key="d0">Calcipotriene</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Calcipotriene (Sorilux, Dovonex) is a topical cream often used in conjunction with 5-fluorouracil to activate the skin’s immune system and treat actinic keratosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imiquimod">
      <data key="d0">Imiquimod</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Imiquimod is an immune response modifier used topically to treat abnormal skin growths by activating the immune system, targeting abnormal skin cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tirbanibulin">
      <data key="d0">Tirbanibulin</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Tirbanibulin (Klisyri) is a topical therapy for actinic keratosis, functioning as a microtubule inhibitor that destroys fast-growing cells causing the condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curettage">
      <data key="d0">Curettage</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Curettage is a method involving scraping off the skin with a spoon-shaped tool, often utilized for the treatment of skin lesions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PDEMA/Mohs Surgery">
      <data key="d0">PDEMA/Mohs Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">PDEMA/Mohs surgery is a precise surgical technique used to treat skin cancers by excising them layer by layer.&lt;SEP&gt;PDEMA/Mohs surgery is a specialized surgical technique used to remove skin cancers while preserving normal tissue, involving meticulous analysis of removed tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node dissection is a surgical method used to remove lymph nodes that may contain cancer during or after anal cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node dissection is a surgical procedure to remove lymph nodes for cancer testing, important for staging and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node dissection is an extensive surgical procedure for removing lymph nodes that may contain cancer cells, often performed under general anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A sentinel lymph node biopsy (SLNB or SNB) is a surgical procedure that removes sentinel lymph nodes to check for cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel lymph node biopsy involves removing tissue samples from sentinel lymph nodes to check for the spread of cancer cells from a primary tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel lymph node biopsy is a surgical procedure to remove the first lymph node(s) that cancer cells are likely to spread to from a primary tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel lymph node biopsy is a surgical procedure used to assess cancer spread by examining sentinel lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel lymph node biopsy is a surgical technique to identify and remove lymph nodes most likely to contain cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The sentinel lymph node biopsy is a surgical procedure to determine if cancer has spread to lymph nodes from a primary tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperkeratosis">
      <data key="d0">Hyperkeratosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperkeratosis refers to the thickening of the outer layer of skin, which is often a symptom associated with actinic keratosis and other skin conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topical Tazarotene">
      <data key="d0">Topical Tazarotene</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Topical Tazarotene is a retinoid medication that is related to vitamin A and is used to treat skin conditions like actinic keratosis by promoting skin cell turnover.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Keratolytics">
      <data key="d0">Keratolytics</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Keratolytics are topical agents such as urea, lactic acid, or salicylic acid that help in softening and breaking down the thickened outer layer of the skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trichloroacetic Acid">
      <data key="d0">Trichloroacetic Acid</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Trichloroacetic acid is used in chemical peels to help remove damaged layers of skin, effective in treating actinic keratosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laser Resurfacing">
      <data key="d0">Laser Resurfacing</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Laser resurfacing is a cosmetic procedure that uses focused light to treat skin imperfections and is sometimes used for more severe cases of actinic keratosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d0">Standard Surgical Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard Surgical Excision is a surgical procedure to remove tumors along with a margin of healthy tissue; this is a common treatment for skin cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard surgical excision involves removing the tumor along with a margin of normal tissue, typically performed in-office for superficial cancer cases like squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard surgical excision involves the removal of cancerous tissue along with a margin of healthy tissue to ensure complete removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Mohs Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohs Surgery is a precise surgical technique used to remove cancerous skin while preserving surrounding healthy tissue, particularly effective for treating high-risk CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs Surgery is a specialized surgical technique used to treat skin cancer by excising the cancerous tissue layer by layer, minimizing damage to surrounding healthy tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs Surgery is a surgical technique used to treat skin cancer by removing cancerous tissue while preserving healthy skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs surgery is a precise surgical technique used for skin cancer whereby layers of cancer-containing skin are removed and examined until no further cancerous cells remain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrodesiccation">
      <data key="d0">Electrodesiccation</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrodesiccation is a method that combines curettage with electrical destruction of remaining cancer cells, aiding in the treatment of skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer surgery refers to procedures aimed at removing cancerous tissues and may require general anesthesia for extensive surgeries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologist">
      <data key="d0">Pathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pathologist examines biopsies and tissue samples to determine the presence and details of conditions like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist analyzes cells and tissues for diagnosis, particularly in biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist examines tissue samples under a microscope to determine if the tissue is cancerous and to classify the type and grade of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a doctor specialized in analyzing laboratory samples and is key for cancer diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor specialized in diagnosing diseases by examining tissue samples and is key in reviewing bone marrow tests for AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor who analyzes biopsies to provide diagnoses and information about cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor who examines tissue samples and helps in diagnosing diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor who examines tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical expert responsible for analyzing biopsy samples and other tissue examinations to diagnose diseases, particularly cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical expert who examines cancerous tissues to determine the type and grade of ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical expert who examines tissue samples microscopically to diagnose diseases like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical professional who examines removed tissue and nodes under a microscope for cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical professional who studies biopsy samples to identify cancer cell patterns and assigns Gleason scores to help determine cancer risk and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical specialist who examines biopsy samples under a microscope to check for cancer and provide diagnostic reports.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical specialist who examines tissue samples to diagnose diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a physician who examines tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose diseases, including cancer from biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a specialized doctor who examines the removed tissue samples to diagnose disease, including types of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist studies tissue samples and cells to diagnose diseases, including cancers from breast biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist, including a hematopathologist, analyzes biopsy samples to diagnose cancer and provide staging and biomarker testing information.&lt;SEP&gt;The pathologist analyzes biopsy samples and provides essential diagnostic information needed to determine the type and stage of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgeon is a medical professional specialized in performing surgeries, including cancer-related operations.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgeon is a medical professional who conducts operations and played a crucial role in diagnosing the narrator's inflammatory breast cancer after multiple visits.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgeon is a physician who performs operations and may be involved in the surgical removal of thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgeon is a qualified medical professional who performs surgical procedures, including surgeries to treat ovarian cancer, depending on the tumor's size and location.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgeon is a specialized medical professional who performs surgical procedures, including thyroid surgeries for cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgeon specializes in performing operations and was pivotal in diagnosing the narrator's inflammatory breast cancer after extensive office visits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External Beam Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">External Beam Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">External beam radiation therapy (EBRT) is a commonly used method of radiation therapy targeting specific areas to kill cancer cells and reduce tumor size.&lt;SEP&gt;External beam radiation therapy (EBRT) targets tumors with radiation directed from a machine.&lt;SEP&gt;External beam radiation therapy is a treatment that uses high-energy rays to target and kill cancer cells in the cervical area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brachytherapy involves placing a radiation source close to or directly in contact with the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of internal radiation therapy that involves placing radioactive material directly inside or near a tumor to localize treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of internal radiation therapy used to treat cervical cancer by placing radioactive material directly inside the body near the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of internal radiation treatment where a radioactive source is placed inside or near the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of radiation treatment where a source of radiation is placed inside or close to the area requiring treatment, often used for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a localized cancer treatment that involves placing a radioactive source directly inside or next to the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is an internal radiation therapy involving the placement of radioactive material inside the body to target cervical tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adjuvant Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Adjuvant Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adjuvant therapy is additional treatment following primary surgery aimed at reducing cancer recurrence risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Adjuvant therapy is systemic treatment administered after surgery to eliminate residual cancer cells and reduce recurrence risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled division of abnormal cells in the body, which can spread to other areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled division of abnormal cells in the body, which may spread to different parts through metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the body, affecting various tissues and organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the body, requiring various treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells, which can significantly impact health and well-being.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a disease characterized by uncontrolled cell growth that can lead to various health problems, including metastasis and tumor formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a disease characterized by uncontrolled cell growth, which can form tumors and metastasize to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a disease characterized by uncontrolled cell growth, which can invade and spread to other parts of the body. The text discusses various types, diagnostics, and treatments related to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a disease characterized by uncontrolled cell growth, which can lead to tumors and spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a disease characterized by uncontrolled cell growth, which can metastasize to various parts of the body and requires detailed imaging for diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a group of diseases characterized by abnormal cell growth that has the potential to invade or spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a group of diseases characterized by the uncontrolled growth and spread of abnormal cells, leading to various physical and emotional challenges for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a group of diseases characterized by uncontrolled cell division and the potential to spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a group of diseases characterized by uncontrolled cell growth and can occur in various parts of the body, including the thyroid.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a group of diseases involving abnormal cell growth with the potential to invade or spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer refers to a collection of diseases caused by uncontrolled cell division that can spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer refers to a group of diseases characterized by uncontrolled cell growth, and it can affect various parts of the body including lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer refers to a range of diseases characterized by the uncontrolled growth and spread of abnormal cells in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A radiation oncologist is a doctor specializing in treating cancer with radiation therapy, important in the management of thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a doctor who specializes in treating cancer with radiation therapy, often involved in thyroid cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a medical doctor specializing in administering radiation therapy for cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a medical professional who utilizes radiation therapy as a treatment option for patients with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a physician specializing in cancer treatment using radiation therapy, frequently involved in lung cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is responsible for prescribing and planning radiation therapy for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist prescribes and administers radiotherapy to eliminate cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist specializes in treating cancer using radiation therapy as part of the patient's treatment plan.&lt;SEP&gt;The radiation oncologist prescribes and manages radiation therapy as part of the treatment plan for bladder cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cisplatin">
      <data key="d0">Cisplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cisplatin is a chemotherapy drug containing platinum, used to treat various cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a chemotherapy drug that can be part of treatment regimens for perianal cancers, often used alongside 5-FU.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a chemotherapy drug used to treat various types of cancer, known for its potential side effects such as kidney damage and hearing problems.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a chemotherapy medication used in the treatment of various cancers, including anal cancer, and is given intravenously.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug commonly used in treating cervical cancer due to its effectiveness at killing cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug commonly utilized in the treatment of cervical cancer, known for its effectiveness in killing cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug that is effective against various cancers but may cause kidney damage and other side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug used to treat ovarian cancer, particularly in platinum-sensitive cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug used to treat squamous cell skin cancer and other types of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug used to treat various cancers, including cervical cancer, often in systemic therapy regimens.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug used to treat various cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carboplatin">
      <data key="d0">Carboplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carboplatin is a chemotherapy drug that contains platinum and is used in treating squamous cell skin cancer and other malignancies.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is a chemotherapy drug used in cancer treatment, primarily effective against ovarian cancer, with differing side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is a chemotherapy drug used in various regimens to treat anal cancer, especially when it recurs or metastasizes.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is a chemotherapy medication used in treating several types of cancer, including ovarian cancer, known for its effectiveness in combination therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug recommended for treating ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is an alternative chemotherapy drug that may be used for patients who cannot tolerate cisplatin's side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is an alternative platinum drug used when cisplatin is not suitable for some patients due to its harsher side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is another chemotherapy drug used in cancer treatment, specifically effective for squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is another platinum-based chemotherapy agent that treats squamous cell skin cancer among other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is another platinum-based chemotherapy agent used in the treatment of ovarian cancer, often in combination with other therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="General Anesthesia">
      <data key="d0">General Anesthesia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">General anesthesia is a medically induced coma used during surgery to ensure patients are unconscious and free of pain during their procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;General anesthesia is a medically induced state of unconsciousness that allows patients to undergo surgical procedures pain-free.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wound Healing">
      <data key="d0">Wound Healing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Wound healing is the process of recovery and repair of tissues after surgery or injury, with various types of healing methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Substance Injection">
      <data key="d0">Substance Injection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Substance injection refers to the technique where a radioactive agent is injected to locate sentinel lymph nodes during surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Graft">
      <data key="d0">Skin Graft</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A skin graft is a surgical procedure that involves transplanting skin from one area to another to aid healing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Intention Healing">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Intention Healing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondary intention healing refers to the natural healing process when a wound is left open to heal without stitches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Targeted Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Targeted therapy drugs specifically attack cancer cells based on genetic markers, offering a tailored treatment option for bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy focuses on specific pathways or mechanisms in cells to treat cancers like primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy involves drugs that target specific cancer cell markers and is often used for particular types of cancers that can't be treated with standard surgery or radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy involves medicines that target specific receptors on cancer cells, such as EGFR inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a cancer treatment that uses drugs or other substances to precisely identify and attack cancer cells based on specific characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a cancer treatment that uses drugs to target specific molecules involved in the growth and spread of cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a focused treatment approach for advanced prostate cancer designed to attack specific cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a form of treatment that uses drugs or substances to precisely identify and attack cancer cells based on specific characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a tailored cancer treatment that specifically targets cancer cell mutations or abnormal proteins, potentially relevant for prostate cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a treatment option that focuses on specific characteristics of cancer cells, such as their genetic profiles, to combat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy refers to drugs that specifically target cancer cells based on genetic changes, providing a personalized approach to bladder cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy specifically addresses molecular targets involved in cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy uses drugs that specifically target cancer cell mutations or pathways, providing a more personalized treatment approach.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy uses drugs to specifically attack cancer cells while minimizing damage to normal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy utilizes medications designed to attack specific cancer cell receptors like the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor (EGFR)">
      <data key="d0">Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor (EGFR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">EGFR is a protein on the surface of cancer cells that regulates cell division; targeting it may hinder cancer growth.&lt;SEP&gt;EGFR is a protein that is overexpressed in some cancer cells, leading to uncontrolled growth and division; targeted therapies block its activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cetuximab (Erbitux)">
      <data key="d0">Cetuximab (Erbitux)</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Cetuximab is an EGFR inhibitor used to treat squamous cell skin cancer and other forms of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cetuximab is an EGFR inhibitor utilized in treating head and neck cancers and certain types of squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapy enhances the immune system's ability to find and destroy cancer cells and uses drugs like PD-1 inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy involves using immune checkpoint inhibitors to treat cancer and is preferred when available.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a biological treatment using the body's immune system to fight cancer and may be used alongside chemotherapy for bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a cancer treatment that utilizes the body's immune system to fight cancer cells, enhancing the natural ability to identify and destroy these cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a form of cancer treatment that uses the body's immune system to fight cancer, being explored for prostate cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a newer treatment modality that enhances the immune system's ability to fight bladder cancer, particularly effective in advanced cases and for tumor recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment aiming to enhance the immune response against tumor cells in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment modality that enhances the immune system's capacity to identify and eliminate cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment option for metastatic anal cancer that aims to enhance the body’s immune response against cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment that boosts the body's immune response against bladder cancer cells, often used in more advanced stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment that enhances the body's immune system to find and destroy cancer cells, utilizing various drugs and techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment that helps the immune system fight cancer and is being explored as a potential option for ovarian cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a treatment that helps the immune system fight cancer and may be used alongside other treatments for primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a type of cancer treatment that harnesses and enhances the innate powers of the immune system to fight cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is an emerging treatment option that uses immune checkpoint inhibitors in some anal cancer patients as an alternative to surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is an innovative treatment option employed in managing advanced-stage prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy uses the body’s immune system to fight cancer, often employing check-point inhibitors that enhance immune response against cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy, specifically with immune checkpoint inhibitors, is a recommended treatment option for eligible patients with advanced squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Programmed Death Ligand 1 (PD-1)">
      <data key="d0">Programmed Death Ligand 1 (PD-1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-1 is a protein that, when inhibited, can enhance immune responses against cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;PD-1 is an immune checkpoint protein that, when inhibited, can boost the immune response against cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cemiplimab (Libtayo)">
      <data key="d0">Cemiplimab (Libtayo)</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Cemiplimab is a PD-1 inhibitor used for squamous cell skin cancer treatment, helping the immune system fight cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cemiplimab is a PD-1 inhibitor used for treating squamous cell skin cancer and enhances immune system functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)">
      <data key="d0">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Pembrolizumab is a PD-1 inhibitor that is effective in treating multiple cancer types, including squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab is an immunotherapy drug that works as a checkpoint inhibitor for treating dMMR/MSI-H colon cancer by enhancing the immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab is an immunotherapy drug used as a checkpoint inhibitor for the treatment of certain types of cancer, including dMMR/MSI-H colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab is another PD-1 inhibitor used to treat squamous cell skin cancer and enhance immune responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab is another immune checkpoint inhibitor used to treat various cancers, increasing immune system activity against tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab, also known as Keytruda, is an immunotherapy drug used to treat certain types of cancers, particularly those with specific mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA)">
      <data key="d0">U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The FDA is a governmental agency responsible for regulating and approving drugs, including those used in cancer treatment and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA is the U.S. regulatory body that approves safe and effective drugs, devices, and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA is the regulatory agency responsible for approving new drugs and ensuring their safety and efficacy for public use.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. FDA is a governmental agency responsible for the regulation and approval of drugs and therapies in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic therapies are treatments that can influence both cancerous and normal cells, potentially leading to various side effects, and are administered intravenously or orally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravenous Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravenous Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravenous therapy involves administering medication directly into the bloodstream through a vein, commonly used for chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravenous therapy refers to the administration of treatments directly into the bloodstream via veins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eligibility Criteria">
      <data key="d0">Eligibility Criteria</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Eligibility criteria are the specific conditions that must be met for participants to join a clinical trial, ensuring comparability among participants.&lt;SEP&gt;Eligibility criteria are the specific guidelines determining who can participate in a clinical trial based on conditions like age and health history.&lt;SEP&gt;Eligibility criteria are the specific requirements that potential participants must meet to enroll in a clinical trial, ensuring safety and consistency among participants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Informed Consent">
      <data key="d0">Informed Consent</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Informed consent is a crucial ethical and legal requirement ensuring that patients understand the risks and benefits of participating in clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Informed consent is a process ensuring that participants understand the nature of a clinical trial, including its risks and benefits, before joining.&lt;SEP&gt;Informed consent is a process through which participants understand the purpose and risks of a clinical trial before agreeing to participate.&lt;SEP&gt;Informed consent is a process through which patients are educated about the details of medical procedures or trials they are considering, ensuring they understand the potential risks and benefits.&lt;SEP&gt;Informed consent is the process where trial participants are informed about the details of a study, including risks, benefits, and rights before enrolling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Cancer Centers">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Cancer Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Cancer Centers are facilities that provide cancer treatment and clinical trial information in the United States.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Cancer Centers are specialized centers in the United States dedicated to cancer treatment and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Cancer Centers are specialized facilities in the United States that focus on providing comprehensive cancer care and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Cancer Centers are a network of comprehensive cancer care facilities committed to improving the quality, effectiveness, and efficiency of cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Cancer Centers are recommended places in the United States for cancer treatment and clinical trials, providing important resources for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Cancer Institute">
      <data key="d0">National Cancer Institute</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. government agency focused on cancer research and provides resources for clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. National Library of Medicine">
      <data key="d0">U.S. National Library of Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) offers resources and information about clinical trials worldwide through clinicaltrials.gov.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANCER Information Service">
      <data key="d0">CANCER Information Service</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">CANCER Information Service (CIS) is a service provided by NCI to assist patients in finding cancer-related information and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topical Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Topical Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Topical therapy involves applying medication directly to the skin and is used for pre-cancerous lesions like actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Placebo">
      <data key="d0">Placebo</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A placebo is a substance with no therapeutic effect, often used in clinical trials as a control to compare against active treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A placebo is an inactive substance that has no therapeutic effect, often used in clinical trials to compare against new treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Placebos are inactive substances used in clinical trials to compare the effect of new treatments against non-active treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Standard Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard treatment refers to the conventional therapies widely accepted and used for cancer treatment prior to participating in a clinical trial.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard treatment refers to the current best-known methods of treating cancer before considering clinical trial interventions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actinic Keratoses">
      <data key="d0">Actinic Keratoses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Actinic keratoses are precancerous skin lesions that arise from prolonged exposure to sunlight and may require treatment options such as topical therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curettage and Electrodesiccation">
      <data key="d0">Curettage and Electrodesiccation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Curettage and Electrodesiccation (C&amp;E) is a minimally invasive surgical method used to treat low-risk squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Curettage and Electrodesiccation (C&amp;E) is a procedure used to treat low-risk squamous cell skin cancer by scraping away cancerous tissue and drying it out with electricity.&lt;SEP&gt;Curettage and Electrodesiccation (C&amp;E) is a treatment method for low-risk skin cancer involving scraping and drying out cancerous tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Curettage and electrodesiccation are surgical procedures used to remove squamous cell skin cancers and precancerous lesions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Risk Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk assessment involves evaluating a patient's potential for cancer progression or recurrence based on various clinical factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk assessment involves evaluating the characteristics of a cancer to determine its potential for recurrence and appropriate treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Local Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that has not yet spread to nearby lymph nodes and is often treatable by surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recurrent Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Recurrent Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurrent tumors are cancerous growths that return after treatment, indicating a high-risk category if they appear post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nerve Damage">
      <data key="d0">Nerve Damage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nerve damage in cancer can indicate a high-risk condition, particularly when tumors invade nearby nerves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Grade">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The tumor grade indicates how fast the glioma is likely to grow, separating them into low-grade and high-grade categories.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor Grade is a classification that determines how aggressive a Squamous Cell Carcinoma may be, based on the appearance of cancer cells under the microscope.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor Grade refers to the classification of cancer cells based on how they appear microscopically. Higher grades indicate a more aggressive cancer type that is likely to spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor grade refers to the classification of tumor cells based on their appearance under a microscope, which can indicate the aggressiveness of the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Organ Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Organ Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An Organ Transplant involves replacing a failing organ with a healthy one from a donor, which often requires immunosuppressive drugs and increases cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;An organ transplant involves transferring an organ from a donor to a recipient, requiring immune suppression, which raises the risk of developing cancers like CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Organ transplants involve medical procedures that can suppress the immune system, raising the risk of developing conditions such as primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Type">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Type</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Type refers to the specific classification of tumors based on tissue origin, which impacts diagnosis and treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphovascular Invasion">
      <data key="d0">Lymphovascular Invasion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphovascular Invasion is a condition where cancer cells invade lymphatic or blood vessels, indicating a higher risk of metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weakened Immune System">
      <data key="d0">Weakened Immune System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Weakened Immune System is a condition that increases susceptibility to infections and cancers, often due to external factors such as certain diseases or treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Corticosteroid Use">
      <data key="d0">Corticosteroid Use</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Corticosteroid Use refers to the treatment involving steroids to reduce inflammation, but can weaken the immune system when used long-term.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-Based Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-Based Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-Based Chemotherapy is a systemic treatment option involving drugs that contain platinum, used alongside radiation for enhanced efficacy against CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-Based Chemotherapy is a type of cancer treatment using platinum compounds, effective for various cancers including CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is a first-line therapy recommended for treating metastatic anal cancer, including options like carboplatin and paclitaxel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Doctors">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Doctors</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Doctors are medical professionals specialized in diagnosing and treating cancer, involved in assessing and advising on treatment paths.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Local CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local CSCC (Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma) refers to the cancer that has not spread to nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Local Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that is confined to the skin and has not spread to nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Local Squamous Cell Carcinoma is a form of skin cancer that has not spread to nearby lymph nodes and is most often treated with surgical methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d0">High-Risk CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-Risk Squamous Cell Carcinoma is a designation for skin cancer that exhibits features indicating a greater chance of recurrence or metastasis, necessitating aggressive treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;High-Risk Squamous Cell Carcinoma refers to local CSCC with features such as deep invasion or close margins that increase the likelihood of recurrence or spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Very High-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Very High-Risk CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Very High-Risk Squamous Cell Carcinoma signifies an advanced stage of skin cancer that poses significant treatment challenges and complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trial</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Clinical Trial is a research study involving patients to test new treatments or drugs, such as chemotherapy or immunotherapy, for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study conducted with participants to evaluate new medical interventions, treatments, or drug therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study that tests new treatments or therapies for ovarian cancer, particularly for those with persistent or recurrent forms of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study that tests new treatments or therapies in patients, offering alternative options for cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial offers experimental treatments, including chemotherapy, targeted therapy, or immunotherapy, for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that test new therapies or drugs on patients and are crucial for advancing cancer treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that test new treatments for primary CNS lymphoma and are recommended for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials for CNS lymphoma provide opportunities for patients to explore innovative treatments while contributing to medical research.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cemiplimab-Rwlc (Libtayo)">
      <data key="d0">Cemiplimab-Rwlc (Libtayo)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cemiplimab-Rwlc, marketed as Libtayo, is an immune checkpoint inhibitor used in immunotherapy for patients with advanced skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Options for CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Options for CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment options for Squamous Cell Carcinoma may include surgery, radiation therapy, systemic therapy, and immunotherapy, depending on the stage and risk of the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trials for CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trials for CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical Trials provide an opportunity to test new treatments like chemotherapy or immunotherapy on patients with Squamous Cell Carcinoma, aiming to improve outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Therapy for CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Therapy for CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation Therapy is a treatment method that uses high-energy rays to target and kill cancer cells, particularly useful when surgery is not an option.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapy with Checkpoint Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapy with Checkpoint Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapy with immune checkpoint inhibitors, like Cemiplimab-Rwlc (Libtayo), is a treatment option for advanced Squamous Cell Carcinoma that enhances the body's immune response against cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regional CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Regional CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regional CSCC refers to squamous cell carcinoma that has spread to nearby lymph nodes and may require aggressive treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Regional Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC) is a form of skin cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Regional Squamous Cell Carcinoma refers to cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes, requiring more advanced treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical Excision is the removal of cancer along with normal tissue surrounding it and is the first-line treatment for various stages of Squamous Cell Carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgery for bladder cancer can involve removing tumors in the early stages or performing radical cystectomy in advanced cases, often being a crucial part of treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery for bladder cancer can range from minor procedures to remove tumors to radical cystectomy for advanced stages, often combined with other therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery involves the physical removal of tumors or cancerous tissues from the bladder, potentially including invasive procedures like bladder removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a common treatment for anal cancer that may involve procedures like abdominoperineal resection when the cancer returns to the anal area.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a common treatment method for gliomas, involving the removal of the tumor to alleviate symptoms and prevent further growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a common treatment option for both early and advanced-stage prostate cancer, which may involve the removal of the prostate gland or affected tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a common treatment option for cancer aimed at removing tumors and affected tissues from the patient's body.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a common treatment option for patients with earlier stages of pancreatic cancer, aiming to remove tumors and improve outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a major treatment option for cervical cancer, particularly in early stages, involving the removal of affected tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a medical procedure aimed at removing tumors and examining lymph nodes, crucial for determining cancer stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a medical procedure performed to remove tumors and affected tissues, which may take several hours and requires preparation, including anesthesia.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a medical procedure that can be part of cancer treatment, impacting staging accuracy through the examination of removed tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a medical procedure that is generally not an option for treating primary CNS lymphoma due to the risks involved.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a medical procedure used to remove tumors and lymph nodes, especially in cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a primary treatment method for early-stage cervical cancer, involving the removal of cancerous tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a procedure performed to remove cancerous tissue or organs, often being a key treatment option in cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery may involve the removal of the prostate gland or affected tissues and is an option for treating both early- and advanced-stage prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-based Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-based Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-based chemotherapy is a common systemic treatment for metastatic cervical cancer, often utilized in combination regimens.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is a preferred systemic therapy option used in conjunction with radiation for treating certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic squamous cell skin cancer (CSCC) refers to cancer that has spread beyond its original site, often requiring different treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy (SBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SBRT is a specialized radiation technique targeting small areas with high doses of radiation to kill cancer cells, used in specific cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Exams">
      <data key="d0">Skin Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin exams are medical check-ups performed by a doctor to monitor and detect any signs of skin cancer, particularly following treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin exams are routine check-ups after cancer treatment to monitor for recurrence of cancer, particularly skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Experts">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Experts</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Experts are authorities in cancer care recommendations who provide guidelines for patients dealing with conditions such as skin cancer and chronic myeloid leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) provides guidelines and recommendations for cancer treatment and follow-up care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tumor is an abnormal growth of cells that can be benign or malignant, with malignant tumors being cancerous and capable of metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;A tumor is an abnormal mass of tissue that can be benign or malignant, and it can spread to other areas of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A tumor is an abnormal mass of tissue that can be benign or malignant, and must be evaluated for diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A tumor is an abnormal mass of tissue that may be cancerous or non-cancerous, and can be identified during scans and biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;A tumor is an abnormal mass of tissue that results from excessive cell division, and may be benign or malignant, potentially leading to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extracapsular Extension">
      <data key="d0">Extracapsular Extension</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extracapsular extension refers to the spread of cancer cells beyond the capsule of lymph nodes, indicating more aggressive disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastasis describes the spread of cancer from the primary tumor to distant parts of the body, significantly impacting treatment and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the process by which cancer cells spread from the original site to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the process through which cancer spreads from the original site to other parts of the body, significantly impacting staging and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the spread of cancer from its primary site to distant organs, significantly impacting the overall prognosis and treatment approaches in breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the process by which cancer spreads from its original site to other parts of the body, influencing treatment and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the process by which cancer spreads from one part of the body to another, important in assessing different cancer stages and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the process where cancer cells spread from the primary tumor in the kidney to form new tumors in other parts of the body, such as lungs or bones.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the process whereby cancer cells spread from the primary tumor to other parts of the body, forming secondary tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the spread of cancer cells from the prostate to other areas of the body, indicating advanced-stage prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Therapy with Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Therapy with Systemic Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This combined treatment approach is used when surgery is not possible, enhancing the effectiveness of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a type of blood cancer caused by a specific genetic abnormality, leading to uncontrolled growth of blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia Chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia Chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Philadelphia chromosome is a genetic abnormality associated with Chronic Myeloid Leukemia, resulting from a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22.&lt;SEP&gt;The Philadelphia chromosome is an abnormal chromosome 22 formed due to the BCR::ABL1 fusion, serving as a hallmark of CML.&lt;SEP&gt;The Philadelphia chromosome is an abnormal chromosome associated with CML, resulting from translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22, which causes cancerous cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood cells are classified into red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, each serving critical roles in the body's function and immunity.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood cells include red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, each serving a vital function in the body, particularly in the context of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CML Treatment Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">CML Treatment Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CML Treatment Guidelines by NCCN outline the recommended practices for monitoring and treating patients with chronic myeloid leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT Scans">
      <data key="d0">CT Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">CT scans are imaging tests used to look for signs of cancer in nearby lymph nodes and other body parts, especially in patients who have undergone treatment for CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scans are imaging tests utilized in the surveillance of cancer to monitor any changes in the body post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood">
      <data key="d0">Blood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood is a liquid connective tissue circulating in the body, utilized in various diagnostic tests, including karyotyping and PCR.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a tissue that carries oxygen and nutrients, composed of red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, playing vital roles in the body’s functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a vital fluid in the body composed of plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, responsible for transporting oxygen and nutrients.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a vital fluid that circulates in the body, used as a sample for various diagnostic tests including karyotyping.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a vital tissue composed of plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, and it plays a key role in carrying oxygen and nutrients throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a vital tissue composed of plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, playing essential roles in oxygen transport and immune function.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a vital tissue composed of plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, responsible for transporting oxygen, nutrients, and waste throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a vital tissue containing plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets essential for various bodily functions including immune response and nutrient transport.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is the fluid that circulates in the body, carrying oxygen and nutrients, and is affected by ALL due to the production of abnormal lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Red Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Red Blood Cells (RBCs or erythrocytes) are responsible for carrying oxygen from the lungs to the rest of the body and returning carbon dioxide back to the lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Red blood cells (RBCs) are responsible for transporting oxygen from the lungs to the rest of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Red blood cells are responsible for carrying oxygen throughout the body, essential for overall body functioning.&lt;SEP&gt;Red blood cells are responsible for transporting oxygen throughout the body, and their count may be low in patients diagnosed with CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">White Blood Cells (WBCs or leukocytes) are a crucial component of the immune system, helping the body fight infections and diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;White blood cells (WBCs) are part of the immune system and help fight infections.&lt;SEP&gt;White blood cells are crucial components of the immune system, responsible for identifying and combating infections and diseases, including cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;White blood cells are crucial components of the immune system, tasked with fighting infections and diseases, and are particularly relevant in the diagnosis of CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;White blood cells are key components of the immune system, including various types such as lymphocytes, which play roles in fighting infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platelets">
      <data key="d0">Platelets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platelets (PLTs or thrombocytes) are involved in blood clotting and help control bleeding when injuries occur.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets are blood cells that help in blood clotting and controlling bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets are small cell fragments essential for blood clotting, and their levels may also be affected in patients with CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets are small cell fragments that play a critical role in blood clotting and controlling bleeding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood stem cells are immature cells in bone marrow that can differentiate into various types of blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood stem cells are immature cells located in bone marrow that can develop into any type of blood cell.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood stem cells are the early cells found in the bone marrow that develop into various types of blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood stem cells are the initial cells in the blood formation process, capable of differentiating into red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood stem cells are undifferentiated cells in the bone marrow that develop into various types of blood cells, including lymphocytes, red cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood stem cells are undifferentiated cells in the bone marrow that give rise to the various types of blood cells, including myeloid progenitor cells, and are essential for normal blood cell production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Guideline Book">
      <data key="d0">Guideline Book</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Guideline Book is a resource provided by NCCN for patients to help them make informed decisions about their cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CML">
      <data key="d0">CML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CML, or Chronic Myeloid Leukemia, is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the overproduction of myeloid progenitor cells and linked to a specific genetic mutation.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a type of cancer that affects blood and bone marrow, characterized by the overproduction of myeloid cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1 gene">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1 gene</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The BCR::ABL1 gene is a fusion gene responsible for the development of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML), resulting from chromosome translocation.&lt;SEP&gt;The BCR::ABL1 gene is an abnormal fused gene that results from the Philadelphia chromosome formation and is responsible for the uncontrolled growth of blood cells in CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CML Specialist">
      <data key="d0">CML Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A CML specialist is a medical professional with expertise in diagnosing and treating Chronic Myeloid Leukemia and can provide targeted care for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NSCLC is a type of lung cancer characterized by the growth of abnormal cells in the lungs, originating from different types of lung cells.&lt;SEP&gt;NSCLC is the most common type of lung cancer, characterized by uncontrolled cell growth in the lungs, forming carcinomas that can metastasize to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-small cell lung cancer refers to a group of lung cancer subtypes, including adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma, making up a larger proportion of lung cancers compared to SCLC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloid progenitor cells are immature blood cells that develop into myeloblasts and other types of non-lymphoid blood cells, playing a crucial role in the formation of blood and are relevant in conditions like CML.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells are precursor cells in bone marrow that differentiate into non-lymphoid blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells are the precursors to myeloblasts and other non-lymphoid blood cells, playing a significant role in CML development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blast Phase CML">
      <data key="d0">Blast Phase CML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blast Phase CML (BP-CML) is an advanced form of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia characterized by the presence of increased numbers of immature lymphoid or myeloid progenitor cells, affecting treatment options and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Blast Phase CML (BP-CML) is an advanced stage of CML where there is an increase in immature blood cells, impacting treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment for CML">
      <data key="d0">Treatment for CML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment for CML focuses on inhibiting the BCR::ABL1 protein to manage blood cell growth and improve patient outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment for Chronic Myeloid Leukemia aims to inhibit the BCR::ABL1 protein's activity and may include medications, targeted therapies, and supportive care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for CML">
      <data key="d0">Testing for CML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for Chronic Myeloid Leukemia involves various diagnostic methods including blood tests and genetic analysis to confirm the presence of the BCR::ABL1 gene and assess disease progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Physician (PCP)">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Physician (PCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Primary Care Physician (PCP) is a medical doctor responsible for managing a patient's overall health care, including updates on treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;A Primary Care Physician is a healthcare provider who manages overall patient care and coordinates with specialists during the treatment of conditions like CML.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary care physician coordinates patient care, oversees general health issues, and collaborates with specialists for cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary care physician is a doctor who oversees the general health of the patient and coordinates their cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary care physician is a personal healthcare provider who coordinates patient care and remains informed about their medical history.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary care physician plays a vital role in coordinating patient care, maintaining communication with the care team, and monitoring health changes.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician acts as the initial point of contact for patients, coordinating care, and managing referrals for cancer testing and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician helps coordinate and inform the care team regarding a patient's ongoing health needs and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician is a doctor who plays a foundational role in the health care system, providing ongoing care and coordination for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician is a healthcare provider who serves as the first point of contact for patients and coordinates overall care, including cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician is a key partner in patient care, responsible for coordinating health and keeping informed about the patient’s status and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Care Support System">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Care Support System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Cancer Care Support System encompasses emotional and logistical assistance provided by family, friends, caregivers, and community resources to help individuals cope with cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC) is a type of lung cancer that originates from specific lung cells and is characterized by varying types including adenocarcinoma, large cell carcinoma, and squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenocarcinoma is a subtype of NSCLC that typically arises from the mucus-producing cells in the alveoli.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a subtype of Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer that originates from the mucus-secreting cells lining the alveoli, commonly found in non-smokers or those with little smoking history.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a subtype of non-small cell lung cancer that originates in the glandular cells of the lungs, often associated with a better prognosis compared to SCLC.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer that develops in glandular tissues, often arising from adenomatous polyps in the rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer that originates in an adenoma, leading to colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer that originates in the glandular cells lining the stomach; it is the most common form of stomach cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cervical cancer that develops in glandular cells of the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cervical cancer that originates in glandular cells and can impact treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cervical cancer that originates in glandular cells of the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of esophageal cancer that starts in the mucus-secreting cells of the esophagus, commonly found in the lower part and increasingly prevalent in North America and Western Europe.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is the most common type of NSCLC, typically forming from cells that line the alveoli and produce mucus.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is the most common type of cancer that forms in the rectum, originating from glandular cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is the most common type of rectal cancer that originates from an adenoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is the most common type of rectal cancer that originates in adenomas and is characterized by invasive growth patterns in the rectal tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Large Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Large Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Large cell carcinoma is a form of NSCLC that can originate from any of the large cells found throughout the lung tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Large cell carcinoma is a subtype of non-small cell lung cancer that is characterized by large, abnormal cells and can grow rapidly and aggressively.&lt;SEP&gt;Large cell carcinoma is a variant of NSCLC that arises from large cells found throughout the airways.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1 NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1 NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1 NSCLC indicates that the cancer is still localized within the lung and has not spread beyond it.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1 NSCLC refers to the early stage of non-small cell lung cancer where the cancer has not spread outside the lung.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2 NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2 NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 NSCLC indicates that the cancer has begun to grow into nearby tissues or lymph nodes, considered locally advanced.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 2 NSCLC indicates that the cancer has started to invade nearby tissues and may affect local lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3 NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3 NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3 NSCLC involves a more extensive spread within the lung and possibly into surrounding lymph nodes, indicating an advanced stage but not metastatic.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 3 NSCLC signifies that the cancer is more advanced, having spread to nearby lymph nodes and structures surrounding the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4 NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4 NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 NSCLC indicates metastatic cancer at the time of diagnosis, which has spread to other organs outside of the lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4 NSCLC is the most advanced stage, where cancer metastasizes to other parts of the body, indicating a severe progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Airways of the Lungs">
      <data key="d0">Airways of the Lungs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Airways of the lungs refer to the network of passages that air travels through to reach the lungs, including the trachea, bronchi, and bronchioli.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trachea">
      <data key="d0">Trachea</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The trachea, commonly known as the windpipe, is the main airway that carries air from the throat to the bronchi in the lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;The trachea, or windpipe, is a crucial structure in the respiratory system that conducts air to the lungs, and is located anatomically in relation to the throat and oropharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchi">
      <data key="d0">Bronchi</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchi are the two main air passages that branch off from the trachea and lead into each lung.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchioli">
      <data key="d0">Bronchioli</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchioli are the smaller airways that further divide from the bronchi within the lungs, leading to the alveoli.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alveoli">
      <data key="d0">Alveoli</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Alveoli are small air sacs in the lungs where oxygen is exchanged with the blood, playing a critical role in respiration.&lt;SEP&gt;Alveoli are tiny air sacs at the end of bronchioli where the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide occurs between the air and blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stages of Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stages of Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages of lung cancer refer to the classification of cancer based on its spread and severity, including stages 1 to 4.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic cancer is a stage where cancer cells have spread from the original site to distant parts of the body, complicating treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer is cancer that has spread from its original site to other parts of the body, posing additional treatment challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer occurs when cancer spreads from the original site to other parts of the body, complicating treatment and management strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond its original site to other parts of the body, making it more complicated to treat.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread far from its original site, which is particularly significant in the context of pancreatic cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread from its original site to other parts of the body, complicating treatment and management.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread from the primary site to other parts of the body, such as lungs and distant lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread to other parts of the body, making it more difficult to treat.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancers that have spread from their original site to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supportive Care">
      <data key="d0">Supportive Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supportive care aims to alleviate symptoms and side effects of cancer and its treatment, enhancing the patient's quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care aims to relieve the symptoms of cancer or the side effects of cancer treatment, improving the quality of life for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care encompasses a range of resources aimed at improving quality of life and comfort for patients with metastatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care encompasses treatments that help manage symptoms and side effects of therapies for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care focuses on improving the quality of life of patients and addressing the side effects of treatment rather than directly targeting the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care includes treatments aimed at improving quality of life during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care includes treatments and interventions aimed at alleviating side effects of ALL therapies and improving patients' quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care involves services to improve the quality of life of cancer patients alongside primary treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care is crucial for managing side effects and maintaining the quality of life during bladder cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care provides additional emotional, physical, and financial support to patients undergoing cancer treatment to enhance their quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care refers to a range of measures taken to improve the quality of life and alleviate symptoms for patients undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care refers to the specialized medical care that addresses the symptoms and challenges faced by cancer patients, enhancing their overall well-being and treatment experience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Nodules">
      <data key="d0">Lung Nodules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung nodules are small masses of tissue in the lungs that can be benign or may represent early-stage cancer, requiring thorough evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pulmonologist">
      <data key="d0">Pulmonologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pulmonologist is a medical doctor specializing in diagnosing and treating diseases related to the lungs and respiratory system.&lt;SEP&gt;A pulmonologist is a medical doctor specializing in respiratory conditions, who plays a crucial role in diagnosing and managing lung cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A pulmonologist is a medical professional specializing in lung diseases and functions as a key member of the care team for patients with lung-related issues.&lt;SEP&gt;A pulmonologist is a specialist in lung diseases who may be involved in diagnosing and treating lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thoracic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Thoracic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A thoracic radiologist is a medical professional specializing in imaging and diagnosing conditions of the chest, including lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic radiologist is a specialist focused on imaging tests for conditions related to the chest, including lung diseases and cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic radiologist is a specialist who uses imaging techniques to diagnose conditions of the chest, including lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic radiologist specializes in imaging techniques to diagnose lung diseases by examining chest images.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thoracic Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Thoracic Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A thoracic surgeon is a medical expert who performs surgical operations within the chest, including procedures for lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic surgeon is a medical expert who performs surgical procedures within the chest, including operations related to lung conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic surgeon is a medical specialist trained to perform surgery on the chest, including procedures for lung cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app offers resources and information on cancer treatment options for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app offers users information and support related to cancer treatment and management.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app provides information and guidelines on cancer treatments, including lung cancer screening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung Cancer is a malignant condition that arises in the lungs, primarily affecting the respiratory system and is categorized into different types including Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer and Small Cell Lung Cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lung cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the lungs' cells, primarily affecting the airways and lung tissue, with various subtypes including small cell lung cancer and non-small cell lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC) is a type of fast-growing lung cancer known for its aggressive nature and unique cellular characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Small Cell Lung Cancer is characterized by small oval-shaped, fast-dividing cancer cells and is recognized for its aggressive nature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Nodule">
      <data key="d0">Lung Nodule</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A lung nodule is a small mass of tissue in the lung that may require monitoring and assessment for cancer risk, as most are non-cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging techniques are procedures utilized to visualize the internal structures of the body, crucial for diagnosing lung conditions and monitoring nodules.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging techniques like CT, MRI, and PET scans are essential tools in oncological diagnostics to visualize and assess cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Lung Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung cancer screening involves tests performed on high-risk individuals to detect lung cancer at early, treatable stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco Smoke">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco Smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tobacco smoke contains numerous carcinogenic compounds and is identified as the primary risk factor for developing lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Factors for Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Risk Factors for Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk factors for lung cancer include elements such as smoking, exposure to secondhand smoke, and environmental agents that can increase the likelihood of developing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemopreventive Agents">
      <data key="d0">Chemopreventive Agents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemopreventive agents are treatments aimed at preventing cancer, and research is ongoing about their efficacy for lung cancer prevention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC) is the most prevalent form of lung cancer that encompasses several subtypes, including adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radon">
      <data key="d0">Radon</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radon is a naturally occurring radioactive gas linked to an increased risk of lung cancer, particularly in individuals with prolonged exposure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asbestos">
      <data key="d0">Asbestos</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Asbestos is a mineral known to cause lung cancer when inhaled, contributing to the risk associated with occupational exposure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Air Pollution">
      <data key="d0">Air Pollution</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Air pollution comprises harmful substances in the air that can exacerbate lung conditions and increase lung cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell lung cancer is a category of lung cancer characterized by small, fast-dividing neuroendocrine cells, accounting for about 10% of lung cancers and is known for its aggressive nature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking">
      <data key="d0">Smoking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking cessation is advocated for cancer patients, with doctors providing support for quitting.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a lifestyle choice that can adversely affect health and treatment outcomes for cancer patients, highlighting the need to seek cessation support.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a major risk factor for bladder cancer, contributing to approximately half of all cases due to harmful chemicals inhaled.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is identified as the primary risk factor for developing small cell lung cancer, with a high correlation between SCLC patients and a history of smoking.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is the act of burning tobacco and inhaling its smoke, significantly increasing the risk of developing various cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is the act of inhaling and exhaling the smoke of burning tobacco, significantly impacting health and increasing cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking refers to the inhalation of tobacco smoke, which increases the risk of various cancers and health complications.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking significantly increases the risk of bladder cancer, impacting treatment outcomes and survival rates for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second-Hand Smoke">
      <data key="d0">Second-Hand Smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Second-hand smoke is the smoke exhaled by another person and from tobacco products, increasing the risk of various lung cancers including SCLC.&lt;SEP&gt;Second-hand smoke refers to the involuntary inhalation of smoke by non-smokers from burning tobacco products, which increases the risk of lung cancer in those exposed.&lt;SEP&gt;Second-hand smoke, which is the smoke exhaled by others or from burning tobacco products, is a recognized risk factor for lung cancers, including SCLC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoma is a type of cancer that arises from epithelial cells and can manifest in various organs, including the nasopharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;Carcinoma is a type of cancer that starts in the epithelial cells and can refer to several different forms, including squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Carcinomas are a type of cancer that begins in the cells of the skin or tissue lining organs, which includes several major types of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Provider (HCP)">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Provider (HCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Health Care Providers are medical professionals who offer guidance and assistance in cancer screening and risk assessment for lung cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers (HCPs) are medical professionals who offer patient care and treatment, including discussions about diagnosis and test results.&lt;SEP&gt;The health care provider is responsible for overseeing patient care, conducting tests, and discussing treatment plans with patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCLC">
      <data key="d0">SCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell lung cancer (SCLC) is a fast-growing type of lung cancer primarily associated with smoking, characterized by aggressive behavior and a high prevalence among smokers.&lt;SEP&gt;Small cell lung cancer (SCLC) is a type of lung cancer primarily associated with smoking and has rapid growth characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophageal cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the esophagus and involves uncontrolled growth of cells in that area.&lt;SEP&gt;Esophageal cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the esophagus when abnormal cells grow uncontrollably; it impacts digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Esophageal cancer is a type of cancer that starts in the cells of the esophagus and progresses through several stages, often classified as adenocarcinoma or squamous cell carcinoma, depending on the type of cells affected.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digestive System">
      <data key="d0">Digestive System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The digestive system consists of multiple organs, including the stomach, liver, and intestines, involved in digestion and nutrient absorption.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system encompasses multiple organs, including the stomach and liver, involved in digestion and nutrient absorption.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system includes the organs responsible for breaking down food, absorbing nutrients, and eliminating waste, consisting of the esophagus, stomach, small intestine, colon, and rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system is responsible for the processing of food, nutrient absorption, and waste elimination, involving organs like the esophagus and stomach.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system is responsible for transporting and breaking down food, absorbing nutrients, and eliminating waste from the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system processes food, absorbs nutrients, and eliminates waste, playing a vital role in overall health and directly related to stomach cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking Cigarettes">
      <data key="d0">Smoking Cigarettes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking cigarettes is identified as the main cause of small cell lung cancer (SCLC), with most patients having a significant lifetime exposure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Age over 70">
      <data key="d0">Age over 70</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Being over 70 years of age is a risk factor that increases the likelihood of developing small cell lung cancer (SCLC).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exposure to Carcinogens">
      <data key="d0">Exposure to Carcinogens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Exposure to cancer-causing chemicals, such as radon and asbestos, is a recognized risk factor for developing lung cancers including SCLC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Provider">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary care provider is often the first medical professional to suspect lung cancer in patients based on symptoms or assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interventional Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Interventional Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An interventional radiologist is a physician who performs minimally invasive procedures using imaging guidance, often involved in cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;An interventional radiologist specializes in minimally invasive procedures guided by imaging technologies and may assist in cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Medical Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical oncologist employs systemic therapies like chemotherapy and immunotherapy in cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is a physician specializing in cancer treatment through chemotherapy and other non-surgical methods.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is a physician specializing in diagnosing and treating cancer, often through systemic therapies such as chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is a physician specializing in the treatment of cancer using medications, including targeted therapies and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is a physician who specializes in treating cancer using systemic therapy such as chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist specializes in cancer treatment with medications, including chemotherapy and targeted therapy for cancers such as pancreatic and prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist specializes in treating cancer using chemotherapy and other medications, often collaborating in treatment planning for ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist specializes in treating cancer using chemotherapy and other systemic therapies, often involved in managing SCLC patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist treats cancer in adults using systemic therapy, including chemotherapy and immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist treats cancer with systemic therapies, such as chemotherapy, tailored to the specific needs of AML patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The medical oncologist administers systemic therapies such as chemotherapy to treat Acute Myeloid Leukemia patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The medical oncologist is responsible for coordinating cancer treatment through chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and other systemic therapies for bladder cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of SCLC">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of SCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of SCLC vary based on tumor location and can prevent the lungs from properly acquiring air, leading to various medical issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Guide SCLC">
      <data key="d0">Guide SCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Guide SCLC provides information on the symptoms, diagnosis, and management of small cell lung cancer, facilitating patient understanding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Siewert Types">
      <data key="d0">Siewert Types</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Siewert types are classifications used in staging esophageal cancer based on the tumor's location relative to the esophagus and stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barrett Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Barrett Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Barrett esophagus is a condition where the squamous cells lining the lower esophagus are replaced by abnormal cells due to chronic reflux, increasing the risk of adenocarcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The esophagus is a muscular tube connecting the throat and the stomach, consisting of multiple layers, including the mucosa, submucosa, muscularis propria, and tunica adventitia.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagus is a muscular tube connecting the throat to the stomach, made up of multiple layers and responsible for transporting food.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagus is a muscular tube that connects the throat to the stomach, playing a crucial role in the digestive process by transporting food and liquids.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver">
      <data key="d0">Liver</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The liver can be a site of metastasis for advanced prostate cancer, where cancer cells may spread.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver can be a site of metastasis for advanced prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a vital organ involved in many metabolic processes, including detoxification and the production of bile for digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a vital organ involved in various bodily functions, and when affected by cancer, it can lead to primary liver cancer or metastasis from other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a vital organ that can be a common site for metastasis for esophageal cancer, especially in later stages.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a vital organ that processes blood, filters toxins, and produces bile, and is typically the origin of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreas">
      <data key="d0">Pancreas</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The pancreas is a key organ in the digestive system that plays a role in metabolic processes and can be associated with liver cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;The pancreas is a vital organ in the digestive system, located below the liver and behind the stomach, producing enzymes and hormones essential for metabolic regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;The pancreas is an organ sometimes considered part of the hepatobiliary system, which is involved in digestion and can also be affected by cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The pancreas is an organ that can be affected by the spread of esophageal cancer and can influence treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The pancreas is an organ that produces digestive enzymes and hormones like insulin to regulate blood sugar levels, playing an essential role in digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung">
      <data key="d0">Lung</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The lung is an organ involved in the respiratory system and is a common site for metastasis in various forms of cancer, including liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The lung is an organ of the respiratory system that is often affected by metastasis in various types of cancer, including liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The lung is another common metastatic site for esophageal cancer, along with the liver and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endoscopy is a diagnostic procedure used to examine the esophagus and obtain tissue samples for the diagnosis of esophageal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Endoscopy is a medical procedure using a flexible tube with a camera to visualize and diagnose conditions within the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagogastric Junction">
      <data key="d0">Esophagogastric Junction</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The esophagogastric junction is the area where the esophagus meets the stomach, critical in preventing reflux and facilitating food passage.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagogastric junction, also known as the gastroesophageal junction, is where the esophagus and stomach meet and is significant in the context of esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mucosa">
      <data key="d0">Mucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mucosa is the innermost layer of the esophagus that lines its interior, where esophageal cancers typically begin.&lt;SEP&gt;Mucosa is the innermost layer of the stomach wall that is in contact with food, essential for secretion and protection of the stomach lining.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Submucosa">
      <data key="d0">Submucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Submucosa is a connective tissue layer in the stomach wall that contains blood vessels and nerve cells, supporting the mucosa and muscularis layers.&lt;SEP&gt;Submucosa is the layer of tissue in the esophagus located above the mucosa, providing structural support and housing blood vessels and nerves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscularis Propria">
      <data key="d0">Muscularis Propria</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscularis propria is the layer of muscle in the esophagus responsible for peristalsis, which helps push food toward the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tunica Adventitia">
      <data key="d0">Tunica Adventitia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tunica adventitia is the outermost layer of the esophagus, composed of connective tissue that provides protection and support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer staging is a categorization process that determines the extent of cancer spread, influencing treatment decisions and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is a method used to describe how advanced cancer is in the body, assessing its spread and severity to inform treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is a process that assesses the extent of cancer growth, helping to determine necessary treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is a process used to classify the severity of cancer and guide treatment decisions, crucial for patient prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is the process of determining the extent and severity of cancer, often influencing treatment and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is the process of determining the extent of cancer in a patient's body, often using systems like TNM to evaluate size and spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging refers to the process of determining the extent and severity of cancer, which influences treatment options and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging refers to the system used to describe the severity and extent of cancer, which is essential for prognosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Planning">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Planning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment planning for stomach cancer is based on the results from diagnostic tests, including imaging studies, biopsies, and patient health status.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment planning involves developing a strategy for managing cancer, based on the type, stage, and location of the tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metrics System">
      <data key="d0">Metrics System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The metrics system is commonly used in medical contexts for measuring dimensions such as tumor size during cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an alliance of leading cancer centers that develops guidelines to improve cancer care nationally and internationally.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines and resources for cancer care, particularly focused on blood cancers like Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines and support for cancer treatment, including resources for Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin Lymphoma is a type of cancer that originates in the lymphatic system, primarily affecting lymph nodes in the upper body.&lt;SEP&gt;Hodgkin lymphoma (HL) is a type of lymphoma characterized by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells, often treated with chemotherapy and radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hodgkin lymphoma is a type of blood cancer affecting the lymphatic system, characterized by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients for Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients for Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">This refers to a specific set of guidelines developed by the NCCN focused on the treatment and care for patients diagnosed with Hodgkin Lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Foundation">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Foundation supports cancer patients and their families by funding and distributing NCCN Guidelines for Patients and advancing innovative cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reed-Sternberg Cells">
      <data key="d0">Reed-Sternberg Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Reed-Sternberg cells are large lymphocytes indicative of classic Hodgkin lymphoma, crucial for diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Reed-Sternberg cells are large lymphocytes that are typically present in Hodgkin Lymphoma, distinguishing it from other types of lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MyChart">
      <data key="d0">MyChart</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">MyChart is a health record account that helps patients track their medical appointments and communicate with their care team.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is a health record system that allows patients to track appointments and communicate with their care team.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online patient portal that allows individuals to access their health records, test results, and communicate with their healthcare team regarding their treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online patient portal that allows patients to access their test results, track appointments, and communicate with their care team.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online patient portal that allows users to manage appointments, review health records, and communicate with their healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online patient portal used for accessing medical records, test results, and communicating with healthcare teams.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online portal that allows patients to track appointments and communicate with their healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Doctor Visits">
      <data key="d0">Doctor Visits</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Doctor Visits are appointments scheduled by patients to consult with healthcare providers about their diagnosis and treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transportation">
      <data key="d0">Transportation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transportation includes means of travel provided by friends or family to assist individuals in getting to medical appointments.&lt;SEP&gt;Transportation refers to the means of getting patients to their medical appointments, often facilitated by family or friends during treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Transportation refers to the means of moving patients to medical appointments, which can be provided by caregivers or friends.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emotional Support">
      <data key="d0">Emotional Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Emotional Support encompasses the psychological and emotional assistance provided by friends, family, or peers to patients during their cancer journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Emotional support involves offering psychological and emotional assistance to individuals facing health challenges, particularly cancer, which can be emotionally taxing.&lt;SEP&gt;Emotional support refers to the encouragement and comfort provided by friends, family, or peers during difficult times, especially during health crises.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Test Results">
      <data key="d0">Test Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Test Results include data from blood tests, imaging studies, and biopsies used to formulate treatment plans for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Test results from various examinations are used to determine treatment plans for individuals diagnosed with liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Test results from various examinations provide critical information for determining treatment plans for individuals diagnosed with liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insurance Forms">
      <data key="d0">Insurance Forms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Insurance Forms are documents required for processing medical claims and coverage for treatments and services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Centers">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Centers are specialized medical facilities that focus on the treatment and research of cancer, often part of the NCCN network.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer centers are specialized medical facilities focusing on the treatment and research of various cancers, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer centers provide specialized healthcare services focusing on cancer diagnosis, treatment, and survivorship support.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer centers provide specialized medical care, support, and resources for patients diagnosed with cancer, including access to social workers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines are regularly updated recommendations provided by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network for the treatment of various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic System">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The lymphatic system is a crucial part of the body's immune system that includes lymph vessels, lymph nodes, spleen, and more, facilitating fluid circulation and immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a network of tissues and organs that help rid the body of toxins and waste, and is involved in immune responses.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a network of tissues and organs that help the body fight infections and diseases by transporting lymph, which contains lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a network of tissues and organs that play a crucial role in the immune response and is involved in the progression of lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a network of vessels that help maintain fluid balance, transport lymph, and is involved in immune responses, particularly in CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a vital part of the immune system, comprising lymph vessels, lymph nodes, and organs that help fight infections.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is an extensive network of vessels and nodes that plays a vital role in the immune system, transporting lymph fluid, nutrients, and waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is crucial for removing waste and diseases, but its impairment may impact the liver and its cancerous developments.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is part of the immune system that helps in fighting infections and diseases, involved in the pathophysiology of lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma (NLPHL)">
      <data key="d0">Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma (NLPHL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NLPHL is a rare subtype of Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by popcorn-shaped cells that can transform into aggressive cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL)">
      <data key="d0">Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DLBCL is a fast-growing type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that can develop from NLPHL in rare cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thymus">
      <data key="d0">Thymus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The thymus is a small gland located in the upper chest where T lymphocytes mature and develop, crucial for the adaptive immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;The thymus is a small organ located in the chest, pivotal for T cell development and can be impacted by T-ALL, potentially causing respiratory issues.&lt;SEP&gt;The thymus is a small organ located in the upper chest where lymphocytes develop into T cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spleen">
      <data key="d0">Spleen</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The spleen is a large organ in the lymphatic system, essential for producing lymphocytes and filtering old blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The spleen is the largest organ of the lymphatic system, responsible for producing lymphocytes and filtering blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgkin Lymphoma in Adults">
      <data key="d0">Hodgkin Lymphoma in Adults</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin lymphoma is a blood cancer that primarily affects adults, characterized by the abnormal enlargement of lymph nodes and can lead to symptoms such as fever, weight loss, and night sweats.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgkin Lymphoma in Children and Adolescents">
      <data key="d0">Hodgkin Lymphoma in Children and Adolescents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin lymphoma also occurs in children and adolescents, with specific guidelines available to address their unique care needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphocytes are a subtype of white blood cells that are integral to the body's immune system, including T cells and B cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell crucial to the immune system, involved in the body’s defense mechanisms against infections and diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell involved in the immune response, with three main types: B cells, T cells, and natural killer (NK) cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell pivotal in the immune response, particularly in fighting infections and malignant cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that are central to the immune response, fighting infections and are involved in the development of lymphomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that are central to the immune response, playing roles in fighting infections and cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that play a crucial role in the immune system and are impacted by ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that plays a vital role in the immune response and is involved in the development of ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are white blood cells that play a pivotal role in the immune system, making antibodies and helping fight infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphatic vessels are channels that transport lymph throughout the body, playing a vital role in immune function and fluid homeostasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph">
      <data key="d0">Lymph</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph is a clear fluid that circulates through the lymphatic system, containing white blood cells and helping to remove toxins and waste.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T Lymphocytes (T Cells)">
      <data key="d0">T Lymphocytes (T Cells)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that play a central role in cell-mediated immunity, helping to destroy infected or cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tonsils">
      <data key="d0">Tonsils</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Tonsils are lymphoid tissues located at the back of the throat that help trap pathogens entering the body through the mouth or nose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classic Hodgkin Lymphoma (CHL)">
      <data key="d0">Classic Hodgkin Lymphoma (CHL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Classic Hodgkin lymphoma is the most common form of Hodgkin lymphoma, characterized by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells and specific clinical manifestations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Questions to Ask">
      <data key="d0">Questions to Ask</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">'Questions to Ask' is a tool provided within the NCCN guidelines to facilitate communication between patients and their healthcare teams.&lt;SEP&gt;Questions to ask is a section in the book providing prompts for parents to facilitate discussions with their child's healthcare team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Serosa">
      <data key="d0">Serosa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Serosa is the outermost layer of the stomach wall, a protective membrane that facilitates movement against surrounding organs.&lt;SEP&gt;The serosa is also known as the serous membrane or visceral peritoneum, which is a thin layer covering organs in the abdominal cavity.&lt;SEP&gt;The serosa, also known as the serous membrane or visceral peritoneum, is a thin layer covering organs in the abdominal cavity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bile Duct Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bile Duct Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bile duct cancer is a type of cancer that affects the bile duct, classified into various stages based on the tumor size and extent of spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Bile duct cancer refers to cancer that starts in the bile ducts, which carry bile to the gallbladder and small intestine, and can potentially affect stomach function.&lt;SEP&gt;Bile duct cancer stages refer to the classification of cancer affecting the bile duct, which can grow or spread into surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoma is a category of cancers that originate in lymphocytes, which are immune system cells found in the lymphatic system.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoma is a type of cancer that originates in lymphocytes, a vital component of the immune system, and includes various types such as follicular lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoma refers to cancers that begin in the lymphatic system and can result from the abnormal growth of lymphocytes, like lymphoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular lymphoma (FL) is a common type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by the slow growth of abnormal B lymphocytes in follicles of lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Follicular lymphoma (FL) is a common type of slow-growing Non-Hodgkin lymphoma that typically forms tumors in lymph nodes but can also develop in other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Follicular lymphoma (FL) is a slow-growing form of non-Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by the abnormal growth of B lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germinal Centers">
      <data key="d0">Germinal Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Germinal centers are temporary structures formed in lymph nodes in response to infection, where B cells proliferate and mature.&lt;SEP&gt;Germinal centers are transient structures within lymph nodes where B cells proliferate and undergo maturation in response to immune challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World Health Organization">
      <data key="d0">World Health Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The World Health Organization (WHO) is a global health authority that categorizes diseases, including various types of lymphoma, based on classification systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The World Health Organization (WHO) is an international public health agency responsible for overseeing global health initiatives and classification systems, including those related to various types of lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The World Health Organization (WHO) is an international public health agency that focuses on improving health systems and establishing disease classifications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Consensus Classification">
      <data key="d0">International Consensus Classification</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The International Consensus Classification (ICC) is an organization that categorizes lymphomas based on histological features and grading systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The International Consensus Classification (ICC) provides a framework for categorizing lymphomas, guiding treatment based on the grading system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">B Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B lymphocytes are a type of lymphocyte responsible for producing antibodies as part of the body's adaptive immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;B lymphocytes, or B cells, are a type of lymphocyte involved in producing antibodies.&lt;SEP&gt;B lymphocytes, or B cells, are a type of white blood cell that produce antibodies to fight infections and can develop into lymphomas if misregulated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) is a broad category of lymphatic cancers that includes a variety of subtypes, including follicular lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) is a common type of blood cancer that affects lymphocytes and the lymphatic system.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) is a diverse group of blood cancers that includes various subtypes, often affecting the lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is a more aggressive type of lymphoma that can result from the transformation of follicular lymphoma, representing a faster-growing cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric-type Follicular Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric-type Follicular Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric-type follicular lymphoma (PTFL) is a variant of FL that typically occurs in children but can also affect adults, characterized by enlarged lymph nodes usually in the head and neck region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome Translocation">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome Translocation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosome translocation is a genetic alteration where parts of chromosomes switch places, which is often associated with follicular lymphoma and contributes to abnormal cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCL2 Protein">
      <data key="d0">BCL2 Protein</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BCL2 protein is an anti-apoptotic protein that when overexpressed can lead to cancer growth, often found in tumors of patients with follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Testing">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer testing includes various tests such as blood tests, biopsies, and imaging that are essential for accurate diagnosis and treatment planning for lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Lymphoma Grade 1">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Lymphoma Grade 1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular Lymphoma Grade 1 refers to the earliest stage of follicular lymphoma, characterized by low-grade cancer cells that tend to grow slowly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Lymphoma Grade 2">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Lymphoma Grade 2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular Lymphoma Grade 2 represents an intermediate stage of follicular lymphoma with a slightly increased growth rate compared to Grade 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Lymphoma Grade 3A">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Lymphoma Grade 3A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular Lymphoma Grade 3A is a higher grade of follicular lymphoma treated either as classic FL or transformed into DLBCL based on specific characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Lymphoma Grade 3B">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Lymphoma Grade 3B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular Lymphoma Grade 3B is rare and is classified as diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, indicating a more aggressive cancer type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Cancer Center is a facility that specializes in the treatment and diagnosis of cancer, providing various medical tests and treatment options for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A Cancer Center is a specialized facility equipped for diagnosing and treating various types of cancers, including different grades of follicular lymphoma and their complications.&lt;SEP&gt;A facility specializing in the diagnosis, treatment, and support for patients with cancer, including various imaging and testing methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for FL">
      <data key="d0">Testing for FL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for FL includes a variety of diagnostic methods like biopsies, imaging tests, and blood tests to determine the presence and grade of follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">B Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B symptoms are significant symptoms experienced in cancers like CLL, including fevers, night sweats, and unexplained weight loss, indicating disease activity.&lt;SEP&gt;B symptoms refer to signs commonly associated with lymphomas, including fever, night sweats, fatigue, and weight loss, which may occur in patients with follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mouth Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Mouth Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mouth cancer is a type of cancer affecting the tissues of the mouth and is subjected to a specific staging system to assess its progression and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Mouth cancer refers to cancer that develops in the oral cavity, including various types and stages, primarily affecting the head and neck area.&lt;SEP&gt;Mouth cancer, also known as oral cancer, affects various parts of the mouth and is classified under head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Mouth cancer, or oral cancer, refers to various cancers that develop in the oral cavity, often highlighted by specific types such as Squamous Cell Carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck cancer encompasses a variety of cancers arising in the head and neck regions, including mouth cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer encompasses various malignancies that can affect the oral cavity, throat, and surrounding structures, significantly impacting health and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer is a type of cancer that affects the regions of the head and neck and can involve complex treatment and care options.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer refers to a group of cancers affecting the oral cavity, throat, and surrounding areas, often linked to risk factors like smoking.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer refers to a group of cancers that arise in the head or neck area, including mouth cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer refers to cancers that affect the head and neck regions, which can impact various functions including speech, swallowing, and dentistry.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers encompass a group of cancers that arise in the head or neck area, including various types such as oral cavity and laryngeal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN.org">
      <data key="d0">NCCN.org</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN.org is a website providing comprehensive cancer treatment guidelines and resources for patients and healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a website providing patient guidelines and information related to cancer treatment and fertility preservation.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an established organization that provides clinical practice guidelines for oncology, including resources for cancer patients and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an online portal providing comprehensive cancer-related information and support to patients and healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization that provides guidelines and resources for cancer treatments and patient information.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization that provides patient guidelines and resources for cancer information and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org provides guidelines and information for cancer patients, including resources for mouth and head and neck cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org provides patient guidelines and information for cancer care, including specifics on various cancer types and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org provides resources and guidelines for patients seeking treatment information for various types of cancer, including ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Cavity">
      <data key="d0">Oral Cavity</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The oral cavity is the first part of the digestive system, comprising the lips, cheeks, tongue, gums, and palate.&lt;SEP&gt;The oral cavity is the initial section of the digestive system, which includes the lips, tongue, gums, and roof of the mouth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mouth">
      <data key="d0">Mouth</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The mouth is a key anatomical structure involved in chewing, speaking, and the initial phases of digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tongue">
      <data key="d0">Tongue</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The tongue is a muscular organ in the mouth crucial for taste, chewing, and speaking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gums">
      <data key="d0">Gums</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Gums are the soft tissue that surrounds the base of the teeth, providing support and structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cheeks">
      <data key="d0">Cheeks</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Cheeks are the fleshy parts of the face that form the sides of the mouth, aiding in holding food while chewing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palate">
      <data key="d0">Palate</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The palate is the roof of the mouth, separating the oral cavity from the nasal cavity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alveolar Ridge">
      <data key="d0">Alveolar Ridge</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The alveolar ridge is the bony ridge in the jaw that holds the sockets of the teeth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Retromolar Trigone">
      <data key="d0">Retromolar Trigone</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The retromolar trigone is a small area located behind the wisdom teeth, significant in oral health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Mucosa">
      <data key="d0">Oral Mucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth, critical for protection and sensation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Depth of Invasion (DOI)">
      <data key="d0">Depth of Invasion (DOI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Depth of invasion (DOI) measures how deeply a tumor grows into surrounding tissues, impacting staging and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Depth of invasion is a medical measure to determine how deep a tumor has penetrated into the oral tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app offers resources and guidelines for cancer patients to understand their condition and manage treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC)">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SCC is the most common type of nasopharyngeal cancer, forming in the thin, flat cells of the mucus membranes of the nasopharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) of the mouth is a type of cancer that begins in the squamous cells of the mouth's epithelial layer.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma is the most common type of mouth cancer, arising from the flat cells in the mucous membranes.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma is the most prevalent type of mouth cancer, originating in the flat cells of the mouth's mucous membranes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Provider">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A health care provider is a professional involved in diagnosing and treating cancer, providing essential guidance during the patient’s treatment journey.&lt;SEP&gt;A health care provider is a professional responsible for diagnosing and managing a patient's medical care, including discussing test results and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;A health care provider is a professional who delivers medical services and guidance regarding health issues, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers (HCPs) encompass various types of professionals who provide medical care, specializing in areas such as breast health.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers (HCPs) include various specialists responsible for diagnosing and treating health-related issues, particularly in breast health.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers are medical professionals who give assistance, advice, and treatment to patients regarding their health concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers are professionals that support cancer care, including managing patient histories and conducting tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers are professionals who assist patients in diagnosing and managing health issues, including cancer treatments and examinations.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers carry out examinations, medical history assessments, and diagnostics to identify health issues such as IBC.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers conduct physical exams and medical histories to detect symptoms and possibly diagnose conditions such as IBC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Support">
      <data key="d0">Social Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Social support refers to the assistance and encouragement from friends, family, and peer groups that can help cancer patients cope with their diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer at NCCN.org">
      <data key="d0">Cancer at NCCN.org</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer at NCCN.org provides comprehensive patient guidelines and resources for cancer treatment and understanding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Location">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Location</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor location is critical in determining the type and stage of cancer, impacting treatment planning and outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Stage">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer stage refers to the extent of cancer in the body, determined through clinical assessments and essential for deciding treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer stage refers to the extent of cancer in the body, which helps in determining the appropriate treatment strategy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for Mouth Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Testing for Mouth Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for mouth cancer involves various procedures to determine the presence and stage of cancer, including imaging and biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prevention">
      <data key="d0">Prevention</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prevention strategies for mouth cancer include lifestyle changes, such as avoiding tobacco and limiting alcohol use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support Groups">
      <data key="d0">Support Groups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Support groups provide community and shared experiences for individuals diagnosed with cancer, aiding emotional and psychological support.&lt;SEP&gt;Support groups provide emotional and practical support to cancer patients and their families, facilitating shared experiences and coping strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Support groups provide emotional and social support for cancer survivors, helping them cope with their experiences and share resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Support groups provide emotional and social support to cancer patients, helping them cope with the challenges of their diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Providers">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Health care providers are professionals involved in diagnosing and treating prostate cancer and advising patients on care options.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers consist of medical professionals working to diagnose and treat health issues, including methods for detecting and managing prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers include professionals like doctors and specialists who offer medical assistance and guidance regarding breast health.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers, including doctors and nurses, play a crucial role in the diagnosis, treatment, and support of cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Portal">
      <data key="d0">Patient Portal</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A patient portal is an online application that allows patients to access their test results, schedule appointments, and communicate with healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;A patient portal is an online platform for patients to access their medical records, including test results and treatment details.&lt;SEP&gt;A patient portal is an online platform that allows patients to access their medical records, including test results and health history.&lt;SEP&gt;A patient portal is an online platform that provides patients access to their medical records and test results.&lt;SEP&gt;Online patient portals are platforms that allow patients to access medical records, test results, and communicate with healthcare teams.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient portals provide online access to test results and health records, facilitating communication with health care providers.&lt;SEP&gt;The patient portal is a secure online platform where patients can access their health information, including diagnostic test results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Support Networks">
      <data key="d0">Social Support Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Social support networks consist of friends, family, and community members who provide assistance and encouragement during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition Expert">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition Expert</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nutrition expert or dietitian provides guidance on dietary choices to support health, particularly before and during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nutrition Expert is a healthcare professional who provides dietary guidance to cancer patients to ensure adequate nutrition before, during, and after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speech or Swallowing Therapist">
      <data key="d0">Speech or Swallowing Therapist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A speech or swallowing therapist evaluates and addresses the communication and swallowing difficulties patients may face due to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG)">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">ECOG is an organization that establishes performance status scales to assess a patient's fitness level, crucial for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;The ECOG is a scale used to assess the performance status of cancer patients, influencing treatment decisions based on their overall health and ability to perform daily tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;The ECOG is a scale used to rate a person's general level of fitness and ability to perform daily tasks, important for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is an organization that establishes performance status scales to evaluate general health in cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Preservation">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Preservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility Preservation encompasses methods to maintain the ability to have children, especially following cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility Preservation includes various medical techniques designed to protect a patient's reproductive ability during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility Preservation involves medical techniques and procedures to help individuals maintain their reproductive capabilities after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility Preservation refers to medical techniques used to protect an individual's reproductive potential before undergoing cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation involves measures taken to protect fertility before cancer treatments that may impair reproductive ability.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation involves medical approaches to safeguard a patient's ability to conceive children before undergoing cancer treatments that may affect fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation involves medical strategies to save or protect the reproductive potential of individuals undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation involves medical techniques aimed at storing eggs, sperm, or embryos for individuals who may wish to have children in the future, especially relevant for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation refers to medical techniques employed to help individuals retain the ability to conceive after undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation refers to medical techniques used to help individuals retain the ability to conceive in the future, particularly before undergoing cancer treatments that may affect fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation refers to the medical techniques that allow individuals to protect their reproductive capabilities before undergoing treatments such as chemotherapy that may impair fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation refers to the methods and techniques used to protect ability to have children during or after cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contraceptive Methods">
      <data key="d0">Contraceptive Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contraceptive methods include various forms of birth control which are recommended during cancer treatment to prevent pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Exam">
      <data key="d0">Physical Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A physical exam includes a comprehensive assessment of a patient's overall health, including checking height, weight, and organ conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam includes various assessments performed by healthcare providers to evaluate a patient's overall health and detect any abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam involves assessing various health indicators such as blood pressure and organ size, fundamental in diagnosing conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is a clinical evaluation by a healthcare provider to assess a patient's overall health status and detect any abnormalities after surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is a routine procedure performed by healthcare providers to assess a patient's general health and detect potential health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is a systematic examination conducted by healthcare providers to assess a patient's overall health, vital signs, and to identify any potential health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is conducted by healthcare providers to assess the health and identify any abnormalities related to breast health.&lt;SEP&gt;The Physical Exam involves assessing a patient's vital signs and the condition of various body systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dental Exam">
      <data key="d0">Dental Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A dental exam involves checking the health of teeth and gums, particularly important for patients with head and neck cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A dental exam is a check-up that assesses the health of a patient's teeth and gums, especially important for those with head and neck cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A dental exam is important for patients with head and neck cancer to monitor oral health, which can be affected by both cancer and its treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distress Screening">
      <data key="d0">Distress Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress screening involves assessing a patient’s emotional and mental well-being in relation to their cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress screening involves assessing emotional responses to a cancer diagnosis, which is important for patient care.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress screening is a process to assess emotional, psychological, and social distress in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress screening is a process used to evaluate emotional suffering in patients, particularly those with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress screening is an evaluative process to identify cancer patients' psychological distress and provide appropriate support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine Tests">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocrine tests evaluate hormone levels that may be affected by cancer treatments, particularly in the head and neck region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hearing Test">
      <data key="d0">Hearing Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A hearing test assesses the patient's hearing abilities, especially after treatment for head and neck tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Hearing tests assess an individual's hearing ability and identify any hearing loss, important in the context of cancer treatments affecting the head and neck.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A nutrition assessment evaluates dietary habits and provides strategies to ensure adequate nutrition during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A nutrition assessment involves evaluating a patient's dietary needs, especially crucial for those undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrition Assessment entails evaluating a patient's dietary needs and status to ensure adequate nutrition before, during, and after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speech and Swallowing Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Speech and Swallowing Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">This assessment tests a patient's ability to speak and swallow, crucial for those undergoing treatment for head and neck cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A fertility specialist assists patients in understanding and preserving reproductive options affected by cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is a medical doctor focused on reproductive health and assisting individuals with fertility preservation options, especially important before cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is a medical professional who helps individuals or couples with reproductive issues, particularly important for cancer patients considering future pregnancies.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is a medical professional who helps patients understand and preserve their fertility, especially in the context of cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is an expert in helping individuals preserve fertility, especially in the context of cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist provides guidance and treatment options for individuals concerned about the impact of cancer treatments on fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;The Fertility Specialist advises and assists cancer patients who wish to preserve their fertility before undergoing treatments that may affect reproductive abilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Patient Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Patient Care Team is a multidisciplinary group that collaborates to provide comprehensive care and support for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Worker">
      <data key="d0">Social Worker</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Social workers provide emotional support and resources to patients and their families during the cancer treatment journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Doctor is a medical professional who plans patient care based on diagnostic information and results.&lt;SEP&gt;A Doctor is a medical professional who receives imaging results from radiologists and uses them to make decisions about patient care and treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;A doctor is a healthcare professional who provides medical advice and treatment to patients, especially concerning pregnancy and cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;Doctors play a crucial role in discussing treatment options and monitoring patients' health, particularly in cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An ultrasound is a medical imaging technique that uses sound waves to produce images of organs and structures inside the body, often used in guiding biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;An ultrasound is an imaging technique used to visualize organs and structures inside the body, often used to guide biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a common imaging technique used to examine thyroid nodules and assess their size, shape, and potential malignancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a diagnostic imaging method that uses sound waves to visualize the ovaries and detect any abnormalities, including tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a diagnostic imaging technique that uses high-energy sound waves to create images of the inside of the body, often used in pregnancy and cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a diagnostic imaging technique that uses sound waves to visualize internal organs and detect abnormalities such as ovarian tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a medical imaging method that uses high-energy sound waves to create images of internal body parts, commonly used in various examinations.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a medical imaging technique often used as the first test to evaluate suspected ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a medical imaging technique that uses sound waves to create images of the inside of the body, often used to guide biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging method that uses high-frequency sound waves to create images of internal body parts.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a safe imaging modality that uses sound waves to visualize internal organs and is useful in assessing certain types of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound uses high-frequency sound waves to create images of internal organs, including identifying abnormalities in soft tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panoramic Dental X-ray">
      <data key="d0">Panoramic Dental X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A panoramic dental x-ray captures a complete view of the mouth, aiding dentists in treatment planning for dental issues.&lt;SEP&gt;A panoramic dental x-ray captures a comprehensive view of the mouth in a single image for planning dental treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A panoramic dental x-ray is a type of imaging that captures the entire mouth in a single image for dental care purposes, including treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A panoramic dental x-ray is an imaging technique that captures a wide view of the mouth in a single image to assist in dental planning and diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A panoramic dental x-ray is an imaging technique that produces a comprehensive view of a patient's entire mouth, including teeth and jaw structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaping">
      <data key="d0">Vaping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaping is an alternative to smoking that can still impact health and cancer treatment effectiveness, suggesting the importance of cessation advice.&lt;SEP&gt;Vaping is the act of using electronic cigarettes or similar devices to inhale vaporized nicotine, which can also increase cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Vaping refers to inhaling vapor produced by an electronic device, which can expose users to harmful substances and increase cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Vaping refers to using e-cigarettes to inhale vaporized nicotine and other substances, which can also influence health outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contraception">
      <data key="d0">Contraception</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contraception encompasses various methods used to prevent pregnancy, particularly emphasized for patients undergoing cancer treatment to avoid complications.&lt;SEP&gt;Contraception refers to methods of preventing pregnancy during and after cancer treatment, as hormonal changes may affect fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Results">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging results refer to the outcomes of diagnostic imaging procedures that help identify and assess diseases, particularly cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Radiologist is a healthcare professional skilled in interpreting imaging studies, such as MRI and CT scans, for diagnosis of diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;A Radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting imaging results, critical in diagnosing and staging cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A Radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting imaging results, crucial for cancer diagnosis and staging.&lt;SEP&gt;A Radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting medical images and diagnosing diseases, particularly in relation to imaging tests.&lt;SEP&gt;A Radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting medical images to diagnose diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in diagnosing diseases through imaging techniques and interpreting scan results.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in diagnosing illnesses through imaging results, playing a crucial role in cancer detection and management.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting images from imaging tests and providing diagnostic reports.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting imaging tests, playing a crucial role in diagnosing and planning treatment for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting medical images to diagnose conditions and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specializing in interpreting imaging tests to diagnose diseases, including cancers, and discussing findings with patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor trained to interpret medical imaging, which can be critical in diagnosing and monitoring thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor who specializes in diagnosing and treating diseases using imaging techniques, crucial in cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical expert responsible for interpreting imaging tests and providing diagnostic reports related to leukemia treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical expert specialized in interpreting imaging studies, including MRI and CT scans, to provide insights on potential tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical expert who specializes in analyzing imaging results, such as MRI and CT scans, to provide diagnosis and treatment recommendations.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical professional specialized in interpreting imaging tests and providing reports to health care providers regarding them.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical specialist who interprets imaging tests, providing critical information about tumor presence and health status in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiologists are medical doctors who interpret imaging tests, such as scans, to detect conditions like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Material">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Material</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast Material refers to substances used in imaging tests to enhance the visibility of internal organs and structures during imaging procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material enhances the clarity of images in body scans and is crucial for accurate medical diagnostics.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a special substance used in imaging tests to enhance the visibility of internal organs and structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a substance used in imaging tests to enhance the quality of internal body images when evaluating leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a substance used in medical imaging to enhance the clarity of images of internal body structures, aiding in diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a substance used in medical imaging to enhance the clarity of images taken of internal body structures, aiding in diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT (Computed Tomography) Scan">
      <data key="d0">CT (Computed Tomography) Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A CT scan is a diagnostic imaging procedure that uses x-ray technology to create detailed images of internal body structures, particularly useful in identifying tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) Scan">
      <data key="d0">MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI scan is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to create high-resolution images of organs and tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET (Positron Emission Tomography) Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET (Positron Emission Tomography) Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET scan is an advanced imaging technique that uses a radioactive tracer to observe metabolic processes in the body and assess cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nicotine">
      <data key="d0">Nicotine</data>
      <data key="d1">chemical</data>
      <data key="d2">Nicotine is a chemical found in tobacco known for its addictive properties, often leading to smoking or vaping habits.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is a chemical found in tobacco that contributes to the addictive properties of smoking and nicotine withdrawal.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is a chemical present in tobacco that is known for its addictive properties and is a significant barrier for individuals attempting to quit smoking.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is a highly addictive chemical found in tobacco, which can complicate cancer treatment and recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is the addictive chemical found in tobacco that encourages continued smoking and can complicate cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dentists">
      <data key="d0">Dentists</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Dentists are medical professionals who specialize in diagnosing and treating oral health issues, including those related to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Oral Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Oral surgeons are specialists who perform surgeries on the mouth, jaws, and related structures, often managing cancer-related procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Histology">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Histology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor histology refers to the microscopic examination of tumor cells to determine their structure and potential behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Studies">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Studies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging studies are diagnostic tests, such as X-rays or CT scans, used to visualize internal structures of the body for cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging studies are medical techniques used to visualize the inside of the body for diagnosis, typically involving methods like CT scans, MRI, or X-rays.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging studies include various techniques like X-rays or MRIs used to visualize internal structures for medical diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging studies include various techniques such as X-rays and MRIs that help assess the extent of disease and assist in treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging studies include various techniques, such as CT scans and MRIs, used to visualize internal structures and assess for tumors or abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1</data>
      <data key="d1">protein</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 is an immune protein expressed on some cancer cells that can inhibit the immune response against the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Programmed Death-Ligand 1 (PD-L1) is a protein that can interact with PD-1 and is evaluated in immunotherapy treatments for cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H refers to microsatellite instability-high, a condition indicating high levels of genetic mutation in cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite Instability-High (MSI-H) is a condition where cells have an increased number of microsatellites and is related to certain cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TMB-H">
      <data key="d0">TMB-H</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TMB-H represents tumor mutational burden-high, a measure of the number of mutations present in tumor DNA, influencing treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor Mutational Burden-High (TMB-H) indicates a high number of mutations in tumor DNA, which can help predict responses to specific cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Teeth">
      <data key="d0">Teeth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Teeth are hard structures in the mouth vital for biting and chewing food and also play a role in the overall health of the mouth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jaws">
      <data key="d0">Jaws</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The jaws are bony structures that support the teeth and are essential for chewing and speaking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Health">
      <data key="d0">Oral Health</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oral health refers to the health of the mouth, including the teeth, gums, and related structures, which can affect overall health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Counseling Services">
      <data key="d0">Counseling Services</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Counseling services provide support and strategies for individuals trying to quit smoking or vaping, especially for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy of Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy of Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy of metastasis involves sampling tissues from cancer spread areas to confirm cancer presence.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy of metastasis involves taking tissue samples from metastatic sites, such as lymph nodes or lungs, to determine the presence of cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy of metastasis is a specific diagnostic procedure to confirm the presence of cancer spread to other body areas, such as lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy of metastasis is performed to confirm the spread of cancer to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes or lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsy of metastasis is a procedure to sample tissue from cancer spread to confirm the presence of cancer in distant locations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutation testing analyzes DNA from a tumor to identify specific mutations that may inform treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutation testing analyzes the genetic makeup of tumors to guide targeted treatments and assess cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutation testing examines tumor DNA for specific mutations to inform treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutation testing is a diagnostic process that looks for specific DNA mutations in cancer cells to guide treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutation testing is a method used to analyze tumor DNA for specific mutations that can inform treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marijuana">
      <data key="d0">Marijuana</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Marijuana may be used by some cancer patients for symptom management, although its effects can vary according to individual circumstances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A cancer care team encompasses various health professionals, including oncologists and specialists, who collaborate to implement treatment plans for cancer patients, including CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Care Team is a group of healthcare professionals who support patients with a cancer diagnosis, providing various services including counseling and treatment monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team comprises healthcare professionals including doctors, nurses, and specialists who support patients diagnosed with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team consists of healthcare professionals, including oncologists, nurses, and social workers, who collaborate to provide comprehensive treatment to cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team consists of healthcare providers involved in managing the care of cancer patients, including physicians, nurses, and specialists.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team consists of specialists including doctors and nurses who work together to provide treatment and support to patients battling breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team consists of various healthcare professionals providing comprehensive care and management to cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team consists of various medical professionals, including specialists who collaborate to diagnose and treat patients with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team includes healthcare professionals who provide support and treatment for patients diagnosed with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team is composed of healthcare professionals who collaborate to provide treatment planning and support for patients diagnosed with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team typically includes a variety of specialists such as gynecologic oncologists, medical oncologists, and radiation oncologists working together to treat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer symptoms are signs that indicate the presence or progress of cancer, which can vary based on cancer type and location.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer symptoms are the physical signs and health issues that may suggest the presence of cancer, including specific indicators for different types of cancer, like jaundice for pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AJCC">
      <data key="d0">AJCC</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) develops staging systems to classify the extent of cancer spread and assist in treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is an organization responsible for creating a staging system to classify cancer severity and inform treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM System">
      <data key="d0">TNM System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM System is a standardized method for staging cancer based on tumor size and its spread to lymph nodes and distant sites.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a classification system established by the AJCC to stage cancer based on Tumor size, lymph Node involvement, and Metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a classification system used by cancer experts to describe the extent of cancer, denoting tumor size and spread through the letters T (tumor), N (node), and M (metastasis).&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a classification system used to stage anal cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a classification system used to stage breast cancer based on tumor size (T), node involvement (N), and distant metastasis (M). It aids in determining the stage and treatment plan for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a classification used to stage bone cancers based on the size of the tumor (T), the involvement of nearby lymph nodes (N), and metastasis (M) to distant organs.&lt;SEP&gt;The Tumor, Node, Metastasis (TNM) staging system is a framework used to classify the extent of cancer spread, important for treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jaw, Teeth, and Gums">
      <data key="d0">Jaw, Teeth, and Gums</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Jaw, teeth, and gums are critical components of oral health that can be impacted by conditions such as head and neck cancer and its treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility">
      <data key="d0">Fertility</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility encompasses a person's ability to conceive and bear children, and its preservation is a concern during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility refers to an individual's capacity to conceive children; treatments for conditions like cancer may impact fertility, necessitating preservation discussions.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility refers to the biological capacity to conceive children, which can be affected by cancer treatments, necessitating discussions with healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA Mutations">
      <data key="d0">DNA Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA mutations are alterations in genetic material that can be analyzed to determine potential cancer therapies and treatment responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prognosis">
      <data key="d0">Prognosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prognosis is a prediction of the likely course and outcome of a disease, which guides treatment options based on a patient's risk group.&lt;SEP&gt;Prognosis refers to the likely course and outcome of a disease, essential for planning patient treatment and care strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Stage">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical stage is assigned before treatment based on physical examinations and imaging tests to evaluate the extent of cancer involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical stage is the preliminary assessment of cancer's extent before surgery, aiding in determining treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical stage refers to the assessment of cancer severity before treatment, based on imaging tests and physical examinations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Clinical Stage is the assessment of cancer before any treatment, based on physical exams, imaging results, and biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;The clinical stage refers to the classification determined before treatment based on tests such as imaging and biopsy to assess cancer extent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologic Stage">
      <data key="d0">Pathologic Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologic stage is determined after surgical procedures by analyzing removed tissues to provide detailed cancer information.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologic stage is the classification of cancer after surgical intervention based on histological examination of the excised tissue, used to guide further treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologic stage refers to the cancer stage determined through examination of tissue removed during surgery, providing detailed insights on tumor progression.&lt;SEP&gt;The Pathologic Stage is determined by examining tissue removed during surgery, providing detailed insights into the extent of cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;The pathologic stage is determined after surgery based on examining tissues removed during the procedure, providing more accurate staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 to Stage IV">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 to Stage IV</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages refer to the classification of cancer severity, ranging from stage 0 (in situ) to stage IV (advanced/metastatic cancer).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of blood cancer originating in the stem cells of bone marrow, characterized by an accumulation of immature white blood cells known as myeloid blasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of blood cancer that originates in the stem cells of bone marrow, characterized by a buildup of immature white blood cells known as myeloblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects blood and bone marrow, characterized by the rapid increase of abnormal myeloblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal white blood cells, which can lead to serious health complications.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that originates in the bone marrow and is characterized by the presence of myeloid blasts in the blood or bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that originates in the bone marrow and results in the production of abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute myeloid leukemia is a fast-growing cancer of the blood and bone marrow that may be a risk for patients treated with certain chemotherapeutic agents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mouth Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d0">Mouth Cancer Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mouth cancer staging refers to the classification of cancer in the mouth, specifically squamous cell carcinoma (SCC), based on the TNM scores and numbered stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph Node Metastasis occurs when cancer spreads to regional lymph nodes, which play a crucial role in the body's immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoma in Situ (Tis)">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoma in Situ (Tis)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoma in Situ (Tis) is a term for very early-stage cancer where cancerous cells are found only in the layer of cells where they originated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4 Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4 Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 cancer refers to cancer that has spread to distant organs or tissues, indicating advanced disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resectable Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Resectable Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A resectable tumor is one that can be completely removed through surgery, influencing treatment decisions and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A resectable tumor is one that can be completely removed through surgery, often associated with better treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unresectable Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Unresectable Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An unresectable tumor is one that cannot be completely removed due to involvement with critical structures or major vessels, complicating treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;An unresectable tumor is one that cannot be fully surgically removed due to its involvement with critical structures, affecting treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL) is a rare subtype of AML characterized by specific genetic abnormalities such as the PML::RARA fusion gene.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL) is a subtype of acute myeloid leukemia characterized by specific genetic factors and treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Myeloid Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Acute Myeloid Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that starts in blood stem cells and leads to the proliferation of abnormal myeloblasts in the bone marrow and blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blasts">
      <data key="d0">Blasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blasts are immature white blood cells that are formed from blood stem cells, and in AML, abnormal myeloblasts crowd out normal blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts refer to immature blood cells that are often increased in patients with leukemia, particularly AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts refer to immature lymphocytes in Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL), which can crowd out normal blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts refer to immature white blood cells involved in the development of blood cells; in AML, these blasts proliferate abnormally in the bone marrow and blood, leading to health complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Testing">
      <data key="d0">AML Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">AML Testing refers to the various medical tests and procedures used to diagnose and treat Acute Myeloid Leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plasma">
      <data key="d0">Plasma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Plasma is the clear yellowish fluid component of blood, primarily composed of water, and serves as a medium for transporting cells and nutrients throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Granulocytes">
      <data key="d0">Granulocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Granulocytes are a type of white blood cell that play a role in the body's response to infection, particularly through their ability to engulf bacteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monocytes">
      <data key="d0">Monocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Monocytes are a type of white blood cell that differentiate into macrophages and dendritic cells, contributing to the immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Marrow Stem Cells are multipotent cells located in the bone marrow responsible for the ongoing production of blood cells throughout a person's life.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone Marrow Stem Cells are the progenitor cells in the bone marrow responsible for producing various types of blood cells through differentiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Treatments">
      <data key="d0">AML Treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML Treatments refer to the medical interventions and care approaches for managing Acute Myeloid Leukemia, including chemotherapy and other targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PML::RARA Fusion Gene">
      <data key="d0">PML::RARA Fusion Gene</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The PML::RARA fusion gene is an abnormal gene found in patients with a rare subtype of Acute Myeloid Leukemia, known as Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BPDCN">
      <data key="d0">BPDCN</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BPDCN (Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm) is a rare and aggressive subtype of Acute Myeloid Leukemia that affects blood, bone marrow, lymph nodes, and skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML">
      <data key="d0">AML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML, or Acute Myeloid Leukemia, is a type of cancer that affects blood and bone marrow, characterized by rapid growth of abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a rapidly progressing cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the accumulation of immature myeloid cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer characterized by the rapid increase of abnormal white blood cells, which can interfere with normal blood cell production.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects blood and bone marrow, often characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal white blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Blood Count (CBC)">
      <data key="d0">Complete Blood Count (CBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a common blood test that evaluates overall health and detects a variety of disorders, including anemia and infection, which are critical for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a common blood test used to evaluate overall health and detect a variety of disorders, including anemia and infection.&lt;SEP&gt;CBC assesses levels of red and white blood cells and platelets, essential for understanding a patient's health status.&lt;SEP&gt;CBC measures the levels of red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets in the blood, providing insights into the body’s overall health and conditions like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;CBC measures various blood components and is essential in diagnosing and monitoring AML treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a blood test that evaluates overall health and detects a variety of disorders, including infections and cancers like AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP)">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) measures various chemicals in the blood to assess metabolic health, often conducted on cancer patients to monitor organ function.&lt;SEP&gt;CMP assesses different substances in the blood, providing information about the kidneys and liver which can be impacted during AML.&lt;SEP&gt;CMP is a blood test that assesses multiple substances to evaluate organ function, particularly the kidneys and liver.&lt;SEP&gt;CMP tests for various substances in the blood, helping to assess kidney and liver function among other health aspects.&lt;SEP&gt;The Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) is a set of tests that evaluates various chemical substances in the blood, providing vital information about organ function and metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;The Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) is a set of tests that provide vital information about the body's metabolism and overall health, particularly kidney and liver function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH)">
      <data key="d0">Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LDH is an enzyme released by dying cells or rapidly growing tumor cells; its levels can provide health insights.&lt;SEP&gt;LDH is an enzyme that can indicate tissue damage and is sometimes elevated in cancer patients, including those with ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;LDH is an enzyme that indicates cell damage. Increased levels can be a sign of conditions like cancer due to fast-growing cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme that helps facilitate energy production and is relevant in evaluating tissue damage or disease processes such as cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme released into the blood from damaged or rapidly dividing cells, often indicating tissue damage or disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B12 and Folic Acid">
      <data key="d0">B12 and Folic Acid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitamin B12 and Folic Acid are essential nutrients for red and white blood cell formation, critical in treatments for blood-related diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical History">
      <data key="d0">Medical History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical history is a comprehensive record of a patient's past health issues and treatments, which is crucial for diagnosing and treating conditions like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical history is a comprehensive record of a person's health issues, treatments, and family health history relevant to medical care.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical history is a detailed account of an individual's past health issues, treatments, and medications, which aids healthcare teams in determining the best treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical history records a patient's past health issues, treatments, and medications, playing a crucial role in determining appropriate treatment for cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a comprehensive record of a patient’s past health issues and treatments, crucial for making informed medical decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a record of a patient's past health issues and treatments, crucial for determining appropriate cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a record of an individual's past health issues and treatments, essential for guiding medical care and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a record of past health issues and treatments that supports effective planning for liver cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is crucial for treatment planning and involves documenting past health issues and treatments, which aids healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history refers to a comprehensive assessment of a patient's past and present health conditions, necessary for accurate diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history refers to a comprehensive overview of a patient's past and current health, which is crucial for diagnosing conditions and planning treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The medical history includes a record of past health conditions and treatments that inform healthcare providers about a patient's overall health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Medical History">
      <data key="d0">AML Medical History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML Medical History includes details about a patient's previous health issues and treatments relevant to Acute Myeloid Leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Clotting Tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood Clotting Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Clotting Tests are conducted to determine how well the blood can clot and to assess potential bleeding issues in AML patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Clotting Tests assess the coagulation process of blood, identifying issues such as coagulopathy associated with leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood clotting tests are assessments that measure how well and how quickly blood clots, which is essential in evaluating patients with leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood clotting tests assess the body's ability to form clots, which is crucial in preventing excessive bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood clotting tests evaluate the components involved in coagulation, essential for preventing and treating abnormal bleeding episodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspirate">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspirate</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow aspirate is a procedure to collect bone marrow samples for diagnostic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone Marrow Aspirate is a procedure used to collect bone marrow for examination, crucial for diagnosing and monitoring AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate is a diagnostic procedure that involves using a hollow needle to extract liquid bone marrow samples for testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic Testing analyzes DNA to identify specific mutations related to AML, helping to tailor treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing analyzes DNA to identify mutations that may influence treatment and prognosis for cancers such as ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing identifies mutations in genes that may increase the risk for diseases such as cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing involves analyzing DNA to detect mutations or changes in genes that may influence disease development or treatment response.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing involves analyzing an individual's DNA to identify any genetic predispositions that may increase their risk for certain types of cancer, including breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing involves analyzing blood or tissue samples for inherited mutations that may influence cancer risk and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing involves analyzing blood or tissue samples to identify genetic mutations or abnormalities that may affect treatment and prognosis of AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is a medical test that identifies changes in chromosomes, genes, or proteins that can indicate a higher risk for prostate and other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is a procedure used to detect mutations in genes that could increase the risk of developing certain diseases, including various cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is a process of analyzing DNA to identify mutations that may indicate a higher risk of developing certain cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is conducted to understand hereditary risks and to check for genetic predispositions to breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is performed to identify mutations in genes associated with increased cancer risk, often recommended for people with a family history of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is used to identify harmful changes in genes and assess the risk of hereditary diseases, including various cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is used to identify specific gene mutations that may influence the development and treatment of blood cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing refers to medical tests that examine DNA and can help determine if hereditary factors are contributing to glioma development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HLA Typing">
      <data key="d0">HLA Typing</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">HLA Typing is a blood test that detects a person's unique human leukocyte antigens to ensure compatibility for organ transplants.&lt;SEP&gt;HLA Typing is a test that determines the compatibility of tissues for transplants and can be relevant in AML treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain CT">
      <data key="d0">Brain CT</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain CT is an imaging test used to detect potential central nervous system involvement in leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain MRI">
      <data key="d0">Brain MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain MRI provides detailed images of the brain and is used when leukemia-related complications are suspected.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lumbar Puncture (LP)">
      <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture (LP)</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Lumbar Puncture is performed to collect cerebrospinal fluid for analysis, particularly relevant in cases of suspected leukemic meningitis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Tests">
      <data key="d0">Heart Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Heart Tests evaluate cardiac function, which can be affected during AML treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Heart tests are various medical evaluations aimed at assessing the heart's performance and diagnosing potential issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uric Acid Levels">
      <data key="d0">Uric Acid Levels</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Uric Acid Levels indicate metabolic processes and can be elevated in patients undergoing treatment for AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Enlarged Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Enlarged Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Enlarged Lymph Nodes can indicate disease spread and are an important assessment point during evaluation for AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitamin B12">
      <data key="d0">Vitamin B12</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitamin B12 is a crucial vitamin required for normal red and white blood cell formation and overall body functions, often monitored in patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Folic Acid">
      <data key="d0">Folic Acid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Folic Acid is essential for the production of new proteins and is monitored alongside Vitamin B12 for blood health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron">
      <data key="d0">Iron</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron is a mineral essential for producing hemoglobin in red blood cells, and its levels can impact overall energy and health status.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron is essential for many body functions, particularly for hemoglobin production in red blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron is vital for hemoglobin production in blood, with levels monitored to prevent deficiency or overload.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) Score">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) Score</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ECOG score measures a patient's performance status, influencing treatment decisions based on their fitness level.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Tests">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Marrow Tests are diagnostic procedures to determine the presence of leukemia and involve sampling the bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are conducted to diagnose conditions such as leukemia by examining samples of bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are diagnostic procedures used to determine the presence of lymphoblasts and diagnose conditions like ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are medical procedures performed to diagnose leukemia, specifically Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) by analyzing the bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests involve sampling and evaluating bone marrow to diagnose diseases such as leukemia and assess blood disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests involve sampling bone marrow to diagnose blood-related conditions, including various forms of leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests may be utilized in the diagnosis of CLL to evaluate blood cell levels and identify underlying issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flow Cytometry">
      <data key="d0">Flow Cytometry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Flow Cytometry is a laboratory method used to analyze and characterize cells, particularly in blood cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique used to analyze the physical and chemical characteristics of blood cells, aiding in the diagnosis of conditions like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique used to analyze the physical and chemical characteristics of cells, particularly useful in identifying blood cancer markers.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique used to analyze the properties of cells, enabling the identification and quantification of specific cell types in blood or bone marrow samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a technique used to analyze the physical and chemical characteristics of cells, particularly to identify markers on cells, often used in immunophenotyping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunophenotyping">
      <data key="d0">Immunophenotyping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunophenotyping is a method that utilizes antibodies to identify specific antigens on cell surfaces, crucial for the diagnosis and classification of blood cancers, including leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is a process that distinguishes white blood cell types through the detection of specific antigens on their surfaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is a process that uses antibodies to detect the presence of specific white blood cell antigens, aiding in the diagnosis of blood cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is the process of identifying specific markers on cells, often used in categorizing blood cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemicals">
      <data key="d0">Chemicals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemicals are substances processed or produced by the liver, and their levels help assess liver function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bile Ducts">
      <data key="d0">Bile Ducts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bile Ducts are channels in the liver that can become blocked, affecting liver health and function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blood Cells (RBC)">
      <data key="d0">Red Blood Cells (RBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Red Blood Cells are crucial for oxygen transport in the body and require Vitamin B12 and Folic Acid for formation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cells (WBC)">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cells (WBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">White Blood Cells are essential for immune function, and their levels can be influenced by Vitamin B12 and Folic Acid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron Deficiency">
      <data key="d0">Iron Deficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron Deficiency is a condition characterized by low iron levels, leading to reduced hemoglobin and potential anemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron Overload">
      <data key="d0">Iron Overload</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron Overload refers to excessive iron in the body, which can lead to serious health complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coagulation Factors">
      <data key="d0">Coagulation Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Coagulation Factors are proteins essential for the blood clotting process, produced by the liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coagulopathy">
      <data key="d0">Coagulopathy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Coagulopathy is a condition where the blood's ability to clot is impaired, often associated with leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia is a type of cancer that affects blood cells, often requiring monitoring of coagulation and bone marrow tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Donor Match">
      <data key="d0">Donor Match</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Donor Match is the compatibility of proteins between a recipient and a donor, critical for successful transplants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transplant refers to the medical procedure of transferring cells or organs from one person to another.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Cell Transplant (HCT)">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Cell Transplant (HCT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopoietic Cell Transplant (HCT) is a procedure where stem cells are transplanted to treat certain blood disorders or cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Guidelines provides resources and information for cancer patients and healthcare professionals on treatment options and standards.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) Guidelines provide recommendations for cancer treatment, including supportive care and adverse effects of therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) creates guidelines for clinical practices in cancer treatment and management.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines for cancer care, including resources for managing distress during treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines for cancer treatment and management, including fertility considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Treatment">
      <data key="d0">AML Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML Treatment refers to targeted therapies aimed at specific biomarkers present in Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) to improve patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunohistochemistry">
      <data key="d0">Immunohistochemistry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a lab technique that uses antibodies to test for specific antigens in a tissue sample, contributing to the understanding of various diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a staining process used to detect specific proteins, including hormone receptors in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia predisposition syndromes are hereditary conditions that increase the risk of developing leukemia, and can influence treatment choices.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia predisposition syndromes refer to a group of hereditary conditions that can increase the risk of developing leukemia in individuals, making family history a factor in treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutation testing identifies specific genetic alterations in cancer cells, informing treatment decisions and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutation testing involves various techniques to identify specific genetic mutations in leukemia cells that can influence treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karyotype">
      <data key="d0">Karyotype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A karyotype is a laboratory procedure that involves the visualization of chromosomes to identify genetic abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A karyotype is a test that provides a visual profile of an individual's chromosomes and can reveal abnormalities in leukemia patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Karyotype is a diagnostic method used to analyze chromosomes in human cells, which can reveal chromosomal abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Karyotype is a diagnostic method used to visualize and analyze chromosomes in human cells to determine chromosomal abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next-Generation Sequencing">
      <data key="d0">Next-Generation Sequencing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) is a modern DNA sequencing technology that provides comprehensive insights into genetic variations associated with diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) is a technology that determines the sequence of DNA to identify mutations in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fluorescence in Situ Hybridization">
      <data key="d0">Fluorescence in Situ Hybridization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fluorescence in Situ Hybridization (FISH) is a technique used to detect and localize the presence or absence of specific DNA sequences on chromosomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarkers">
      <data key="d0">Biomarkers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarkers are biological indicators found in the body, such as specific proteins or genetic changes, that can signal cancerous conditions and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are biological indicators, including mutations and proteins that help in tailoring cancer treatments and evaluating prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are biological indicators, often genetic mutations, that help classify tumors and predict patient outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are biological indicators, such as PD-L1, that help guide treatment options in cancer therapy, influencing choices for systemic therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are specific biological indicators that can guide treatment decisions for ovarian cancer, including BRCA1 and BRCA2 mutations, and others.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are specific indicators present on the surface of or within cells, which can be used to diagnose conditions like cancer and target treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers in cancer, such as HER2, are proteins or genes that can be tested to influence treatment decisions and assess cancer characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gene Expression Profiling">
      <data key="d0">Gene Expression Profiling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene expression profiling is a technique used to measure the activity of genes to understand the molecular basis of diseases such as AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosomes">
      <data key="d0">Chromosomes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosomes are structures within cells that contain DNA, carrying the genetic information essential for development and function.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes are structures within cells that contain DNA, determining genetic traits and health; normal human cells contain 23 pairs.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes are structures within cells that contain DNA; mutations and structural changes in chromosomes can be associated with various cancers, including AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes are structures within cells that package DNA and contain genes, which carry instructions for producing proteins essential for cellular function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA">
      <data key="d0">DNA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is the hereditary material in humans and most organisms, containing the instructions for development, functioning, growth, and reproduction.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is the molecule that carries genetic information in cells, with mutations impacting processes like cell division and function, leading to conditions such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Cell Donors">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Cell Donors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopoietic cell donors are biological family members who may be tested for leukemia predisposition to determine suitability for transplants or treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematopoietic cell donors are individuals who may provide stem cells or bone marrow for transplantation to treat blood cancers like AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genomic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genomic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genomic testing is a type of genetic testing focused on analyzing the entire genome to identify mutations that relate to diseases such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Molecular testing involves analyzing the genetic material in tumor samples to identify specific mutations, informing treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular testing involves analyzing tumor mutations to determine potential treatment paths and suitability for clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular testing involves examining genetic material to detect specific mutations or alterations in cells associated with diseases, including blood cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular testing is a diagnostic approach that analyzes genetic changes in leukemia cells to inform treatment strategies and predict disease progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Point Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Point Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Point mutation is a change in a single nucleotide in the DNA sequence that can lead to genetic diseases, including various types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deletion">
      <data key="d0">Deletion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deletion refers to the loss of a segment of DNA, which can result in missing genes or regulatory regions, impacting cell function and contributing to malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insertion">
      <data key="d0">Insertion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Insertion is a mutation involving the addition of one or more nucleotide base pairs into a DNA sequence, which can disrupt normal cell function and lead to diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inversion">
      <data key="d0">Inversion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inversion is a chromosome rearrangement where a segment of a chromosome is reversed end to end, potentially leading to genetic disorders or cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Translocation">
      <data key="d0">Translocation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Translocation is a genetic alteration where parts of chromosomes break off and reattach to different chromosomes, often associated with cancers like leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Response">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Response</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment response refers to the effectiveness of a given therapeutic approach in combating diseases such as AML, influenced by genetic makeup and mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next-generation sequencing (NGS)">
      <data key="d0">Next-generation sequencing (NGS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NGS is a high-throughput method for determining DNA sequences, allowing for the detection of mutations in genes with greater detail.&lt;SEP&gt;NGS is an advanced method in genomics that allows for rapid sequencing of DNA and detection of mutations within genes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)">
      <data key="d0">Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PCR is a laboratory technique used to amplify DNA sequences, enabling the detection of specific genetic material even in small samples.&lt;SEP&gt;PCR is a technique used to amplify DNA sequences, allowing for the detection of minimal quantities of leukemic cells.&lt;SEP&gt;PCR is a widely used technique in molecular biology to amplify DNA sequences, which can help in detecting abnormalities at very low concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is a technique used to amplify specific DNA sequences, allowing for sensitive detection of genetic mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is a widely used technique for amplifying DNA segments, facilitating the detection of genetic alterations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG)">
      <data key="d0">Electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An ECG is a test that records electrical signals of the heart, helping assess heart rhythms and conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;An ECG is a test that records the electrical activity of the heart to assess heart rate and rhythm.&lt;SEP&gt;An electrocardiogram measures the electrical activity of the heart and is essential for diagnosing heart conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Echocardiogram (Echo)">
      <data key="d0">Echocardiogram (Echo)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An echocardiogram is a sonographic test that provides live images of the heart's size and function, aiding in cardiovascular diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;An echocardiogram is an ultrasound of the heart that provides moving pictures of its structure and function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cardiologist">
      <data key="d0">Cardiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A cardiologist is a heart specialist who evaluates and monitors heart conditions and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A cardiologist is a physician focusing on diagnosing and treating heart problems and related disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;A cardiologist is a specialized doctor responsible for diagnosing and treating heart-related conditions and managing cardiac care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Echocardiogram">
      <data key="d0">Echocardiogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An echocardiogram is a medical imaging procedure that uses sound waves to visualize the heart's structure and function.&lt;SEP&gt;An echocardiogram uses sound waves to create pictures of the heart's structure and function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lumbar Puncture">
      <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A lumbar puncture collects cerebrospinal fluid for testing cancer cells, especially important in lymphoma cases.&lt;SEP&gt;A lumbar puncture is a medical procedure used to collect cerebrospinal fluid, often utilized when central nervous system involvement in AML is suspected.&lt;SEP&gt;A lumbar puncture is a medical procedure used to remove spinal fluid for testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A lumbar puncture, also known as a spinal tap, is a medical procedure to collect cerebrospinal fluid for diagnostic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;A lumbar puncture, or spinal tap, is a procedure that collects cerebrospinal fluid to help confirm a diagnosis of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Team (MDT)">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Team (MDT)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Multidisciplinary Team (MDT) is a group of healthcare professionals from different specialties that collaborates to plan and manage patient care in cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A multidisciplinary team (MDT) consists of healthcare professionals from various fields who collaborate in planning and delivering cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Multidisciplinary Team (MDT) is a group of healthcare professionals from different specialties working together to create tailored treatment plans for AML patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The Multidisciplinary Team is composed of healthcare professionals with varied expertise who collaborate to provide comprehensive treatment plans for AML patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematologist">
      <data key="d0">Hematologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A hematologist is a medical doctor specializing in blood diseases and conditions, including blood cancers like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A hematologist is a medical expert specializing in blood diseases and blood cancers, playing a key role in the treatment of AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A hematologist specializes in treating blood diseases and plays a critical role in managing patients with Acute Myeloid Leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow biopsy is a procedure that extracts marrow to check for cancerous cells in patients with lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone marrow biopsy is a procedure used to diagnose various conditions, including blood cancers, by examining bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone marrow biopsy is a sample of bone marrow taken to evaluate for diseases like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow biopsy is a procedure that removes a small piece of bone and marrow to diagnose conditions such as leukemia, providing essential samples for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Catheter">
      <data key="d0">Catheter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A catheter is a flexible tube used in medical procedures to allow the administration of treatment and other essential care techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;A catheter is a flexible tube used to drain urine from the bladder through the stoma into the pouch.&lt;SEP&gt;A catheter is a long, thin tube inserted into a patient's vein to administer treatments such as chemotherapy, fluids, or blood products.&lt;SEP&gt;A catheter is a thin tube used for delivering systemic therapy, fluids, and blood products intravenously, typically placed in a large vein.&lt;SEP&gt;A catheter is a thin tube used to deliver medications, such as chemotherapy drugs, directly into the abdomen or bloodstream.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Port">
      <data key="d0">Port</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A port is a small device placed under the skin that provides easy access to the bloodstream for the delivery of medications and fluids during treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A port is a small device placed under the skin to facilitate the delivery of treatment, fluids, and blood products, ensuring patient comfort.&lt;SEP&gt;A port is a small medical device placed under the skin to facilitate intravenous access for administering chemotherapy and other treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A port is a small medical device surgically implanted under the skin to allow easy access to the bloodstream for drug delivery, commonly used in chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard of Care">
      <data key="d0">Standard of Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard of Care refers to the best-known treatment method based on clinical trials and medical research for a given disease or condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cytarabine">
      <data key="d0">Cytarabine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cytarabine is a chemotherapy drug used as part of the treatment regimen for AML, often combined with other agents.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine is a specific medication used in chemotherapy regimens to treat AML, often combined with other drugs for enhanced effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine is an antimetabolite chemotherapy drug used to treat AML and may be administered in various doses and regimens.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine, also known as Ara-C, is an antimetabolite used in various regimens to treat AML, often combined with other therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin">
      <data key="d0">Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gemtuzumab ozogamicin is a targeted therapy used specifically for AML involving CD33 proteins, combining an antibody with a chemotherapy drug.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLT3 Mutations">
      <data key="d0">FLT3 Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FLT3 mutations are genetic abnormalities that can affect treatment options and outcomes for patients with AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypomethylating Agents">
      <data key="d0">Hypomethylating Agents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hypomethylating agents are drugs that help treat leukemia by blocking excessive methyl groups that prevent normal gene function.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypomethylating agents are therapeutic drugs that inhibit the binding of methyl groups to DNA, enabling the expression of silenced genes in leukemia treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antimetabolites">
      <data key="d0">Antimetabolites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Antimetabolites are a category of chemotherapy drugs that prevent the building blocks of DNA from being used, with various examples utilized in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clofarabine">
      <data key="d0">Clofarabine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clofarabine is an example of an antimetabolite drug used in chemotherapy to treat certain types of cancer, including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fludarabine">
      <data key="d0">Fludarabine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fludarabine is an antimetabolite chemotherapy agent that disrupts DNA synthesis and is used in various cancer regimens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Idarubicin">
      <data key="d0">Idarubicin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Idarubicin is an anthracycline chemotherapy drug commonly used to treat AML, known for its effectiveness in damaging DNA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Daunorubicin">
      <data key="d0">Daunorubicin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Daunorubicin is an anthracycline chemotherapeutic agent that is essential in treating AML and various other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Daunorubicin is an anthracycline chemotherapy drug commonly used to treat various types of leukemia, including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mitoxantrone">
      <data key="d0">Mitoxantrone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mitoxantrone is an anthracycline drug used in chemotherapy treatments, effective against several types of leukemia including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dual-Drug Liposome">
      <data key="d0">Dual-Drug Liposome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dual-Drug Liposome is a combination treatment strategy, such as CPX-351, utilizing both an antimetabolite and an anthracycline.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Azacitidine">
      <data key="d0">Azacitidine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Azacitidine is a hypomethylating agent that helps reactivate silenced genes in leukemia cells, supporting their maturation into normal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Azacitidine is a hypomethylating agent used in cancer therapy aimed at AML, helping in the reactivation of silenced genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Azacitidine, a hypomethylating agent, helps reactivate silenced genes in leukemia cells, promoting the maturation of these cells into normal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Azacitidine, also known as Vidaza, is a hypomethylating agent used in the treatment of certain types of leukemia by blocking methyl groups from binding to DNA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Decitabine">
      <data key="d0">Decitabine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Decitabine is another example of a hypomethylating agent that targets methyl groups in the treatment of leukemia and specific cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Decitabine, also referred to as Dacogen, is another hypomethylating agent utilized in treating leukemia by reactivating silenced genes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Binding Factor (CBF)">
      <data key="d0">Core Binding Factor (CBF)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Core Binding Factor (CBF) refers to a genetic feature often seen in some types of leukemia, affecting treatment approaches and prognoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer affecting the ovaries, often presenting late with few symptoms that can include bloating, pelvic pain, and changes in urinary habits.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that affects the ovaries and often presents with vague or late symptoms, making it a challenging condition to diagnose and treat.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that arises from the ovaries and is treated through chemotherapy and maintenance therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries and can be identified by various symptoms and biomarkers.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries and can spread to other parts of the body, characterized by various treatment approaches including surgery and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries and has various stages indicating progression and severity.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and can involve various treatment approaches, including surgery and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and can require complex treatment options, including maintenance therapy and management of recurrences.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is associated with increased risk factors for prostate cancer when considering familial connections.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer refers to a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries and can spread to other parts of the female reproductive system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-grade Serous Carcinoma (HGSC)">
      <data key="d0">High-grade Serous Carcinoma (HGSC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HGSC is the most common type of epithelial ovarian cancer known for its aggressive nature and potential to spread quickly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-grade Endometrioid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">High-grade Endometrioid Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade endometrioid carcinoma is a significant subtype of ovarian cancer that may originate from the endometrium.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Less Common Ovarian Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Less Common Ovarian Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Less common ovarian cancers include types of epithelial and non-epithelial tumors that are not frequently diagnosed but exist.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologic Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologic Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A gynecologic oncologist is a medical doctor specializing in the diagnosis and treatment of cancers related to the female reproductive system.&lt;SEP&gt;A gynecologic oncologist is a physician specialized in the diagnosis and treatment of cancers in the female reproductive system, particularly ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A gynecologic oncologist is a specialist in treating cancers of the female reproductive system, using methods such as chemotherapy and surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fallopian Tube Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Fallopian Tube Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fallopian tube cancer develops in the fallopian tubes and is related to the ovarian cancer spectrum in terms of treatment and diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovaries">
      <data key="d0">Ovaries</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ovaries are a pair of reproductive organs in females responsible for producing eggs and hormones vital for sexual reproduction and menstrual cycles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fallopian Tubes">
      <data key="d0">Fallopian Tubes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The fallopian tubes are the structures that transport eggs from the ovaries to the uterus and are critical in the fertilization process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uterus">
      <data key="d0">Uterus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The uterus is a hollow organ in females where fetal development occurs during pregnancy, also playing a key role in menstruation.&lt;SEP&gt;The uterus is a key organ in the female reproductive system where babies develop; changes in the cervix can lead to cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervix">
      <data key="d0">Cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The cervix is the lower part of the uterus that connects to the vagina and is where cervical changes and cancers can develop.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower part of the uterus that opens into the vagina and has a significant role during childbirth and menstrual cycles.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower part of the uterus that opens into the vagina, where most cervical cancers begin.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower portion of the uterus that opens into the vagina and is the primary site for the development of cervical cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vagina">
      <data key="d0">Vagina</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The vagina is a muscular tube that connects the cervix to the outside of the body, serving multiple functions in the female reproductive system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epithelial Layer">
      <data key="d0">Epithelial Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The epithelial layer is the surface tissue of the ovaries where most ovarian cancers begin, specifically affecting their health and function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stages of Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stages of Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages are classifications used to describe the extent of cancer spread from its original site, essential for treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Peritoneal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Primary Peritoneal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary peritoneal cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the lining of the abdominal cavity and is related to ovarian cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Exam">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A pelvic exam is a diagnostic procedure where a healthcare provider examines a woman's reproductive organs for abnormalities, potentially indicating ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pelvic exam is a procedure where a healthcare provider examines a woman's reproductive organs for abnormalities, often including checks for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pap Test">
      <data key="d0">Pap Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pap test is a medical procedure that collects cells from the cervix to test for cervical cancer, but not ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pap test is an examination of cells taken from the cervix to screen for cervical cancer, and it is not diagnostic for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominal Exam">
      <data key="d0">Abdominal Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An abdominal exam involves a physical assessment of the abdomen to detect signs of ovarian cancer or other health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;An abdominal exam is a physical examination where a doctor assesses the abdomen for abnormalities, including signs of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Provider">
      <data key="d0">Health Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A health provider is a qualified individual or organization offering medical services and care related to cancer diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A health provider is a qualified individual or organization that gives medical services to patients, involved in diagnosing and treating ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Factors">
      <data key="d0">Health Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health factors refer to various conditions or characteristics that can affect a patient's treatment options and cancer outcomes, such as tumor size and location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormonal Changes">
      <data key="d0">Hormonal Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormonal changes can cause symptoms similar to those of ovarian cancer, complicating diagnosis and patient understanding of their condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ascites">
      <data key="d0">Ascites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ascites is the accumulation of fluid in the abdominal cavity, which can be a symptom or complication of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ovarian cancer">
      <data key="d0">ovarian cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and may require various diagnostic and treatment procedures including surgery, biopsies, and genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT scan">
      <data key="d0">CT scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A CT scan is a detailed imaging procedure that takes multiple pictures from different angles to create a 3-D representation for diagnostic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;A CT scan is a medical imaging procedure that uses x-rays and computer technology to produce detailed images of the inside of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET scan">
      <data key="d0">PET scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET scan is a type of imaging test that helps detect cancer by using a radioactive tracer to observe metabolic activity in cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan uses a radiotracer to show how cells use sugar, allowing identification of cancerous areas in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chest x-ray">
      <data key="d0">chest x-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A chest x-ray is a quick, painless imaging test to determine if cancer has spread to the lungs, often used in initial diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="diagnostic laparoscopy">
      <data key="d0">diagnostic laparoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic laparoscopy is a minimally invasive procedure to assess the extent of cancer within the abdomen and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="biopsy">
      <data key="d0">biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy involves the removal of tissue samples to determine the presence of cancer cells, crucial for diagnosing ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy is a medical procedure that involves taking a tissue sample from the body for examination, often performed to diagnose cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy is a procedure in which a sample of bladder tissue is examined for cancer cell presence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA genes">
      <data key="d0">BRCA genes</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA1 and BRCA2 are human genes that, when mutated, significantly increase the risk of developing ovarian and breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lynch syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Lynch syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Lynch syndrome is a genetic condition that increases the risk of certain cancers, including hereditary ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing bladder cancer among other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="genetic testing">
      <data key="d0">genetic testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic testing is a medical test that identifies mutations in genes associated with hereditary cancer, essential for patients diagnosed with ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="pathologist">
      <data key="d0">pathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pathologist is a medical doctor specialized in diagnosing diseases by examining tissues, cells, and organs, playing a key role in cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="health expert">
      <data key="d0">health expert</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A health expert in genetics provides guidance on hereditary cancer risks and testing for gene mutations related to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="care team">
      <data key="d0">care team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The care team consists of healthcare professionals who provide support and treatment to patients, especially those undergoing procedures like cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="sedative">
      <data key="d0">sedative</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sedative is a type of medication used to help patients relax, especially during medical procedures in which anxiety may be an issue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="sugar radiotracer">
      <data key="d0">sugar radiotracer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sugar radiotracer is a compound used in PET scans to visualize how cells utilize sugar, helping in the detection of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="initial surgery">
      <data key="d0">initial surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Initial surgery refers to the first surgical procedure conducted to remove cancerous tissue, often including biopsies for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="fine-needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy">
      <data key="d0">fine-needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A fine-needle aspiration biopsy is a minimally invasive technique for obtaining a small tissue sample from a tumor for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="core biopsy">
      <data key="d0">core biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A core biopsy is performed to collect larger tissue samples from a tumor using a hollow needle, aiding in cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paracentesis">
      <data key="d0">paracentesis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Paracentesis is a medical procedure that involves the removal of fluid from the abdominal cavity for diagnostic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="family history">
      <data key="d0">family history</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family history refers to the record of health conditions within a family, which can influence individual cancer risk assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="genetic counselor">
      <data key="d0">genetic counselor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A genetic counselor is a trained expert who provides information and guidance on genetic conditions and the implications for patients and their families.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="health history">
      <data key="d0">health history</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health history encompasses previous medical conditions and treatments that are essential for assessing cancer risk and treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Gene Testing">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Gene Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer gene testing includes testing for mutations in specific genes like BRCA that can influence treatment options and familial risk assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA Genes">
      <data key="d0">BRCA Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA genes are critical in DNA repair and their mutations are significant biomarkers for ovarian cancer treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA genes are critical in DNA repair, and mutations in these genes are significant biomarkers for ovarian cancer treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA genes are genes that, when mutated, significantly increase the risk of developing ovarian and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Registered Dietitian">
      <data key="d0">Registered Dietitian</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A registered dietitian is a healthcare professional who specializes in nutrition and can provide dietary guidance for patients with health conditions like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colonoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Colonoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the colon for abnormalities, often performed periodically as part of cancer screening.&lt;SEP&gt;A colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the colon for abnormalities, with follow-up procedures recommended based on findings.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a diagnostic procedure that allows examination of the large bowel and is often employed to check for issues related to gastrointestinal health.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure recommended as part of surveillance follow-up testing for individuals who have had stage 1 colon cancer surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure that allows for examination of the colon and rectum and the removal of polyps to prevent cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the colon and rectum for abnormalities, also used for removing polyps.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the colon and rectum for abnormalities, including polyps and cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to remove polyps from the colon and rectum, preventing potential cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a minor surgical procedure used to remove most polyps from the colon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Upper Endoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Upper Endoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Upper endoscopy is a procedure used to examine the upper gastrointestinal tract and can provide insights related to diet and health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Markers">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Markers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor markers are substances found in blood or tissues that may indicate the presence of cancer, helping monitor disease progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor markers are substances found in blood, urine, or tissue that are indicators of cancer presence and can assist in diagnosis and monitoring treatment response.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor markers are substances found in blood, urine, or tissue that can indicate the presence of cancer and assist in diagnosis and monitoring treatment response.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor markers are substances released by cancer cells that can be detected in urine and blood tests, assisting in the diagnosis and monitoring of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor markers are substances that can indicate the presence of cancer within the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CA-125 Test">
      <data key="d0">CA-125 Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The CA-125 test is a tumor marker test commonly used to monitor ovarian cancer by measuring CA-125 protein levels in the blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker testing analyzes biopsied tumor tissue for specific mutations, proteins, or molecular features crucial for treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing analyzes tumor or blood samples to identify specific molecular characteristics of the cancer for targeted therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing analyzes tumor samples to understand molecular characteristics related to cancer spread and risk assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing biological markers in a sample to identify mutations and proteins that influence cancer treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing biological samples to detect specific indicators of cancer, aiding in diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing tissue samples for specific biomarkers that can provide detailed information about gliomas and help in treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing tumor samples for specific mutations or proteins related to cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing tumors for specific characteristics that can guide treatment and eligibility for clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves assessing the presence of specific proteins, genes, or mutations in cancer cells to guide treatment options and predict outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing is a diagnostic method used to identify specific biological markers associated with cancer, aiding in treatment planning for NPC.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing is a method of analyzing cancer-related markers in the body to inform treatment decisions in patients, especially those with recurrent cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing is a recommended process to determine eligibility for targeted therapy or immunotherapy in cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing is a technique used to identify biological markers that may indicate the presence or type of cancer, specifically bone cancer in this context.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing refers to various assessments used to identify specific proteins or genetic changes in cancer patients that influence treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker tumor testing analyzes genetic mutations and proteins to guide cancer treatment and identify potential therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker tumor testing analyzes genetic mutations and proteins to guide cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LCOCs">
      <data key="d0">LCOCs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Less common ovarian cancers (LCOCs) refer to rare types of ovarian cancers that may present different tumor markers and diagnostic challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutritional Health">
      <data key="d0">Nutritional Health</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nutritional health pertains to the dietary habits and nutrient intake of individuals, particularly impacting those with health conditions such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
