      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">β-hCG is a hormone that can be a marker for certain ovarian cancers, especially in conjunction with other indicators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)">
      <data key="d0">Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">AFP is a protein that can be found in higher levels during certain types of ovarian cancers and is monitored as a tumor marker.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CEA is a protein in the blood that can be elevated in patients with colorectal cancer, used for monitoring cancer spread or recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;CEA is a protein typically elevated in the blood of individuals with colorectal cancer, serving as a potential biomarker for diagnosis and monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;CEA is a substance that can be elevated in various cancers, including ovarian cancer, and is used for monitoring treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CA 19-9">
      <data key="d0">CA 19-9</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">CA 19-9 is a tumor marker that can indicate the presence of certain cancers, potentially including ovarian cancer under specific circumstances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HE4">
      <data key="d0">HE4</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">HE4 is a marker that is often used in conjunction with others to assess ovarian cancer risk and treatment effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hysterectomy and BSO">
      <data key="d0">Hysterectomy and BSO</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hysterectomy and bilateral salpingooophorectomy (BSO) are surgical procedures commonly performed to treat ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="In Vitro Fertilization (IVF)">
      <data key="d0">In Vitro Fertilization (IVF)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">In Vitro Fertilization (IVF) is a reproductive technology that may be used for women who wish to become pregnant after undergoing surgery for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laparotomy">
      <data key="d0">Laparotomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Laparotomy is a surgical procedure involving a long incision in the abdomen, often used for staging or cytoreductive surgery in ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Laparotomy refers to a surgical procedure involving a large incision through the abdomen, often used to treat cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laparoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Laparoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Laparoscopy is a minimally invasive surgical procedure sometimes employed for early-stage ovarian cancer, performed through small abdominal cuts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">medical procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor testing encompasses various diagnostic procedures used to determine the presence of specific DNA mutations, proteins, and other molecular features to guide treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor testing evaluates tissue samples from tumors to identify molecular characteristics that inform cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor testing involves analyzing a tissue sample from a tumor to identify molecular components and assess cancer characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor testing involves analyzing tumor samples for genetic mutations that can inform treatment decisions in ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB) refers to the total number of mutations in the genes of a tumor, which can influence treatment decisions and prognosis in cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden (TMB) quantifies the number of mutations within a tumor's genome, which may influence treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden (TMB) quantifies the number of mutations within a tumor, indicating possible treatment pathways using targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Instability (MSI)">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Instability (MSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite Instability (MSI) is a condition of genetic hypermutability that can be indicative of certain types of cancer, particularly those associated with MMR deficiencies.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite instability (MSI) is a phenomenon in which microsatellites become unstable, often seen in various cancer types, especially when MMR proteins fail.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite instability is a condition of genetic hypermutability that can occur in certain cancers, including ovarian cancer, influencing treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite instability refers to genetic errors in DNA that can influence cancer behavior and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair (MMR)">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair (MMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch Repair (MMR) is a system within the cell that corrects DNA replication errors and its dysfunction can lead to cancers such as ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2">
      <data key="d0">HER2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 is a gene associated with certain aggressive types of breast cancer, and its status is evaluated through biomarker testing.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 is a protein that can be overexpressed in some breast cancers, influencing treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 is a protein that can promote the growth of cancer cells; its expression is often tested in breast and other cancers to determine treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 is a protein that can promote the growth of cancer cells; its status in breast cancer is crucial for treatment planning and management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAF V600E Mutation">
      <data key="d0">BRAF V600E Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The BRAF V600E mutation is a specific point mutation in the BRAF gene that can affect treatment strategies in various cancers, including ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Folate Receptor Alpha (FRα)">
      <data key="d0">Folate Receptor Alpha (FRα)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Folate Receptor Alpha (FRα) is a protein that may be overexpressed in gynecological cancers, including ovarian cancer, and is a target for certain therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RET Mutations">
      <data key="d0">RET Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RET mutations are genetic alterations that can drive tumorigenesis and may be found in various cancers, including ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NTRK Gene Fusion">
      <data key="d0">NTRK Gene Fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NTRK gene fusion is a genetic alteration that leads to the production of a fusion protein, which can drive tumor growth and is targeted by specific therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CBC (Complete Blood Count)">
      <data key="d0">CBC (Complete Blood Count)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">CBC is a blood test used to evaluate overall health and detect a variety of disorders, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemistry Profile">
      <data key="d0">Chemistry Profile</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A chemistry profile is a blood test that measures various substances and enzymes, helping to assess systemic health during cancer evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interval Cytoreductive Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Interval Cytoreductive Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Interval cytoreductive surgery (ICS) is a procedure performed after chemotherapy to remove remaining cancer tissues, particularly for advanced cases of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Joint Committee on Cancer">
      <data key="d0">American Joint Committee on Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) developed the staging system which is utilized to classify cancer stages and inform treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) developed the staging system, which helps assess the extent of cancer and informs treatment choices.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is an organization that develops cancer staging systems to describe disease progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics">
      <data key="d0">International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) is an organization that provides standardized staging systems for gynecological cancers, including ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Vein Thrombosis">
      <data key="d0">Deep Vein Thrombosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is a condition where blood clots form in veins, often a risk associated with surgery and cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Omentum">
      <data key="d0">Omentum</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The omentum is a fatty layer of tissue in the abdomen that covers and supports the organs, playing a role in the surgical removal process in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microscopic Metastases">
      <data key="d0">Microscopic Metastases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microscopic metastases refer to cancer cells that have spread beyond the primary tumor, often undetectable without a microscope, and important in determining treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peritoneal Washing">
      <data key="d0">Peritoneal Washing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Peritoneal washing is a medical procedure involving the rinsing of the abdominal cavity with a solution to collect samples for detecting cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Menopause">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Menopause</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical menopause occurs when both ovaries are removed, leading to a sudden drop in estrogen levels and symptoms similar to natural menopause.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT)">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) is a treatment designed to relieve menopausal symptoms by supplying hormones that the body no longer produces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Regimens">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Regimens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy regimens consist of specific combinations of drugs used to treat ovarian cancer, varying based on individual patient health and cancer characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy regimens refer to the specific combinations of chemotherapy drugs and schedules used in the treatment of cancer, particularly anal cancer, including Mitomycin with Capecitabine, 5-FU, and Cisplatin.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy regimens such as Carboplatin and Paclitaxel are recommended options for treating returning cancer in lymph nodes, particularly after previous therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paclitaxel">
      <data key="d0">Paclitaxel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Paclitaxel is a chemotherapy drug commonly used in treating ovarian cancer to inhibit cancer cell growth by preventing cell division.&lt;SEP&gt;Paclitaxel is a chemotherapy drug that is frequently used in combination regimens for treating cervical and other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Paclitaxel is a chemotherapy drug used in treatment regimens for ovarian cancer, often in combination with carboplatin.&lt;SEP&gt;Paclitaxel is a chemotherapy medication that is often combined with carboplatin to treat recurrent anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bevacizumab">
      <data key="d0">Bevacizumab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bevacizumab is a targeted cancer treatment that inhibits the growth of blood vessels to tumors, often used in conjunction with other chemotherapy drugs.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab, also known as Avastin, is a medication that stops the growth of new blood vessels feeding tumors, commonly used in chemotherapy for ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab, known as Avastin, is a targeted biologic therapy that helps block blood vessel growth in tumors and is often combined with chemotherapy for cervical cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that results in blood clots and can be a complication following surgeries like those for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Recovery">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Recovery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical recovery refers to the period after surgery when a patient heals, which may include hospital stay and gradual return to normal activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Doctors">
      <data key="d0">Doctors</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Doctors are medical professionals who diagnose and treat health conditions, including cancer, and are responsible for staging cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIGO Stages">
      <data key="d0">FIGO Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">FIGO stages are a standardized system for categorizing the severity and spread of ovarian cancer into distinct stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bevacizumab (Avastin)">
      <data key="d0">Bevacizumab (Avastin)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Bevacizumab is a targeted therapy used to treat certain types of ovarian cancer, particularly in cases of recurrence or as part of a chemotherapy regimen.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab, also known as Avastin, is a medication that may be added to chemotherapy regimens to inhibit tumor growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage 1A">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage 1A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A is the earliest stage of ovarian cancer where cancer is present in one ovary and the outer sac is intact without external cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage 1B">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage 1B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1B refers to ovarian cancer present in both ovaries while the outer surfaces are intact, with no cancer cells in ascites or washings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage 1C">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage 1C</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1C indicates cancer in one or both ovaries with complications such as capsular breakage or cancer cells in abdominal fluids.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage 2A">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage 2A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2A indicates that cancer from one or both ovaries has spread to uterus and/or fallopian tubes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage 2B">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage 2B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2B signifies that cancer has spread to other organs or tissues in the pelvis like bladder or colon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage 3A1">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage 3A1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3A1 denotes that cancer is in one or both ovaries and has spread to retroperitoneal lymph nodes, with varying sizes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage 3A2">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage 3A2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3A2 describes cancer that has spread to the lining of the abdomen but remains microscopic in size, including potential lymph node involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage 3B">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage 3B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3B identifies visible cancer on the abdominal lining measuring less than 2 cm, often with lymph node involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage 3C">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage 3C</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3C signifies visible cancer larger than 2 cm on the abdominal lining with possible spread to the liver or spleen surfaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage 4">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage 4</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 represents the most advanced stage where cancer has spread to distant parts of the body, such as other organs including liver or spleen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Pleural Effusion">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Pleural Effusion</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Malignant pleural effusion is a condition in Stage 4A characterized by cancer cells in the fluid around the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Spill">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Spill</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical spill refers to the condition in Stage 1C1 where the capsule of the ovary breaks during surgery, allowing cancer cells to spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neoadjuvant Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Neoadjuvant Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neoadjuvant therapy involves administering chemotherapy before surgery to shrink tumors, particularly in ovarian cancer cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Neoadjuvant therapy refers to systemic treatment given before surgery to shrink tumors or reduce cancer burden.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adjuvant Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Adjuvant Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adjuvant chemotherapy refers to treatment given after surgery to eliminate any remaining cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peripheral Neuropathy">
      <data key="d0">Peripheral Neuropathy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Peripheral neuropathy is a side effect of chemotherapy that results in nerve damage, causing pain and numbness in extremities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Cycles">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Cycles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment cycles are the intervals of chemotherapy administration followed by rest periods, allowing recovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cycle of Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Cycle of Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A cycle of treatment refers to a specific duration in which chemotherapy drugs are administered followed by a recovery phase.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Serous Tumors">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Serous Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade serous tumors are a common type of aggressive ovarian cancer which may require more intensive treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Intraperitoneal chemotherapy involves delivering chemotherapy drugs directly into the abdominal cavity, allowing for higher doses to reach cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inspectorate">
      <data key="d0">Inspectorate</data>
      <data key="d1">entity</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization that monitors the efficacy of chemotherapy and the patient’s response, including managing side effects during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARP Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">PARP Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PARP inhibitors are a class of drugs used in the treatment and maintenance of ovarian cancer, especially for those with BRCA mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;PARP inhibitors are drugs that inhibit the PARP protein, which is involved in repairing DNA damage in cancer cells, leading to cancer cell death.&lt;SEP&gt;PARP inhibitors are targeted cancer therapies used in maintenance therapy for breast and ovarian cancers with specific genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Olaparib">
      <data key="d0">Olaparib</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Olaparib is a PARP inhibitor used in maintenance therapy for patients with ovarian cancer, given for up to 2 years.&lt;SEP&gt;Olaparib is a specific PARP inhibitor indicated for use in maintenance therapy after initial treatment of BRCA-mutated ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Olaparib, known as Lynparza, is a PARP inhibitor utilized in treating certain breast cancers, particularly in patients with BRCA mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Niraparib">
      <data key="d0">Niraparib</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Niraparib is a PARP inhibitor that serves as an alternative maintenance therapy option for patients unable to take Olaparib.&lt;SEP&gt;Niraparib is another PARP inhibitor prescribed as a maintenance therapy option for up to 3 years for ovarian cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rucaparib">
      <data key="d0">Rucaparib</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Rucaparib is a PARP inhibitor that can be administered alone for up to 2 years in the treatment of ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Rucaparib is another PARP inhibitor used for treating ovarian cancer and is indicated for patients with BRCA mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Avastin">
      <data key="d0">Avastin</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Avastin is the brand name for bevacizumab, a medication that inhibits the growth of new blood vessels supplying tumors, used in the treatment of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maintenance Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Maintenance Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Maintenance therapy for intravesical BCG involves continued treatment to prevent cancer recurrence and is typically conducted on a specified schedule following initial treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Maintenance therapy is an ongoing treatment after initial cancer treatment designed to reduce the risk of cancer recurrence or progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Combination Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Combination Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Combination therapy in cancer treatment often refers to using multiple medications, such as chemotherapy and targeted therapies like PARP inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CA-125">
      <data key="d0">CA-125</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">CA-125 is a tumor marker blood test used to monitor treatment and check for cancer recurrence, particularly in ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;CA-125 is a tumor marker used to monitor the status of ovarian cancer after treatment, indicating recurrence or response to therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA Mutation">
      <data key="d0">BRCA Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">genetic_marker</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA mutations are genetic alterations that increase the risk of breast and ovarian cancers, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myelodysplastic Syndrome (MDS)">
      <data key="d0">Myelodysplastic Syndrome (MDS)</data>
      <data key="d1">health_condition</data>
      <data key="d2">Myelodysplastic syndrome is a disorder caused by poorly formed or dysfunctional blood cells that may develop due to cancer treatments such as chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy side effects encompass various unwanted symptoms experienced by patients during or after treatment, including fatigue, nausea, and infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Olaparib (Lynparza)">
      <data key="d0">Olaparib (Lynparza)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Olaparib, also known as Lynparza, is a PARP inhibitor that is used for maintenance treatment of ovarian cancer, particularly in BRCA mutation-positive patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Niraparib (Zejula)">
      <data key="d0">Niraparib (Zejula)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Niraparib, known as Zejula, is a PARP inhibitor used for maintenance therapy in ovarian cancer patients, offering an alternative to Olaparib.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rucaparib (Rubraca)">
      <data key="d0">Rucaparib (Rubraca)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Rucaparib, branded as Rubraca, is a PARP inhibitor indicated for treatment in patients with recurrent ovarian cancer and specific genetic profiles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Survivor">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Survivor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An ovarian cancer survivor is an individual who has completed treatment for ovarian cancer and shares insights on the life-altering experiences associated with the disease and recovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mirvetuximab Soravtansine-Gynx (Elahere)">
      <data key="d0">Mirvetuximab Soravtansine-Gynx (Elahere)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Mirvetuximab Soravtansine-Gynx is a specific targeted therapy used for treating ovarian cancer with the folate receptor alpha biomarker.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Cytoreductive Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Cytoreductive Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondary cytoreductive surgery refers to the surgical procedure aimed at removing all visible cancer during a recurrence treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA1 and BRCA2 Mutations">
      <data key="d0">BRCA1 and BRCA2 Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA1 and BRCA2 mutations are genetic markers that indicate a higher risk for certain cancers, including ovarian cancer, and guide treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormonal Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Hormonal Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormonal therapy is a treatment option for certain types of ovarian cancer that can help manage hormone-sensitive cancer growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Throat Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Throat cancer refers to cancers that develop in the throat, including the oropharynx, and is connected to various risk factors, diagnostics, and treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer refers to malignancies occurring in the throat area, which can be staged to assess the spread of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer refers to malignancies that occur in the throat (oropharynx) and is specifically characterized by the development of tumors in that area, most commonly in the form of squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oropharynx">
      <data key="d0">Oropharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The oropharynx is a part of the throat located behind the mouth, important in both the respiratory and digestive systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is a part of the throat that includes critical structures such as the soft palate, tonsils, and the base of the tongue, and is the primary site of throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is a part of the throat that includes the back of the roof of the mouth, tonsils, and the base of the tongue, and is a focal point in discussions about throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is a section of the pharynx that includes various anatomical structures such as the soft palate and the tonsils.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Volume Center">
      <data key="d0">High-Volume Center</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A high-volume center specializes in cancer treatment, equipped with extensive experience and resources to handle complex cases effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;High-volume centers are hospitals or clinics that specialize in the treatment of specific types of cancer and are recommended for patients due to their experience and outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Guides for Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Patient Guides for Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer is an educational resource that provides information on various types of cancer and their treatment options for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Head and Neck">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Head and Neck</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell carcinoma of the head and neck (SCCHN) is a common type of cancer that occurs in the mucosal linings of the oral cavity, pharynx, and larynx, significantly impacting the throat cancer category.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mucus Membranes">
      <data key="d0">Mucus Membranes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mucus membranes are linings in the body that are involved in the production of mucus, which can be the site for the development of squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Larynx">
      <data key="d0">Larynx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The larynx, also known as the voice box, is the section of the throat that facilitates speech and acts as a passage for air, playing a significant role in throat anatomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Voice Box">
      <data key="d0">Voice Box</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The voice box is another term for the larynx, which is instrumental in sound production and an important site for potential cancer development in the throat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Testing">
      <data key="d0">HPV Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV testing detects the presence of Human Papillomavirus, often used to evaluate the risk of throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV testing helps in diagnosing certain types of throat cancer and is performed through biomarker testing and biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV testing is a diagnostic method to detect the presence of human papillomavirus in cancer patients, particularly important in diagnosing and staging throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV testing is a diagnostic procedure recommended for individuals diagnosed with throat cancer to assess viral involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility counseling is advice offered to individuals undergoing cancer treatment regarding their reproductive health and planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility counseling provides guidance to individuals with cancer, addressing concerns related to fertility due to cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco and Alcohol Use">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco and Alcohol Use</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tobacco and Alcohol Use refers to the consumption of these substances, which can adversely affect cancer treatment outcomes and healing.&lt;SEP&gt;Tobacco and alcohol usage is associated with increased risks for developing head and neck cancers and impacts treatment efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tobacco and alcohol use are significant risk factors for developing head and neck cancers and can impact treatment effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Throat">
      <data key="d0">Throat</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The throat is the part of the anatomy that includes the oropharynx and plays a critical role in various bodily functions, including breathing and swallowing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A cancer diagnosis refers to the medical determination of cancer presence, affecting emotional and mental wellbeing.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer diagnosis involves identifying the presence of cancer through tests and examinations, including assessing the family health history.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer diagnosis is the process of identifying cancer in a patient through various tests, including imaging and biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer diagnosis refers to the medical identification of cancer in a patient, which can lead to emotional and psychological distress.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing Procedures">
      <data key="d0">Testing Procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing procedures are essential diagnostic processes used to assess health conditions in patients, including imaging tests for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Testing procedures for prostate cancer include various diagnostic tests such as blood tests, digital exams, and imaging techniques to assess health status and cancer stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Testing procedures refer to the various methods, such as imaging and biopsies, used to determine the presence of cancer and inform treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support Services">
      <data key="d0">Support Services</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Support services involve various resources available to patients, including counseling and therapy for emotional well-being during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Record Organization">
      <data key="d0">Medical Record Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical record organization refers to the systematic management of health documents, including insurance forms and test results, crucial for patient care efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Team">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment team comprises medical professionals who collaborate to create and manage a patient's cancer treatment plan, addressing side effects and overall care.&lt;SEP&gt;The Treatment Team involves various specialists working together to create and manage a personalized treatment plan for a cancer patient.&lt;SEP&gt;The treatment team comprises healthcare professionals involved in devising and executing a patient's treatment plan based on biopsy results.&lt;SEP&gt;The treatment team consists of healthcare professionals who work together to manage and provide care for patients undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The treatment team consists of various healthcare professionals who collaborate to develop and implement a personalized treatment plan for patients with bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The treatment team refers to the group of healthcare professionals that collaborate to provide comprehensive care to cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distress">
      <data key="d0">Distress</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress refers to an unpleasant experience affecting mental, physical, social, or spiritual aspects, commonly experienced by cancer patients during treatment and diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mind and Body Therapists">
      <data key="d0">Mind and Body Therapists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mind and Body Therapists are healthcare professionals who provide psychological support and therapies to help cancer patients cope with emotional distress.&lt;SEP&gt;Mind and body therapists are professionals that assist cancer patients in coping with the emotional and psychological impacts of their diagnosis through various therapeutic techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B is a viral infection that affects the liver, which can become reactivated in cancer patients undergoing specific treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis C">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis C</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis C is another liver infection caused by a virus that may be influenced by cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Function Tests">
      <data key="d0">Lung Function Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung Function Tests are assessments that measure how well a patient's lungs are working, essential for patients undergoing cancer treatment that may affect lung function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weight Loss Monitoring">
      <data key="d0">Weight Loss Monitoring</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Weight Loss Monitoring involves tracking a patient's weight during treatment to prevent malnutrition and ensure overall health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cessation Support">
      <data key="d0">Cessation Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cessation Support encompasses resources and counseling services offered to help patients stop smoking or vaping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Guidelines for Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Patient Guidelines for Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient Guidelines for Cancer is an organization providing resources to educate patients about their cancer care and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16 testing">
      <data key="d0">p16 testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">p16 testing refers to the process used for assessing the presence of the p16 protein, which is related to certain types of cancer, including throat cancers linked to HPV.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV">
      <data key="d0">HPV</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV (Human Papillomavirus) is an extremely common sexually transmitted virus and a significant risk factor for the development of anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV (human papillomavirus) is a group of viruses that can infect skin and mucosal surfaces and is associated with several cancers, including throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV, or human papillomavirus, is a sexually transmitted virus linked to an increased risk of developing anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV, or human papillomavirus, is a virus that can lead to various types of cancer, including anal, cervical, and head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Papillomavirus (HPV) is a sexually transmitted virus that is the most significant risk factor associated with the development of anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Human papillomavirus (HPV) is a common sexually transmitted infection that is the primary cause of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI scan">
      <data key="d0">MRI scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI scan is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses powerful magnets and radio waves to create images of organs and structures inside the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormonal Birth Control">
      <data key="d0">Hormonal Birth Control</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormonal birth control is a method of preventing pregnancy that uses hormones to regulate ovulation and menstrual cycles, with potential implications for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormonal birth control methods are contraceptive options that use hormones to prevent pregnancy, which may be discussed with patients before cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormonal birth control methods are contraceptives that may be used to prevent pregnancy during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barrier Methods">
      <data key="d0">Barrier Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Barrier methods are contraceptive techniques that physically block sperm from reaching the egg, including condoms and diaphragms, recommended during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Barrier methods include various contraceptives such as condoms, diaphragms, and cervical caps which are used to prevent pregnancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Barrier methods of contraception include devices that physically prevent sperm from reaching the egg, relevant for patients concerned about fertility during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intrauterine Devices (IUDs)">
      <data key="d0">Intrauterine Devices (IUDs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IUDs are a form of barrier contraception that can be discussed as an option for preventing pregnancy during treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrauterine devices are a form of long-term birth control that can be used to prevent pregnancy, important for patients undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrauterine devices are long-term contraceptive devices placed in the uterus to prevent pregnancy, with specific recommendations for cancer treatment patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contraceptive Sponge">
      <data key="d0">Contraceptive Sponge</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The contraceptive sponge is a barrier method used to prevent pregnancy; it is a soft round sponge containing spermicide.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Menstruation">
      <data key="d0">Menstruation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Menstruation refers to the monthly shedding of the uterine lining, which may be affected by cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16 Protein">
      <data key="d0">p16 Protein</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">p16 protein is a biomarker tested in cancer patients to understand the presence of HPV infection and gauge the nature of certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy Samples">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy Samples</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsy samples are small tissue samples taken for testing to diagnose diseases, including cancer, and to determine the presence of infections like HPV.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT or CAT Scan">
      <data key="d0">CT or CAT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">CT scans use x-rays to create detailed images of internal body structures, crucial for cancer diagnosis and monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Machine">
      <data key="d0">MRI Machine</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI machine is used to conduct MRI scans, utilizing strong magnets and radio waves to visualize internal organs without ionizing radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;The MRI machine uses powerful magnets to create images of the inside of the body without radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metal in Body">
      <data key="d0">Metal in Body</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Presence of metal in the body refers to any metallic implants or devices that can influence imaging results during MRI scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG-PET/CT combines positron emission tomography with computed tomography using a radiotracer to identify cancerous tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging scan that utilizes fluorodeoxyglucose to enhance the detection of cancerous tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fiberoptic Nasopharyngolaryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Fiberoptic Nasopharyngolaryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This procedure involves the use of a fiberoptic scope to examine the larynx and surrounding structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Laryngoscopy is a medical examination of the throat and voice box using a specialized scope, crucial for identifying tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Open MRI">
      <data key="d0">Open MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An Open MRI is a type of magnetic resonance imaging that features an open design to reduce feelings of claustrophobia for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Open MRI refers to a type of magnetic resonance imaging that features an open design, allowing patients to feel less claustrophobic compared to traditional closed MRIs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Closed MRI">
      <data key="d0">Closed MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Closed MRI is the more common form of MRI that has a cylindrical design, which creates a more enclosed space for imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;Closed MRI is the more common type of MRI that has a tube-shaped design which can cause discomfort for patients with claustrophobia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sodium Fluoride PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">Sodium Fluoride PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sodium fluoride PET/CT is a specific type of imaging scan used for assessing bone metastasis in cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Biopsy (FNB)">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Biopsy (FNB)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fine-Needle Biopsy (FNB) is a minimally invasive procedure used to extract samples from lumps or masses for diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Biopsy is a technique similar to FNA but may involve different needle sizes for tissue sampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiotracer">
      <data key="d0">Radiotracer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Radiotracer is a substance injected into the body that emits small amounts of radiation for the purpose of imaging and detecting issues within bones or organs.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiotracer is a radioactive substance used in imaging tests like PET scans to highlight areas of interest in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiotracer refers to small amounts of radioactive materials used in PET scans to visualize metabolic activity, particularly in cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiotracers are radioactive compounds used in imaging to observe the function of organs and systems within the body, crucial for conducting PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiotracers are substances used in various medical imaging techniques that emit radiation to highlight areas of interest within the body for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Numbing Spray">
      <data key="d0">Numbing Spray</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Numbing spray is a topical anesthetic applied before procedures such as laryngoscopy or endoscopy to reduce discomfort.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound Probe">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound Probe</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An ultrasound probe, or transducer, is a device used to emit sound waves and capture images of internal structures during an ultrasound.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panorex">
      <data key="d0">Panorex</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Panorex, or panoramic dental x-ray, provides a comprehensive image of the mouth, including teeth and jaw structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscope">
      <data key="d0">Endoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An endoscope is a flexible tube with a camera and light that allows doctors to view the interior of a patient's body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laryngoscope">
      <data key="d0">Laryngoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A laryngoscope is a tool used during laryngoscopy to examine the larynx and associated vocal structures.&lt;SEP&gt;The laryngoscope is a medical instrument used in direct laryngoscopy to visualize the throat and obtain tissue samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fiberoptic laryngoscopy is a medical procedure using a flexible scope to examine the throat, typically done under local anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Direct Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Direct Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Direct laryngoscopy is a more detailed examination of the throat performed under general anesthesia using a laryngoscope.&lt;SEP&gt;Direct laryngoscopy is a more detailed examination of the throat performed under general anesthesia with a laryngoscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Esophagoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophagoscopy is a procedure where a device is guided into the esophagus to detect abnormal growths, performed under anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Bronchoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchoscopy involves inserting a device to examine the airways and may be used for cancer detection or treatment, performed under anesthesia.&lt;SEP&gt;Bronchoscopy involves inserting a device to examine the airways and may be used for cancer detection or treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panendoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Panendoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Panendoscopy is a comprehensive procedure enabling doctors to examine multiple parts of the upper digestive tract using various endoscopes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Programmed Death-Ligand 1 (PD-L1)">
      <data key="d0">Programmed Death-Ligand 1 (PD-L1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 is an immune checkpoint protein that can be expressed on cancer cells, indicating potential treatment pathways involving immune therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;PD-L1 is an immune protein that can suppress anti-tumor response in cancer, influencing treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Numbing Medicine">
      <data key="d0">Numbing Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Numbing medicine is used in fiberoptic laryngoscopy to minimize discomfort during the procedure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tissue Sample">
      <data key="d0">Tissue Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tissue sample is collected during laryngoscopy for biopsy to identify tumors or abnormalities in the throat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">FNA is a procedure to collect tissue samples from enlarged lymph nodes for testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration is a minimally invasive procedure used to collect cells from thyroid nodules to assess for cancer, although it cannot definitively diagnose follicular or oncocytic cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration is a minimally invasive procedure used to collect tissue samples from a mass or lump, commonly utilized in biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration is a type of biopsy that uses a thin needle to obtain a tissue or fluid sample.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Needle Biopsy (CNB)">
      <data key="d0">Core Needle Biopsy (CNB)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Core Needle Biopsy involves using larger needles to retrieve more substantial tissue samples for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deficient Mismatch Repair (dMMR)">
      <data key="d0">Deficient Mismatch Repair (dMMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deficient Mismatch Repair indicates the failure of cellular mechanisms to correct errors in DNA, relevant to cancer diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) indicates the absence or dysfunction of MMR proteins, leading to increased mutation rates in DNA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Combined Positive Score (CPS)">
      <data key="d0">Combined Positive Score (CPS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CPS is a measurement of PD-L1 expression in tumors and immune cells to assess eligibility for certain cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Throat Cancer (Oropharyngeal Cancer)">
      <data key="d0">Throat Cancer (Oropharyngeal Cancer)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Throat cancer, specifically oropharyngeal cancer, is a type of cancer that occurs in the throat and can be influenced by factors such as HPV status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy is a form of cancer treatment that enhances the immune system's ability to fight cancer by inhibiting proteins that suppress the immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;Immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy represents a type of treatment that helps the immune system recognize and attack cancer cells by targeting specific proteins like PD-1 and PD-L1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Checkpoint inhibitor therapy involves using drugs that help the immune system recognize and attack cancer cells more effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Checkpoint inhibitor therapy is a type of immunotherapy designed to inhibit proteins that prevent immune cells from attacking cancer cells, thus enhancing the body’s immune response against tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PDL1">
      <data key="d0">PDL1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PDL1 is a protein expressed on the surface of cancer cells and some immune cells, playing a crucial role in the immune checkpoint pathway, which can inhibit T cell activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CPS (Combined Positive Score)">
      <data key="d0">CPS (Combined Positive Score)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CPS is a measure used to assess the level of PD-L1 expression in the tumor and immune cells, which may influence treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor p16 Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor p16 Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor p16 testing is a diagnostic method used to detect p16 protein in tumor cells, often linked to HPV-related cancers, particularly in the oropharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden High (TMB-H)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden High (TMB-H)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TMB-H indicates a high number of mutations present in a tumor, which can suggest a better response to certain immunotherapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cell-Free DNA Testing">
      <data key="d0">Cell-Free DNA Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cell-free DNA testing refers to the analysis of circulating tumor DNA from a blood sample to monitor cancer status and treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Throat Exam">
      <data key="d0">Throat Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A throat exam includes procedures to visualize the throat region, often using tools like a scope, to diagnose issues such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dental Health">
      <data key="d0">Dental Health</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dental health refers to the maintenance of healthy teeth and gums, which can be affected by cancer treatments targeting the head and neck.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Impact">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Impact</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility impact describes how certain cancer treatments may affect a patient's ability to conceive in the future.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes called lymphoblasts in the bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer originating in lymphocytes, the white blood cells that are pivotal to the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is another type of blood cancer that affects immature white blood cells, distinct from AML but relevant in the discussion of leukemias.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a type of blood cancer affecting lymphoblasts, with variants primarily involving B or T cells and presenting in various age groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment for ALL">
      <data key="d0">Treatment for ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment for ALL varies based on the type and age at diagnosis, and it involves methods aimed at eliminating the cancerous lymphoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P16-positive or P16-negative Cancer Stages">
      <data key="d0">P16-positive or P16-negative Cancer Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">These stages refer to whether throat cancer is associated with the human papillomavirus (HPV) and influence treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM Scores">
      <data key="d0">TNM Scores</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TNM Scores categorize cancer by Tumor size (T), Node involvement (N), and Metastasis (M), crucial for staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 to Stage 4">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 to Stage 4</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The stages of cancer range from stage 0, indicating in situ cancers, to stage IV, indicating advanced disease spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV-related Cancer">
      <data key="d0">HPV-related Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV-related Cancer refers to cancers influenced by the human papillomavirus, specifically within throat cancer stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Throat Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Throat Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Throat tumors can metastasize to nearby lymph nodes and are a focus in throat cancer staging and treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locoregional Disease">
      <data key="d0">Locoregional Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locoregional Disease describes tumors that have spread to nearby tissues or lymph nodes but not to distant sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Disease">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic Disease indicates cancer that has spread to other parts of the body, impacting treatment options and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic disease refers to cancer that has spread from its original site to distant locations in the body, complicating treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoma in Situ">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoma in Situ</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoma in Situ refers to early-stage cancer where abnormal cells are present but have not invaded nearby tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Carcinoma in situ is a flat patch of cancer cells that has not yet invaded deeper layers of tissue, classified as stage 0 bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing cancer starting in lymphocytes, characterized by the production of immature lymphoblasts that hinder proper blood function.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing type of blood cancer that primarily impacts lymphocytes, a crucial component of the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell ALL">
      <data key="d0">B-cell ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell ALL, or B-ALL, is a subtype of ALL that starts in B-cell lymphocytes and is generally more prevalent than its T-cell counterpart.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell ALL">
      <data key="d0">T-cell ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell ALL, or T-ALL, originates from T-cell lymphocytes and may lead to complications such as an enlarged thymus affecting breathing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma is characterized by the growth of B-cell lymphoblasts primarily in the lymph nodes rather than in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma, or T-LL, occurs when T-cell lymphoblasts grow mainly in the thymus instead of the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric refers to Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) cases found in infants, children, and young adults aged 18 and under.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AYAs">
      <data key="d0">AYAs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AYAs, or Adolescent and Young Adults, refers to individuals aged 15 to 39 who may receive either pediatric or adult cancer care based on their situation.&lt;SEP&gt;AYAs, or Adolescents and Young Adults, refers to individuals aged 15 to 39 at the time of their initial cancer diagnosis, who may receive treatment in pediatric or adult centers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">B-cell Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell involved in the immune response that can develop into Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia when they become abnormal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">T-cell Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell lymphocytes are another type of white blood cell that can give rise to T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia under certain conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoblasts">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoblasts are immature lymphocytes that proliferate in Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia and can crowd out normal blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoblasts are immature lymphoid cells that can also increase in advanced CML phases, affecting treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoblasts are immature white blood cells that proliferate in ALL, leading to reduced normal blood cell production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burkitt Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Burkitt Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Burkitt lymphoma is a rare subtype of B-cell lymphoblastic leukemia that shares similarities with B-ALL but is treated differently.&lt;SEP&gt;Burkitt lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that shares similarities with Burkitt leukemia in terms of cellular origin and treatment, but is distinct in its clinical management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progenitor cells are early blood-forming cells that can develop into specific types of blood cells, including lymphocytes, through differentiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Progenitor cells are early descendants of stem cells that can differentiate into various types of blood cells, including lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric Cancer Centers">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric Cancer Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric cancer centers specialize in treating children and adolescents with various types of cancers, including ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adolescents and Young Adults (AYAs)">
      <data key="d0">Adolescents and Young Adults (AYAs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AYAs are individuals aged 15 to 39 at the time of their initial cancer diagnosis, who may require specialized treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antibodies">
      <data key="d0">Antibodies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Antibodies are proteins produced by B-cell lymphocytes that help in identifying and neutralizing pathogens like bacteria and viruses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN®">
      <data key="d0">NCCN®</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN® (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) is a non-profit organization that provides guidelines and information for cancer care and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALL">
      <data key="d0">ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ALL (Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia) is a type of cancer affecting blood and bone marrow, characterized by the overproduction of lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a type of cancer that affects the lymphoid progenitor cells, leading to an increase in lymphoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-ALL is the more common form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, involving abnormal lymphoblasts and requiring targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;B-ALL refers to B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, which starts in B-cell lymphocytes and is more common than T-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that affects B lymphocytes and has distinct treatment protocols.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that starts in B-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-ALL">
      <data key="d0">T-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-ALL refers to a type of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that originates in T-cell lymphocytes, often requiring specialized treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;T-ALL stands for T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, which originates in T-cell lymphocytes and can cause an enlarged thymus.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL) is a less common subtype of ALL, originating in T-cell lymphocytes and requiring specialized treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that starts in T-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Rectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Rectal cancer is a common cancer that starts in the rectum and can be treated effectively, especially when detected early.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that develops from the tissues of the rectum, which can be initially diagnosed through polyps or lesions.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the rectum, often originating from polyps on the rectal lining, and can present as adenocarcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the rectum, often requiring surgical interventions like LAR or APR based on tumor location and stage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoid Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoid Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoid progenitor cells are derived from blood stem cells and are precursors to lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell affected in ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoid progenitor cells are precursor cells in bone marrow that differentiate into lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Tests">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility tests assess reproductive health and are important for cancer patients undergoing treatments that may affect fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Advocacy Groups">
      <data key="d0">Patient Advocacy Groups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient Advocacy Groups support individuals with leukemia and other cancers, providing resources, information, and guidance in navigating care options.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient advocacy groups provide support and resources for individuals affected by cancer, helping them navigate treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colorectal Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colorectal Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines provide evidence-based recommendations for colorectal cancer screening and patient management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polypectomy">
      <data key="d0">Polypectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure performed during a colonoscopy to remove polyps and prevent cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure performed during a colonoscopy to remove polyps from the colon, aiming to prevent potential cancer growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure performed during a colonoscopy to remove polyps that may develop into cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure performed to remove polyps from the rectal wall, helping to prevent the progression to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure used to remove polyps from the colon, helping to prevent the development of colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a surgical procedure carried out during a colonoscopy to remove polyps from the colon.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a surgical procedure for removing polyps from the rectal lining, which can prevent the progression to malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Testing">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch Repair Testing is a diagnostic process for identifying mutations in genes that are responsible for repairing DNA and determining treatment options for colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mismatch repair testing involves checking rectal cancer tumors for mutations in genes that repair DNA, helping to guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Mismatch repair testing is a genetic test for rectal cancer that checks for mutations in genes responsible for repairing DNA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ctDNA Testing">
      <data key="d0">ctDNA Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">ctDNA testing, or circulating tumor DNA testing, is an innovative method for detecting small fragments of tumor DNA in the blood, offering insights into cancer dynamics post-treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;ctDNA testing, or liquid biopsy, is a developing method for detecting small DNA fragments released by tumors into the bloodstream, useful for tracking cancer presence post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperplastic Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Hyperplastic Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperplastic polyps are benign growths in the colon that typically do not develop into cancer, and are commonly found during colonoscopies.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperplastic polyps are generally benign and rarely turn into cancer, forming in the colon and rectum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory polyps are another type of benign growth that can occur in the colon, often due to inflammation, and usually do not lead to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory polyps are non-cancerous growths that can occur due to inflammation in the colon and rarely progress to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN is an organization that produces guidelines for cancer care, including recommended screening and treatment protocols for colorectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN is an organization that provides guidelines for cancer care, including patient guidelines for treatment options for various stages of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endorectal Ultrasound (EUS)">
      <data key="d0">Endorectal Ultrasound (EUS)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endorectal Ultrasound employs sound waves to produce images of the rectum, assisting in evaluating rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Endorectal Ultrasound is a procedure that utilizes sound waves to visualize the rectum, often used in examining rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sperm Banking">
      <data key="d0">Sperm Banking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sperm Banking is a fertility preservation method that involves freezing a man’s sperm for future reproductive use.&lt;SEP&gt;Sperm Banking is a technique that allows males to freeze and store sperm for potential future use, aiding in fertility preservation.&lt;SEP&gt;Sperm banking is a fertility preservation technique that allows individuals to store sperm for future use, particularly relevant before cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Sperm banking is a process that allows men to freeze their sperm before cancer treatments potentially affect their fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Egg Freezing">
      <data key="d0">Egg Freezing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Egg Freezing is a fertility preservation option involving the removal and freezing of a woman's eggs for later use.&lt;SEP&gt;Egg Freezing is the process of retrieving and freezing unfertilized eggs to preserve a woman's ability to conceive in the future.&lt;SEP&gt;Egg freezing is a fertility preservation method for women that involves harvesting and freezing eggs for future use.&lt;SEP&gt;Egg freezing is a fertility preservation option that allows unfertilized eggs to be collected, frozen, and stored for later use.&lt;SEP&gt;Egg freezing is the process of extracting and freezing a woman's eggs for future use, commonly discussed among cancer patients about to undergo treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Tissue Banking">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Tissue Banking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian Tissue Banking is a fertility preservation method involving the freezing of ovarian tissue for future reproductive use.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian Tissue Banking is a procedure where part or all of an ovary is removed and preserved for future use in fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian tissue banking is a method that involves freezing ovarian tissue containing eggs for potential future transplantation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Transposition">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Transposition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian Transposition is a surgical procedure that moves the ovaries out of the radiation field to protect them during treatment for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian Transposition is a surgical procedure to relocate ovaries away from radiation exposure during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian Transposition is a surgical process that moves ovaries out of the radiation field to protect them during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian transposition is a surgical procedure that relocates the ovaries out of the radiation field to protect them during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian transposition is a surgical procedure to move ovaries out of the radiation field during cancer treatment to preserve fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast">
      <data key="d0">Contrast</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast is a substance used in imaging tests to enhance the visibility of internal structures in scans, critical for accurate diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steroid Medication">
      <data key="d0">Steroid Medication</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Steroid medications are often administered prior to imaging tests to reduce the risk of allergic reactions associated with contrast usage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A polyp is an overgrowth of cells on the inner lining of the rectal wall, which can be benign or malignant; adenomas are common precursors to rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are abnormal tissue growths on the lining of the rectum that can be benign but have the potential to develop into cancerous lesions.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are abnormal tissue growths that can develop in the colon and potentially lead to colorectal cancer if not removed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection (ESD)">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection (ESD)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endoscopic submucosal dissection is a minimally invasive procedure for removing early-stage rectal cancers or tumors without causing additional damage to surrounding tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Endoscopic submucosal dissection is a minimally invasive surgical technique used for the removal of early-stage rectal cancers while preserving surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Local Excision">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Local Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Transanal local excision is a surgical method for removing rectal tumors through the anus, aimed at treating low-risk cancers with minimal invasiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Transanal local excision is a surgical technique that allows for the removal of rectal tumors through the anus, aiming minimally invasive surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI of the Pelvis">
      <data key="d0">MRI of the Pelvis</data>
      <data key="d1">technology</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI of the pelvis is a specific imaging technique used to assess the extent and staging of rectal cancer, providing detailed images of the rectal area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT of the Chest and Abdomen">
      <data key="d0">CT of the Chest and Abdomen</data>
      <data key="d1">technology</data>
      <data key="d2">CT scan of the chest and abdomen helps detect metastasis of rectal cancer, determining if cancer has spread to the liver, lungs, or other organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flexible Sigmoidoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Flexible Sigmoidoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Flexible sigmoidoscopy is a medical procedure for examining the lower part of the colon, typically used for monitoring after treatment of rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endorectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Endorectal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">technology</data>
      <data key="d2">Endorectal ultrasound is an imaging technique used to evaluate rectal tumors and guide surgical decisions, especially in early-stage cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T1 Tumor">
      <data key="d0">T1 Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A T1 tumor is an early-stage cancer that has not grown beyond the submucosal layer of the rectal wall, generally considered for ESD or local excision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recurrence Monitoring">
      <data key="d0">Recurrence Monitoring</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurrence monitoring refers to the follow-up procedures employed after the treatment of rectal cancer to check for any signs of cancer returning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Transabdominal Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Transabdominal surgery is a preferred treatment method for tumors that have invaded the muscular layer of the rectal wall.&lt;SEP&gt;Transabdominal surgery is a surgical approach for tumors that have invaded deeper layers of the rectal wall, often required for more advanced stages of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemoradiation">
      <data key="d0">Chemoradiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy as a primary treatment for non-metastatic anal cancers, maximizing treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy for a synergistic effect in treating non-metastatic anal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy for enhanced effectiveness in treating locally advanced cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy, often recommended for cervical cancer treatment, especially in advanced-stage cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy, often used in the treatment of more advanced stages of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation involves the combined use of chemotherapy and radiation therapy to treat cancers, often indicated for advanced cervical cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy, typically used for locally advanced cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combined treatment approach involving chemotherapy and radiation therapy, typically employed for locally advanced cancers to reduce tumor size.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combined treatment approach using chemotherapy and radiation therapy to enhance effectiveness against bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combined treatment method that includes chemotherapy and radiation therapy, often used in cancer treatment to target and eliminate cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combined treatment modality using chemotherapy and radiation therapy together to enhance treatment efficacy for bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combined treatment strategy involving chemotherapy and radiation therapy, recommended for non-metastatic anal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a treatment strategy combining EBRT and chemotherapy, commonly recommended for locally advanced cervical cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is the combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy administered simultaneously to improve treatment efficacy for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation refers to the combined use of chemotherapy and radiation therapy to treat cancer, often used for locally advanced stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Anterior Resection (LAR)">
      <data key="d0">Low Anterior Resection (LAR)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Low Anterior Resection is a surgical procedure that removes tumors in the mid to upper rectum and may involve reconnecting the colon.&lt;SEP&gt;Low Anterior Resection is a type of transabdominal surgery that removes tumors from the mid to upper rectum while preserving bowel function when possible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)">
      <data key="d0">Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Abdominoperineal Resection is a surgery for lower rectal tumors, involving the removal of the rectum, anus, and creating a permanent colostomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal Resection is a surgical procedure for rectal tumors located in the lower rectum and anus, involving the creation of a permanent colostomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the anus and creation of a permanent colostomy when treating anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the anus, rectum, and part of the sigmoid colon, commonly used in anal cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection is a radical surgical procedure to remove the anus and surrounding tissue in cases of recurrence of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidney cancer is a disease characterized by the abnormal growth of cells in the kidneys, which play a crucial role in filtering blood and producing hormones.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidney cancer is characterized by the uncontrolled growth of cells in the kidneys, with various subtypes such as renal cell carcinoma (RCC).&lt;SEP&gt;Kidney cancer primarily refers to cancers that begin in the tissues of the kidneys, with renal cell carcinoma (RCC) being the most common subtype.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidney cancer primarily starts in the renal tubules and is the most prevalent urinary tract cancer, with multiple subtypes and treatment challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)">
      <data key="d0">Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RCC is the most prevalent form of kidney cancer, comprising about 90% of cases, and it typically arises in the renal tubules.&lt;SEP&gt;Renal cell carcinoma is the most prevalent type of kidney cancer, traditionally beginning in the renal tubules, and encompasses various subtypes including clear and non-clear cell types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wilms Tumor (Nephroblastoma)">
      <data key="d0">Wilms Tumor (Nephroblastoma)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Wilms tumor is a type of kidney cancer primarily seen in children, characterized by the presence of cancer cells in the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial carcinoma starts in the cells lining the renal pelvis and ureter, and although related to kidney cancer, it is treated differently.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell RCC (ccRCC)">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell RCC (ccRCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear cell RCC is the most common subtype of renal cell carcinoma, observed in about 70% of RCC cases, characterized by pale or clear-looking cells under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Clear Cell RCC (nccRCC)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Clear Cell RCC (nccRCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-clear cell RCC refers to various subtypes of renal cell carcinoma that do not present as clear cells and are identified through histological examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Irinotecan">
      <data key="d0">Irinotecan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Irinotecan is a chemotherapy medication used primarily to treat cancer, particularly effective in cases related to the gastrointestinal tract, and is associated with various side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidneys">
      <data key="d0">Kidneys</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The kidneys are organs in the urinary tract that filter waste from the blood to produce urine and regulate fluid balance.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidneys are vital organs located in the abdominal cavity responsible for filtering blood, producing hormones, and regulating fluid balance in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Tubules">
      <data key="d0">Renal Tubules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal tubules are tiny structures in the kidneys where blood is filtered, and waste is converted into urine, playing a crucial role in kidney function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ureters">
      <data key="d0">Ureters</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Ureters are muscular tubes that transport urine from the kidneys to the bladder, functioning as conduits for urinary excretion.&lt;SEP&gt;Ureters are tubes that carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder for storage before excretion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder">
      <data key="d0">Bladder</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The bladder is a hollow organ that stores urine until it is expelled from the body during urination.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is an organ that holds urine and plays a critical role in the urinary system.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is an organ that holds urine, playing an essential role in the urinary system.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is an organ that stores urine, located beneath the prostate in the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urethra">
      <data key="d0">Urethra</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Urethra is the duct through which urine is expelled from the bladder, which may be affected during bladder cancer surgeries.&lt;SEP&gt;The urethra is the duct through which urine is discharged from the bladder to the outside of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The urethra is the tube that carries urine from the bladder and out of the body, closely associated with prostate function.&lt;SEP&gt;The urethra is the tube that carries urine from the bladder out of the body, surrounded by the prostate gland.&lt;SEP&gt;The urethra is the tube that carries urine from the bladder to the outside of the body, completing the urinary system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gerota’s Fascia">
      <data key="d0">Gerota’s Fascia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gerota's fascia is a layer of connective tissue that surrounds the kidneys and adrenal glands, crucial for providing structural support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Kidney Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced kidney cancer refers to cancer in the kidneys that has progressed beyond Gerota's fascia, indicating a more serious stage of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell RCC Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell RCC Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear cell RCC is a subtype of renal cell carcinoma characterized by distinctive histological features seen under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">This resource provides patient-centric information and treatment options for bladder cancer, highlighting the importance of thorough understanding and care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clear cell RCC">
      <data key="d0">Clear cell RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear cell RCC (ccRCC) is the most common subtype of renal cell carcinoma, occurring in approximately 70% of RCC cases, characterized by pale or clear cells under microscopic examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-clear cell RCC">
      <data key="d0">Non-clear cell RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-clear cell RCC (nccRCC) represents a group of subtypes of renal cell carcinoma that includes papillary RCC and chromophobe RCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary RCC">
      <data key="d0">Papillary RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary RCC is the most common subtype of non-clear cell RCC, appearing under a microscope as long, thin, finger-like growths, found in about 10% of RCC cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Papillary RCC is the most prevalent subtype of non-clear cell RCC, typically characterized by long, thin, finger-like projections observed under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromophobe RCC">
      <data key="d0">Chromophobe RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromophobe RCC is a subtype of renal cell carcinoma with pale, larger cells, having a distinct morphology compared to clear and non-clear cell types.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromophobe RCC is characterized by pale cells that are larger than clear cells and have a distinct appearance under the microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Translocation RCC">
      <data key="d0">Translocation RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Translocation RCC can occur at any age but is often diagnosed in children, featuring unique gene fusions that promote cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Translocation RCC refers to kidney tumors that are often diagnosed in younger individuals, characterized by specific genetic fusions that drive cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomatoid RCC">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomatoid RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomatoid RCC is a variant of renal cell carcinoma that exhibits sarcomatoid features, generally associated with more aggressive behavior and faster growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Sarcomatoid RCC is characterized by sarcomatoid features in any RCC, indicating a more aggressive variant that can spread more rapidly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear cell RCC (ccRCC) is the most common subtype of renal cell carcinoma, occurring in about 70% of RCC cases, characterized by pale or clear cells under microscopic examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d0">Non-Clear Cell RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-clear cell RCC (nccRCC) includes several subtypes of renal cell carcinoma, which differ in cell appearance and behavior, with papillary RCC being the most common among them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Collecting Duct RCC">
      <data key="d0">Collecting Duct RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Collecting duct RCC develops in the cells lining the collecting ducts of the kidney and is a rare, aggressive subtype of renal cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Medullary Carcinoma (RMC)">
      <data key="d0">Renal Medullary Carcinoma (RMC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal medullary carcinoma is a rare subtype of kidney cancer often found in young patients of African descent, known for its aggressive behavior and poor prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic tumors are cancers that have spread from the original site to other parts of the body, such as bones, requiring different treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic tumors, also called metastases, are new tumors formed when cancer cells spread from the original site to distant parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sickle Cell Trait">
      <data key="d0">Sickle Cell Trait</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sickle cell trait refers to a genetic condition where individuals carry one sickle-cell gene but do not typically exhibit symptoms, although they carry the potential for sickling disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sickle Cell Disease">
      <data key="d0">Sickle Cell Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sickle cell disease is a group of inherited red blood cell disorders characterized by abnormal hemoglobin, leading to various complications and health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support System">
      <data key="d0">Support System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A support system consists of friends, family, and peers who provide emotional and practical help during diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A support system includes family, friends, and peers providing emotional and practical assistance during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A support system is a network of friends, family, or peers who provide assistance during diagnosis and treatment of health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Meals">
      <data key="d0">Meals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Meals refer to food prepared and provided by friends or family to support individuals during treatment or recovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Telehealth Visits">
      <data key="d0">Telehealth Visits</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Telehealth visits are remote consultations between patients and healthcare providers, often conducted via phone or video conferencing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopoietic stem cells are a specific type of blood stem cell responsible for generating all types of blood cells through differentiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematopoietic stem cells are specialized stem cells that produce blood cells and are crucial for maintaining blood health, particularly during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">T Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T lymphocytes are a category of lymphocytes that play a critical role in the immune response, particularly in targeting cancer cells and infections.&lt;SEP&gt;T lymphocytes, or T cells, are a type of lymphocyte that helps regulate immune responses and directly attack infected or tumor cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Killer Cells">
      <data key="d0">Natural Killer Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Natural killer cells are a type of lymphocyte that play a crucial role in the innate immune response by attacking tumor cells and viral-infected cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Leukemias">
      <data key="d0">Acute Leukemias</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute leukemias are a category of blood cancers characterized by rapid growth of immature blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Leukemias">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Leukemias</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic leukemias are a slower-growing type of blood cancer that tends to produce more mature blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burkitt Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Burkitt Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Burkitt leukemia is a rare subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) that is treated differently than standard B-ALL and is associated with B-cell lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Burkitt leukemia is a rare subtype of mature B-cell ALL, treated differently than standard B-ALL, yet closely related to Burkitt lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma (B-LL)">
      <data key="d0">B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma (B-LL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma is a condition where B-cell lymphoblasts grow predominantly in the lymph nodes instead of the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma (T-LL)">
      <data key="d0">T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma (T-LL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma occurs when T-cell lymphoblasts predominantly grow in the thymus rather than the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA)">
      <data key="d0">Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA) is a person aged 15 to 39 years at the time of initial cancer diagnosis who may receive treatment at various oncology centers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adult Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Adult Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An Adult Cancer Center is a medical facility that specializes in providing treatment for adult patients diagnosed with cancer, including ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN)">
      <data key="d0">Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN) is an aggressive subtype of leukemia that can manifest in blood, bone marrow, lymph nodes, and skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Blastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm (BPDCN) is a rare form of leukemia that may require a lymph node biopsy for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blood Cells (RBCs)">
      <data key="d0">Red Blood Cells (RBCs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Red Blood Cells, or erythrocytes, are responsible for carrying oxygen from the lungs to the rest of the body and returning carbon dioxide for exhalation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cells (WBCs)">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cells (WBCs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">White Blood Cells, or leukocytes, play a crucial role in the immune system by fighting infections and foreign invaders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platelets (PLTs)">
      <data key="d0">Platelets (PLTs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platelets (PLTs) are blood components that help prevent bleeding by forming clots, playing a crucial role in the body's hemostatic process.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets (PLTs) are cell fragments in the blood that play a significant role in blood clotting, preventing excessive bleeding during injuries.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets, or thrombocytes, are blood cells that help control bleeding by clumping and clotting blood vessel injuries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloblasts">
      <data key="d0">Myeloblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that are precursors to granulocytes and play a key role in the development of blood cells, particularly in the context of AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are very immature blood cells that can proliferate uncontrollably in CML, referred to as blasts in this context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gene Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Gene Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene mutations refer to changes in the DNA sequence that can influence the development of various subtypes of leukemia, including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Specialist">
      <data key="d0">AML Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An AML specialist is a medical professional with expertise in diagnosing and treating Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer care encompasses the various treatment options available for patients diagnosed with cancer, including surgery, chemotherapy, and radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer care involves a range of treatments and supportive services provided to patients diagnosed with cancer, emphasizing the importance of patient-centered approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer care involves medical treatment, support systems, and guidelines to help patients navigate their treatment options and improve outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow aspirate and biopsy are procedures used to collect bone marrow samples for diagnosing various blood disorders, including AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy are procedures used to collect samples from the bone marrow to diagnose and monitor blood cancers such as AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) Typing">
      <data key="d0">Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) Typing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">HLA typing is a test to determine compatibility for bone marrow transplants and is relevant for patients with AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET/CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An FDG-PET/CT scan combines positron emission tomography with computed tomography for improved accuracy in cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;An FDG-PET/CT scan combines positron emission tomography with computed tomography to improve the accuracy of cancer detection and evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;An FDG-PET/CT scan is an imaging test that helps evaluate the metabolic activity of cancer cells, used in AML to check for extra-medullary disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Differential Count">
      <data key="d0">Differential Count</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A differential count is a blood test that analyzes the number of each type of white blood cell and their balance, which can indicate various health conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A differential count is a blood test that analyzes the number of each type of white blood cell, which can indicate various health conditions, especially concerning blood diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hemoglobin (Hgb)">
      <data key="d0">Hemoglobin (Hgb)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hemoglobin (Hgb) is a crucial measurement that indicates the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood and can signify the need for a transfusion if levels are low.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin (Hgb) is a key measurement in blood tests that indicates the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood and can show the need for a transfusion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC)">
      <data key="d0">Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC) measures the number of neutrophils, a type of white blood cell essential for fighting bacterial infections.&lt;SEP&gt;The Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC) measures the number of neutrophils in the blood, which are crucial for fighting bacterial infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Creatinine">
      <data key="d0">Creatinine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Creatinine is a waste product cleared from the blood by the kidneys, and its levels are indicative of kidney function.&lt;SEP&gt;Creatinine is a waste product generated by muscle metabolism and is filtered out of the blood by the kidneys; levels are indicative of kidney function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN)">
      <data key="d0">Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) is a measure of nitrogen in the blood from urea, an indicator of kidney function and overall metabolic state.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) is a waste product that, when elevated, can signal impaired kidney function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrolytes">
      <data key="d0">Electrolytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrolytes are charged particles essential for the function of nerves, muscles, and other systems in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrolytes are minerals in blood that carry an electric charge and are critical for various physiological functions, including muscle contraction and hydration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Tests (LFTs)">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Tests (LFTs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver Function Tests (LFTs) assess the health and functionality of the liver by measuring enzymes, proteins, and substances produced by the liver.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver Function Tests (LFTs) measure various chemicals in the blood to assess the liver’s health and functionality.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver function tests measure liver enzymes to determine the liver's health, important for monitoring patients undergoing cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitamin B12 and Folic Acid">
      <data key="d0">Vitamin B12 and Folic Acid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitamin B12 and folic acid are vital nutrients needed for red and white blood cell formation, and their levels are monitored to ensure proper hematopoiesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Vitamin B12 and folic acid are vitamins necessary for the production of red and white blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA)">
      <data key="d0">Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Human leukocyte antigens (HLA) are proteins important for the immune response, and they are crucial for organ transplant compatibility.&lt;SEP&gt;Human leukocyte antigens (HLA) are proteins on cell surfaces that help the immune system recognize which cells belong to the body, crucial for organ transplantation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Status (PS)">
      <data key="d0">Performance Status (PS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance Status (PS) assesses a patient’s functional capacity and ability to perform daily activities, influencing clinical decision-making.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status assesses a patient's general health and ability to conduct daily activities, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS)">
      <data key="d0">Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The KPS is a performance status scale that assesses a patient's functional impairment and capabilities in daily tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;The Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is a standard measurement used to assess the functional status of cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunohistochemistry (IHC)">
      <data key="d0">Immunohistochemistry (IHC)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a laboratory technique used to study the presence of specific proteins in tissue samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a special staining method used to identify and study specific proteins in tissue samples through microscopy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Testing for AML">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Testing for AML</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic testing for AML involves analyzing leukemia cells for specific mutations and changes to guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS Scale">
      <data key="d0">PS Scale</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The PS Scale is a performance status measure used in oncology, including Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) score and Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS), to evaluate the overall health of cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopathology Review">
      <data key="d0">Hematopathology Review</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopathology review is the specialized evaluation of blood-related diseases, particularly relevant in assessing bone marrow samples for conditions like AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Changes">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic changes in leukemia cells can influence the disease's behavior and response to treatment, requiring thorough testing and assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic changes refer to alterations in DNA sequences that occur in leukemia cells, which are significant for diagnosis, treatment, and prognosis in AML patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RUNX1::RUNX1T Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">RUNX1::RUNX1T Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RUNX1::RUNX1T Leukemia refers to a genetic predisposition syndrome that can increase the risk of developing leukemia, which can be inherited from biological parents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Testing">
      <data key="d0">Blood Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood testing involves analyzing blood samples to detect genetic changes associated with leukemia and assess treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fluorescence in situ Hybridization (FISH)">
      <data key="d0">Fluorescence in situ Hybridization (FISH)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">FISH is a laboratory technique used to identify genetic abnormalities in CLL cells, which can indicate specific treatment needs.&lt;SEP&gt;FISH is a method used to detect genetic abnormalities in leukemia cells by using fluorescent probes that bind to specific DNA segments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS)">
      <data key="d0">Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">NGS is a modern sequencing technology used to determine the DNA sequence, identifying mutations that may affect a patient's treatment for leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) is a high-throughput method that allows deep analysis of DNA sequences to detect mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) is a method used to analyze DNA sequences in detail to identify mutations affecting gene functionality.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-generation sequencing is an advanced genetic testing method used to assess multiple mutations in cancer cells simultaneously.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family History of Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Family History of Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Having a family history of leukemia can affect treatment decisions and the likelihood of developing the disease, emphasizing the importance of genetic factors in leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Testing Methods">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Testing Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical testing methods refer to various medical techniques, such as biopsies and blood tests, that are employed to diagnose and monitor leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Procedures">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic procedures encompass a range of medical tests and techniques used to detect leukemia and assess genetic predisposition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Real-Time PCR">
      <data key="d0">Real-Time PCR</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Real-time PCR is a sensitive technique that allows for the detection of specific gene rearrangements related to leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RT PCR">
      <data key="d0">RT PCR</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Reverse Transcriptase PCR (RT PCR) is a method used to analyze gene rearrangements in leukemia cells, aiding in diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Cells">
      <data key="d0">AML Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML cells refer to abnormal blood cells associated with Acute Myeloid Leukemia, which are targeted for genetic testing and treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemic Meningitis">
      <data key="d0">Leukemic Meningitis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemic meningitis occurs when leukemia spreads to the membranes surrounding the brain and spinal cord, detected through MRI scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">Diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnosis encompasses the process by which healthcare professionals identify the presence of leukemia through various tests and evaluations.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnosis involves identifying the presence of a disease, such as cancer, often through imaging and testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation">
      <data key="d0">Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment method that uses high-energy waves to target and destroy cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation is energy emitted in the form of particles or electromagnetic waves, which is a concern in imaging techniques like X-rays but not in MRI scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Claustrophobia">
      <data key="d0">Claustrophobia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Claustrophobia is a psychological condition characterized by an irrational fear of confined spaces, which can impact a patient's ability to undergo certain medical imaging tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ejection Fraction">
      <data key="d0">Ejection Fraction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ejection fraction is a measurement of the percentage of blood that is pumped out of the heart's left ventricle with each heartbeat, which is crucial for assessing heart function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Plan">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment plan is a comprehensive outline tailored to a patient's specific needs and conditions, detailing the chosen course of action for managing diseases such as cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A treatment plan is a comprehensive strategy developed by healthcare providers to decide the best course of action for treating cancer, tailored to individual needs.&lt;SEP&gt;A treatment plan is developed based on risk assessment and test results, guiding how to best manage prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Treatment Plan is a tailored strategy developed by the care team based on test results and patient discussions regarding therapy choices.&lt;SEP&gt;The treatment plan is a comprehensive strategy developed by the cancer care team that outlines the steps and options for treating the patient's cancer based on the diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;The treatment plan outlines the approach for administering cancer therapies, including diagnosis, treatment types, dosages, and duration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Preferred Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Preferred Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Preferred therapies are the most evidence-based options for treating AML that have been shown to be effective and safe in clinical settings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recommended Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Recommended Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Recommended therapies are alternatives to preferred therapies which may still provide beneficial effects in treating AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gemtuzumab ozogamicin">
      <data key="d0">Gemtuzumab ozogamicin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gemtuzumab ozogamicin (GO) is a targeted therapy linked to a chemotherapy drug that targets CD33 proteins on leukemic blasts to deliver treatment directly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a type of blood cancer caused by the BCR::ABL1 gene fusion, leading to an overproduction of white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Myeloid Leukemia is a malignant disease characterized by excessive production of abnormal white blood cells, commonly detected by the Philadelphia chromosome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Philadelphia chromosome is a genetic abnormality associated with CML, resulting from the translocation of chromosome 9 and 22.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intrathecal Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intrathecal Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrathecal chemotherapy involves delivering chemotherapy directly into the cerebrospinal fluid for treating cancer that has spread to the CNS.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrathecal chemotherapy is a method used to administer chemotherapy directly into the cerebrospinal fluid when cancer cells are found there.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrathecal chemotherapy is a treatment method where chemotherapy is injected directly into the spinal fluid to target cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia Cells">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia cells are abnormal blood cells that proliferate uncontrollably, leading to various forms of leukemia such as AML and CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CD33">
      <data key="d0">CD33</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CD33 is a cell surface protein commonly found on the surface of leukemia cells, making it a target for therapies like Gemtuzumab ozogamicin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL1 is a fused gene created from a chromosomal rearrangement involving chromosomes 9 and 22, associated with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).&lt;SEP&gt;BCR::ABL1 is a genetic mutation associated with a specific type of ALL, relevant for determining treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing Treatment CML Phases">
      <data key="d0">Testing Treatment CML Phases</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing Treatment CML Phases is a process discussed in cancer care literature that outlines the diagnostic and treatment phases for CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Document Organization">
      <data key="d0">Document Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Organizing medical documents, including insurance and test results, is vital for effective communication with healthcare providers during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma is a type of lymphoma where T-cell lymphoblasts grow primarily in the thymus rather than in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric Center">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric Center</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pediatric center specializes in treating children and adolescents with diseases such as ALL, adapting treatment approaches to their unique biological and psychological needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mature B-cell ALL">
      <data key="d0">Mature B-cell ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mature B-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, closely related to Burkitt leukemia, and is characterized by the proliferation of mature B-cell lymphoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell Lymphoblasts">
      <data key="d0">B-cell Lymphoblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell lymphoblasts are immature B cells that proliferate in certain types of leukemia and lymphomas, including B-ALL and Burkitt leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Childhood Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Childhood Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Childhood cancer refers to cancers that occur in children and adolescents, often requiring specialized treatment protocols distinct from adult cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A pediatric cancer center focuses on diagnosing and treating children's cancers, providing tailored care for young patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pancreatic cancer is a common and severe type of cancer that starts in the pancreas, significantly affecting digestion and metabolic functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic cancer is a type of cancer that arises from the tissues of the pancreas, characterized by uncontrolled growth of pancreatic cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the pancreas and is characterized by its five stages, ranging from stage 0 to stage 4, with varying degrees of spread and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic cancer is mentioned as part of familial patterns that can increase the risk of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Testing">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility testing involves various assessments to determine reproductive capacity and potential impacts of treatment on fertility for children with ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steroids">
      <data key="d0">Steroids</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Steroids are a type of medication commonly used in the treatment of ALL to help control the disease and suppress the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiagent Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Multiagent Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiagent chemotherapy refers to the use of various drugs in combination to treat ALL, aiming to target cancer cells more effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Breast-Ovarian Cancer Syndrome (HBOC)">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Breast-Ovarian Cancer Syndrome (HBOC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary Breast-Ovarian Cancer Syndrome is a genetic condition linked to an increased risk of developing breast and ovarian cancers, which may contribute to the risk of pancreatic cancer in families.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peutz-Jeghers Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Peutz-Jeghers Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is a genetic condition characterized by the development of benign polyps in the gastrointestinal tract and an increased risk of various cancers, including pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Malignant Melanoma Syndrome (FAMMM)">
      <data key="d0">Familial Malignant Melanoma Syndrome (FAMMM)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Familial Malignant Melanoma Syndrome is a genetic predisposition that increases the risk of melanoma and other cancers, including pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreatic Adenocarcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Pancreatic Adenocarcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pancreatic adenocarcinomas are the most common type of pancreatic cancer, originating from the exocrine cells that line the pancreatic ducts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Factors for Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Risk Factors for Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk factors for pancreatic cancer include lifestyle choices such as smoking and heavy alcohol use, exposure to cancer-causing chemicals, and certain medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tobacco use is a lifestyle risk factor for pancreatic cancer, contributing significantly to the likelihood of developing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alcohol">
      <data key="d0">Alcohol</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Alcohol consumption is associated with an increased risk of certain cancers, warranting advice for limited intake or abstinence.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy alcohol use is identified as a lifestyle factor that increases the risk of pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diabetes">
      <data key="d0">Diabetes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diabetes is a health condition that has been linked to a higher risk of developing pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Inflammation of the Pancreas">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Inflammation of the Pancreas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic inflammation of the pancreas, which can stem from various medical conditions, is a recognized risk factor for pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Tests">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic tests analyze an individual's DNA to identify inherited mutations that may predispose them to certain types of cancer, guiding personalized treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic tests are used to detect hereditary conditions linked to an increased risk of pancreatic cancer, helping to guide patient management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Plans">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Plans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment plans for pancreatic cancer are individualized based on the specific conditions and needs of the patient, considering factors like cancer stage and genetics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Team">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Team</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A multidisciplinary team comprises various healthcare professionals who collaborate to provide comprehensive care for pancreatic cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A multidisciplinary team is composed of various healthcare professionals working together to provide comprehensive care to cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Evidence-Based Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Evidence-Based Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Evidence-based cancer care refers to the treatment approaches based on scientific research and expert consensus to ensure effective patient management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Consult">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Consult</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The multidisciplinary consult involves a meeting of various specialists to discuss a patient's diagnosis and treatment options concerning cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic radiologist interprets imaging studies like x-rays and provides critical information about the presence of cancer within the urinary tract.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic radiologist interprets imaging tests such as x-rays to detect signs of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic radiologist interprets imaging tests to help diagnose cancer, utilizing MRI, CT scans, and mammograms.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic radiologist is a medical doctor who specializes in interpreting medical images and plays a crucial role in diagnosing cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gastroenterologist">
      <data key="d0">Gastroenterologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A gastroenterologist is a medical specialist in digestive system disorders and is involved in diagnosing pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical oncologist is a physician who specializes in the surgical treatment of cancer, essential for some patients with prostate or pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical oncologist performs surgeries to remove tumors and cancerous tissues as part of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palliative Care Provider">
      <data key="d0">Palliative Care Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A palliative care provider focuses on improving the quality of life for patients with serious illnesses, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mental Health Provider">
      <data key="d0">Mental Health Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A mental health provider supports the emotional and psychological needs of cancer patients through therapy or counseling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nurse">
      <data key="d0">Nurse</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nurse plays a crucial role in patient care and support within the cancer care team, often providing frontline care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Key Points">
      <data key="d0">Key Points</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Key points summarize important information or highlights regarding cancer care and diagnosis, providing essential guidance to patients and caregivers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging encompasses various techniques such as X-rays, CT scans, and MRIs used to visualize internal organs and detect abnormalities like tumors during cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging involves various techniques such as CT, MRI, and FDG-PET scans used to visualize internal organs and indications of cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging techniques such as CT, MRI, PET, and transvaginal ultrasound are utilized to determine the extent of cancer before treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Options">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer treatment options encompass various approaches including chemotherapy, radiation therapy, immunotherapy, and surgery for managing cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment options include various methods like surgery, intravesical therapy, systemic therapy, and radiation therapy tailored to the patient's cancer stage and type.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment options include various methods such as surgery, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, and palliative care, tailored to the patient's specific needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jaundice">
      <data key="d0">Jaundice</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Jaundice is a medical condition characterized by yellowing of the skin and eyes, which can be a symptom of pancreatic cancer among other health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-Stage Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-Stage Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage cancer refers to the initial phase of cancer development, often without noticeable symptoms, and has a better prognosis due to timely detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate">
      <data key="d0">Prostate</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Prostate is a gland near the bladder and can be involved in surgical procedures when treating bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a gland in the male reproductive system located just below the bladder, important for sexual reproduction and fluid production for semen.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a gland in the male reproductive system that plays a crucial role in sexual reproduction by producing seminal fluid.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a gland located below the bladder in men, responsible for producing seminal fluid and often affected by prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a gland located in the male reproductive system, significant in discussions of prostate cancer symptoms and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a gland that surrounds the urethra and is central to male reproductive health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms may include blood in urine, burning during urination, unexplained weight loss, and pain in the back or hips, indicating potential prostate issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of prostate cancer include frequent urination, difficulty urinating, and pain in the groin or pelvic area among others.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of prostate cancer include frequent urination, difficulty urinating, and pain in the groin or pelvic area amongst others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Age">
      <data key="d0">Age</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Age is recognized as a significant risk factor for prostate cancer, with higher incidence rates in men aged 65 and above.&lt;SEP&gt;Age is the leading risk factor for prostate cancer, with the likelihood of developing it increasing as individuals grow older.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Factors">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic factors involve inherited mutations that can increase the risk of prostate cancer, such as mutations in the BRCA2 gene.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Race">
      <data key="d0">Race</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Race has been identified as a factor in the risk of prostate cancer, with Black males at a higher risk compared to white males.&lt;SEP&gt;Race is a demographic factor considered in prostate health assessments, as some races are at a higher risk for prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Race is a demographic factor influencing the likelihood of developing prostate cancer, with disparities noted among different ethnic groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diet and Lifestyle">
      <data key="d0">Diet and Lifestyle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diet and lifestyle choices can affect the risk of prostate cancer, with high-fat diets potentially increasing and healthy diets potentially decreasing the risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aggressive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Aggressive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aggressive cancer refers to cancer that tends to grow and spread more rapidly than average, impacting treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage prostate cancer is a classification of prostate cancer that may not require immediate treatment, depending on individual risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Early-stage prostate cancer is characterized by cancer that has not spread beyond the prostate and is generally more treatable.&lt;SEP&gt;Early-stage prostate cancer is highly treatable and often curable, with patients having many treatment options available.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced-stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Advanced-stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced-stage prostate cancer is characterized by the cancer spreading outside the prostate gland to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced-stage prostate cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond the prostate to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes or organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Semen">
      <data key="d0">Semen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Semen is a fluid consisting of sperm and fluids produced from the prostate and seminal vesicles, playing a key role in reproduction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Erectile Dysfunction">
      <data key="d0">Erectile Dysfunction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Erectile dysfunction is the inability to achieve or maintain an erection, which can be a symptom of prostate issues or cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Test">
      <data key="d0">PSA Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PSA test measures the level of prostate-specific antigen in the blood to help detect prostate cancer or other prostate disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;The PSA test is a blood test measuring prostate-specific antigen levels, used to detect potential prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The PSA test is a blood test measuring prostate-specific antigen levels, used to help diagnose prostate cancer and monitor treatment efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;The PSA test measures levels of prostate-specific antigen in the blood, serving as a critical screening tool for prostate cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Exam">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Digital Rectal Exam is a physical examination where a doctor checks the prostate gland for abnormalities, typically used alongside other tests to evaluate prostate health.&lt;SEP&gt;A digital rectal exam is a physical examination technique used to assess the prostate for abnormalities, often indicating the need for further testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Digital rectal exams are recommended to monitor the status of anal cancer post-diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital rectal exam is a common procedure to check the prostate for abnormalities and is often paired with the PSA test.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital rectal exam is a procedure in which a doctor examines the prostate gland for abnormalities using a finger inserted into the rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital rectal exam is a procedure where a doctor assesses the prostate through the rectum, checking for irregularities that might signal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Active Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Active Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Active surveillance is a management strategy for early-stage prostate cancer that involves monitoring without immediate treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Active surveillance is a management strategy for early-stage prostate cancer that involves regular monitoring without immediate treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Active surveillance is a management strategy for prostate cancer where the disease is monitored closely rather than treated immediately.&lt;SEP&gt;Active surveillance is a monitoring strategy for small, low-risk thyroid cancers, postponing invasive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone Therapy for prostate cancer includes treatments that alter hormone levels, especially testosterone, to slow or manage cancer growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy includes various treatments that manipulate hormone levels to manage the growth of hormone-sensitive cancers like prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy involves treatments that block hormones or hormones' effects to manage certain types of breast cancer effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy is a treatment for advanced-stage prostate cancer that aims to slow cancer growth by reducing hormone levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy is one of the treatment options available for managing advanced-stage prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy is used to lower hormone levels in the body to help manage advanced-stage prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bones">
      <data key="d0">Bones</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Bones can be affected in cases of metastatic prostate cancer, indicating the spread of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lungs">
      <data key="d0">Lungs</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The lungs are another organ that can be affected in cases of metastatic prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Localized Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Localized Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Localized prostate cancer refers to cancer that remains within the prostate gland and has not spread to surrounding areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic prostate cancer occurs when cancer spreads outside the prostate gland to other parts of the body, including lymph nodes, bones, liver, or lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic prostate cancer refers to cancer that has spread to distant parts of the body, complicating treatment and management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA) Test">
      <data key="d0">Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA) Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The PSA test is a blood test used to measure the level of PSA in the blood, which may indicate prostate cancer if levels are elevated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA">
      <data key="d0">PSA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is a protein made inside the prostate gland, elevated levels of which can indicate potential health issues, including prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood and Urine Tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood and Urine Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood and urine tests are diagnostic procedures used to assess various health indicators, including hormone levels and markers for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Gland">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The prostate gland is a small walnut-sized gland that produces seminal fluid for sperm transport and is the site where prostate cancer develops.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate gland is a small, walnut-sized gland in males that produces the fluid that nourishes and transports sperm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Level">
      <data key="d0">PSA Level</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA level is a blood test result that indicates the amount of prostate-specific antigen in the bloodstream, which can signify the risk for prostate cancer depending on various factors such as age and race.&lt;SEP&gt;PSA level refers to the measurement of prostate-specific antigen in the blood, which can indicate possible prostate cancer when elevated.&lt;SEP&gt;The PSA level refers to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen in the blood, used as a biomarker for prostate health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Exam Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Exam Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The digital rectal exam procedure involves a doctor inserting a finger into the rectum to palpate the prostate for abnormalities, crucial in assessing prostate health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The health care team includes various medical professionals involved in patient care and management, particularly regarding prostate health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aging">
      <data key="d0">Aging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aging is a biological process that can affect prostate health and increase the risk of developing prostate cancer and other health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic testing encompasses various procedures and tests performed to determine the presence of prostate cancer or other conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Methods">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging methods are techniques used to visualize internal body structures, helping to identify abnormalities like tumors in the prostate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Scan">
      <data key="d0">Bone Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Bone Scan is a diagnostic imaging test that detects metastatic cancer growth in bones by introducing a radioactive tracer to visualize bone activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A Bone Scan is a medical imaging test that utilizes a radiotracer to detect areas of bone damage or abnormalities, showing up as bright spots on the imaging results.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone scan is a diagnostic test that detects the spread of cancer to the bones, using a radioactive tracer to create images of the bone structure.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone scan is a nuclear imaging technique that identifies areas of increased or decreased bone activity to check for disease conditions including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone scan is a procedure that uses a radioactive tracer to identify whether cancer has spread to the bones and to monitor treatment effects.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone scan is an imaging test that helps detect bone damage and cancerous deposits, using a radiotracer.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone scan is an imaging test used to determine whether cancer has spread to the bones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA/PET Imaging">
      <data key="d0">PSMA/PET Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">PSMA/PET Imaging is a specialized form of PET scan that targets prostate-specific membrane antigen, helping to locate and assess prostate cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Agent">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Agent</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Contrast Agent, sometimes known as contrast dye, is a substance used to enhance the visibility of internal structures in imaging tests like MRI and CT scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast agent is a substance used in CT and MRI scans to enhance the visibility of internal structures in medical imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urologist">
      <data key="d0">Urologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A urologist is a medical doctor specializing in diseases affecting the urinary tract and male reproductive organs, including conducting prostate biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologist is a medical specialist focused on urinary tract issues, including bladder cancer treatment and management.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologist is a specialized doctor who treats diseases related to the urinary system and male reproductive organs, often performing biopsy procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologist is a specialized physician who diagnoses and treats diseases related to the urinary tract and male reproductive organs, including performing biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologist specializes in the urinary system and male reproductive organs, diagnosing and treating bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologist specializes in the urinary system and the male reproductive organs, playing a critical role in diagnosing and treating bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA1">
      <data key="d0">BRCA1</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA1 is a gene associated with an increased risk of breast and ovarian cancers, and mutations in it may also impact prostate cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene that, when mutated, can increase the risk of developing breast, ovarian, and prostate cancers, and can be identified through genetic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene whose mutations are associated with an increased risk of various cancers, including breast and prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene whose mutations are linked to an increased risk of breast, ovarian, and other cancers, including prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA2">
      <data key="d0">BRCA2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA2 is a gene related to cancer risk, particularly breast and prostate cancers, due to inherited mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is a gene similar to BRCA1, mutations of which are associated with an elevated risk of certain cancers, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is a gene that, when mutated, increases the risk for several cancers, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is another gene associated with an increased risk of several cancers, similar to BRCA1, and is relevant in prostate cancer risk assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATM">
      <data key="d0">ATM</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">ATM is a gene involved in DNA damage response, and mutations in ATM are linked to an increased risk of various cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;ATM is a gene that can lead to heightened cancer risk if mutated and is assessed during genetic testing for hereditary cancer syndromes.&lt;SEP&gt;ATM is a gene that, when mutated, can increase cancer risk, including prostate cancer and other types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic lymph nodes are part of the lymphatic system and can be affected by cancer when cancer spreads from the prostate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anesthesia">
      <data key="d0">Anesthesia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anesthesia is the medical practice of using drugs to prevent pain during procedures, such as prostate biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Anesthesia refers to the medications used to induce a temporary loss of sensation or awareness during medical procedures, such as biopsies or surgeries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Sample">
      <data key="d0">Core Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core sample refers to a small tissue sample taken during a biopsy to be tested for cancer presence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Scans">
      <data key="d0">MRI Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI scans are imaging tests that provide detailed pictures of the prostate and can assist in diagnosing cancer when used with biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complications">
      <data key="d0">Complications</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Complications in medical procedures refer to unexpected adverse effects, such as infection or bleeding that may arise from a biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germline Testing">
      <data key="d0">Germline Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Germline testing is a procedure done with blood, urine, or saliva to identify inherited mutations impacting cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Germline testing is a type of genetic testing that identifies inherited mutations in genes that may increase cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inherited Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Inherited Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An inherited mutation refers to genetic alterations passed down from parents which can increase the likelihood of developing cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEK2">
      <data key="d0">CHEK2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CHEK2 is a gene involved in DNA repair, with mutations linked to heightened risk of breast and prostate cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;CHEK2 is a gene that provides instructions for a protein that plays a role in controlling cell growth; mutations can lead to cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MLH1">
      <data key="d0">MLH1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MLH1 is a gene associated with DNA mismatch repair, with mutations indicating an increased susceptibility to certain cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;MLH1 is a gene whose mutations are associated with increased risk for colorectal and uterine cancers, among others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSH2">
      <data key="d0">MSH2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSH2 is a gene essential for DNA repair mechanisms, and its mutations can raise cancer risk, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;MSH2 is a gene related to DNA mismatch repair, with mutations leading to higher risks of certain cancers including colorectal and prostate cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSH6">
      <data key="d0">MSH6</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSH6 is a gene linked to hereditary cancer predisposition, particularly affecting colorectal and prostate cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;MSH6 is a gene related to DNA repair, and mutations can contribute to a higher risk of developing various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PALB2">
      <data key="d0">PALB2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PALB2 is a gene that interacts with BRCA1 and BRCA2 in DNA repair, and mutations are associated with an elevated cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;PALB2 mutations have been associated with increased risks of breast cancer and potentially prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PMS2">
      <data key="d0">PMS2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PMS2 is a gene involved in DNA repair processes, and its mutations are related to increased cancer susceptibility.&lt;SEP&gt;PMS2 is a gene involved in DNA repair, mutations of which can increase the risk of colorectal and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counselor">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counselor</data>
      <data key="d1">profession</data>
      <data key="d2">A genetic counselor is a professional who assists patients in understanding genetic testing and the implications of genetic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor is a specialist trained to provide guidance on genetic testing and assist patients in interpreting results.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor is a trained expert who assists individuals in understanding genetic testing results and evaluates whether genetic testing is appropriate based on medical history.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor is a trained professional who helps individuals understand genetic testing and its implications for health and disease.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor provides information and support to individuals and families regarding genetic testing and inherited cancer syndromes, including those related to colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germline Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Germline Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Germline mutations are inherited changes in DNA that can increase the risk of various cancers, impacting family health histories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Sample">
      <data key="d0">Blood Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood sample is often used in genetic germline testing to identify inherited mutations related to cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urine Sample">
      <data key="d0">Urine Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A urine sample may be utilized in genetic germline testing to help detect inherited mutations in genes associated with cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Saliva Sample">
      <data key="d0">Saliva Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A saliva sample is one of the biological materials that can be collected for germline testing to evaluate genetic cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA)">
      <data key="d0">Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA) is a protein produced by the prostate gland, and high levels in the blood can indicate prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Biomarker Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Molecular biomarker testing analyzes tumor characteristics to assess cancer risk and guide treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter What’s Next?">
      <data key="d0">Chapter What’s Next?</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">This chapter outlines the next steps after testing, focusing on how results are integrated into a comprehensive risk management plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group is a classification system that interprets Gleason scores and indicates the aggressiveness of prostate cancer through five groups, with higher groups representing more aggressive cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade Group is a classification used to categorize prostate cancer based on its aggressiveness and is informed by the Gleason score.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade Group is a system used to classify cancer aggressiveness based on the Gleason score, ranging from 1 to 5, with 1 indicating low risk and 5 indicating high risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gleason Score">
      <data key="d0">Gleason Score</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gleason score is a numerical value assigned based on the patterns of cancer cells found in biopsy samples, used to predict the aggressiveness of prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Gleason Score is a grading system for prostate cancer that indicates how aggressive the cancer is based on the pattern of cancerous cells in biopsied tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;The Gleason score is a grading system that assesses the aggressiveness of prostate cancer by evaluating the pattern of cancer cells in biopsy samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Group">
      <data key="d0">Risk Group</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk group categorizes patients based on the likelihood of disease progression and is determined using various factors including PSA levels and biopsy results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Stage">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor stage is a classification that determines the extent of cancer in the body, influencing prognosis and treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy Results">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsy results provide critical information about cancer presence and characteristics, which helps in determining the most effective treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Density">
      <data key="d0">PSA Density</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA Density is a measurement used in conjunction with prostate cancer diagnostics, indicating the concentration of prostate-specific antigen in the bloodstream which can signify cancer presence.&lt;SEP&gt;PSA density is a calculated measure that accounts for prostate size relative to PSA levels, used to assess cancer risk more accurately.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transrectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Transrectal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Transrectal ultrasound is a medical imaging technique used to measure prostate size as part of prostate cancer evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seminal Vesicles">
      <data key="d0">Seminal Vesicles</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Seminal Vesicles are glands located near the prostate that may be removed during bladder cancer surgery, depending on the spread of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Seminal vesicles are glands located above the prostate that contribute to the production of semen.&lt;SEP&gt;The seminal vesicles are glands that produce and store fluid that is a significant component of semen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 1">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 1 signifies a Gleason score of 6, indicating that the cancer is of the lowest aggressiveness, thus representing a low risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 2">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 2 is assigned to cancers with a Gleason score of 7, indicating an intermediate risk level as it reflects a moderate increase in aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 3">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 3 is for cancers with a Gleason score also of 7, but with a higher first number in the score indicating a more serious condition than Grade Group 2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 4">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 4</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 4 signifies a Gleason score of 8 or 9, predicting high risk for prostate cancer due to more aggressive characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 5">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 5</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 5 indicates a Gleason score of 9 or 10, representing very high risk cancer with the highest degree of aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)">
      <data key="d0">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BPH is a common condition in older men that causes symptoms similar to prostate cancer, such as urinary difficulties, but is not cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Age Risk Factor">
      <data key="d0">Age Risk Factor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Age is a significant risk factor for prostate cancer, with increased likelihood of diagnosis as men age, particularly over 65 years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family History as Risk Factor">
      <data key="d0">Family History as Risk Factor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Having a close family member with prostate cancer increases an individual's risk of developing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guideline for Patients: Early-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guideline for Patients: Early-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guideline provides information and recommendations for patients with early-stage prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Black Males">
      <data key="d0">Black Males</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Black males are identified as being at higher risk for developing prostate cancer compared to other ethnic groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the breast, often originating from epithelial cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a disease where breast cells grow uncontrollably, often spreading to other parts of the body. It includes invasive forms such as metastatic breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a malignant tumor that develops from breast cells and may involve hormone receptors for growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that develops from the cells of the breast, often manifesting as invasive cancer that spreads from milk ducts or lobules.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the cells of the breast, with various forms including ductal and lobular carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the cells of the breasts and can affect anyone, though it is more common in those assigned female at birth.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the cells of the breasts and may require various assessments such as staging and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the breast tissue and can affect both men and women, requiring various diagnostic and treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the breast tissue, commonly classified and staged using the TNM system, which assesses tumor size, lymph node involvement, and distant metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the breast tissue, which includes ducts and lobules.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is cited as a cancer that may increase prostate cancer risk due to family history.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer refers to a malignant tumor that develops from breast cells, commonly starting in ducts or lobules.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer refers to various malignancies that arise from the cells of the breast, predominantly in the ducts or lobules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Obesity">
      <data key="d0">Obesity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Obesity is listed as a contributing factor that increases the risk of developing prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced-stage prostate cancer is characterized by the spread of cancer beyond the prostate to other organs or lymphatic systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced-stage prostate cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond the prostate gland to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced-stage prostate cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond the prostate to other body parts, often requiring aggressive treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA2 Gene">
      <data key="d0">BRCA2 Gene</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The BRCA2 gene is associated with increased risk of various cancers, including prostate cancer when mutated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage prostate cancer is a form of the disease that has not spread beyond the prostate, often growing slowly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthy Diet">
      <data key="d0">Healthy Diet</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A healthy diet consists of nutrition that may reduce the risk of developing prostate cancer, focusing on fruits and vegetables.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exercise">
      <data key="d0">Exercise</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Exercise is an activity that can help decrease the aggressiveness of prostate cancer and improve overall health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Screening Tests">
      <data key="d0">Screening Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Screening tests are medical assessments used to detect prostate cancer at an early stage, which may improve treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Detection Methods">
      <data key="d0">Detection Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Detection methods for prostate cancer include various tests and evaluations that help identify the presence of the disease in patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatments">
      <data key="d0">Treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatments for prostate cancer vary and can include surgery, radiation, hormone therapy, and chemotherapy, tailored to individual patient needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Best Practices">
      <data key="d0">Best Practices</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Best practices in prostate cancer treatment refer to the latest research-based approaches that healthcare teams should follow for optimal patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Tests">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic Tests are medical procedures performed to identify diseases or conditions in patients, including imaging techniques like x-rays, MRIs, and scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic tests encompass various methods such as imaging (CT, MRI) and blood tests used to identify and assess cancerous conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic tests include procedures used to confirm cancer, assess its stage, and plan treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA-PET">
      <data key="d0">PSMA-PET</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PSMA-PET scan is a specialized type of PET scan that targets prostate-specific membrane antigen to monitor prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Enlarged Glands">
      <data key="d0">Enlarged Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Enlarged glands, or lymph nodes, can indicate potential health issues including cancers, and they may be assessed during a physical examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Relatives">
      <data key="d0">Blood Relatives</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood relatives are family members related by genetics, whose health history may provide insights into hereditary diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment refers to medical interventions applied after diagnosis, which can include surgery, medication, or therapy for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quality of Life">
      <data key="d0">Quality of Life</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Quality of life encompasses a patient's overall enjoyment of life and ability to engage in normal activities, often impacted by health conditions and their treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Quality of life measures an individual's overall well-being and ability to perform regular activities, often assessed during medical evaluations.&lt;SEP&gt;Quality of life refers to the general well-being of individuals, especially in the context of health and the ability to perform daily activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Test">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An imaging test is a procedure that obtains pictures of the inside of the body to assist in diagnosing and monitoring diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen (PSMA)">
      <data key="d0">Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen (PSMA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSMA is a protein commonly found on prostate cancer cells, which is targeted by specific tracers in imaging studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate Specific Membrane Antigen (PSMA) is a protein found on the surface of prostate cancer cells, which is targeted by diagnostic tracers for imaging and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA-PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">PSMA-PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PSMA-PET scan is an imaging technique used to locate prostate cancer cells by visualizing the PSMA protein on the surface of these cells.&lt;SEP&gt;PSMA-PET scan is a diagnostic imaging technique used to detect prostate cancer by visualizing the presence of PSMA on cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultrasound imaging helps guide biopsies of the prostate and involves high-energy sound waves to visualize the prostate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transrectal Ultrasound (TRUS)">
      <data key="d0">Transrectal Ultrasound (TRUS)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">TRUS is a specific type of ultrasound imaging that helps guide the biopsy needle into the prostate through the rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;Transrectal Ultrasound (TRUS) is a method for performing biopsies of the prostate, allowing real-time imaging during the procedure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Chicago Medicine">
      <data key="d0">University of Chicago Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">University of Chicago Medicine is a prominent healthcare institution that conducts significant research and treatment in the field of oncology, particularly prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;University of Chicago Medicine is a prominent medical center that provides comprehensive care and specializes in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">biological concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mutation is a change in the DNA sequence that can alter how genes function and may lead to diseases like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A mutation is a change in the DNA sequence that can lead to abnormal protein function, contributing to diseases such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Gene Testing">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Gene Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">medical procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary gene testing is performed to detect inherited mutations that increase the risk of developing various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Androgen Deprivation Therapy (ADT)">
      <data key="d0">Androgen Deprivation Therapy (ADT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ADT is a treatment for prostate cancer that reduces levels of testosterone to slow the growth of tumors, involving either hormonal medications or surgical options like orchiectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Androgen Deprivation Therapy (ADT) is a treatment that lowers testosterone levels, mainly used for managing prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Androgen deprivation therapy is a medical treatment designed to reduce levels of androgens, particularly testosterone, to treat conditions like prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Staging">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Staging refers to the system used by cancer experts to classify the extent of cancer in patients based on imaging scans, biopsies, and blood tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor staging is a systematic process used to determine the extent of cancer progression in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Node Metastasis (TNM)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Node Metastasis (TNM)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM system is a classification system that describes the characteristics of cancer including tumor size (T), spread to lymph nodes (N), and metastasis (M) to other body parts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Groups">
      <data key="d0">Risk Groups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk Groups are classifications used to determine the likelihood of cancer progression and inform treatment decisions for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Early-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Early-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide standardized recommendations and information for patients diagnosed with early-stage prostate cancer regarding treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leuprolide">
      <data key="d0">Leuprolide</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Leuprolide is an LHRH agonist that is commonly used to treat prostate cancer by reducing androgen levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Leuprolide is an LHRH agonist used in hormone therapy to treat prostate cancer by lowering testosterone levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Leuprolide, also known as Lupron Depot, is a GnRH agonist utilized in endocrine therapy to manage hormone receptor-positive cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Degarelix">
      <data key="d0">Degarelix</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Degarelix is an LHRH antagonist that works by reducing testosterone production in prostate cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Degarelix is an LHRH antagonist used in hormone therapy for prostate cancer, helping to quickly lower testosterone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Castration">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Castration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical castration is a procedure intended to lower testosterone levels, often used in conjunction with hormone therapy for prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical castration is a procedure that removes the testicles to eliminate testosterone production, often utilized in advanced prostate cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bicalutamide">
      <data key="d0">Bicalutamide</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Bicalutamide is an anti-androgen medication that helps to lower testosterone levels in prostate cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bicalutamide is an anti-androgen medication used in hormone therapy for prostate cancer, effective at blocking testosterone effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinomas are a subtype of cancer originating from epithelial cells, which include breast carcinomas, that line the inner and outer surfaces of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Nodes (ALNs)">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Nodes (ALNs)</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located in the armpit that can be involved in breast cancer metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located near the armpit and play a role in drainage of lymph fluid from the breast.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located near the armpit that are often examined for breast cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located under the arm that play a role in the spread of breast cancer and lymphatic drainage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LHRH Agonists">
      <data key="d0">LHRH Agonists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LHRH agonists are medications that stimulate the pituitary gland to produce more luteinizing hormone, thereby lowering testosterone production in prostate cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goserelin">
      <data key="d0">Goserelin</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Goserelin is a type of LHRH agonist used in prostate cancer treatment to reduce testosterone levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Goserelin, known as Zoladex, is a GnRH agonist used in hormone therapies for breast cancer to suppress ovarian function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Triptorelin">
      <data key="d0">Triptorelin</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Triptorelin is another LHRH agonist that helps in managing prostate cancer through the reduction of testosterone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anti-Androgens">
      <data key="d0">Anti-Androgens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anti-androgens are medications that block the effects of androgens, such as testosterone, in prostate cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nilutamide">
      <data key="d0">Nilutamide</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Nilutamide is an anti-androgen that is effective in the treatment of prostate cancer by blocking testosterone effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flutamide">
      <data key="d0">Flutamide</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Flutamide is another anti-androgen used in hormone therapy for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LHRH Antagonists">
      <data key="d0">LHRH Antagonists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LHRH antagonists are medications that directly reduce levels of testosterone by blocking luteinizing hormone release.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Relugolix">
      <data key="d0">Relugolix</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Relugolix is an LHRH antagonist available in tablet form, which is taken daily for managing prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive Breast Cancer refers to breast cancer that has spread beyond the ducts or lobules into surrounding breast tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer is a form of breast cancer that has spread from the milk ducts or lobules into surrounding breast tissue or lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer is a type of cancer that has grown outside the ducts or lobules into the surrounding breast tissue, enabling it to spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer occurs when cancer cells spread from the breast ducts or lobules into surrounding breast tissue or lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer refers to cancer that has spread from the milk ducts or lobules into surrounding breast tissue or nearby lymph nodes, indicating a more advanced stage than DCIS.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Terminal Duct Lobular Units (TDLUs)">
      <data key="d0">Terminal Duct Lobular Units (TDLUs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TDLUs are the glands within the breast where milk is produced, and they are often the origin of breast carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic breast cancer refers to breast cancer that has spread outside the breast and lymph nodes to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC)">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic breast cancer refers to breast cancer that has spread beyond the breast and lymph nodes to other areas of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Carcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ductal carcinomas are the most common types of breast cancer, originating in the cells lining the milk ducts of the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobular Carcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Lobular Carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobular carcinomas are another subtype of breast cancer that begins in the lobules, which are the glands that produce milk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Primary Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary tumor in breast cancer is the original mass of cancer cells that forms in the breast tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary tumor is the initial mass of cancer cells that forms at the site of cancer origin.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary tumor is the originating tumor in which cancer begins, and its size is crucial for determining the stage of the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Testing for Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for breast cancer includes various medical assessments to determine the presence of cancer, its type, and the best treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptor Status">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptor Status</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone receptor status refers to tests performed on breast cancer tissue to determine if hormone receptors are present, influencing treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone receptor status testing checks if breast cancer cells have receptors for hormones, which can influence treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 Status">
      <data key="d0">HER2 Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 status indicates the level of the HER2 protein, which influences cancer cell growth and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 status is a test that determines the presence of HER2 protein on cancer cells, guiding targeted therapies for breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 status is a test to measure the level of human epidermal growth factor receptor 2, which can affect treatment decisions in breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 status refers to the presence and quantity of the HER2 protein in cancer cells, which can influence treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Cancer Risk Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Cancer Risk Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic cancer risk testing assesses an individual's genetic profile to determine the likelihood of developing certain types of cancer, including bone cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing assesses an individual's likelihood of developing breast cancer based on genetic factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing evaluates hereditary risks associated with breast cancer and other cancers, informing patient treatment and screening options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Records">
      <data key="d0">Health Records</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Health records are documentation of a patient's medical history, tests, diagnoses, and treatments, crucial for informed medical decision-making.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Anatomy">
      <data key="d0">Breast Anatomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The breast is a glandular organ made up of ducts, lobules, fat, and supportive tissue, essential for milk production in females.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondary tumors are tumors that develop in other parts of the body as a result of the spread of cancer cells from the primary tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located in the armpit that can be affected by the spread of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located near the armpit that help drain lymph from breast tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located under the arm that play a crucial role in the lymphatic system, particularly in breast cancer spread assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Status">
      <data key="d0">Performance Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance Status is an assessment of a patient's ability to carry out ordinary daily activities, which helps determine treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is a measure of a patient's general well-being and ability to perform daily activities, influencing treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status refers to a patient's ability to perform everyday activities, which can influence treatment options for brain tumor patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS)">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DCIS is a noninvasive form of breast cancer found only in the ducts of the breast, typically categorized as stage 0 cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS) is a noninvasive cancer found in breast duct cells that does not spread outside the ducts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast">
      <data key="d0">Breast</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The breast is a glandular organ located on the chest, consisting of milk ducts, lobules, connective tissue, and blood vessels, playing a vital role in lactation and being a common site of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The breast is an organ and a gland found on the chest, composed of milk ducts, fat, nerves, and connective tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;The breast is an organ located on the chest, consisting of milk ducts, fat, nerves, lymph and blood vessels, and connective tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Mammogram is a specific imaging test that utilizes low-dose x-rays to create images of the breast, primarily used for breast cancer screening and diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A mammogram is a specialized X-ray technique used to examine breast tissue for signs of cancer or abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A mammogram is a specific type of imaging test using X-rays to examine breast tissue for any signs of breast cancer, either as part of routine screening or for diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A mammogram is a type of imaging test used to screen for breast cancer and is available in 2D and 3D forms.&lt;SEP&gt;A mammogram is an X-ray examination of the breast used to detect signs of breast cancer, necessary for both screening and diagnostic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;A mammogram is an imaging test used to screen for breast cancer and other breast abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Carcinoma In Situ">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Carcinoma In Situ</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS) is a noninvasive form of breast cancer found in the cells that line the ducts in the breast. It is classified as stage 0 cancer and is treated to prevent its progression to invasive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Breast Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast tissue consists of glands that produce milk, ducts that carry milk, and supporting structures like fat and connective tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast tissue consists of various structures such as lobules, ducts, fat, and connective tissues, and is where breast cancer originates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Breast Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Breast Specialist is an expert in breast health who treats diseases related to the breast and performs necessary diagnostic procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;A breast specialist is a healthcare professional focused on diagnosing and treating conditions related to breast health.&lt;SEP&gt;A breast specialist is a medical professional who specializes in diagnosing and treating conditions of the breast, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A breast specialist is an expert focused on the diagnosis and treatment of breast diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ductal carcinoma is the most common type of breast cancer, originating in the cells lining the milk ducts of the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Lobular Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobular carcinoma starts in the lobules (milk glands) of the breast and is another common subtype of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transgender Individuals">
      <data key="d0">Transgender Individuals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transgender individuals are those whose gender identity differs from the sex they were assigned at birth, and specific guidelines regarding their screening are referenced.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for DCIS">
      <data key="d0">Testing for DCIS</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for DCIS refers to the diagnostic process to evaluate Ductal Carcinoma In Situ, a type of breast cancer indication.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen Receptor Status">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen Receptor Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen receptor status is a key factor in determining the type of treatment for breast cancer, assessing whether cancer cells have receptors for estrogen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Cancer Stages">
      <data key="d0">Breast Cancer Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast cancer stages refer to the classification of cancer based on the extent of tumor size and spread to lymph nodes or other organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Diagnostic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Breast Diagnostic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A breast diagnostic radiologist interprets imaging tests like mammograms and MRIs, performing procedures such as needle biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast diagnostic radiologists interpret mammograms and other imaging tests and perform needle biopsies to detect breast conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologist">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A gynecologist diagnoses and treats diseases related to the female reproductive organs.&lt;SEP&gt;A gynecologist is a medical doctor specializing in the female reproductive system, dealing with various conditions including breast health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lactation Consultant">
      <data key="d0">Lactation Consultant</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A lactation consultant specializes in assisting and educating mothers on breastfeeding techniques and practices.&lt;SEP&gt;A lactation consultant specializes in educating and supporting breastfeeding mothers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound Technologists">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound Technologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultrasound technologists are healthcare professionals who operate ultrasound machines to gather imaging data necessary for breast examinations.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound technologists operate ultrasound machines to visualize breast tissue for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Practice Providers">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Practice Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced practice providers include nurse practitioners and physician assistants that monitor patient health and deliver care.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced practice providers include nurse practitioners and physician assistants who can diagnose and treat a variety of conditions, including breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced practice providers include nurse practitioners and physician assistants who provide a wide range of health care services, including breast cancer prevention and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced practice providers, including nurse practitioners and physician assistants, support the care team by monitoring patients' health and providing comprehensive care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Obstetrician">
      <data key="d0">Obstetrician</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An obstetrician specializes in pregnancy and childbirth.&lt;SEP&gt;An obstetrician specializes in providing care during pregnancy and childbirth, while also being knowledgeable about reproductive health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Provider (PCP)">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Provider (PCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary care provider delivers comprehensive healthcare, including preventive services and management of various health conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary care provider gives a wide range of care, including preventive and curative services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobules">
      <data key="d0">Lobules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobules are glands in the breast that produce milk and are one of the potential sites where breast cancer can begin.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobules are structures within the breast that produce milk.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobules are the glandular structures in the breast responsible for producing milk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ducts">
      <data key="d0">Ducts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ducts are thin tubes that carry milk from lobules to the nipple.&lt;SEP&gt;Ducts are tubular passages in the breast that transport milk from the lobules to the nipple.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC) is a rare and aggressive form of breast cancer characterized by the blockage of lymph vessels, leading to swollen, red, and warm breast tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory breast cancer is a rare and aggressive form of breast cancer characterized by blocked lymph vessels, causing breast swelling and redness.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory breast cancer is an aggressive form of breast cancer characterized by rapid changes in appearance and symptoms due to lymphatic blockage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Ductal Carcinoma (IDC)">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Ductal Carcinoma (IDC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive ductal carcinoma is the most common type of breast cancer, occurring in the milk ducts and spreading to surrounding tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive ductal carcinoma is the most prevalent type of breast cancer, originating in the milk ducts and invading nearby tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Milk Ducts">
      <data key="d0">Breast Milk Ducts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast milk ducts are structures that transport milk from the lobules to the nipple and can be the origin of breast cancer, including IBC.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast milk ducts are structures that transport milk from the lobules to the nipple, and are often involved in the early stages of breast cancer, including IBC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tests">
      <data key="d0">Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests refer to various medical assessments such as blood tests and biopsies performed to diagnose and evaluate breast cancer, especially IBC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tests for IBC">
      <data key="d0">Tests for IBC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests for IBC encompass various medical assessments, including blood tests, imaging tests, and biopsies that help diagnose and stage the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Exam">
      <data key="d0">Breast Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A breast exam is a clinical evaluation performed by healthcare providers to detect lumps and other abnormal changes in breast tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nurse or Assistant">
      <data key="d0">Nurse or Assistant</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nurse or assistant is typically present during medical examinations and procedures, providing support and ensuring patient comfort.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alkaline Phosphatase">
      <data key="d0">Alkaline Phosphatase</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Alkaline phosphatase is an enzyme measured in blood tests, and elevated levels can indicate that cancer has spread primarily to the bones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pregnancy Test">
      <data key="d0">Pregnancy Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pregnancy test is performed to ensure patients are not pregnant before beginning treatment, crucial for safety during cancer therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Pregnancy tests are conducted to determine if individuals are pregnant before offering certain medical treatments, a crucial consideration in cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Tests for Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Tests for Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging tests for cancer, such as X-rays and MRIs, are used to visualize tumors and assess cancer spread, integral in the diagnosis and treatment process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone X-ray">
      <data key="d0">Bone X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Bone X-ray is an imaging technique that uses low-dose radiation to produce a picture of bones, helping to identify tumors or other abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone X-ray is a radiographic technique specifically used to visualize bone anatomy for detecting fractures, tumors, or abnormalities in bone density.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Diagnostic Mammogram is a specialized x-ray of the breast used to examine specific areas of concern, particularly for tumor detection and sizing during evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic mammogram is a specialized X-ray of the breast, aimed at identifying abnormalities after a screening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast MRI">
      <data key="d0">Breast MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Breast MRI is a specific type of MRI that focuses on imaging the breast tissue, typically used to complement mammograms when further investigation is warranted.&lt;SEP&gt;A Breast MRI is an imaging test that involves using magnets and radio waves to obtain detailed images of the breast, often performed in conjunction with mammograms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spine MRI">
      <data key="d0">Spine MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Spine MRI is an imaging examination that utilizes magnetic resonance technology to detect conditions that may affect the spine, such as cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Screening Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Screening Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Screening Mammogram is a routine x-ray examination of the breast performed on individuals without symptoms to detect early signs of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Mammogram Results">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Mammogram Results</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic Mammogram Results are evaluations performed to assess specific areas of concern in the breast, often leading to immediate further testing based on findings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sodium Fluoride PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">Sodium Fluoride PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Sodium Fluoride PET-CT combines positron emission tomography and computed tomography to provide detailed images of bone health, especially regarding cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;A Sodium Fluoride PET-CT is a combined imaging test that may replace a Bone Scan for assessing bone-related health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;A Sodium Fluoride PET-CT is a type of PET-CT scan that uses sodium fluoride as a radiotracer to assess bone metastases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Damage">
      <data key="d0">Bone Damage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Damage refers to any injury or condition that compromises the integrity of bone structure, potentially indicated by imaging results such as bone scans or x-rays.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allegations of Contrast Sensitivity">
      <data key="d0">Allegations of Contrast Sensitivity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Allegations of Contrast Sensitivity is a medical condition where individuals may react negatively to contrast agents used in imaging tests, necessitating caution during diagnostic procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET-CT scan">
      <data key="d0">PET-CT scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET-CT scan is a medical imaging procedure that combines Positron Emission Tomography (PET) with Computed Tomography (CT) to detect cancer and assess its spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">FDG PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG PET-CT is a PET-CT scan utilizing the radiotracer fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) to identify cancer cells, particularly in lymph nodes and at distant sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FES PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">FES PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">FES PET-CT is a specialized PET-CT scan that employs a radioactive form of estrogen to identify cancers that are estrogen receptor-positive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Node Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An axillary lymph node biopsy is a procedure to sample lymph nodes in the armpit to check for cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mastectomy">
      <data key="d0">Mastectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A mastectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of breast tissue to treat or prevent breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mastectomy is a surgery to remove the breast, often performed to eliminate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mastectomy is a surgical procedure to remove a breast, often performed to treat or prevent breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mastectomy is a surgical procedure to remove one or both breasts, primarily used as a treatment for breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lumpectomy">
      <data key="d0">Lumpectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Lumpectomy is a breast-conserving surgery that removes a tumor along with a small margin of surrounding tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumpectomy is a surgical procedure to remove a tumor and some surrounding tissue in the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="F-18 Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)">
      <data key="d0">F-18 Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG is a radiotracer used in PET scans, particularly effective in identifying cancer cells due to their high glucose metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sodium Fluoride">
      <data key="d0">Sodium Fluoride</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sodium Fluoride is a radiotracer that can be used in PET scans to identify bone metastases and other bone-related conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Core Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Core biopsy is a procedure that uses larger needles to extract a core of tissue from a suspicious area for diagnostic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vacuum-Assisted Core Biopsy (VACB)">
      <data key="d0">Vacuum-Assisted Core Biopsy (VACB)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Vacuum-Assisted Core Biopsy is a technique that uses suction to collect a tissue sample using a special device to increase the efficiency of sampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Incisional Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Incisional Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Incisional biopsy is a surgical procedure that removes a portion of a tumor for diagnosis and analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excisional Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Excisional Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An excisional biopsy involves removing all or part of a lymph node for cancer testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Excisional biopsy is a procedure that involves the complete removal of an abnormal area including the surrounding tissue for diagnostic evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">An Axillary Lymph Node is a lymph node located in the armpit area often examined during cancer diagnosis for metastasis evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathology Report">
      <data key="d0">Pathology Report</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pathology report is a document that outlines the findings from analyzed medical tests, used to inform treatment plans for conditions like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathology report is a document that provides detailed information about tissue samples examined under a microscope, particularly in diagnosing diseases like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathology report is a formal document that contains results from tissue samples and tests, which informs treatment decisions for conditions like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;The pathology report details the diagnosis and stage of cancer based on tissue biopsies, providing essential information for treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy procedure involves the removal of tissue to diagnose a condition, in this context particularly focused on identifying cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Biopsy Procedure involves extracting a small tissue sample from a tumor, which is critical for confirming a diagnosis of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentinel Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Sentinel Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sentinel lymph nodes are the first lymph nodes most likely to be affected by cancer cells spreading from a primary tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptor Testing">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptor Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone receptor testing is a diagnostic process done on tumors to evaluate the presence of estrogen or progesterone receptors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ER Positive Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">ER Positive Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ER positive breast cancer has estrogen receptors that promote growth; these tumors may respond to endocrine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptor Positive">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptor Positive</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone receptor positive breast cancer is characterized by the presence of hormone receptors that can be targeted in treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptor Negative">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptor Negative</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone receptor negative breast cancer lacks both estrogen and progesterone receptors and may grow faster.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen Receptor (ER)">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen Receptor (ER)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen Receptor (ER) is a protein that can influence the growth of some breast cancers and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The estrogen receptor (ER) status in breast cancer indicates whether the cancer cells might grow in response to estrogen, guiding treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;The estrogen receptor is a protein that, when stimulated by estrogen, signals breast cancer cells to grow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progesterone Receptor (PR)">
      <data key="d0">Progesterone Receptor (PR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progesterone Receptor (PR) is a protein that, like ER, can impact breast cancer treatment efficacy and strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;The progesterone receptor (PR) status is a relevant biomarker in breast cancer that indicates whether progesterone can support cancer cell growth.&lt;SEP&gt;The progesterone receptor binds progesterone and can also promote growth in breast cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA">
      <data key="d0">BRCA</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA refers to genes that help prevent tumor growth, and mutations in these genes increase the risk of multiple types of cancer, including breast and ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CEA">
      <data key="d0">CEA</data>
      <data key="d1">tumor_marker</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA) is a tumor marker that can indicate the presence of certain cancers, including breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CA 15-3">
      <data key="d0">CA 15-3</data>
      <data key="d1">tumor_marker</data>
      <data key="d2">CA 15-3 is a protein that may be elevated in breast cancer patients and is used in monitoring disease progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-1">
      <data key="d0">PD-1</data>
      <data key="d1">protein</data>
      <data key="d2">Programmed Death Protein 1 (PD-1) is an immune protein that can suppress the immune response to cancer cells when expressed on their surface.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCR">
      <data key="d0">PCR</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is a technique used to amplify DNA, allowing for the detection of abnormal cells within a sample.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Risk Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Risk Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic risk testing identifies hereditary mutations that may increase cancer risk, providing crucial information for family health history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutational burden is a measure of the number of mutations in tumor DNA, which can inform the effectiveness of immunotherapy treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden measures the total number of mutations within a tumor's DNA, providing insights into how a tumor may respond to treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune checkpoint inhibitors are a class of drugs designed to enhance the body's immune response against cancer cells by targeting specific proteins like PD-1 and PD-L1.&lt;SEP&gt;Immune checkpoint inhibitors are a type of cancer treatment that helps the immune system fight cancer, such as Pembrolizumab (Keytruda).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Genetic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary genetic testing identifies genetic mutations passed down from parents that may increase the risk of developing certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Team">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The cancer treatment team comprises medical professionals who collaboratively recommend testing and treatments customized to an individual's cancer type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Instability">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Instability</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite instability is a condition caused by defects in the mismatch repair system of DNA, leading to increased susceptibility to certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Staging of Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Staging of Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Staging of cancer is the process of determining the extent of cancer's spread, which informs treatment plans and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOG Scale">
      <data key="d0">ECOG Scale</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) performance status scale is a tool used in clinical oncology to assess a patient’s level of functioning and fitness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Siblings">
      <data key="d0">Siblings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Siblings are family members who share at least one parent, they are an integral part of understanding family health histories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Half-Siblings">
      <data key="d0">Half-Siblings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Half-siblings share one parent and play a role in family history discussions for health considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aunts and Uncles">
      <data key="d0">Aunts and Uncles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aunts and uncles are relatives who can provide valuable insights into family health history and genetic predispositions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nieces and Nephews">
      <data key="d0">Nieces and Nephews</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nieces and nephews are the children of one’s siblings or half-siblings, contributing to the broader family health narrative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grandparents">
      <data key="d0">Grandparents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grandparents are ancestors who can offer important information about genetic conditions and health histories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grandchildren">
      <data key="d0">Grandchildren</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grandchildren are the offspring of one’s children and can be affected by inherited health traits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOG Performance Status">
      <data key="d0">ECOG Performance Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ECOG Performance Status is a measure used to assess a patient's fitness level and ability to perform daily activities, influencing treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Breast Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast cancer treatment includes various therapeutic strategies developed based on diagnostic and staging assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary cancer refers to cancer types with genetic links, often requiring familial screening for early detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Size">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Size</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor size is a crucial factor in cancer staging, denoting the dimensions of the primary tumor, categorized in centimeters or millimeters, influencing treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor size is a key parameter in staging breast cancer, indicating the dimensions of the primary tumor, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Status">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node status defines whether cancer has spread to the nearby lymph nodes, which is crucial for treatment planning and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node status refers to the presence of cancer in nearby lymph nodes, which is a critical element in the staging process for breast cancer and affects treatment strategy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distant Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Distant Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distant metastasis refers to the spread of breast cancer to other organs, affecting treatment options and patient prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor 2 (HER2)">
      <data key="d0">Human Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor 2 (HER2)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 is a protein that can promote the growth of cancer cells, and its positive status in breast cancer patients may necessitate targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen Receptor (ER) Status">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen Receptor (ER) Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ER status determines whether the cancer cells express estrogen receptors that can promote cancer growth, influencing hormonal treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progesterone Receptor (PR) Status">
      <data key="d0">Progesterone Receptor (PR) Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PR status indicates the presence of progesterone receptors in breast cancer, which affects treatment decisions and overall prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Approaches">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Approaches</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment approaches for breast cancer may include surgery, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, and targeted therapies, depending on the cancer stage and characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IBC">
      <data key="d0">IBC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC) is a rare and aggressive form of breast cancer that can develop rapidly and is often diagnosed at a more advanced stage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Nurse">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Nurse</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An oncology nurse provides direct patient care, administers treatments, and supports patients throughout their cancer journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Noninvasive Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Noninvasive Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Noninvasive Breast Cancer is cancer that has not spread from its point of origin, such as Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 breast cancer, or DCIS, indicates noninvasive cancer that has not spread to surrounding tissue or lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage I Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage I Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage I breast cancer indicates the cancer is invasive but still localized, meaning it has not spread to the lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage II Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage II Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage II breast cancer suggests a more advanced stage than Stage I, and may involve regional lymph nodes but is still treatable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage III Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage III Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage III breast cancer indicates more extensive disease, involving lymph nodes but not distant metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage IV Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage IV Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage IV breast cancer is metastatic, meaning it has spread to other parts of the body beyond the breast and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Practice Providers (APPs)">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Practice Providers (APPs)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced Practice Providers (APPs) are healthcare professionals who provide advanced care, including nurse practitioners and physician assistants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Workers">
      <data key="d0">Social Workers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Social workers are professionals who assist individuals in addressing and managing issues in their daily lives, including mental, behavioral, and emotional problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory breast cancer is a specific and aggressive type of breast cancer that requires targeted treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory breast cancer is a type of breast cancer characterized by rapid changes in the breast skin and a specific treatment approach involving systemic therapy followed by surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Social Workers">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Social Workers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical social workers are specialized professionals who diagnose and treat mental, behavioral, and emotional issues in individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Research Team">
      <data key="d0">Research Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A research team is a group of professionals that collect data and coordinate care for clinical trials, contributing to advancements in cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;The research team is a group of medical experts who oversee and manage clinical trials, ensuring participants understand procedures and risks involved.&lt;SEP&gt;The research team manages clinical trials and oversees the participant's safety and adherence to study protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Overview">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Overview</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The treatment overview outlines the approaches and types of therapies available for managing inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 Proteins">
      <data key="d0">HER2 Proteins</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 proteins are involved in cell growth and division and are significant in determining treatment pathways for certain breast cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified Radical Mastectomy">
      <data key="d0">Modified Radical Mastectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Modified radical mastectomy is a surgical procedure where the entire breast and some lymph nodes are removed, potentially also including part of the chest wall muscle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT)">
      <data key="d0">External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">EBRT is a method of delivering a beam of high-energy x-rays to the location of the cancer, recommended for various stages of cervical cancer based on risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT is a method of delivering radiation to the cervix and surrounding areas as a treatment for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT is a treatment method involving the external delivery of high-energy radiation to the cervix to destroy cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT is a type of radiation therapy that targets tumors with high-energy rays but can affect fertility if ovaries are in the treatment field.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT uses an external machine called a linear accelerator to direct radiation at the breast and lymph nodes for cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy is a treatment method using radiation to target and kill cancer cells at the tumor site.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy is a treatment that uses high-energy waves to kill cancer cells, specifically targeting the tumor and nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;External beam radiation therapy is a treatment method utilizing high-energy x-rays to kill or shrink cancer cells in thyroid cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Drug Interactions">
      <data key="d0">Drug Interactions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Drug interactions can occur when certain supplements or medications affect the efficacy of chemotherapy drugs, necessitating careful management by healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2-targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d0">HER2-targeted Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2-targeted therapy addresses HER2-positive breast cancer by using drugs that inhibit growth signals from the HER2 protein.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDK4/6 Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">CDK4/6 Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">CDK4/6 inhibitors are used to control hormone receptor-positive breast cancer; these include drugs like abemaciclib and palbociclib.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mTOR Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">mTOR Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">mTOR inhibitors are medications that block the activity of the mTOR protein, which helps regulate cell growth and division, particularly in cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;mTOR inhibitors are utilized to control cell growth in cases where endocrine therapy becomes ineffective, examples include everolimus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node radiation therapy, also known as regional nodal irradiation (RNI), targets lymph nodes for cancer treatment to control local cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fast-Dividing Cells">
      <data key="d0">Fast-Dividing Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fast-dividing cells include cancer cells and some normal cells that chemotherapy aims to target in order to manage or eliminate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Turmeric">
      <data key="d0">Turmeric</data>
      <data key="d1">supplement</data>
      <data key="d2">Turmeric is a herbal supplement that may interact with certain chemotherapy drugs, potentially affecting treatment efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gingko Biloba">
      <data key="d0">Gingko Biloba</data>
      <data key="d1">supplement</data>
      <data key="d2">Gingko biloba is a herbal supplement that might impact the effectiveness of systemic therapies, necessitating discussions with healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Green Tea Extract">
      <data key="d0">Green Tea Extract</data>
      <data key="d1">supplement</data>
      <data key="d2">Green tea extract is a dietary supplement that can interact with cancer treatments, making it important to discuss with healthcare teams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="St. John's Wort">
      <data key="d0">St. John's Wort</data>
      <data key="d1">supplement</data>
      <data key="d2">St. John's Wort is a herbal supplement known to interact with various medications, including chemotherapy drugs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antioxidants">
      <data key="d0">Antioxidants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Antioxidants are compounds that can impact the effects of cancer treatments and may require careful management during therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Doxorubicin">
      <data key="d0">Doxorubicin</data>
      <data key="d1">chemotherapy drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Doxorubicin is an anthracycline chemotherapy drug used in treating various cancers, including breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Docetaxel">
      <data key="d0">Docetaxel</data>
      <data key="d1">chemotherapy drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Docetaxel is a taxane chemotherapy drug used in breast cancer treatment to inhibit cancer cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Capecitabine">
      <data key="d0">Capecitabine</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Capecitabine is a chemotherapy medication used to treat colon cancer, often used in conjunction with other treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Capecitabine is an antimetabolite chemotherapy drug administered orally to treat certain types of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy agent used particularly for colon cancer, which is often administered after surgery to reduce recurrence risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy drug that converts to fluorouracil in the body and is used to treat breast and colorectal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy medication used in combination with radiation therapy for anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epirubicin">
      <data key="d0">Epirubicin</data>
      <data key="d1">chemotherapy drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Epirubicin is an anthracycline chemotherapy drug utilized alongside other treatments for managing breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Everolimus">
      <data key="d0">Everolimus</data>
      <data key="d1">mTOR inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d2">Everolimus is an mTOR inhibitor that is used to help endocrine therapy work again in breast cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Everolimus, marketed as Afinitor, is an mTOR inhibitor that helps restore the effectiveness of endocrine therapy in breast cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palbociclib">
      <data key="d0">Palbociclib</data>
      <data key="d1">CDK4/6 inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d2">Palbociclib is a CDK4/6 inhibitor that is used in combination with endocrine therapy in treating hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ribociclib">
      <data key="d0">Ribociclib</data>
      <data key="d1">CDK4/6 inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d2">Ribociclib is another CDK4/6 inhibitor used alongside endocrine therapy to control breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fulvestrant">
      <data key="d0">Fulvestrant</data>
      <data key="d1">endocrine therapy</data>
      <data key="d2">Fulvestrant is an endocrine therapy used in hormone receptor-positive breast cancer to reduce cancer growth signals.&lt;SEP&gt;Fulvestrant, marketed as Faslodex, is a drug that acts as an estrogen receptor degrader for treating hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tamoxifen">
      <data key="d0">Tamoxifen</data>
      <data key="d1">endocrine therapy</data>
      <data key="d2">Tamoxifen is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) used in hormone therapy for breast cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;Tamoxifen is an endocrine therapy drug used predominantly in estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ibrance">
      <data key="d0">Ibrance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ibrance is a medication used in cancer treatment, specifically targeting hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kisqali">
      <data key="d0">Kisqali</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kisqali is a cancer medication that also targets hormone receptor-positive breast cancer, used similarly to Ibrance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exemestane">
      <data key="d0">Exemestane</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Exemestane is a medication often combined with mTOR inhibitors like Everolimus for treating breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Exemestane is a medication specifically used as an aromatase inhibitor, employed in hormone receptor-positive breast cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Talazoparib">
      <data key="d0">Talazoparib</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Talazoparib, marketed as Talzenna, is another PARP inhibitor effective for breast cancer patients with specific genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA1 and BRCA2">
      <data key="d0">BRCA1 and BRCA2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA1 and BRCA2 are genes where mutations increase the risk of breast cancer and determine eligibility for PARP inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpelisib">
      <data key="d0">Alpelisib</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Alpelisib, known as Piqray, is a PIK3CA inhibitor used in breast cancer tailored treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pembrolizumab">
      <data key="d0">Pembrolizumab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pembrolizumab, also known as Keytruda, is an immune checkpoint inhibitor used as a treatment for cancers with specific biomarkers, including cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab, under the brand name Keytruda, is an example of immunotherapy used to treat various cancers including breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocrine therapy involves blocking hormones to treat hormone receptor-positive breast cancer, effectively slowing tumor growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine therapy is a treatment that aims to manipulate hormone levels and is used in contexts such as cancer treatment, potentially affecting menstrual cycles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bilateral Oophorectomy">
      <data key="d0">Bilateral Oophorectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bilateral oophorectomy is a surgical procedure to remove both ovaries, which may be part of endocrine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Ablation">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Ablation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian ablation is a procedure that permanently stops the ovaries from producing hormones, utilized in endocrine therapy for breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian ablation uses radiation to permanently stop hormone production from the ovaries as a cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GnRH Agonists">
      <data key="d0">GnRH Agonists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">GnRH agonists are medications that inhibit gonadotropin-releasing hormone, thus reducing hormone levels and treating hormone-sensitive cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;GnRH agonists, like goserelin and leuprolide, are used to suppress ovarian hormone production in breast cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aromatase Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Aromatase Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aromatase inhibitors are a class of drugs that lower estrogen levels in postmenopausal women, helping to treat hormone-sensitive breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Aromatase inhibitors prevent the conversion of androgens to estrogens, impacting hormone-sensitive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Endocrine Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Endocrine Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical endocrine therapy refers to procedures such as bilateral oophorectomy that directly affect hormone production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Menopause">
      <data key="d0">Menopause</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Menopause is the period marking the end of menstrual cycles, which affects hormonal treatment options for breast cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testosterone">
      <data key="d0">Testosterone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testosterone is a hormone that can be involved in breast cancer treatments for assigned males during endocrine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mTOR">
      <data key="d0">mTOR</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">mTOR is a protein that plays a critical role in cell growth and division and is targeted by specific cancer therapies to enhance treatment effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Suppression">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Suppression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian suppression is a treatment strategy that temporarily halts hormone production from the ovaries, pivotal in certain breast cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Androgens">
      <data key="d0">Androgens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Androgens are male hormones produced in both males and females, impacting hormone-sensitive cancer growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ethinyl Estradiol">
      <data key="d0">Ethinyl Estradiol</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ethinyl estradiol is a synthetic estrogen often included in hormone treatments for various medical conditions, including hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amenorrhea">
      <data key="d0">Amenorrhea</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Amenorrhea is the absence of menstrual periods, which can occur during or following hormone-related treatments for breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Amenorrhea refers to the absence of menstrual periods, which can occur during premenopause or as a result of certain treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Luteinizing Hormone-Releasing Hormone">
      <data key="d0">Luteinizing Hormone-Releasing Hormone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone (LHRH) is responsible for signaling the production of sex hormones, playing a central role in various cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Women in Premenopause">
      <data key="d0">Women in Premenopause</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Women in premenopause experience regular ovarian function, which can influence treatment options and outcomes for breast cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary CNS Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary CNS Lymphoma is a form of cancer that starts in the central nervous system, often presenting as tumors in the brain.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer primarily affecting the brain and nervous system, characterized by symptoms such as memory problems, coordination difficulties, and personality changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of cancer that originates in the central nervous system, characterized by the presence of one or multiple tumors in the brain.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is an uncommon cancer that affects the central nervous system, requiring multidisciplinary treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer that originates in the central nervous system, affecting lymphocytes in the brain and spinal cord.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the brain and central nervous system, often presenting with multiple tumors on scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the brain and central nervous system, often requiring various treatment modalities including chemotherapy and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the brain tissue and may have various testing and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the central nervous system, often requiring aggressive treatment such as chemotherapy and radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the central nervous system, primarily affecting the brain and spinal cord.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Central Nervous System (CNS)">
      <data key="d0">Central Nervous System (CNS)</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Central Nervous System (CNS) comprises the brain and spinal cord and is the primary area affected by primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The central nervous system (CNS) is comprised of the brain and spinal cord, playing a critical role in processing information and controlling responses within the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV">
      <data key="d0">HIV</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV is the immunodeficiency virus that can weaken the immune system and is frequently linked to the onset of primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV, or human immunodeficiency virus, is a virus that can weaken the immune system and increase cancer risk, including anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) is another risk factor for anal cancer, compromising the immune system and increasing cancer susceptibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epstein-Barr Virus">
      <data key="d0">Epstein-Barr Virus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) is a virus that can be associated with nasopharyngeal cancer and is used in staging to assess prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is a common virus that can cause infectious mononucleosis and is associated with certain types of cancer, particularly nasopharyngeal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Epstein-Barr virus is a common virus that can remain dormant in the body and is associated with an increased risk of developing primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The Epstein-Barr virus is known to be associated with certain types of cancers, including nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Older Adults">
      <data key="d0">Older Adults</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Older adults, particularly those in their 60s and 70s, have a higher incidence of primary CNS lymphoma compared to younger populations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Premenopause">
      <data key="d0">Premenopause</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Premenopause is a transitional phase leading to menopause, where the ovaries gradually produce less estrogen and progesterone, affecting menstrual cycles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Brain Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A brain tumor is an abnormal growth of cells in the brain that can be cancerous or non-cancerous. Gliomas are one of the primary types of brain tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A brain tumor is an abnormal growth of cells in the brain, which can lead to various health complications, including primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Brain tumors can vary in type and severity, causing various symptoms and health issues, including nausea, vomiting, and cognitive difficulties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunodeficiency Disorder">
      <data key="d0">Immunodeficiency Disorder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An immunodeficiency disorder is a condition that weakens the immune system's ability to fight infections and increases the risk of diseases like lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Autoimmune Disease">
      <data key="d0">Autoimmune Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An autoimmune disease, such as lupus, occurs when the immune system mistakenly attacks the body’s own tissues, potentially increasing cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Treatment Options">
      <data key="d0">Medical Treatment Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical treatment options refer to various therapies available for diseases like cancer, helping patients make informed decisions about their care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNS">
      <data key="d0">CNS</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Central Nervous System (CNS) is the part of the nervous system that includes the brain and spinal cord, where primary CNS lymphoma can occur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cell Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cell Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stem cell transplant is a potential treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, where healthy stem cells are used to replace destroyed ones from intensive treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell transplant is a potential treatment method for restoring blood formation after high-dose chemotherapy in patients with primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tests for Diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">Tests for Diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests for diagnosis are a series of medical examinations, including biopsies and imaging, to confirm the presence of primary CNS lymphoma and assess its extent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Online Resources">
      <data key="d0">Online Resources</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Online resources provide information and support for patients and caregivers dealing with primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Online resources provide information and support to patients and caregivers facing primary CNS lymphoma, helping them navigate treatment options and care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnosis Tests">
      <data key="d0">Diagnosis Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnosis tests include various procedures such as imaging and biopsies used to confirm the presence of primary CNS lymphoma and assess its extent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Remission">
      <data key="d0">Remission</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Remission refers to a period during which the signs and symptoms of primary CNS lymphoma decrease significantly, sometimes lasting for months or years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A stereotactic biopsy is a medical procedure used to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma by removing a tissue sample for microscopic examination.&lt;SEP&gt;A stereotactic biopsy is a precise method for obtaining a tissue sample from a brain tumor, useful for difficult-to-reach tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A stereotactic biopsy is a surgical procedure performed by a neurosurgeon to obtain a tissue sample of a glioma tumor using precision imaging techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure that allows precise sampling of brain tumors located in complex regions of the brain.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic biopsy is a precise method for extracting tissue samples from hard-to-reach areas of the brain to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic biopsy is the precise method used to diagnose brain tumors located in difficult areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gadolinium">
      <data key="d0">Gadolinium</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">Gadolinium is a contrast agent injected during MRI scans to improve the clarity of the images, aiding in the diagnosis of CNS conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Gadolinium is a contrast agent used during MRI scans to enhance the visibility of abnormal tissues in imaging results for CNS evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Tissue Sample">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Tissue Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Tumor Tissue Sample is a small portion of tumor taken for testing, essential for diagnosing primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurological Illnesses">
      <data key="d0">Neurological Illnesses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neurological Illnesses include a variety of conditions affecting the nervous system, which can exhibit symptoms similar to those of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Growth">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Growth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Growth can refer to the development and increase in size of cancerous or non-cancerous masses in the body, including in the CNS.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gadolinium Contrast">
      <data key="d0">Gadolinium Contrast</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">Gadolinium Contrast is specifically used during MRI scans to enhance the visibility of tumors for better diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stephanie E. Weiss, MD, FASTRO">
      <data key="d0">Stephanie E. Weiss, MD, FASTRO</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Stephanie E. Weiss is a medical doctor specializing in oncology, prominently involved in the analysis and diagnostics of lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET/CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET/CT scan combines positron emission tomography and computed tomography to provide detailed images of the body's internal structures, aiding in cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET/CT scan is an imaging test used to find cancer by taking detailed pictures of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eye Exam">
      <data key="d0">Eye Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An eye exam is a procedure used to check for the presence of lymphoma in the eye, particularly in patients diagnosed with primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cerebrospinal Fluid">
      <data key="d0">Cerebrospinal Fluid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cerebrospinal fluid is a liquid surrounding the brain and spinal cord, which can be analyzed to detect the presence of cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitreous">
      <data key="d0">Vitreous</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The vitreous is the clear gel that fills the eye, which can be affected by certain types of lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optic Nerves">
      <data key="d0">Optic Nerves</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The optic nerves transmit visual information from the retina to the brain and are directly connected to the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Retina">
      <data key="d0">Retina</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The retina is a layer at the back of the eye that converts light into signals for the brain and can be involved in lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Slit Lamp">
      <data key="d0">Slit Lamp</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A slit lamp is a specialized microscope used by eye doctors to examine the structure of the eye in detail.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Distress During Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Distress During Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines provide resources and information to help patients manage stress and distress during cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound of the Testicles">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound of the Testicles</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An ultrasound of the testicles is an imaging technique that may be used to examine for signs of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CBC">
      <data key="d0">CBC</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Complete blood count (CBC) is a common blood test measuring elements in the blood, including red and white blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CMP">
      <data key="d0">CMP</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP) encompasses 14 blood tests to assess various health metrics including blood sugar and electrolyte levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDH">
      <data key="d0">LDH</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme levels test which can indicate cell damage, particularly in cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV Blood Test">
      <data key="d0">HIV Blood Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The HIV blood test is essential for patients with primary CNS lymphoma to assess the impact of the virus on treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B Test">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B testing is necessary before immunotherapy treatment for patients with previous exposure or at risk for the virus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Corticosteroids">
      <data key="d0">Corticosteroids</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Corticosteroids are anti-inflammatory medications often used in conjunction with chemotherapy to manage symptoms and side effects of cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Corticosteroids are used as part of the treatment plan for patients with primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Medications used in cancer treatment to reduce inflammation and manage symptoms associated with cancer and its treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cell Rescue">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cell Rescue</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stem cell rescue is a medical procedure involving harvesting stem cells and transplanting them after high-dose chemotherapy to support recovery.'&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue is a procedure aimed at restoring healthy blood cell production in the bone marrow after intensive chemotherapy by reintroducing the patient's own stem cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue is a procedure to restore stem cells after high-dose treatment for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue is a treatment procedure aimed at restoring bone marrow function after high doses of chemotherapy or radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapy Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapy Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment that utilizes the body's immune system to fight cancer, often used in conjunction with other treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antiretroviral Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Antiretroviral Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment specifically for patients with HIV that is essential alongside cancer treatment for those with coexisting conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Psychological Distress">
      <data key="d0">Psychological Distress</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Emotional suffering that can be experienced by cancer patients, often requiring support and intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoma Testing">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoma Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Various medical tests used to confirm the presence and extent of lymphoma in patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDA">
      <data key="d0">FDA</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The FDA (Food and Drug Administration) is a U.S. federal agency responsible for approving drugs and treatments based on their safety and efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA (Food and Drug Administration) is a government agency responsible for protecting public health through the regulation of food, drugs, and medical devices.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a government agency responsible for approving new drugs and treatment approaches after clinical trials demonstrate safety and effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANCER (6237)">
      <data key="d0">CANCER (6237)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">CANCER (6237) is a service provided by the NCI that offers assistance in finding clinical trials and cancer-related information.&lt;SEP&gt;CANCER (6237) is a service provided by the NCI's Cancer Information Service that assists patients in finding clinical trials and obtaining cancer information.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Information Service provides support and information about cancer and clinical trials to patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCI">
      <data key="d0">NCI</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is an organization that conducts and supports cancer research and provides information about cancer treatments and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapy Drug">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapy Drug</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapy drugs are treatments designed to harness the body's immune system to fight cancer cells more effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Drugs">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Drugs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy drugs are medically administered substances used to treat cancer by killing cancer cells, usually delivered intravenously during treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy drugs are medications used to destroy rapidly dividing cancer cells, often used in combination for treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">CNS Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CNS Lymphoma refers to cancer that originates in the central nervous system, necessitating specialized and prompt treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the brain and spinal cord, requiring specialized treatment protocols.&lt;SEP&gt;CNS lymphoma refers to lymphomas that occur within the central nervous system, presenting specific challenges for treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trial Eligibility Criteria">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trial Eligibility Criteria</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Eligibility criteria are specific rules that determine who may participate in a clinical trial, ensuring participant suitability for the study.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Active Monitoring">
      <data key="d0">Active Monitoring</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Active monitoring is a strategy where patients are observed for changes in their condition instead of immediate intervention, often used in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM)">
      <data key="d0">U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) provides access to various clinical trial information through its clinicaltrials.gov website.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) provides comprehensive information resources related to clinical trials, including treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) provides public access to clinical trial information through its website clinicaltrials.gov.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine is a resource for accessing medical literature and clinical trial information, facilitating patient access to trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine provides access to biomedical information and conducts research about various health conditions including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intraocular Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intraocular Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Intraocular chemotherapy involves directly infusing chemotherapy into the eye to treat primary CNS lymphoma that occurs there.&lt;SEP&gt;Intraocular chemotherapy is a treatment method for primary CNS lymphoma where chemotherapy is directly infused into the eye.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Dose Methotrexate">
      <data key="d0">High-Dose Methotrexate</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">High-dose methotrexate is a potent chemotherapy drug used primarily to treat primary CNS lymphoma, noted for its effectiveness and potential severe side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;High-dose methotrexate is an effective chemotherapy drug utilized in the treatment of primary CNS lymphoma, overcoming the blood-brain barrier to target lymphoma cells.&lt;SEP&gt;High-dose methotrexate is the main chemotherapy drug used to treat primary CNS lymphoma, known for its effectiveness but also for its potential side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy (WBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy (WBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">WBRT is a radiation technique used to treat primary CNS lymphoma throughout the entire brain, effective for multiple tumor sites.&lt;SEP&gt;WBRT is a radiation treatment technique that is commonly used for managing primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;WBRT is a specific type of radiation therapy used to treat primary CNS lymphoma by affecting the entire brain area to manage multiple tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cycles">
      <data key="d0">Cycles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cycles refer to the scheduled intervals at which chemotherapy drugs are administered, which can last 14, 21, or 28 days to facilitate recovery between treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Side effects are unintended effects that occur as a result of cancer treatment, which can influence a patient's quality of life and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are unintended reactions or conditions resulting from treatment that can vary based on the drug, its dosage, and the individual's response.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are unintended symptoms or reactions experienced by patients undergoing cancer treatments, varying based on treatment type.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are unwanted symptoms caused by cancer treatment that can impact a patient's well-being and quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects refer to the unintended consequences of radiation therapy and systemic treatments, including fatigue, nausea, and long-term complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Failure">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Failure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidney failure is a serious potential side effect from high-dose chemotherapy treatments like methotrexate, where the kidneys may become unable to filter waste effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rituximab">
      <data key="d0">Rituximab</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Rituximab is an immunotherapy agent often used alongside chemotherapy for enhancing treatment effectiveness in lymphoma cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is an immunotherapy drug used to treat primary CNS lymphoma by harnessing the body's immune system against cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab, also known as Rituxan, is a monoclonal antibody used as immunotherapy to target specific proteins on the surface of cancer cells in primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab, also known as Rituxan, is an immunotherapy drug often combined with high-dose chemotherapy to enhance treatment efficacy for lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Dose Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">High-Dose Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">High-dose chemotherapy is a treatment approach that involves administering intense chemotherapy to eliminate cancer cells but also harms healthy cells in the bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;High-dose chemotherapy is an intensive treatment regimen used to eliminate cancer cells, but it also destroys healthy stem cells in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eyes">
      <data key="d0">Eyes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The eyes can be affected by primary CNS lymphoma and are sometimes treated directly with radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cognition Problems">
      <data key="d0">Cognition Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cognition problems refer to issues affecting memory, thinking, and speaking abilities, often resulting from conditions like radiation therapy or cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine Motor Skills">
      <data key="d0">Fine Motor Skills</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fine motor skills involve small muscle movements, such as handwriting and tying shoes, which may be impeded due to neurological issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allergic Reactions">
      <data key="d0">Allergic Reactions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Allergic reactions are adverse responses of the immune system that can occur as side effects of medications, including cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CD20 Proteins">
      <data key="d0">CD20 Proteins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CD20 proteins are surface markers found on certain types of B cells, which are targeted by monoclonal antibodies like rituximab in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Visual Problems">
      <data key="d0">Visual Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Visual problems can arise as side effects from radiation therapy, affecting a patient's eyesight temporarily or permanently.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptom Tracking">
      <data key="d0">Symptom Tracking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptom tracking involves monitoring and recording side effects experienced during treatment to help healthcare providers manage care effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rituxan">
      <data key="d0">Rituxan</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Rituxan is the brand name for rituximab, a drug used in immunotherapy for cancer treatment targeting CD20 proteins on B cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Care">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Care</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Multidisciplinary care involves a coordinated approach among various health care providers to manage patient care effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Multidisciplinary care involves various healthcare professionals collaborating to provide comprehensive treatment and support for patients with complex conditions like CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Methotrexate">
      <data key="d0">Methotrexate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug critical in treating CNS lymphoma by limiting the cancer cells' ability to replicate through folate inhibition.&lt;SEP&gt;Methotrexate is the main chemotherapy drug used to treat primary CNS lymphoma, affecting rapidly dividing cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stem cells are undifferentiated cells that can develop into various cell types and are harvested for medical procedures such as stem cell rescue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient">
      <data key="d0">Patient</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A patient undergoing stem cell rescue is someone who has cancer and requires treatment to restore healthy blood-forming stem cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuro-Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Neuro-Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neuro-oncologist is a specialist focused on the diagnosis and treatment of brain and spinal cord tumors, including CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recovery Process">
      <data key="d0">Recovery Process</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The recovery process involves the steps and time it takes for a patient to regain health and strength following treatments such as chemotherapy and stem cell rescue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pain Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Pain Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pain specialist is a healthcare provider who focuses on managing and alleviating pain experienced by patients, especially in cancer treatment contexts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Psychologist">
      <data key="d0">Psychologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A psychologist provides mental health support to patients, helping them cope with the emotional challenges of cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Induction Phase">
      <data key="d0">Induction Phase</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Induction Phase in lymphoma treatment aims to eliminate as many cancer cells as possible and typically lasts several months.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Consolidation Phase">
      <data key="d0">Consolidation Phase</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Consolidation Phase follows the Induction Phase, focusing on destroying remaining cancer cells and preventing recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colon Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Colon Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colon polyps are abnormal growths on the inner lining of the colon that can vary in shape and size. They can be benign or precursors to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Colon polyps are non-cancerous growths that can develop into colon cancer if not removed; most common type is adenoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Induction Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Induction Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Induction treatment for CNS lymphoma is the initial phase of therapy aimed at eliminating as many cancer cells as possible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spinal Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Spinal Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Spinal treatment focuses on addressing lymphoma that may be present in the spine or spinal fluid as part of CNS lymphoma care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eye Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Eye Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Eye treatment refers to interventions aimed at managing any ocular involvement from CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenomas">
      <data key="d0">Adenomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenomas are benign polyps found in the colon that can develop into colorectal cancer over time if not monitored or removed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Correlating Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">Correlating Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Correlating guidelines by organizations such as the NCCN support patient education and informed decision-making regarding cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal cancer includes cancers that originate in the colon or rectum and is largely influenced by the presence of colon polyps.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal cancer is a type of cancer that affects the colon and rectum, often diagnosed in various stages and linked to specific risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the colon or rectum, often developing from benign polyps, affected by genetic factors and lifestyle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anus">
      <data key="d0">Anus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The anus is the final part of the digestive tract where stool is expelled from the body, corresponding to the end of the colon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis">
      <data key="d0">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP) is a genetic disorder that leads to the development of numerous polyps in the colon and rectum, significantly elevating cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is a hereditary condition that predisposes individuals to several types of cancer, including thyroid cancer, due to genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Blood Count">
      <data key="d0">Complete Blood Count</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a blood test that measures the number of different blood cells, providing insight into a person's overall health and potential cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoembryonic Antigen">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoembryonic Antigen</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA) is a protein that can be measured in the blood, with elevated levels potentially indicating the presence or spread of colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non-Metastatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has not spread beyond its original location and may be treated with surgical and other aggressive options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colectomy">
      <data key="d0">Colectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Colectomy is a surgical procedure for removing the part of the colon that contains cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Colectomy is a surgical procedure to remove all or part of the colon and may involve various preparatory steps and recovery instructions.&lt;SEP&gt;Colectomy is a surgical procedure to remove all or part of the colon, typically performed to treat colon cancer or other colon-related issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Colectomy is a surgical procedure to remove part or all of the colon, often performed for non-metastatic colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ctDNA">
      <data key="d0">ctDNA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) is a form of genetic material released into the bloodstream from cancer cells, providing insights into cancer progression and treatment efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) is a marker used in testing to monitor cancer, particularly useful in the context of colorectal cancer detection and monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="colorectal cancer">
      <data key="d0">colorectal cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal cancer is a type of cancer that affects the colon and rectum, often associated with various risk factors, including family history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ctDNA testing">
      <data key="d0">ctDNA testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ctDNA testing, also known as liquid biopsy, is a method of detecting small pieces of DNA released by tumor cells in the blood, used for monitoring cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="sperm banking">
      <data key="d0">sperm banking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sperm banking is a method of preserving semen for future use, often discussed with male patients prior to cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="egg freezing">
      <data key="d0">egg freezing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Egg freezing is a fertility preservation method where unfertilized eggs are frozen for future use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="family health history">
      <data key="d0">family health history</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family health history refers to the record of health conditions and diseases in a person's family, crucial for understanding cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mental Health">
      <data key="d0">Mental Health</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mental health refers to the emotional and psychological well-being of individuals, emphasizing self-care and prioritizing oneself in the context of health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pedunculated Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Pedunculated Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pedunculated polyps are a type of colon polyp characterized by a stalk and a round top, protruding from the colon wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sessile Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Sessile Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sessile polyps are flatter colon polyps without a stalk, appearing directly on the colon wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A malignant polyp is a polyp in which cancer has just started to grow, posing a risk of cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colostomy">
      <data key="d0">Colostomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Colostomy is a procedure that connects a part of the colon to an opening on the abdomen when safe reconnection is not possible.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy is a surgical procedure that connects the remaining part of the colon to an opening in the abdominal wall, allowing stool to exit the body into a bag.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage of Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage of Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The stage of cancer describes the extent of the cancer's spread within the body and is critical for treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Joint Committee">
      <data key="d0">American Joint Committee</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Joint Committee provides guidelines and information pertinent to cancer staging and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive cancer occurs when cancer cells spread beyond their original site, particularly if not detected early enough for treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polyp Removal">
      <data key="d0">Polyp Removal</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Polyp removal refers to procedures such as polypectomy that extract polyps from the colon during a colonoscopy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resection">
      <data key="d0">Resection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Resection is a surgical procedure aimed at removing a tumor and can serve both diagnostic and therapeutic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Resection is a surgical procedure that involves removing a larger section of the colon when polyps are suspected to be cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anastomotic Leak">
      <data key="d0">Anastomotic Leak</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An anastomotic leak is a complication that can occur after surgical reconnection of the colon, leading to leakage of intestinal contents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hernia">
      <data key="d0">Hernia</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A hernia is a condition in which an internal organ pushes through a weak spot in the surrounding muscle or connective tissue, which can be a complication post-surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC)">
      <data key="d0">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The AJCC is a professional organization that provides tools and systems, such as the TNM system, for staging cancer including colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nivolumab (Opdivo)">
      <data key="d0">Nivolumab (Opdivo)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Nivolumab is an immune checkpoint inhibitor used in cancer immunotherapy to enhance the body’s immune response against cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Nivolumab is an immunotherapy drug that can be used alone or with ipilimumab (Yervoy) for treating specific colon cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Nivolumab is an immunotherapy drug used to treat various cancers, including colon cancer, often in combination with other therapies to enhance efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)">
      <data key="d0">5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">5-Fluorouracil is a chemotherapeutic agent commonly used to treat multiple types of cancer, including colon cancer, in conjunction with other treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;5-Fluorouracil is a chemotherapy drug that treats various forms of cancer, including colon cancer, typically administered along with leucovorin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAPEOX">
      <data key="d0">CAPEOX</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CAPEOX is a chemotherapy regimen consisting of capecitabine and oxaliplatin, used specifically for high-risk colon cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;CAPEOX is a chemotherapy regimen used for treating high-risk colon cancers, consisting of capecitabine and oxaliplatin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOLFOX">
      <data key="d0">FOLFOX</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FOLFOX is a chemotherapy regimen that combines oxaliplatin with leucovorin and 5-FU, often used for treating colon cancer after surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;FOLFOX is a chemotherapy regimen that includes a combination of oxaliplatin, leucovorin, and 5-fluorouracil, typically used for colon cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC)">
      <data key="d0">American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">AJCC is an authoritative body that provides medical guidelines and the TNM classification system for staging cancers, including colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The AJCC is responsible for developing the TNM classification system for staging cancer, including anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) is a type of cancer affecting the nasopharynx, commonly occurring in certain populations such as those in Asia, Native America, and North Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharynx">
      <data key="d0">Pharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The pharynx is part of the throat, connecting the nasal cavity to the esophagus and larynx, and consists of several sections including the nasopharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance Follow-Up Testing">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance Follow-Up Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surveillance follow-up testing is initiated after treatment to monitor for any signs of cancer recurrence, including physical exams and imaging tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasal Cavity">
      <data key="d0">Nasal Cavity</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The nasal cavity is the space inside the nose that connects to the nasopharynx and is crucial for the anatomy of the respiratory system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypopharynx">
      <data key="d0">Hypopharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The hypopharynx is a part of the pharynx located near the larynx and esophagus and is involved in swallowing and air passage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinogenic Risk Factors">
      <data key="d0">Carcinogenic Risk Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinogenic risk factors contribute to the development of nasopharyngeal cancers and may include environmental and genetic predispositions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Survivorship Care">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Survivorship Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer survivorship care focuses on the long-term health and well-being of individuals who have undergone treatment for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Exams">
      <data key="d0">Physical Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical exams are part of the recommended follow-up for cancer survivors to check for potential signs of recurrence or new health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical exams are routine check-ups conducted by oncologists to monitor patients' health after cancer treatment, typically scheduled at specific intervals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NPC">
      <data key="d0">NPC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NPC, or nasopharyngeal carcinoma, is a type of cancer affecting the nasopharynx, which connects the nasal cavity to the pharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck cancers are a group of cancers that arise in the head and neck area of the body, requiring specialized treatment based on tumor location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A comprehensive cancer center specializes in evaluating and treating various types of cancer, including NPC, with expert healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)">
      <data key="d0">Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">EBV DNA testing may be carried out as part of the diagnosis and monitoring of nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharynx">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The nasopharynx is the upper part of the throat behind the nose, playing a critical role in connecting the nasal cavity to the throat and is the site affected by NPC.&lt;SEP&gt;The nasopharynx is the upper part of the throat behind the nose, where carcinoma can develop.&lt;SEP&gt;The nasopharynx is the upper part of the throat situated behind the nose, where conditions like squamous cell carcinoma can develop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support Networks">
      <data key="d0">Support Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Support networks refer to friends, family members, or peers that provide emotional and practical support to cancer patients during their treatment journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guidelines for Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guidelines for Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN provides guidelines and resources to assist patients in managing cancer treatment and care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthcare Provider">
      <data key="d0">Healthcare Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Healthcare providers are medical professionals who diagnose and treat cancer, assisting patients with test results and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Healthcare providers are professionals who offer medical services and play a crucial role in diagnosing and treating patients, especially those with cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dental Cleanings">
      <data key="d0">Dental Cleanings</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Dental cleanings are regular checkup procedures performed by dentists to maintain oral health, especially significant for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck cancer treatment refers to the various medical interventions employed to manage and treat cancers in the head and neck region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mental Health Services">
      <data key="d0">Mental Health Services</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Mental health services include therapies and supports available to help patients cope with psychological impacts of diseases such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Gland">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The thyroid gland is a butterfly-shaped organ located in the neck, responsible for producing hormones that regulate metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;The thyroid gland is an endocrine gland located in the neck, which can be affected by cancers and treatments impacting the head and neck region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pituitary Gland">
      <data key="d0">Pituitary Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The pituitary gland is a crucial gland in the endocrine system located at the base of the brain that oversees several hormonal functions that can be impacted by cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Audiogram">
      <data key="d0">Audiogram</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An audiogram is a chart that represents an individual's hearing ability, useful for assessing hearing changes due to head and neck cancers or their treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal cancer (NPC) is a type of cancer that originates in the nasopharynx and is characterized by specific staging and treatment considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of cancer arising in the nasopharynx that is often related to the Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) and may require specific testing for diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1 Testing">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1 Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 testing is conducted to determine the presence of the PD-L1 protein on cancer cells, which influences treatment decisions involving immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor HPV Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor HPV Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor HPV testing identifies the presence of human papillomavirus (HPV) in tumor samples, which may be related to certain cancers, including throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H/dMMR Mutation">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H/dMMR Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H/dMMR mutation refers to a genetic condition in which some cancers have DNA mutations that prevent errors from being corrected, leading to instability in microsatellites.&lt;SEP&gt;The MSI-H/dMMR mutation indicates a type of genetic instability in cancers that can affect treatment options and outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped structures that are part of the lymphatic system and play a crucial role in the immune response and the spread of cancer. They can be sites for metastasis in various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM Staging System">
      <data key="d0">TNM Staging System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM staging system categorizes cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis to other organs.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM staging system is a standardized classification used to describe the extent of cancer spread based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM staging system is a widely used method to describe the extent of cancer spread, based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and distant metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC)">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC) is a type of cancer that originates in the nasopharynx, requiring specific staging methods for treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Stage">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The surgical stage is another term for the pathologic stage, providing vital details about cancer after surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical assessment involves tests and evaluations performed to determine the stage of cancer before treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Size of Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Size of Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The size of the tumor is a crucial factor in cancer staging, affecting prognosis and treatment decisions, measured in centimeters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Decisions">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Decisions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment decisions are made based on cancer staging, prognosis, and individual patient circumstances, guiding the therapeutic approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Papillomavirus (HPV)">
      <data key="d0">Human Papillomavirus (HPV)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV is a common sexually transmitted infection known for its association with the development of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV is a viral infection that is the most prevalent sexually transmitted infection and is a primary cause of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Human papillomavirus is a sexually transmitted virus associated with various cancers, including anal cancer and may lead to long-term health concerns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ectocervix">
      <data key="d0">Ectocervix</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The ectocervix is the outer part of the cervix and is composed of squamous cells that can undergo dysplastic changes leading to cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The ectocervix is the outer part of the cervix that features squamous cells and is where many cervical cancers start.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocervix">
      <data key="d0">Endocervix</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The endocervix is the inner part of the cervix that connects the vagina to the uterus and secretes mucus.&lt;SEP&gt;The endocervix is the inner part of the cervix, which connects to the uterus and secretes mucus that plays roles in reproduction and menstrual cycles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Dysplasia">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Dysplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical dysplasia refers to abnormal changes in cervical cells that may lead to cervical cancer if not treated effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical dysplasia refers to abnormal changes in the cells on the surface of the cervix which may progress to cervical cancer if untreated.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical dysplasia refers to areas of abnormal cells found on the cervix, indicating potential pre-cancerous changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM Staging">
      <data key="d0">TNM Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TNM staging is a system used to describe the extent of cancer, notably for solid tumors, evaluating Tumor size (T), lymph Node involvement (N), and Metastasis (M).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenosquamous Carcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Adenosquamous Carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenosquamous carcinomas are cancers containing both squamous and glandular cells, referred to as 'mixed' tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix">
      <data key="d0">Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuroendocrine carcinoma of the cervix (NECC) is a rare and aggressive form of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaccination">
      <data key="d0">Vaccination</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaccination against HPV can prevent infection by various strains of HPV associated with higher cancer risks, especially recommended for adolescents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (HSIL)">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (HSIL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (HSIL) refers to moderate or severe dysplasia of cervical cells and is considered a pre-cancerous condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (LSIL)">
      <data key="d0">Low-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (LSIL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (LSIL) indicates mild dysplasia (CIN 1) and is often monitored for progression to higher grades of dysplasia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIN 1">
      <data key="d0">CIN 1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CIN 1 refers to mild dysplasia identified in cervical cells, classified under low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesions (LSIL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIN 2">
      <data key="d0">CIN 2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CIN 2 indicates moderate dysplasia and is associated with higher risks of progression to cervical cancer if left untreated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIN 3">
      <data key="d0">CIN 3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CIN 3 represents severe dysplasia and is closely monitored due to its high likelihood of advancing to cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaccine">
      <data key="d0">Vaccine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The vaccine against HPV helps prevent infection by targeting various strains that increase the risk of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A cervical biopsy is a medical procedure for diagnosing cervical cancer by removing tissue samples from the cervix for examination.&lt;SEP&gt;A cervical biopsy is a procedure where tissue samples are taken from the cervix to diagnose cervical cancer or precancerous conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical biopsy is a diagnostic procedure to test for dysplasia and cancer by removing samples of cervical tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical cancer treatment protocols vary depending on the type and stage of cancer, focusing on eradicating cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cone Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cone Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A cone biopsy is a procedure used to diagnose and treat cervical cancer, involving the removal of a cone-shaped section of tissue from the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone biopsy is a procedure that removes a cone-shaped section of the cervix to treat small cervical cancers or gather further information.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone biopsy is a surgical procedure that removes a cone-shaped piece of cervical tissue for diagnostic and therapeutic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone biopsy is a surgical procedure that removes a cone-shaped piece of tissue from the cervix for diagnostic and treatment purposes, often used for early-stage cervical cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone biopsy is both a test and treatment option for cervical cancer, involving the removal of a cone-shaped section of the cervix to assess cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cold Knife Conization">
      <data key="d0">Cold Knife Conization</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cold Knife Conization is a technique used in cone biopsy, where a surgical scalpel is employed to remove cervical tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEEP">
      <data key="d0">LEEP</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">LEEP (Loop Electrosurgical Excision Procedure) is a method for cone biopsy that uses a thin electrified wire to remove cervical tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computed Tomography (CT) Scan">
      <data key="d0">Computed Tomography (CT) Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A CT scan is an imaging procedure that provides detailed images of areas inside the body to assess the extent of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scans provide detailed cross-sectional images of the body and can be an alternative to MRI for diagnosing brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Positron Emission Tomography (PET) Scan">
      <data key="d0">Positron Emission Tomography (PET) Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET scan is an imaging test that uses radiotracers to visualize cancer cells, often combined with a CT scan for better clarity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocervical Curettage">
      <data key="d0">Endocervical Curettage</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocervical curettage is a procedure performed to scrape tissue samples from the cervical canal, often done after a cone biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Examination">
      <data key="d0">Physical Examination</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A physical examination is a clinical assessment of a patient’s body condition, including vital signs and a pelvic exam for cervical cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Tests">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver function tests assess the state of the liver by measuring enzyme levels, which can indicate liver health and cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Plan">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A cancer treatment plan is a strategy established by healthcare providers to address a patient's specific cancer diagnosis, including surgeries and therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET">
      <data key="d0">PET</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Positron Emission Tomography (PET) is an imaging test that uses radiotracers to visualize metabolic activity in tissues, particularly for detecting cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cervix">
      <data key="d0">cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The cervix is the lower part of the uterus that opens into the vagina, and is involved in various medical imaging procedures to assess health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="vagina">
      <data key="d0">vagina</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The vagina is a muscular tube that connects the external genitals to the uterus, and is examined during various medical imaging procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="uterus">
      <data key="d0">uterus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The uterus is a female reproductive organ that can be imaged using advanced techniques like MRI to assess for cancer or other conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="small cell neuroendocrine carcinoma of the cervix">
      <data key="d0">small cell neuroendocrine carcinoma of the cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell neuroendocrine carcinoma of the cervix (NECC) is a rare type of cervical cancer that warrants specific imaging recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="transvaginal ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">transvaginal ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Transvaginal ultrasound is a procedure that uses sound waves to create images of the female reproductive organs, often providing clear views of the cervix and surroundings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ovarian transposition">
      <data key="d0">ovarian transposition</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian transposition is a surgical procedure to relocate the ovaries to protect them from radiation during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="oocyte preservation">
      <data key="d0">oocyte preservation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Oocyte preservation involves freezing unfertilized eggs for future use, often discussed in the context of fertility preservation during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Cystoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cystoscopy is a medical procedure that allows doctors to see inside the bladder using a cystoscope.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the urethra and bladder for signs of cancer and to potentially remove tissue samples for biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure that allows doctors to visually examine the bladder using a thin tube with a camera, helping diagnose and determine the extent of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure that utilizes a viewing device inserted through the urethra to inspect the bladder for cancer or other conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure where a camera is inserted into the bladder to visualize and potentially biopsy abnormal areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Proctoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Proctoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Proctoscopy is a procedure used to examine the inside of the rectum and anus using a proctoscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SmokeFree.gov">
      <data key="d0">SmokeFree.gov</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">SmokeFree.gov is a resource for individuals seeking support to quit smoking or vaping, especially important for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;SmokeFree.gov is a website that provides resources and information for individuals looking to quit smoking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BeTobaccoFree.gov">
      <data key="d0">BeTobaccoFree.gov</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">BeTobaccoFree.gov offers guidance and resources to assist individuals in quitting tobacco use.&lt;SEP&gt;BeTobaccoFree.gov offers support and guidance for quitting tobacco use, emphasizing public health initiatives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility-Sparing Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Fertility-Sparing Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility-sparing treatment options are available for patients with early cervical cancer, allowing them to retain the possibility of future pregnancies.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility-sparing treatment refers to medical methods used to preserve reproductive function in patients undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility-sparing treatment refers to surgical procedures aimed at preserving reproductive capability while treating early-stage cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surrogacy">
      <data key="d0">Surrogacy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surrogacy is an arrangement where a surrogate carries a pregnancy for another individual or couple, often discussed in terms of fertility preservation options after cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reproductive Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d0">Reproductive Endocrinologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A reproductive endocrinologist is a medical specialist who focuses on infertility and reproductive health, crucial for patients considering fertility preservation.&lt;SEP&gt;Reproductive endocrinologists are specialists in fertility and hormonal disorders who may provide guidance for patients considering fertility-sparing options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing Under Anesthesia">
      <data key="d0">Testing Under Anesthesia</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Examinations under anesthesia (EUAs) are procedures performed while a patient is sedated, used for deeper evaluation and diagnostics in cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cystoscope">
      <data key="d0">Cystoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A cystoscope is a medical instrument used during cystoscopy to visualize the bladder and urinary tract.&lt;SEP&gt;A cystoscope is a medical instrument used in cystoscopy to visualize the bladder and urethra, allowing for the removal of tissue samples or tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Proctoscope">
      <data key="d0">Proctoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A proctoscope is an instrument used in proctoscopy to examine the rectum and anus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO)">
      <data key="d0">International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">FIGO is an international organization responsible for developing staging systems for various gynecological cancers, including cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;FIGO is an organization that provides a staging system for cervical cancer to determine the extent of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minimally Invasive Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Minimally Invasive Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Minimally invasive surgery involves making small cuts for procedures and is associated with reduced pain and shorter recovery times.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d0">Trachelectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Trachelectomy is a type of surgery that removes the cervix and is intended to preserve fertility by leaving the uterus intact.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hysterectomy is a surgical procedure for removing the uterus, frequently considered in cases of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the uterus and can also include the cervix, with different types depending on cancer stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the uterus and possibly other reproductive organs, often considered for cervical cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the uterus, often recommended for later-stage cervical cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIGO System">
      <data key="d0">FIGO System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The FIGO system is a staging system used to assess the extent of cervical cancer and inform treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT)">
      <data key="d0">Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IMRT is an advanced radiation technique that allows precise targeting of cancerous tumors while minimizing damage to surrounding tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT is an advanced technique of radiation therapy that allows radiation oncologists to target cancer more precisely while minimizing damage to surrounding healthy tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT is an advanced technique of radiation therapy that targets cancerous tumors while minimizing damage to adjacent healthy tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy is an advanced form of radiation therapy that allows for high doses of radiation to be directed at cancer while minimizing exposure to surrounding healthy tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy is an advanced technique used in radiation therapy that allows for precise targeting of cancer while sparing healthy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">SBRT is a precise form of radiation therapy that targets tumors with minimal damage to surrounding tissues, used for certain cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy is a highly precise radiation treatment that delivers high doses to targeted tumors, primarily in metastatic cases of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy is a specialized form of EBRT used to treat metastatic cervical cancer, delivering high doses of radiation precisely to tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT)">
      <data key="d0">Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Menopausal Hormone Therapy is used to alleviate side effects caused by premature menopause due to radiation treatment for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic nodes are lymph nodes found near the cervix and are usually the first ones affected by cervical cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelvic nodes are the lymph nodes near the cervix that are often the first to be affected by cervical cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Para-aortic Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Para-aortic Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Para-aortic nodes are lymph nodes located in the abdomen that may also be removed during cervical cancer treatment and staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Para-aortic nodes are lymph nodes located in the abdomen that may be involved in the spread of cervical cancer and can also be removed during surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Atrophy">
      <data key="d0">Atrophy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Atrophy is a medical condition involving the thinning, drying, and inflammation of the vaginal lining due to insufficient estrogen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen is a hormone that plays a key role in women's health; low levels can lead to long-term risks such as cardiovascular disease and osteoporosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cardiovascular Disease">
      <data key="d0">Cardiovascular Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cardiovascular disease refers to a broad range of conditions affecting the heart and blood vessels, often influenced by hormonal factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Osteoporosis">
      <data key="d0">Osteoporosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteoporosis is a condition characterized by weak and brittle bones, often resulting from hormonal changes or deficiencies, such as low estrogen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EBRT">
      <data key="d0">EBRT</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a common treatment for cancer, used to target and destroy cancerous cells through external radiation exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a form of radiation treatment used to target tumors in various locations.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a method of delivering high-energy rays from outside the body to kill cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;External beam radiation therapy (EBRT) is a common treatment for cervical cancer where radiation is directed at the cancerous area from outside the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Dose-Rate Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">High Dose-Rate Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High dose-rate (HDR) brachytherapy is a method of brachytherapy that delivers radiation in short bursts to minimize exposure to surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Dose-Rate Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Low Dose-Rate Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low dose-rate (LDR) brachytherapy involves delivering radiation over a longer duration, usually beneficial for specific cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intracavitary Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intracavitary Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intracavitary brachytherapy specifically involves placing radioactive sources inside the cervix, uterus, and vagina to treat cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Intracavitary brachytherapy specifically targets the cervix and uterus by placing radioactive sources inside these organs to treat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Dose-Rate Brachytherapy (HDR)">
      <data key="d0">High Dose-Rate Brachytherapy (HDR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HDR brachytherapy is a technique where radiation is delivered in high doses over short periods, aimed at treating cervical cancer effectively while sparing surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Dose-Rate Brachytherapy (LDR)">
      <data key="d0">Low Dose-Rate Brachytherapy (LDR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LDR brachytherapy involves the gradual delivery of radiation over a longer time frame, appropriate for certain cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interstitial Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Interstitial Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Interstitial brachytherapy is a specialized technique where radioactive material is placed directly within or next to the tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Cycle">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Cycle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A chemotherapy cycle is a treatment schedule that consists of a period of drug administration followed by a rest period, allowing the body to recover.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Side Effects of Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Side Effects of Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Side effects of chemotherapy vary but commonly include nausea, fatigue, hair loss, and an increased risk of infection, due to the impact on healthy and cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Cancer Institute (NCI)">
      <data key="d0">National Cancer Institute (NCI)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCI is the U.S. government's principal agency for cancer research and training, providing extensive information and resources for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCI is a part of the U.S. National Institutes of Health and is involved in cancer research and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a component of the U.S. National Institutes of Health, focused on cancer research and providing information about clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a part of the U.S. government focused on cancer research and providing information about cancer treatments, including clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute is a component of the U.S. government that conducts and supports cancer research, including clinical trial information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase trials are stages of clinical trials that test drugs for dosing, safety, efficacy, and long-term effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mouth Sores">
      <data key="d0">Mouth Sores</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mouth sores are painful lesions that can occur as a side effect of chemotherapy treatments, including those with Cisplatin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hair Loss">
      <data key="d0">Hair Loss</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hair loss is a common side effect of chemotherapy that can affect patients undergoing cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fatigue">
      <data key="d0">Fatigue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fatigue can manifest as physical, cognitive, or emotional exhaustion in patients suffering from brain tumors and their symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatigue is a prevalent symptom experienced by patients undergoing chemotherapy, often affecting their daily life and treatment adherence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Increased Risk of Infection">
      <data key="d0">Increased Risk of Infection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Increased risk of infection reflects a significant concern for chemotherapy patients, as their immune systems may be compromised.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bleeding or Bruising">
      <data key="d0">Bleeding or Bruising</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bleeding or bruising easily can occur in chemotherapy patients due to platelet count reduction from certain drugs like Cisplatin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nerve Damage (Neuropathy)">
      <data key="d0">Nerve Damage (Neuropathy)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nerve damage, also known as neuropathy, can be a side effect of chemotherapy, affecting the patient's sensory and motor functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ringing in the Ears (Tinnitus)">
      <data key="d0">Ringing in the Ears (Tinnitus)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ringing in the ears, or tinnitus, can occur due to Cisplatin treatment, indicating potential hearing impairment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Damage">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Damage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidney damage is a potential side effect of Cisplatin, particularly in patients with pre-existing kidney issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment team">
      <data key="d0">Treatment team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The treatment team consists of healthcare providers who guide cancer patients through their treatment options and manage their care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Investigation of New Treatments">
      <data key="d0">Investigation of New Treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Investigation of new treatments refers to the process of developing and testing novel drugs, devices, or therapies in clinical settings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Treatments">
      <data key="d0">Standard Treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard treatments refer to established therapies that have been widely accepted as effective for specific types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenosquamous Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenosquamous Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenosquamous carcinoma is a rare form of cervical cancer that contains both glandular and squamous cell characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Trachelectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radical trachelectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the cervix and surrounding tissue, used in the treatment of cervical cancer while preserving the uterus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphovascular Space Invasion (LVSI)">
      <data key="d0">Lymphovascular Space Invasion (LVSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LVSI indicates the presence of tumor cells within blood vessels or lymphatics, influencing treatment decisions in cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-stage Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-stage Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage cancer refers to cancer that is localized and has not spread significantly, making it typically more treatable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locally Advanced Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Locally Advanced Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locally advanced cancer is a classification indicating that the cancer has spread to nearby structures or lymph nodes but has not metastasized distantly.&lt;SEP&gt;Locally advanced cancer refers to cervical cancers that have grown beyond the cervix but have not metastasized to distant organs, requiring specific treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurrence describes the return of cancer after a period of remission, necessitating further treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to cancer that has returned after treatment, potentially requiring further intervention.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to the return of cancer after initial treatment, which may happen locally or metastatically.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1A1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1A1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A1 is an early stage of cervical cancer where the tumor is small and has not spread, often treated with cone biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1A2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1A2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A2 is a slightly more advanced early-stage cervical cancer with specific criteria regarding tumor size and lymphovascular invasion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1B1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1B1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1B1 indicates a larger early-stage cervical cancer with lymphovascular considerations, typically treated with cone biopsy or trachelectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1B2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1B2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1B2 represents an advanced form of early-stage cervical cancer, where fertility-sparing options are generally not recommended.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Lymph Node Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Lymph Node Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic lymph node evaluation is a diagnostic procedure to determine if cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaginal Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Vaginal Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaginal brachytherapy is a form of internal radiation treatment aimed at the cervix, often used in conjunction with EBRT.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Observation">
      <data key="d0">Observation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Observation involves monitoring a patient closely after treatment, typically without immediate additional interventions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum chemotherapy is a specific class of chemotherapy often used in conjunction with other treatments for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum chemotherapy is a type of cancer treatment that uses platinum-based drugs and is often administered alongside radiation in advanced cervical cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1A">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A is a classification of cervical cancer indicating that the cancer is invasive but remains small and localized within the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2A">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2A is a classification of cervical cancer indicating that the cancer has spread beyond the cervix but not to the pelvic wall or lower third of the vagina.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic lymph nodes are key structures examined in cervical cancer treatment to determine whether the cancer has spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Staging">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical staging is a process in which lymph nodes are surgically removed and tested to assess the spread of cancer during treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extrafascial Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Extrafascial Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extrafascial hysterectomy is a surgical procedure commonly recommended for women with certain stages of cervical cancer and involves the removal of the uterus while preserving the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified Radical Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Modified Radical Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Modified radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that may be recommended for cervical cancer when more extensive intervention is necessary, involving the removal of the uterus and surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node evaluation is a critical diagnostic step during hysterectomy procedures for cervical cancer to determine if cancer has spread to lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LVSI">
      <data key="d0">LVSI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphovascular space invasion (LVSI) is a significant factor in cervical cancer staging that indicates the presence of cancer within lymphatic or vascular spaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure where the uterus, surrounding tissues, and sometimes other organs are removed, used in treating certain cancer cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Radical hysterectomy is a surgical treatment for cervical cancer that involves the removal of the uterus, cervix, surrounding tissue, and sometimes lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC)">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NECC is a rare type of cervical cancer characterized by small neuroendocrine cells, known for its aggressive nature and specific treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-up Visits">
      <data key="d0">Follow-up Visits</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up visits are appointments after cancer treatment to discuss health changes and evaluate potential recurrence, often adjusted based on individual risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pap Tests">
      <data key="d0">Pap Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pap tests, or cervical cytology, are screenings performed to detect abnormal cells in the cervix, often part of post-treatment monitoring strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Elective Treatment Options">
      <data key="d0">Elective Treatment Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Elective treatment options are various treatment strategies considered based on individual patient needs, particularly after cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploratory Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Exploratory Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Exploratory surgery is a procedure to investigate the extent of cancer spread, guiding further treatment decisions when recurrence is suspected.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Exenteration Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Exenteration Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic exenteration surgery is a radical procedure that involves removing multiple organs in the pelvis to cure cancer by eliminating all affected tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ostomy Bag">
      <data key="d0">Ostomy Bag</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">An ostomy bag is a collection device used to collect waste from the body after surgical diversion of the urinary or bowel systems.&lt;SEP&gt;An ostomy bag is a pouch worn outside the body to collect waste after diversion surgeries, commonly required following procedures like pelvic exenteration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Exenteration">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Exenteration</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic exenteration is a radical surgical procedure that involves the removal of multiple pelvic organs, often performed to treat severe cases of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ileal Conduit">
      <data key="d0">Ileal Conduit</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">An ileal conduit is a type of urinary diversion created after bladder removal that allows urine to exit the body through a stoma created in the abdominal wall.&lt;SEP&gt;Ileal Conduit is a type of urinary diversion involving using a section of the small intestine to create a tube for urine to exit the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Double-Barreled Wet Colostomy (DBWC)">
      <data key="d0">Double-Barreled Wet Colostomy (DBWC)</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">DBWC is a technique where both urinary and fecal diversions occur through a single stoma, allowing for separate discharge of urine and stool.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma (MGZL) is a type of lymphoma with overlapping features of primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma and Hodgkin lymphoma, primarily treated with chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Mediastinal B-cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary Mediastinal B-cell Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma (PMBL) is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that is primarily managed with chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palliative Care">
      <data key="d0">Palliative Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Palliative care is a specialized medical care focused on providing relief from symptoms and stress of serious illness, often used in metastatic cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tisotumab vedotin-tftv">
      <data key="d0">Tisotumab vedotin-tftv</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tisotumab vedotin-tftv, known as Tivdak, is an antibody-drug conjugate used for treating certain cervical cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cemiplimab">
      <data key="d0">Cemiplimab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cemiplimab, marketed as Libtayo, is another immune checkpoint inhibitor approved for certain cancers, including some types of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Immunotherapy Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Immunotherapy Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide detailed information on managing side effects and considerations for immunotherapy treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped gland in the front of the neck responsible for producing hormones that regulate various body functions, including metabolism and heart rate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid cancer includes various types of cancers affecting the thyroid gland, which can be treated through surgeries and other therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the thyroid gland, and its treatment may involve TSH suppression and radioactive iodine therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer refers to cancer that forms in the thyroid gland, which is located in the neck and regulates metabolism through hormone production.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer refers to malignant growths in the thyroid gland, often starting as nodules and characterized by symptoms such as lumps in the neck or trouble swallowing.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer refers to several types of cancer that originate in the thyroid gland, which can be either treated or monitored based on their growth rate and symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Nodules">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Nodules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid nodules are small, often round areas of abnormal growth within the thyroid gland, predominantly benign and usually asymptomatic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cowden Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Cowden Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cowden syndrome, also known as PTEN hamartoma tumor syndrome, is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing thyroid and other cancers due to genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Older Persons">
      <data key="d0">Older Persons</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Older persons are identified as a demographic group that has a higher rate of conditions such as bone marrow involvement and advanced-stage diseases, particularly in the context of lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphomas">
      <data key="d0">Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mediastinal gray zone lymphomas (MGZL) are a type of cancer that exhibits features of both classical Hodgkin lymphoma and non-Hodgkin lymphoma, complicating treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DLBCL">
      <data key="d0">DLBCL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL) is a common type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that requires distinct diagnostic criteria when found outside of the mediastinum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemoimmunotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Chemoimmunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemoimmunotherapy is a treatment approach that combines chemotherapy with immunotherapy agents such as rituximab to enhance treatment efficacy in certain cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Hormones">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Hormones</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid hormones (T3 and T4) are critical substances produced by the thyroid gland that regulate a variety of metabolic processes in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iodine">
      <data key="d0">Iodine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Iodine is a mineral essential for the production of thyroid hormones, found in certain foods and iodized salt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parathyroid Glands">
      <data key="d0">Parathyroid Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The parathyroid glands are small glands located behind the thyroid that regulate calcium levels in the bloodstream, influencing overall metabolic health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Personal and Family History">
      <data key="d0">Personal and Family History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A person's or family's past medical history can significantly influence the risk of developing thyroid cancer, particularly for hereditary syndromes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Hormone">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Hormone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid hormone is produced by the thyroid gland and regulates metabolism, influencing various bodily functions. Low levels indicate hypothyroidism, while high levels indicate hyperthyroidism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Nodule">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Nodule</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A thyroid nodule is a growth in the thyroid gland that can be benign or malignant. The evaluation of nodules is crucial for diagnosing potential thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Papillary Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary carcinoma is the most common type of thyroid cancer, characterized by slow growth and better prognosis as compared to other types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular carcinoma is the second most common type of thyroid cancer, generally presenting a similar slow-growth profile to papillary carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anaplastic thyroid cancer is a rare, aggressive form of thyroid cancer often affecting older adults, with a poor prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobectomy">
      <data key="d0">Lobectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobectomy is a surgical procedure to remove a lobe of the thyroid gland containing cancerous nodules, frequently used for small, low-risk cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure to remove part of the thyroid gland, which may be appropriate for small, low-risk thyroid cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure where a lobe of the thyroid gland is removed, often being a treatment option for less invasive thyroid conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure where a lobe of the thyroid gland is removed, often performed on patients with thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure where a part of the thyroid is removed, used as a treatment option for certain low-risk cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Total Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d0">Total Thyroidectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Total Thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the entire thyroid gland, commonly recommended for patients with papillary thyroid cancer that has spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy involves the complete removal of the thyroid gland and is a common treatment for thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy is a procedure that removes the entire thyroid gland, often necessary for aggressive types of thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure that involves removing the entire thyroid gland, often necessitated for more serious thyroid cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the entire thyroid gland, typically indicated for aggressive thyroid cancers or larger tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy refers to treatments aimed at restoring adequate hormone levels in patients with thyroid deficiency, often involving medications like Levoxyl or Synthroid.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid hormone replacement therapy involves the administration of thyroid hormones post-surgery to maintain normal levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid hormone replacement therapy is a treatment that involves administering levothyroxine to replace or supplement thyroid hormones in patients who have undergone thyroid surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid hormone replacement therapy is critical for patients post-total thyroidectomy to compensate for the loss of hormone production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medullary Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Medullary Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medullary thyroid cancer can be inherited and is another type of thyroid cancer that requires special attention due to its distinct genetic patterns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Levoxyl">
      <data key="d0">Levoxyl</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Levoxyl is a medication commonly used for thyroid hormone replacement therapy, aimed at maintaining normal levels of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Synthroid">
      <data key="d0">Synthroid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Synthroid is also a medication for thyroid hormone replacement, similar in function to Levoxyl, used to manage thyroid hormone levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Levothyroxine">
      <data key="d0">Levothyroxine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Levothyroxine is a medication used to replace thyroid hormone for individuals who have had a thyroidectomy or low thyroid hormone levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Levothyroxine is a synthetic form of thyroid hormone used to treat thyroid hormone deficiency, typically taken as a daily pill.&lt;SEP&gt;Levothyroxine is a synthetic thyroid hormone used in hormone replacement therapy to maintain normal levels of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) following thyroid surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive iodine therapy is a treatment for thyroid cancer that utilizes iodine-131 to target and kill thyroid cells, including cancerous ones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyrotropin Alfa (Thyrogen)">
      <data key="d0">Thyrotropin Alfa (Thyrogen)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyrotropin Alfa, known as Thyrogen, is a medication used to stimulate TSH levels in patients undergoing treatment for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d0">Endocrinologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An endocrinologist is a medical doctor specializing in hormonal disorders, often involved in treating thyroid cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;An endocrinologist is a medical specialist focusing on hormone-related disorders, including thyroid conditions and cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nuclear Medicine Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Nuclear Medicine Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nuclear medicine specialist is a physician who uses radioactive materials for diagnosis and therapy, including thyroid treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH)">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH) is a hormone produced by the pituitary gland that regulates thyroid hormone levels in the body, crucial for metabolism and growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) is produced by the pituitary gland and plays a crucial role in regulating thyroid hormone levels, monitored in cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anxiety">
      <data key="d0">Anxiety</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anxiety is a common side effect experienced by patients receiving thyroid hormone replacement therapy, potentially stemming from hormonal imbalance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Racing Heart">
      <data key="d0">Racing Heart</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Racing heart refers to a condition that may occur as a side effect of improper dosing of thyroid hormone, leading to increased heart rates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Calcium Supplements">
      <data key="d0">Calcium Supplements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Calcium supplements are often recommended for patients undergoing thyroid cancer treatment to help strengthen bones affected by hormone therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitamin D Supplements">
      <data key="d0">Vitamin D Supplements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitamin D supplements may be suggested alongside calcium to support bone health during and after thyroid cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d0">Thyroidectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of all or part of the thyroid gland, often performed in patients with thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. FDA">
      <data key="d0">U.S. FDA</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is responsible for approving new drugs, devices, and treatment methods for safety and effectiveness in cancer therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) oversees the approval and regulation of pharmaceuticals and medical procedures, including those used in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma (PTC)">
      <data key="d0">Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma (PTC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma (PTC) is the most common type of thyroid cancer, often seen with good treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI)">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI) is a treatment used after thyroid surgery to eliminate remaining cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive iodine therapy is a treatment used to destroy remaining thyroid cancer cells after surgical removal of most of the thyroid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transportation Costs">
      <data key="d0">Transportation Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transportation costs may arise during participation in a clinical trial, as participants may need to travel for study-related appointments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Childcare Costs">
      <data key="d0">Childcare Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Childcare costs refer to expenses incurred by participants in a clinical trial for care during study-related appointments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Standard Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard Cancer Care includes the regular treatment provided to cancer patients, which is billed and may be partially covered by insurance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nuclear Medicine Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Nuclear Medicine Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nuclear medicine doctor specializes in using radioactive materials for diagnosis and treatment, relevant for thyroid cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAI Therapy">
      <data key="d0">RAI Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">RAI therapy is a targeted treatment for thyroid cancer that involves administering radioactive iodine to destroy cancerous thyroid cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy is a treatment for thyroid cancer that involves using radioactive iodine to destroy cancerous tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy is used to destroy remaining cancer cells after thyroid removal surgeries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Differentiated Thyroid Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Differentiated thyroid cancers include papillary, follicular, and oncocytic types, which generally have positive treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Papillary Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary thyroid cancer (PTC) is the most prevalent type of thyroid cancer with favorable prognosis outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular thyroid carcinoma (FTC) is the second most common type of thyroid cancer, known for its potential to invade blood vessels and require specific treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Oncocytic Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncocytic carcinoma, formerly known as Hürthle cell carcinoma (HCC), is a less common type of thyroid cancer characterized by aggressive behavior and a higher likelihood of lymph node involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIFTP">
      <data key="d0">NIFTP</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NIFTP (Noninvasive Follicular Thyroid Neoplasm with Papillary-like Nuclear Features) is a low-risk, noninvasive thyroid tumor that doesn't require further treatment following surgical removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Completion Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d0">Completion Thyroidectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Completion thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure recommended when concerning or high-risk features are found during the examination of a thyroid tumor, necessitating the removal of the remaining thyroid tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroglobulin (Tg) Level">
      <data key="d0">Thyroglobulin (Tg) Level</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The thyroglobulin (Tg) level is a blood test marker used to evaluate the presence of remaining thyroid cancer cells following thyroid surgery and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologic Examination">
      <data key="d0">Pathologic Examination</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologic examination involves studying the tissue removed during surgery to determine the presence and characteristics of thyroid tumors or cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TSH">
      <data key="d0">TSH</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) is a hormone produced by the pituitary gland that regulates thyroid function and is measured in blood tests for cancer monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroglobulin (Tg)">
      <data key="d0">Thyroglobulin (Tg)</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroglobulin is a protein produced by thyroid tissue, and its levels in the blood are used as a tumor marker for thyroid cancer surveillance.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroglobulin is a protein that indicates active thyroid tissue; elevated levels post-surgery may suggest cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neck Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Neck Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Neck ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging procedure used to monitor the thyroid gland and detect any abnormalities or recurrence of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Neck ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging test used to visualize the thyroid gland and surrounding tissues to detect potential cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lenvatinib (Lenvima)">
      <data key="d0">Lenvatinib (Lenvima)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Lenvatinib is a targeted oral medication for advanced thyroid cancer that inhibits tumor growth by blocking specific mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Lenvatinib is a targeted therapy medication used to treat advanced thyroid cancer that cannot be surgically removed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sorafenib (Nexavar)">
      <data key="d0">Sorafenib (Nexavar)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Sorafenib is another targeted therapy option for patients with advanced thyroid cancer that is not amenable to surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Sorafenib is another targeted therapy used for treating advanced thyroid cancer, effective against specific genetic alterations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cabozantinib (Cabometyx)">
      <data key="d0">Cabozantinib (Cabometyx)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Cabozantinib is a targeted therapy for advanced thyroid cancer, used when other treatments have failed or cancer progresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Cabozantinib is recommended for patients whose thyroid cancer worsens after treatment with other targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Differentiated High-Grade Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Differentiated High-Grade Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Differentiated high-grade carcinoma is a type of cancer that typically arises in the thyroid and is known for aggressive behavior and poor prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monitoring and Follow-Up Care">
      <data key="d0">Monitoring and Follow-Up Care</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Monitoring and follow-up care involves regular testing and examinations to track a patient's recovery and identify any recurrence of cancer post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-Body RAI Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Whole-Body RAI Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Whole-body radioactive iodine imaging is a diagnostic procedure used to locate cancer spread after treatment with RAI therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anti-Thyroglobulin Antibodies (Tg ab)">
      <data key="d0">Anti-Thyroglobulin Antibodies (Tg ab)</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">Anti-thyroglobulin antibodies are immune proteins that can interfere with the detection of thyroglobulin in patients who have undergone thyroid treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NED (No Evidence of Disease)">
      <data key="d0">NED (No Evidence of Disease)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NED is the term used when no signs of cancer are detected in a patient who has undergone treatment, indicating a successful outcome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Long-Term Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Long-Term Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Long-term surveillance consists of ongoing monitoring through exams and tests to ensure that no cancer recurrence occurs over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for Biomarkers">
      <data key="d0">Testing for Biomarkers</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for biomarkers involves analyzing cancer cells for specific genetic changes that can inform targeted therapy options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients®">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients®</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® is a trusted resource that provides clear care recommendations made by experts in the field of cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® provide detailed patient guidance on cancer treatment and related issues, emphasizing patient choices and care teams.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® provide structured recommendations and treatment options for cancer care, including bladder cancer and anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bone Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone cancer is a group of cancers that originate in the bone or cartilage, with distinctions made between primary and secondary types.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone cancer refers to malignancies originating in bone tissue, and it can be diagnosed through various testing methods including biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone cancer refers to malignancies that occur in the bones, often requiring specialized testing and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone cancer refers to malignant tumors that form in the bone tissue, with specific staging and grading systems to assess their severity and spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone cancer refers to various types of cancer that occur in the bones, including different subtypes that require specific treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Osteosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Osteosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteosarcoma is a common type of bone cancer that arises in the cells that form bone tissue, often occurring near growth plates.&lt;SEP&gt;Osteosarcoma is the most common type of bone cancer and typically requires a combination of therapies for treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ewing Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Ewing Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ewing sarcoma is a type of bone cancer that begins in the bone marrow and commonly affects long bones or flat bones like the pelvis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ewing sarcoma is a type of bone cancer that begins in the bones or in soft tissue around the bones and is usually treated with systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chordoma">
      <data key="d0">Chordoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chordoma is a rare bone cancer that develops in notochordal cells in the spine, commonly found in the sacrum or base of the skull.&lt;SEP&gt;Chordoma is a type of bone cancer that can be treated with surgery or radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chondrosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Chondrosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chondrosarcoma is a type of bone cancer that is typically treated with surgery to remove the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Chondrosarcoma starts in the cells forming cartilage and is one of the common variants of bone cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Giant Cell Tumor of Bone">
      <data key="d0">Giant Cell Tumor of Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Giant cell tumor of bone (GCTB) is generally not cancerous but requires aggressive treatment to prevent bone damage.&lt;SEP&gt;The Giant Cell Tumor of Bone (GCTB) is a type of bone tumor characterized by overgrowth of cells in the metaphysis of bones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients®: Soft Tissue Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients®: Soft Tissue Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients®: Soft Tissue Sarcoma provides detailed recommendations for the management of soft tissue sarcomas including treatment options and patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients®: Multiple Myeloma">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients®: Multiple Myeloma</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients®: Multiple Myeloma offers comprehensive guidelines on the treatment and management of multiple myeloma, a type of blood cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Guide Other Systemic Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Guide Other Systemic Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Guide Other Systemic Therapies refers to information about alternative systemic treatments that may be applicable if first-line therapies are unavailable or inappropriate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Cancer Testing">
      <data key="d0">Bone Cancer Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone cancer testing refers to the various medical tests and assessments that can help diagnose and stage different types of bone cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Patient Guidelines provide detailed recommendations and information for cancer patients regarding care and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP)">
      <data key="d0">Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ALP is an enzyme that can indicate bone tumors or liver disorders when found at high levels in the blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-ray">
      <data key="d0">X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An X-ray is a form of radiation imaging used to see inside the body, helping to detect various health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrasting Material">
      <data key="d0">Contrasting Material</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast materials are substances used in imaging tests to improve the quality of images taken of the body's internal structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bone marrow tests">
      <data key="d0">bone marrow tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow tests are diagnostic procedures that assess the health of bone marrow, usually to diagnose conditions related to blood and bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ewing sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Ewing sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ewing sarcoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the bones or soft tissue, characterized by genetic translocations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="genetic cancer risk testing">
      <data key="d0">genetic cancer risk testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic cancer risk testing identifies hereditary mutations that may increase the likelihood of developing certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="biomarker testing">
      <data key="d0">biomarker testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker testing analyzes tissue samples for DNA mutations and protein levels, providing information for cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tracer">
      <data key="d0">tracer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tracer is a radioactive substance used in PET scans to help identify cancer cells by revealing metabolic activity in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="fludeoxyglucose (FDG)">
      <data key="d0">fludeoxyglucose (FDG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fludeoxyglucose is a radiotracer used in FDG-PET scans that helps visualize cancerous tissues by highlighting areas of high sugar metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="core needle biopsy">
      <data key="d0">core needle biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core needle biopsy involves removing a small cylinder of tissue from a tumor for analysis, often guided by imaging techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="surgical biopsy">
      <data key="d0">surgical biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical biopsy involves the removal of a sample of tissue through an incision in the body, often used for diagnosing tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bone marrow aspirate">
      <data key="d0">bone marrow aspirate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow aspirate is a procedure that draws liquid from the bone marrow to assess its health and identify abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bone marrow biopsy">
      <data key="d0">bone marrow biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow biopsy collects a solid tissue sample from the bone marrow for diagnostic testing, typically involving a hollow needle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="microsatellite instability (MSI)">
      <data key="d0">microsatellite instability (MSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite instability refers to the accumulation of mutations in short, repeated sequences of DNA, which can contribute to cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="deficient mismatch repair (dMMR)">
      <data key="d0">deficient mismatch repair (dMMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deficient mismatch repair indicates a failure to correct DNA replication errors, contributing to specific cancer types, such as some colorectal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellites are short, repeated strings of DNA that can become unstable in certain cancers due to genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB-H)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB-H)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutational burden-high (TMB-H) indicates that a tumor has a high number of mutations, which may guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ivosidenib (Tibsovo)">
      <data key="d0">Ivosidenib (Tibsovo)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Ivosidenib is a targeted therapy specifically designed to treat cancers associated with IDH1 mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Ivosidenib, marketed as Tibsovo, is a targeted therapy used for cancers with IDH1 mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ossification">
      <data key="d0">Ossification</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ossification is the process of bone formation that begins in the womb and finishes during adolescence, which can be disrupted by certain types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Somatic Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Somatic Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Somatic mutation refers to genetic alterations occurring in non-germline cells that can contribute to different types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H/xMMR Mutation">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H/xMMR Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H/xMMR mutation describes a condition in cancer where errors in DNA are not repaired, resulting in increased instability in microsatellites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair (MMR) Proteins">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair (MMR) Proteins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch repair (MMR) proteins are crucial for correcting DNA replication errors, and their malfunction can lead to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genomic Aberration Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genomic Aberration Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genomic aberration testing involves examining tumor or blood samples for mutations that may impact cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IDH1 Mutation">
      <data key="d0">IDH1 Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IDH1 mutation affects cellular metabolism and is associated with certain types of cancers, making it a target for specific therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Core Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Core needle biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure used to sample tissue from tumors for diagnostic evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical biopsy is a more extensive procedure used for definitive diagnosis of cancer, involving the excision of tumor tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvis">
      <data key="d0">Pelvis</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The pelvis is a basin-shaped structure that supports the spinal column and protects abdominal organs, playing a role in cancer staging and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The pelvis is a basin-shaped structure that supports the spinal column and protects the abdominal organs, playing a vital role in the human skeletal system.&lt;SEP&gt;The pelvis is a structured location in the body that is relevant for cancer staging and can be a site for metastatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spinal Column">
      <data key="d0">Spinal Column</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The spinal column, also known as the vertebral column, comprises a flexible series of bones called vertebrae and is crucial for supporting the body and protecting the spinal cord.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Size and Spread">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Size and Spread</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor size and spread are critical factors in determining the stage of cancer, involving measurements in centimeters and assessing the involvement of lymph nodes and distant organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physis">
      <data key="d0">Physis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Physis refers to the growth plate in bones, made of cartilage and instrumental in bone growth during development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diaphysis">
      <data key="d0">Diaphysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diaphysis is the middle region of a long bone, primarily composed of compact bone, and serves as the main shaft of the bone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skeletal Maturity">
      <data key="d0">Skeletal Maturity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skeletal maturity is the stage of development when a person's bones have reached their full growth, typically resulting in a physeal scar where the growth plate has closed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regional Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Regional Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regional lymph nodes are lymph nodes located near the tumor that help filter harmful substances and play a role in cancer staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coccyx">
      <data key="d0">Coccyx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Coccyx, also known as the tailbone, is a small bone at the base of the vertebral column and an essential part of the skeletal system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Grade">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer grade describes the appearance and abnormality of cancer cells under a microscope, with implications for treatment and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer grade indicates how abnormal the cancer cells look under a microscope, which helps predict how quickly the cancer is likely to grow and spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer grade refers to how abnormal cancer cells look under a microscope and is an important factor in assessing the cancer's aggressiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer grade describes how abnormal the tumor cells appear under a microscope, with higher grades indicating more aggressive cancers that grow and spread faster.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone">
      <data key="d0">Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone is a common site for metastasis from primary cancers, indicating the spread of cancer to distant sites in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone is often a distant site for metastasis indicating the spread of cancer from its primary site to other locations in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)">
      <data key="d0">Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatocellular Carcinoma is the most common type of liver cancer arising from hepatocytes and can be aggressive in nature.&lt;SEP&gt;Hepatocellular Carcinoma is the most common type of primary liver cancer, characterized by the uncontrolled growth of liver cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma is a less common liver cancer that arises in the bile ducts within the liver.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma is the second most common type of primary liver cancer, arising from bile duct cells in the liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gallbladder">
      <data key="d0">Gallbladder</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The gallbladder is a small organ that stores and concentrates bile from the liver, essential for digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;The gallbladder is a small organ that stores bile produced by the liver and can also be involved in the biliary system related to liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The gallbladder is a small organ that stores bile produced by the liver, releasing it into the small intestine to aid in the digestion of fats.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GX">
      <data key="d0">GX</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">GX indicates that the cancer grade cannot be determined, reflecting uncertainty in the evaluation of tumor cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G1">
      <data key="d0">G1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">G1 means well-differentiated tumor cells that resemble normal cells, associated with a lower grade and generally slower growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G2">
      <data key="d0">G2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">G2 represents moderately differentiated tumor cells with intermediate characteristics in appearance and growth rates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G3">
      <data key="d0">G3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">G3 indicates poorly differentiated tumor cells that appear abnormal and tend to grow more aggressively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bile">
      <data key="d0">Bile</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bile is a digestive fluid produced by the liver and stored in the gallbladder, playing a critical role in fat digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mixed-Type Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Mixed-Type Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Mixed-Type Tumor is a rare cancer that contains both hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Liver Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic Liver Cancer refers to cancer that has spread to the liver from other locations in the body, such as the colon.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic liver cancer occurs when cancer spreads to the liver from another part of the body, highlighting the liver's role in cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatobiliary System">
      <data key="d0">Hepatobiliary System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Hepatobiliary System consists of the liver, gallbladder, and bile ducts, which play critical roles in digestion and processing nutrients, but can also be affected by cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Hepatobiliary System encompasses the liver, gallbladder, and bile ducts, crucial for bile production and digestive health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatocellular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Hepatocellular Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC) is a type of liver cancer that the document focuses on, discussing its risk factors and screening methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC) is the most common type of liver cancer, which can develop from chronic liver diseases like cirrhosis and hepatitis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cirrhosis">
      <data key="d0">Cirrhosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cirrhosis is a chronic liver disease characterized by the scarring of the liver, which can increase the risk of developing liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cirrhosis is a liver disease that increases the risk of developing liver cancer and is mentioned in the context of screening recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B Virus">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B Virus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B is a viral infection that can lead to chronic liver disease and is a known risk factor for liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis C Virus">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis C Virus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis C is another viral infection that can cause chronic liver issues and is associated with increased liver cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound (US)">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound (US)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultrasound is a recommended screening test that uses sound waves to visualize the liver to detect abnormalities associated with liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha-Fetoprotein (AFP) Test">
      <data key="d0">Alpha-Fetoprotein (AFP) Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Alpha-Fetoprotein test is a blood test that checks for elevated levels of a protein that could indicate liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Liver Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the liver, with Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC) being the most prevalent type.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver cancer refers to cancers that start in the liver, specifically the focus of discussion in this document regarding diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Screening Program">
      <data key="d0">Screening Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A liver cancer screening program involves regular tests to detect liver cancer early in individuals at high risk, significantly improving treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cirrhosis Patients">
      <data key="d0">Cirrhosis Patients</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cirrhosis patients are individuals who have developed scarring in the liver, making them particularly susceptible to liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Hepatitis B">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Hepatitis B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Hepatitis B is a long-term infection that can lead to liver damage, cirrhosis, and an increased risk of liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Hepatitis B is another condition mentioned that elevates the risk for liver cancer, emphasizing the need for screening programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Alcohol Use">
      <data key="d0">High Alcohol Use</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High alcohol use contributes to liver damage and is a known risk factor for the development of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease is a condition characterized by excess fat buildup in the liver of individuals who drink little or no alcohol, raising the risk of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Hemochromatosis">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Hemochromatosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic Hemochromatosis is an inherited disorder causing excessive iron accumulation in the liver, which can increase the risk of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Biliary Cholangitis">
      <data key="d0">Primary Biliary Cholangitis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary Biliary Cholangitis is a chronic liver disease that damages the bile ducts, potentially leading to cirrhosis and increased risk for liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha-1-Antitrypsin Deficiency">
      <data key="d0">Alpha-1-Antitrypsin Deficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Alpha-1-Antitrypsin Deficiency is a genetic condition that can result in liver disease and increase the risk of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Screening Tests">
      <data key="d0">Liver Screening Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver screening tests like ultrasound and AFP testing are essential for early detection of liver cancer, especially in high-risk populations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Cancer Screening Program">
      <data key="d0">Liver Cancer Screening Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The program focuses on regularly testing individuals at risk for liver cancer to detect the disease early when treatment is more effective.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach">
      <data key="d0">Stomach</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The stomach is a hollow organ in the digestive system that plays a crucial role in the digestion process.&lt;SEP&gt;The stomach is a hollow organ where food is mixed with digestive juices for breakdown; it plays a vital role in the digestive system.&lt;SEP&gt;The stomach is an organ in the digestive system that aids in food breakdown and is discussed in detail regarding its function and structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chyme">
      <data key="d0">Chyme</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chyme is a semi-liquid mass of partly digested food that is formed in the stomach and released into the small intestine for further digestion and nutrient absorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Chyme is the liquid mixture formed in the stomach after food is broken down, which then enters the small intestine for further digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Intestine">
      <data key="d0">Small Intestine</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The small intestine is a long, coiled organ where most digestion and absorption of nutrients occurs, following the stomach in the digestive process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colon">
      <data key="d0">Colon</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The colon is part of the large intestine and is responsible for water absorption and the formation of waste material for excretion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectum">
      <data key="d0">Rectum</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The rectum is the final section of the large intestine, serving as a temporary storage site for waste before it is expelled from the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle Layer">
      <data key="d0">Muscle Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The muscle layer of the bladder is critical in determining the invasiveness of cancer and subsequently its stage.&lt;SEP&gt;The muscle layer of the stomach wall, also known as muscularis propria, helps in mixing and moving the contents of the stomach during digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-Stage Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-Stage Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage stomach cancer has not grown beyond the first layer (mucosa) of the stomach wall and is typically small, often not affecting lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Early-stage stomach cancer is characterized by the cancer not having spread beyond the innermost layer (mucosa) of the stomach wall and requiring timely intervention for treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gastroesophageal Junction">
      <data key="d0">Gastroesophageal Junction</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The gastroesophageal junction is the specific location where the esophagus connects to the stomach, critical for preventing reflux.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stomach cancer refers to cancers that develop in the stomach, often starting in the mucosa and progressing through its layers. It can be categorized into early-stage, locoregional, or metastatic types, depending on how far it has spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H. pylori">
      <data key="d0">H. pylori</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">H. pylori is a type of bacteria that can cause infection in the stomach and is often associated with stomach cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;H. pylori is a type of bacteria that infects the stomach lining and is known to be a significant risk factor for stomach cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Testing for Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for stomach cancer involves a variety of diagnostic procedures including imaging, endoscopy, and biopsies to determine the presence and extent of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Testing for stomach cancer involves various procedures such as imaging tests, endoscopy, and biopsies used to assess the presence and extent of cancer, guiding treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locoregional Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Locoregional Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locoregional or locally advanced stomach cancer has invaded other layers of the stomach wall and/or spread to lymph nodes or nearby organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic stomach cancer has spread to distant parts of the body, commonly affecting the liver, peritoneum, or distant lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptom Development">
      <data key="d0">Symptom Development</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptom development in stomach cancer typically progresses slowly, with many early changes going unnoticed until later stages of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A glioma is a type of tumor that grows in the brain, stemming from glial cells which support nerve function and are the most common type of malignant brain tumor in adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma is a type of tumor that arises from glial cells in the brain and can be classified based on histology, grade, and biomarkers.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma refers to a type of tumor that develops from glial cells in the brain and spinal cord, causing various neurological symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma refers to a type of tumor that develops from glial cells in the brain and spinal cord, often leading to various neurological symptoms and complications.&lt;SEP&gt;Gliomas are a type of brain tumor that can cause significant neurological symptoms, including vision problems and changes in personality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Astrocytomas">
      <data key="d0">Astrocytomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Astrocytomas are a common type of glioma tumor that arises from star-shaped brain cells called astrocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Astrocytomas are a type of glioma that resemble star-shaped glial cells called astrocytes, which maintain the chemical environment for nerve cell communication.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oligodendrogliomas">
      <data key="d0">Oligodendrogliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oligodendrogliomas are another category of glioma tumors originating from oligodendrocytes, which support nerve cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Oligodendrogliomas look like oligodendrocytes, glial cells that protect electrical signaling in nerve cells, and are a subtype of gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glioblastomas">
      <data key="d0">Glioblastomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Glioblastomas are aggressive tumors classified as high-grade gliomas, known for their rapid growth and invasive nature.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioblastomas are the most destructive type of glioma tumor, originating from precursor cells that develop into astrocytes and oligodendrocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Central Nervous System">
      <data key="d0">Central Nervous System</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The central nervous system comprises the brain and spinal cord, where gliomas can develop and affect normal brain functions.&lt;SEP&gt;The central nervous system consists of the brain and spinal cord, where gliomas commonly affect brain function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Tumor Diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">Brain Tumor Diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain tumor diagnosis involves identifying gliomas often after they begin to cause symptoms, necessitating various tests and procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Tumor Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Brain Tumor Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment options for gliomas commonly include surgery, radiation therapy, and chemotherapy, often used in combination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glial Cells">
      <data key="d0">Glial Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Glial cells are supportive cells in the brain that can become cancerous and form gliomas when abnormal growth occurs.&lt;SEP&gt;Glial cells are supportive cells in the nervous system that give rise to gliomas when abnormalities occur, leading to abnormal growth patterns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of gliomas include headaches, seizures, personality changes, nausea, and motor skill difficulties, complicating diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Function Problems">
      <data key="d0">Brain Function Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain function problems can arise from gliomas, manifesting as issues with memory, movement, and overall cognitive ability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support for Caregivers">
      <data key="d0">Support for Caregivers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Support for caregivers involves providing assistance to individuals who help glioma patients manage their condition and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Support for caregivers involves providing emotional and physical assistance to patients dealing with the challenges posed by gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-Up Visits">
      <data key="d0">Follow-Up Visits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up visits are necessary to monitor glioma patients after treatment to assess tumor growth and manage symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Periodic Follow-Up Visits">
      <data key="d0">Periodic Follow-Up Visits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Periodic follow-up visits are recommended for glioma patients to monitor tumor status and manage ongoing symptoms and treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Therapies">
      <data key="d0">New Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">New therapies include experimental treatments targeting specific types of gliomas, currently under investigation in clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of gliomas can include headaches, seizures, cognitive decline, and changes in behavior, impacting daily life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Brain Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain imaging refers to various techniques used to visualize gliomas for diagnosis and monitoring, such as MRI and CT scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Open Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Open Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">medical procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">An open biopsy is a surgical method to remove tissue for testing when other methods are not possible.&lt;SEP&gt;Open biopsy involves creating a larger surgical opening to access a tumor for diagnosis when other methods are unsuitable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nausea">
      <data key="d0">Nausea</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nausea is a common symptom that can be caused by the pressure of a brain tumor on the brain, leading to discomfort and distress.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vomiting">
      <data key="d0">Vomiting</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vomiting is often triggered by disturbances in the brain caused by a tumor and can significantly affect a patient's quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cognitive Difficulties">
      <data key="d0">Cognitive Difficulties</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cognitive difficulties refer to problems with thinking, memory, and speech that can arise from the presence of a brain tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weakness">
      <data key="d0">Weakness</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Weakness, particularly on one side of the body, is a symptom that may arise in patients with brain tumors, impacting their mobility and daily functioning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Personality Changes">
      <data key="d0">Personality Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Personality changes can occur in patients with brain tumors, especially in areas of the brain that regulate mood and behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vision Problems">
      <data key="d0">Vision Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vision problems such as blurred or double vision may be experienced by patients with gliomas due to their location in respect to the visual cortex.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sensory Disturbances">
      <data key="d0">Sensory Disturbances</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sensory disturbances, including numbness or tingling on one side of the body, can indicate a brain tumor presence, affecting the sensory pathways.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurological Exam">
      <data key="d0">Neurological Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A neurological exam involves tests assessing a patient's mental state, reflexes, and motor skills to evaluate the potential impact of a brain tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)</data>
      <data key="d1">technology</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI is a key imaging technology used to visualize the brain and is particularly effective at detecting gliomas and other brain abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurosurgeon">
      <data key="d0">Neurosurgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neurosurgeon is a medical doctor specialized in performing surgeries on the brain and nervous system, including biopsies for tumor analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuropathologist">
      <data key="d0">Neuropathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neuropathologist is a specialized physician who examines tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose brain tumors and analyze their characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Astrocytoma">
      <data key="d0">Astrocytoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Astrocytoma is a classification of glioma characterized by cells that resemble astrocytes, often assessed through histology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IDH Gene">
      <data key="d0">IDH Gene</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The IDH gene is a specific genetic mutation that can serve as a biomarker for certain types of gliomas, notably astrocytomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Craniotomy">
      <data key="d0">Craniotomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A craniotomy is a surgical procedure where a section of the skull is removed to access the brain, often performed during open biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Histology">
      <data key="d0">Histology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Histology is the study of the microscopic structure of tissues, crucial for diagnosing cancer types based on cell appearance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 1 Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Grade 1 Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 1 gliomas are tumors that grow slowly and are usually benign, often treated with surgery alone. They are more common in children.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 2 Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Grade 2 Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 2 gliomas consist of tumor cells that appear somewhat abnormal and can invade normal tissue, potentially returning as higher-grade tumors after many years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 3 Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Grade 3 Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 3 gliomas have tumor cells that do not resemble normal cells and can rapidly increase in number while invading nearby tissues, often requiring additional therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 4 Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Grade 4 Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 4 gliomas, such as glioblastomas, consist of cells that look very abnormal and grow and spread very quickly, necessitating aggressive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CLL is a slow-growing blood cancer characterized by the accumulation of abnormal lymphocytes in the blood and bone marrow, often requiring targeted treatments for management.&lt;SEP&gt;CLL is a type of blood cancer characterized by the slow growth of abnormal B lymphocytes, often requiring no immediate treatment and potentially leading to crowding out of healthy cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer that originates from a specific type of white blood cells known as lymphocytes, leading to high white blood cell counts and other related health complications.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer that primarily affects the blood and bone marrow, leading to the overproduction of abnormal lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumors are abnormal growths of tissue that can be benign or malignant. They can grow slowly or aggressively depending on their classification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gliomas are a type of tumor that originates in the glial cells of the brain and spinal cord, classified based on their grade from 1 to 4, indicating their aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutations are abnormal changes in the DNA sequence of cells that can disrupt normal cell functions and are often linked to the development of cancers, including gliomas and CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Lymphocytic Lymphoma (SLL)">
      <data key="d0">Small Lymphocytic Lymphoma (SLL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small Lymphocytic Lymphoma (SLL) is closely related to CLL and is characterized by the presence of cancerous cells primarily in lymph nodes and the spleen.&lt;SEP&gt;Small Lymphocytic Lymphoma (SLL) is essentially the same cancer as CLL but is primarily found in lymph nodes and the spleen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopathologist">
      <data key="d0">Hematopathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A hematopathologist is a medical doctor specializing in diagnosing diseases of the blood, including the evaluation of blood samples and lymphatic tissues for cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;A hematopathologist is a medical specialist who diagnoses diseases of the blood and immune systems, including cancers like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;A hematopathologist is a medical specialist who diagnoses diseases related to blood and immune cells, and is crucial in identifying conditions like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;A hematopathologist is a specialized doctor who diagnoses diseases related to blood, including cancers like CLL, through laboratory tests and examinations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Richter Transformation">
      <data key="d0">Richter Transformation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Richter Transformation is a rare and aggressive transformation of CLL into a more aggressive cancer, such as diffuse large B-cell lymphoma or Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter Transformation refers to a rare complication in CLL where the disease progresses to a more aggressive form, such as diffuse large B-cell lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation is a condition referenced in relation to lymphatic system cancers, specifically B-cell lymphomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation is a condition that indicates a change in the type of lymphoma, specifically in relation to cutaneous lymphomas and their treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation is a serious progression of CLL where the disease evolves into a more aggressive form of cancer, requiring altered treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation refers to a condition where CLL transforms into a more aggressive form of cancer, indicating advanced disease progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Test">
      <data key="d0">Blood Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood test is a diagnostic tool used to confirm the presence of CLL by analyzing the blood for abnormal cell counts and types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CD20">
      <data key="d0">CD20</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CD20 is a protein typically expressed on the surface of B cells, utilized as a biomarker in the diagnosis and treatment of certain blood cancers, including chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL).&lt;SEP&gt;CD20 is a protein typically found on the surface of B cells and is used as a marker in identifying certain types of cancer, particularly in hematology.&lt;SEP&gt;CD20 is a surface protein found on certain cancerous B cells, including those in CLL, and is a target for specific cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minimal Residual Disease">
      <data key="d0">Minimal Residual Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Minimal Residual Disease refers to the small number of cancer cells that may remain in a patient's body after treatment, indicating the effectiveness of therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Cell Surface">
      <data key="d0">B Cell Surface</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The B Cell Surface is the outer membrane of B cells, where important proteins like CD20 are located, playing a crucial role in immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;The B Cell Surface refers to the outer membrane of B cells, where proteins like CD20 are located, playing a key role in immune function and cellular signaling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLL Diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">CLL Diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">CLL Diagnosis is the medical process of identifying Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia, which includes various tests and examinations to confirm the presence of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;CLL Diagnosis refers to the process of identifying Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia, typically involving various laboratory tests and examinations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Status Score">
      <data key="d0">Performance Status Score</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The performance status score is a measurement of a patient’s ability to perform daily activities, used in assessing their overall health in relation to treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The performance status score reflects a patient's ability to perform daily activities and is essential for determining treatment options and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of CLL">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of CLL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of CLL include fevers, night sweats, and unexplained weight loss, which may indicate the presence of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLL">
      <data key="d0">CLL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer that starts from white blood cells and can affect blood cell counts and overall health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beta-2 microglobulin">
      <data key="d0">Beta-2 microglobulin</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">Beta-2 microglobulin is a protein found on most cells, with high levels indicating the possible growth of CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnosis Testing">
      <data key="d0">Diagnosis Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnosis testing for CLL often includes differential counts, metabolic panels, and various lab tests to assess blood and health status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Metabolic Panel">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Metabolic Panel</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A comprehensive metabolic panel is a blood test that assesses various chemicals in the blood to evaluate overall health, organ function, and detect diseases, including CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;The Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) measures important chemicals, proteins, and other substances in the blood to assess organ function and health.&lt;SEP&gt;The Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) measures important chemicals, proteins, and substances in the blood to assess kidney and liver function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Testing">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow testing involves taking samples from the bone marrow to diagnose conditions like CLL and to determine causes for abnormal blood cell counts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker Tests">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker tests are laboratory tests that identify specific biological indicators related to CLL, which can help in tailoring treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA Sequencing">
      <data key="d0">DNA Sequencing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA sequencing is a process used to identify mutations in specific genes related to CLL, such as TP53 and IGHV, which play critical roles in cell survival and cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karyotype Testing">
      <data key="d0">Karyotype Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Karyotype testing analyzes chromosomes for abnormalities in CLL cells, helping to inform treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous B-cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous B-cell Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cutaneous B-cell Lymphoma (CBCL) is a type of cancer that affects B-cells in the skin, typically requiring accurate testing for diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) is a type of cancer originating from B cells, presenting as nodules under the skin, often related to the lymphatic system.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) is a type of skin lymphoma that originates from B lymphocytes, manifesting as nodules or papules on the skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Cutaneous Lymphomas">
      <data key="d0">Primary Cutaneous Lymphomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary cutaneous lymphomas are a group of lymphomas that occur in the skin and are categorized as non-Hodgkin lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (CTCL) is a type of cancer that develops from T lymphocytes and primarily affects the skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papules">
      <data key="d0">Papules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papules are small bumps on the skin that can indicate various skin conditions, including cutaneous lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bladder Cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the bladder and often requires surgical intervention such as cystectomy or TURBT for treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a condition characterized by the abnormal growth of cells in the bladder, often assessed through various imaging and diagnostic tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of cells in the inner lining of the bladder, often leading to tumors and requiring medical diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer affecting the urinary bladder, necessitating careful diagnosis and treatment through a multidisciplinary care approach.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that affects the bladder and can be treated with various therapies including chemotherapy and radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that affects the bladder and may be treated with various therapies, including intravesical therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that affects the urinary bladder, requiring a multidisciplinary care team for effective treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the bladder's lining and has various stages and grades according to its progression and growth rate.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the urothelial cells of the bladder, and can exhibit various grades and stages affecting treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the bladder's inner lining, characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells and potentially presenting in varying stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that occurs when abnormal cells grow uncontrollably in the inner layer of the bladder, and can present in various stages and requires multiple treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that starts in the bladder, often requiring treatment options based on its stage and grade.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelium">
      <data key="d0">Urothelium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelium is the inner lining of the bladder that prevents urine from leaking back into the body, and it can be the site where bladder cancer originates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tissue Tests">
      <data key="d0">Tissue Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Tissue tests involve examining samples from tissues to identify disease presence, often used in cancer diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Documents">
      <data key="d0">Medical Documents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical documents include records such as insurance forms and medical history that are crucial for effective healthcare.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-up Testing">
      <data key="d0">Follow-up Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up testing refers to ongoing medical tests conducted after initial treatment to monitor for cancer recurrence or progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="urothelium">
      <data key="d0">urothelium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelium refers to the innermost layer of the urinary tract, including the bladder, kidneys, ureters, and urethra, where bladder cancer can originate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bladder cancer">
      <data key="d0">bladder cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that affects the bladder, which can cause various symptoms and requires specific treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that can originate in various parts of the urinary tract, particularly in the urothelium, and is associated with symptoms like blood in urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="squamous cell carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">squamous cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell carcinoma is one of the less common types of bladder cancer, originating from squamous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenocarcinoma is another rare type of bladder cancer that arises from glandular cells within the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="small cell carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">small cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell carcinoma is a rare form of bladder cancer that is less common compared to other types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="hematuria">
      <data key="d0">hematuria</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematuria is the medical term for blood in urine, often a primary symptom indicating the presence of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cystoscopy">
      <data key="d0">cystoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cystoscopy is a diagnostic procedure that allows health care professionals to look inside the bladder to diagnose bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="urinalysis">
      <data key="d0">urinalysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinalysis is a test used to analyze urine samples for signs of bladder cancer and other conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="risk factors for bladder cancer">
      <data key="d0">risk factors for bladder cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk factors for bladder cancer include smoking, age, family history, and exposure to certain chemicals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Tract">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Tract</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The urinary tract is a system in the body that includes the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra, responsible for urine production and excretion.&lt;SEP&gt;The urinary tract is part of the body's system responsible for producing and expelling urine, including the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a type of bladder cancer that has grown into the muscle layer of the bladder wall, indicating a more advanced stage of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a type that has not yet invaded the bladder's muscle layer and is still confined to the inner lining.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic bladder cancer is an advanced stage of bladder cancer where the disease has spread beyond the bladder and may require specific treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic bladder cancer is an advanced stage where the cancer has spread beyond the bladder to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes and organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of bladder cancer can include blood in urine, frequent urination, urgency to urinate, and painful urination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood in Urine">
      <data key="d0">Blood in Urine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood in urine, or hematuria, is often the first noticeable symptom of bladder cancer, indicating possible tumor presence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Diagnosis Tests">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Diagnosis Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer diagnosis tests include methods like cystoscopy, urinalysis, and biopsy, used to confirm the presence of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family History of Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Family History of Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A family history of bladder cancer can increase an individual's risk, indicating a potential genetic link to the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemical Exposure">
      <data key="d0">Chemical Exposure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Exposure to chemicals in certain industries, such as dyes and rubber, has been associated with an increased risk of developing bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urine Tests">
      <data key="d0">Urine Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">testing</data>
      <data key="d2">Urine tests are diagnostic procedures performed to detect the presence of abnormal cells or substances indicative of bladder cancer and to monitor therapy effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Urine tests are used to detect abnormal cells and other indicators of bladder cancer, contributing to diagnosis and monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The multidisciplinary care team consists of various healthcare professionals who work together to provide comprehensive treatment for bladder cancer patients, ensuring coordinated care.&lt;SEP&gt;The multidisciplinary care team consists of various medical professionals involved in the treatment of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary care doctor oversees the general health of the patient and facilitates communication between the patient and the multidisciplinary cancer care team.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care doctor provides general medical care and facilitates communication about treatment options among the cancer care team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urologic Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Urologic Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A urologic oncologist is a specialized physician who performs surgeries and provides treatments for cancers affecting the urinary tract and reproductive organs.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologic oncologist is a surgeon specializing in cancers of the urinary tract, employing surgical and intravesical therapy as treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharmacist">
      <data key="d0">Pharmacist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pharmacist prepares and dispenses medications such as chemotherapy drugs and provides essential information on their proper use and possible side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;A pharmacist prepares chemotherapy and other medications, offering treatment advice and managing medication protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anesthesiologist">
      <data key="d0">Anesthesiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An anesthesiologist administers anesthesia during surgical procedures to alleviate pain.&lt;SEP&gt;An anesthesiologist is responsible for administering anesthesia during surgical procedures to ensure the patient remains comfortable and pain-free.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutritionists">
      <data key="d0">Nutritionists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Nutritionists offer dietary advice and help patients select the best nutritional plans suited to their cancer treatment needs.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutritionists provide dietary guidance tailored to patients' health conditions, particularly cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Psychologists and Psychiatrists">
      <data key="d0">Psychologists and Psychiatrists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Psychologists and psychiatrists assist patients in managing the emotional and psychological aspects of living with cancer, including mental health challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;These mental health experts help patients manage psychological issues related to cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counselors">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counselors</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic counselors interpret family history and its implications for treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic counselors interpret genetic information and advise patients about their hereditary risks and implications for treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urine Cytology">
      <data key="d0">Urine Cytology</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Urine cytology is a diagnostic test that analyzes urine for abnormal cells, potentially indicating the presence of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Urine cytology is a lab test used to analyze urine for the presence of cancer or precancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cystoscopy Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Cystoscopy Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cystoscopy allows for direct visualization of the urethra and bladder to detect signs of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a minimally invasive procedure used to visualize the inside of the bladder and urethra, crucial for diagnosing bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Function Test">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Function Test</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">The kidney function test assesses kidney health by measuring waste products to ensure proper organ function during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidney function test measures waste products in the blood to assess kidney health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Test">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Test</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">The liver function test evaluates enzymes produced by the liver to gauge its health.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver function test measures liver enzymes to evaluate the liver's health, which can be affected by cancer and its treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ureteroscopy">
      <data key="d0">Ureteroscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Ureteroscopy is a procedure that extends the examination to the upper urinary tract, specifically the ureters and inner kidneys, if abnormalities are suspected.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blue Light Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Blue Light Cystoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Blue light cystoscopy is a newer technology that improves tumor detection in the bladder by using a special dye injected for better visibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Narrow-Band Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Narrow-Band Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Narrow-band imaging is a technique that enhances the visibility of tumors during cystoscopy without the need for dye.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flexible Cystoscope">
      <data key="d0">Flexible Cystoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A flexible cystoscope is a type of cystoscope that allows for easier access and visualization of the bladder for diagnostic procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rigid Cystoscope">
      <data key="d0">Rigid Cystoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A rigid cystoscope is a more invasive instrument used for cystoscopy, capable of removing small tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiologist's Report">
      <data key="d0">Radiologist's Report</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A radiologist's report conveys the findings from imaging tests, which aids in the patient's diagnosis and treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT Scan of Abdomen and Pelvis">
      <data key="d0">CT Scan of Abdomen and Pelvis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific CT scan used to check for cancer spread beyond the bladder in the abdomen and pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Test Results">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Test Results</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer test results refer to the outcomes of various diagnostic procedures performed to determine the presence or status of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Tract Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Tract Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinary tract cancer includes various types of cancers affecting the bladder, ureters, and kidneys, necessitating advanced imaging for diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urogram">
      <data key="d0">Urogram</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_test</data>
      <data key="d2">A urogram is an imaging test that uses contrast to visualize the lining of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder to identify potential issues such as pain or blood in urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Renal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_test</data>
      <data key="d2">A renal ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging test that provides real-time images of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder, similar to the technology used for viewing a fetus in the womb.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pyelogram">
      <data key="d0">Pyelogram</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_test</data>
      <data key="d2">A pyelogram is an imaging procedure that uses x-rays and contrast solution to diagnose blockages or abnormalities in the kidneys or ureters, often performed during a cystoscopy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDC.gov/tobacco">
      <data key="d0">CDC.gov/tobacco</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">CDC.gov/tobacco is a governmental website that provides information on tobacco use and its health effects, focusing on prevention and cessation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Grading">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Grading</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer grading classifies cancer cells based on how abnormal they appear under a microscope, indicating the aggressiveness of the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder Wall">
      <data key="d0">Bladder Wall</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The bladder wall consists of several layers that the cancer may invade, influencing staging and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inner Lining">
      <data key="d0">Inner Lining</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The inner lining, or urothelial layer, is the first layer affected by bladder cancer and plays a crucial role in determining the stage of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Cells">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial cells are the cells that line the bladder and are the primary origin of most bladder cancers, specifically urothelial cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Urothelial cells are the specific type of cells that line the bladder and are the origin point for most bladder cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 bladder cancer involves abnormal cells that have grown on the lining of the bladder without invading deeper tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 0 bladder cancer signifies the earliest form of the disease, affecting only the inner lining of the bladder without further invasion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1 bladder cancer describes a tumor that has started to invade the layer of connective tissue in the bladder wall.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1 bladder cancer indicates that the cancer has grown through the inner lining into the connective tissue layer but not into the muscle layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 bladder cancer indicates growth into the muscle layer of the bladder, affecting treatment intensity.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 2 bladder cancer indicates that the tumor has invaded the muscle layer of the bladder wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3 bladder cancer describes a condition where the tumor has penetrated into the fat layer surrounding the bladder and may affect nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 3 bladder cancer indicates that the disease has grown into surrounding fatty tissue and may spread to lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 bladder cancer signifies advanced disease with potential spread to distant organs and lymph nodes, requiring aggressive treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4 bladder cancer, classified as metastatic, refers to cancer that has spread outside the bladder to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravesical Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravesical Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravesical Therapy is a treatment used in conjunction with TURBT to target bladder tumors more effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy involves placing cancer medicine directly into the bladder to treat cancer effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy is a treatment method that involves delivering medication directly into the bladder to combat cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy is a treatment method where medicine is directly instilled into the bladder to target cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Removal">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Removal</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor removal is a surgical process aimed at excising cancerous tumors from the bladder while preserving as much of the bladder tissue as possible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor (TURBT)">
      <data key="d0">Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor (TURBT)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">TURBT is a specific surgical procedure used to remove bladder tumors through the urethra without external incisions.&lt;SEP&gt;TURBT is a surgical procedure that examines and removes tumors from the bladder wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Grade Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Low Grade Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low grade cancer, indicated by the abbreviation LG, refers to cancer cells that are slower growing and less aggressive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Grade Cancer">
      <data key="d0">High Grade Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High grade cancer, referred to as HG, indicates cancer cells that are more aggressive and are expected to grow faster.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Side Effects of Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Side Effects of Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Side effects of cancer treatments can vary greatly, affecting patient quality of life and necessitating supportive care throughout treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Survey Feedback">
      <data key="d0">Survey Feedback</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The survey feedback provided by patients is used to enhance the understanding and quality of information regarding bladder cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urology">
      <data key="d0">Urology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urology is the branch of medicine that deals with diseases of the urinary tract and male reproductive system, including bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TURBT">
      <data key="d0">TURBT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">TURBT stands for Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor, a surgical procedure used to examine and remove tumors from the bladder wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cystectomy">
      <data key="d0">Cystectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cystectomy is a surgical procedure for bladder cancer that involves the removal of the bladder, and may involve the removal of nearby lymph nodes and other organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Cystectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Cystectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radical Cystectomy is a major surgical procedure to treat muscle-invasive bladder cancer by removing the entire bladder and surrounding tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Radical cystectomy is a major surgical procedure involving the removal of the bladder and surrounding organs in cases of muscle-invasive bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Partial Cystectomy">
      <data key="d0">Partial Cystectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Partial Cystectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of a part of the bladder and is rarely used for bladder cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Diversion">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Diversion</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinary Diversion is a surgical procedure required after bladder removal to provide a new way for urine to exit the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Urinary diversion is a surgical procedure to redirect urine flow from the bladder to an alternate site, often due to medical conditions affecting the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neobladder">
      <data key="d0">Neobladder</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Neobladder is a surgical option where a segment of the small intestine replaces the bladder, allowing for urine to be expelled more naturally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Continent Cutaneous Pouch (Indiana Pouch)">
      <data key="d0">Continent Cutaneous Pouch (Indiana Pouch)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Continent Cutaneous Pouch, also called Indiana pouch, is a method of urinary diversion that allows urine to be stored in a pouch made from a portion of the intestine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Incontinence">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Incontinence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinary Incontinence is a condition that may occur with a neobladder, where urine may come out unexpectedly.&lt;SEP&gt;Urinary incontinence refers to the involuntary leakage of urine, which can occur unexpectedly and may be a side effect of bladder cancer or its treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vas Deferens">
      <data key="d0">Vas Deferens</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Vas Deferens is a tube that carries sperm away from the testicles, which may be affected in bladder cancer surgeries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Diversion Types">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Diversion Types</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinary Diversion Types refer to various surgical methods used to reroute urine flow after bladder removal, including Ileal Conduit, Neobladder, and Indiana Pouch.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indiana Pouch">
      <data key="d0">Indiana Pouch</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Indiana Pouch is a type of continent cutaneous pouch created from a section of the intestine, used as a urinary diversion method.&lt;SEP&gt;The Indiana pouch is a type of urinary diversion developed to connect the small intestine to a stoma, allowing for urine drainage without an ostomy bag.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indiana University">
      <data key="d0">Indiana University</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Indiana University is an educational institution where surgeons developed the Indiana pouch, which is used for urinary diversion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravesical BCG Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravesical BCG Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravesical BCG therapy uses a weakened bacterium to stimulate the immune system against bladder cancer and is commonly used for high-grade cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TURBT Surgery">
      <data key="d0">TURBT Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">TURBT (Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor) surgery is a procedure used to remove bladder tumors and is often followed by intravesical treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCG Shortage">
      <data key="d0">BCG Shortage</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The BCG shortage refers to the global scarcity of the BCG treatment for bladder cancer, impacting patient access and care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade bladder cancer is a type of aggressive cancer that is more likely to spread and is typically treated with BCG therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravesical Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravesical Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravesical chemotherapy delivers chemotherapy drugs directly into the bladder to eliminate cancer cells and reduce recurrence risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical chemotherapy is a localized treatment specifically targeting the bladder, differing from systemic chemotherapy which affects the entire body.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical chemotherapy specifically targets the inside of the bladder, differing from systemic chemotherapy that affects the whole body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patients">
      <data key="d0">Patients</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Patients are individuals receiving medical care, particularly in the context of cancer treatment and survivorship.&lt;SEP&gt;Patients are individuals receiving medical treatment, particularly those affected by bladder cancer and involved in various treatment modalities described in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Keytruda">
      <data key="d0">Keytruda</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Keytruda (pembrolizumab) is a checkpoint inhibitor immunotherapy used to enhance the immune system's ability to identify and fight cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Opdivo">
      <data key="d0">Opdivo</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Opdivo (nivolumab) is a checkpoint inhibitor drug utilized in immunotherapy to treat bladder cancer among other types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bavencio">
      <data key="d0">Bavencio</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Bavencio (avelumab) is an immunotherapy drug that acts as a checkpoint inhibitor for bladder cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tecentriq">
      <data key="d0">Tecentriq</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Tecentriq (atezolizumab) is an immunotherapy approach targeting the immune system to combat bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Padcev">
      <data key="d0">Padcev</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Padcev (enfortumab vedotin-ejfv) is a targeted therapy utilized in bladder cancer treatment that acts on a specific protein found on cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Balversa">
      <data key="d0">Balversa</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Balversa (erdafitinib) is a targeted therapy for bladder cancer patients with specific mutations in the FGFR3 gene.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trodelvy">
      <data key="d0">Trodelvy</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Trodelvy (sacituzumab govitecan-hziy) is a targeted therapy focusing on certain proteins prominent on bladder cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trimodal Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Trimodal Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Trimodal therapy refers to a three-pronged treatment approach involving TURBT surgery, radiation, and chemotherapy for bladder cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Trimodal therapy refers to a treatment method that combines three therapies, aiming to preserve the bladder while treating cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic chemotherapy is a cancer treatment that affects the whole body by using drugs that circulate through the bloodstream to kill cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Harsh Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Harsh Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Harsh side effects refer to the negative impacts on healthy cells resulting from chemotherapy, affecting patients' day-to-day activities and well-being.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dose-Dense Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Dose-Dense Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dose-dense chemotherapy involves administering treatments more frequently to speed up the recovery process between doses, particularly for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Cycles">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Cycles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy cycles consist of a series of treatments followed by a rest period, often repeated to maximize effectiveness in killing cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Checkpoint Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Checkpoint Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Checkpoint inhibitors are a class of drugs used in immunotherapy, blocking proteins that prevent the immune system from attacking cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDA Approval">
      <data key="d0">FDA Approval</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FDA approval is a regulatory process through which new treatments or drugs are evaluated for safety and effectiveness before they are made widely available to patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Platinum Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Non-Platinum Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-platinum chemotherapy refers to alternative treatments for patients who cannot tolerate platinum-based chemotherapies due to underlying health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="clinical trials">
      <data key="d0">clinical trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical trials are research studies that explore new treatments for diseases, particularly cancer, evaluating their effects on patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that involve patients and serve to test new treatments and therapies for various medical conditions, including anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="supportive care">
      <data key="d0">supportive care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supportive care refers to healthcare that alleviates symptoms and improves the quality of life for cancer patients throughout their treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 2 Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 2 Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 2 trials are clinical trials that study the effectiveness of a drug or approach against a specific type of cancer after initial safety tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 3 Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 3 Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 3 trials are pivotal clinical trials that compare a new treatment against a standard treatment to determine its efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 4 Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 4 Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 4 trials study the long-term safety and efficacy of a treatment that has been approved by the FDA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Floor Exercises">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Floor Exercises</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic floor exercises are a set of physical exercises aimed at strengthening the pelvic floor muscles, often recommended to help regain control over urinary functions after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal cancer is a rare form of cancer that originates in the anal canal or perianal region, and its treatment may involve various therapies including chemotherapy and radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a rare type of cancer that occurs in the anal canal, often linked to specific risk factors such as HPV infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer occurring in the anal canal or perianal region, most commonly as squamous cell carcinoma, often linked with HPV infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the anal canal or the perianal region, primarily as squamous cell carcinoma associated with HPV infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the anal region, often associated with HPV and characterized by abnormal cell growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the anus, with specific monitoring and treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the tissues of the anus and may require surgical intervention, chemotherapy, and radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Canal">
      <data key="d0">Anal Canal</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The anal canal is the last part of the gastrointestinal tract, where anal cancer can develop.&lt;SEP&gt;The anal canal is the portion of the digestive system leading from the rectum to the anus and is significant in anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The anal canal is the short section of the digestive tract that leads from the rectum to the anus, playing a crucial role in the occurrence of anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The anal canal is the terminal part of the gastrointestinal tract where anal cancer can originate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perianal Region">
      <data key="d0">Perianal Region</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The perianal region encompasses the area around the anal opening, which is also susceptible to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The perianal region includes the anal opening and surrounding tissues, and is the area where anal cancer can develop.&lt;SEP&gt;The perianal region includes the skin surrounding the anal opening and is where anal cancer may develop, affecting local tissue health.&lt;SEP&gt;The perianal region surrounds the anal opening and is also susceptible to the development of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Exam (DRE)">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Exam (DRE)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Digital Rectal Exam (DRE) is a clinical procedure used to detect abnormalities in the anal canal and assess cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Resolution Anoscopy (HRA)">
      <data key="d0">High-Resolution Anoscopy (HRA)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">High-Resolution Anoscopy (HRA) is a diagnostic procedure that allows for a detailed examination of the anal canal for cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal surgeons are medical professionals specializing in the evaluation and treatment of anal diseases, including anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SISCCA">
      <data key="d0">SISCCA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SISCCA refers to a classification of more advanced anal cancer that may not require certain tests.&lt;SEP&gt;SISCCA, or superficially invasive squamous cell carcinoma, is an early stage of anal cancer often discovered accidentally during other procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Strains">
      <data key="d0">HPV Strains</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV strains are various types of the human papillomavirus, some of which are classified as high-risk due to their association with cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Dysplasia">
      <data key="d0">Anal Dysplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal dysplasia refers to pre-cancerous lesions in the anal region that may develop into anal cancer if not treated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Risk HPV Types">
      <data key="d0">High-Risk HPV Types</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-risk HPV types include strains such as HPV16, which are more likely to lead to anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crohn’s Disease">
      <data key="d0">Crohn’s Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Crohn’s disease is an autoimmune disorder that affects the digestive tract and is associated with an increased risk of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Professionals">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Professionals</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Health care professionals, including primary care physicians, gynecologists, and gastroenterologists, are involved in the diagnosis and treatment of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectal Bleeding">
      <data key="d0">Rectal Bleeding</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Rectal bleeding is a common symptom of anal cancer, which may manifest as blood in stool or on toilet paper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pain near the Anus">
      <data key="d0">Pain near the Anus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pain near the anus can be a symptom of anal cancer or related conditions and should not be ignored.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Anoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Anoscopy is a procedure recommended for evaluating the anal canal and rectum, especially for patients treated for lymph nodes, to be performed every 6 to 12 months for 3 years.&lt;SEP&gt;Anoscopy is a procedure used to examine the anal canal and is relevant for diagnosing anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal squamous cell carcinoma is a form of cancer diagnosed by biopsy that may lead to advanced treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inguinal Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Inguinal Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Inguinal lymph nodes are located in the groin area and are significant in the spread of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV Testing">
      <data key="d0">HIV Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV testing is recommended as individuals with HIV have an increased risk of developing anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologic Exam">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologic Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Gynecologic exam is recommended for cervical cancer screening, which relates to the risks associated with anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Michael">
      <data key="d0">Michael</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Michael is a survivor of anal cancer who emphasizes the importance of mentoring individuals who are undergoing similar experiences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Semen Cryopreservation">
      <data key="d0">Semen Cryopreservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Semen cryopreservation is a fertility preservation option involving the freezing of sperm for future use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 anal cancer refers to the presence of abnormal cells in the anal canal or perianal region that may develop into cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage I Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage I Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage I anal cancer is characterized by a tumor of 2 cm or smaller without any spread to lymph nodes or distant areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage II Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage II Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage II anal cancer involves tumors larger than 2 cm, with specific classifications of IIA and IIB based on size and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage III Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage III Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage III anal cancer has three substages and may involve cancer spreading to nearby lymph nodes or invading nearby organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage IV Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage IV Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage IV anal cancer indicates that the cancer has metastasized to distant organs such as the liver or lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage IV anal cancer refers to the advanced stage of anal cancer where cancer has spread beyond the primary site, often requiring comprehensive treatment approaches including radiation and chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oocyte Cryopreservation">
      <data key="d0">Oocyte Cryopreservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oocyte cryopreservation involves freezing unfertilized eggs for potential use in future fertility treatments, critical for those undergoing cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oophoropexy">
      <data key="d0">Oophoropexy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oophoropexy is a surgical procedure aimed at preserving ovarian function during radiation therapy by repositioning the ovaries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrocautery">
      <data key="d0">Electrocautery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrocautery is a treatment method that uses electricity to cut tissue and stop bleeding, which may be used for anal precancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stages I – III Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stages I – III Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages I to III refer to the classifications of non-metastatic anal cancer, indicating the cancer has not spread beyond the pelvic area.&lt;SEP&gt;Stages I – III refer to non-metastatic anal cancer stages, indicating that the cancer has not spread beyond the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic anal cancer refers to advanced stages of anal cancer where the disease has spread beyond the original site to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic anal cancer refers to anal cancer that has spread beyond the pelvis, primarily to the liver or lungs, and requires specific treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Over-the-Counter Ointments">
      <data key="d0">Over-the-Counter Ointments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Over-the-counter ointments, like Aquaphor or Silvadene, are used to soothe skin irritation during radiation treatment for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Common Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Common Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The common side effects of anal cancer treatment include skin irritation, diarrhea, fatigue, and potential long-term effects on fertility and sexual health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mitomycin">
      <data key="d0">Mitomycin</data>
      <data key="d1">medicine</data>
      <data key="d2">Mitomycin is a chemotherapy agent that can be combined with other drugs to treat perianal cancer when radiation therapy is involved.&lt;SEP&gt;Mitomycin is a chemotherapy drug often used in combination with other medications for treating anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-FU">
      <data key="d0">5-FU</data>
      <data key="d1">medicine</data>
      <data key="d2">5-FU (Fluorouracil) is a chemotherapy agent that is administered to treat various cancers, including anal cancer, often in combination with other drugs.&lt;SEP&gt;5-FU (Fluorouracil) is a chemotherapy drug that can be part of treatment regimens for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;5-FU, or fluorouracil, is a chemotherapy medication used to treat various cancers, often used in combination with other drugs.&lt;SEP&gt;5-FU, or fluorouracil, is a common chemotherapy drug used for treating various cancers, including perianal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Perianal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Perianal cancer is a type of cancer affecting the area surrounding the anus, with treatment options varying based on its grade and staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Perianal cancer refers to cancer occurring in the area around the anus, often treated initially with chemoradiation for stages I to III.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Excision">
      <data key="d0">Local Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local excision is a surgical treatment option for early-stage, low-risk perianal cancers involving the removal of the tumor and surrounding tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PICC Line">
      <data key="d0">PICC Line</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A PICC (Peripherally Inserted Central Catheter) line is a type of long IV line inserted into a vein in the arm that is used to administer chemotherapy over a prolonged period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Central Venous Catheter">
      <data key="d0">Central Venous Catheter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A central venous catheter is a medical device inserted into a large vein, allowing for the administration of chemotherapy and other medications over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hand-Foot Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Hand-Foot Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hand-Foot Syndrome is a side effect of certain chemotherapy treatments, resulting in redness, swelling, and pain on the palms and soles of the feet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Margin">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Margin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical margins refer to the healthy tissue surrounding a cancerous lesion removed during surgery; clear margins indicate effective removal of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The surgical margin is the normal tissue surrounding a tumor that is removed during surgery, crucial for ensuring that the cancer has been completely excised.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feedback Survey">
      <data key="d0">Feedback Survey</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The feedback survey is an initiative by NCCN aimed at improving cancer care by collecting patient experiences and suggestions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Janet">
      <data key="d0">Janet</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Janet is an anal cancer survivor who emphasizes the normalcy of the condition and the need for awareness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-Up Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Follow-Up Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up surveillance includes various testing methods to monitor for the return of anal or perianal cancer after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-grade Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Low-grade Perianal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low-grade perianal cancer is a classification of cancer that is less aggressive and has better treatment outcomes than higher grades.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intermediate-grade Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Intermediate-grade Perianal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intermediate-grade perianal cancer indicates a moderate level of differentiation, often requiring more careful monitoring and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-grade Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">High-grade Perianal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade perianal cancer is a more aggressive form of cancer that requires immediate and intensive treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Undifferentiated Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Undifferentiated Perianal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Undifferentiated perianal cancer does not have specific features and can be challenging to treat, requiring thorough evaluations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Re-excision">
      <data key="d0">Re-excision</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Re-excision is a surgical procedure performed when initial margins are inadequate or cancer cells remain post-surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-Up Testing">
      <data key="d0">Follow-Up Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up testing is a monitoring process conducted post-treatment to detect signs of cancer returning, involving various examinations and imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Examination (DRE)">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Examination (DRE)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Digital rectal examination is a clinical procedure to examine the rectal area for abnormalities, recommended as part of cancer follow-up.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Exams">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node exams are conducted to assess potential spread of cancer to lymph nodes, crucial in determining cancer staging and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progressive Disease">
      <data key="d0">Progressive Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progressive disease indicates the cancer has continued to grow or spread despite treatment, necessitating reevaluation and change of treatment strategy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persistent Disease">
      <data key="d0">Persistent Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Persistent disease refers to the presence of cancer that remains stable or unchanged after treatment, requiring ongoing monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Remission">
      <data key="d0">Complete Remission</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Complete remission occurs when no cancer is detected in the body following treatment, indicating effective management of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgery for Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Surgery for Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgery for anal cancer may involve various procedures to remove cancerous tissue and is often necessary in cases of advanced disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle Flap Reconstruction">
      <data key="d0">Muscle Flap Reconstruction</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle flap reconstruction is a surgical technique using tissue from another body area to aid healing after APR in cases of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Exams (DREs)">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Exams (DREs)</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Digital rectal exams are recommended every 3 to 6 months for 5 years after treatment for recurrence in patients who did not have APR.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Groin Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Groin Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Groin dissection is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of lymph nodes in the groin if they are known to have cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Surveillance imaging is conducted through methods like CT scans to monitor for recurrence of anal cancer after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified DCF Regimen">
      <data key="d0">Modified DCF Regimen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Modified DCF Regimen consists of three chemotherapy drugs: docetaxel, cisplatin, and fluorouracil, and is used to treat certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oxaliplatin">
      <data key="d0">Oxaliplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oxaliplatin is another platinum-based chemotherapy drug used mainly for colorectal cancer treatment, known for specific nerve damage risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thom">
      <data key="d0">Thom</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Thom is a cancer survivor, sharing personal experiences and insights into navigating treatment and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Thom is an anal cancer survivor who shares his experience of recovering from cancer and emphasizes perseverance through treatment challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IV Hydration">
      <data key="d0">IV Hydration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IV hydration is a medical procedure used to administer fluids directly into a vein, often used to mitigate the side effects of chemotherapy on kidney function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palliative Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Palliative Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Palliative radiation therapy is aimed at relieving symptoms caused by cancer instead of curing it, often used to reduce the size of tumors or alleviate pain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trials Phase I">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trials Phase I</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase I clinical trials focus on assessing the safety and side effects of a new drug or treatment in a small group of participants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trials Phase II">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trials Phase II</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase II clinical trials look at the effectiveness of a drug or treatment against a specific type of cancer, often involving a larger group of participants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trials Phase III">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trials Phase III</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase III clinical trials compare a new treatment against standard treatments to evaluate effectiveness, usually conducted with a large number of participants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trials Phase IV">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trials Phase IV</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase IV trials are conducted after a treatment has been approved, focusing on long-term safety and efficacy in the general population.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melissa">
      <data key="d0">Melissa</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Melissa is an anal cancer survivor who shares her experience and emphasizes the importance of finding an oncology team knowledgeable about rare cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="anal cancer">
      <data key="d0">anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal cancer is a rare type of cancer that arises from the tissues of the anal canal, requiring specialized treatment and care.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a rare type of cancer that requires specialized treatment and follow-up care, including clinical trials and support during and after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chemoradiation">
      <data key="d0">chemoradiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemoradiation is a combined treatment approach using chemotherapy and radiation, recommended for most non-metastatic anal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combined treatment method utilizing both chemotherapy and radiation therapy, commonly used for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is an effective treatment combining chemotherapy and radiation therapy to treat anal cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMRT">
      <data key="d0">IMRT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IMRT (Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy) is an advanced method of radiation treatment recommended for anal cancer to minimize harm to surrounding tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT (Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy) is an advanced radiation technique that tailors therapy to minimize damage to surrounding tissues in anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="survivorship care plan">
      <data key="d0">survivorship care plan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A survivorship care plan is developed by oncologists to outline follow-up care and monitor for cancer recurrence after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="oncology team">
      <data key="d0">oncology team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An oncology team comprises medical professionals specializing in cancer treatment, ensuring comprehensive care for patients with anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chemo drugs">
      <data key="d0">chemo drugs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemo drugs are medications used in chemotherapy treatments, essential for fighting cancer by targeting and killing rapidly dividing cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="survivorship">
      <data key="d0">survivorship</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Survivorship refers to the post-treatment phase of cancer care focusing on physical, emotional, and lifestyle aspects after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="copays">
      <data key="d0">copays</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Copays are the fixed amounts patients are required to pay for medical services, often applied to treatments related to cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DRE">
      <data key="d0">DRE</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">DRE (Digital Rectal Exam) is a medical procedure recommended for monitoring patients post-anal cancer treatment to detect any recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCI’s Cancer Information Service (CIS)">
      <data key="d0">NCI’s Cancer Information Service (CIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCI’s Cancer Information Service (CIS) provides essential support and information for individuals seeking guidance on cancer treatments and trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chemoradiation regimens">
      <data key="d0">chemoradiation regimens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemoradiation regimens refer to specific combinations of chemotherapy and radiation treatments recommended for effective management of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Cancer Foundation">
      <data key="d0">Anal Cancer Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Anal Cancer Foundation focuses on raising awareness and funding for anal cancer research and support for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The Anal Cancer Foundation focuses on raising awareness and funding research for anal cancer and provides resources for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The Anal Cancer Foundation is an organization that provides support and resources for anal cancer patients and survivors, including a Peer-To-Peer Support Program to connect patients with trained volunteers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Floor Physical Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Floor Physical Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic floor physical therapy includes exercises and techniques to strengthen pelvic floor muscles, crucial for bowel and bladder control, especially after anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ostomy Care">
      <data key="d0">Ostomy Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ostomy care involves specialized support and guidance for individuals with ostomies, helping them adjust and maintain an active lifestyle post-surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fiber Supplements">
      <data key="d0">Fiber Supplements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fiber supplements, such as psyllium-based products like Metamucil, are used to help manage bowel changes and slow down stool movement, aiding those with digestive issues following treatments like anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Anal Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal cancer treatment encompasses various medical interventions aimed at treating anal cancer, including radiation therapy and other therapeutic approaches, which can lead to various side effects, particularly affecting bowel and sexual health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digestive System Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Digestive System Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Digestive system cancer refers to cancers that affect the organs responsible for digestion, including the anal area, which may have unique nutritional needs and treatment considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Floor">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Floor</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The pelvic floor is a group of muscles supporting pelvic organs, playing an essential role in bowel, bladder control, and sexual function, which can be affected post-cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sexual Health Therapists">
      <data key="d0">Sexual Health Therapists</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Sexual health therapists are professionals specialized in helping individuals cope with sexual side effects from cancer treatment, providing strategies to manage and address concerns related to sexual health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kegel Exercises">
      <data key="d0">Kegel Exercises</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kegel exercises are activities aimed at strengthening pelvic floor muscles, which can help improve bladder control and sexual function after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Survivorship Mental Health">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Survivorship Mental Health</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer survivorship mental health focuses on the psychological aspects and support systems necessary for individuals recovering from cancer treatments, addressing emotional challenges like anxiety and depression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Therapist">
      <data key="d0">Physical Therapist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A physical therapist specializing in pelvic floor therapy provides hands-on techniques and at-home exercise recommendations to improve pelvic health post-cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AlsCancer Survivorship">
      <data key="d0">AlsCancer Survivorship</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer survivorship encompasses the care and support resources available to individuals who have been treated for cancer, including mental health and lifestyle recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acupuncture">
      <data key="d0">Acupuncture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acupuncture is among the complementary therapies suggested for managing side effects and improving well-being during or after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Care Decisions">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Care Decisions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer care decisions involve a patient's choices and discussions with their doctor, which are pivotal throughout the treatment process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aspirin">
      <data key="d0">Aspirin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aspirin is suggested as a preventive measure for future cancers, impacting patient management in oncology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation treatment is a therapeutic approach used for cancer treatment, which can weaken bones in the pelvic area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Density Monitoring">
      <data key="d0">Bone Density Monitoring</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone density monitoring is a recommended practice for patients receiving radiation to assess their fracture risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complementary and Alternative Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Complementary and Alternative Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Complementary and alternative therapies encompass various practices that may improve comfort and well-being during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shared Decision-Making">
      <data key="d0">Shared Decision-Making</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Shared decision-making is a collaborative process between patients and doctors regarding treatment choices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second Opinion">
      <data key="d0">Second Opinion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Seeking a second opinion is a common and encouraged practice for patients to confirm treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Counseling services may be offered to patients for support in quitting smoking and coping with cancer-related issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Integrative Oncology Programs">
      <data key="d0">Integrative Oncology Programs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Integrative oncology programs combine conventional cancer treatments with complementary approaches to enhance patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Cancer Society">
      <data key="d0">American Cancer Society</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Cancer Society is a nationwide voluntary health organization dedicated to eliminating cancer through research, education, advocacy, and patient services.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Cancer Society is a nationwide, community-based voluntary health organization that supports cancer research and provides patient support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Hope Network">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Hope Network</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Hope Network is an organization that offers emotional support to cancer patients through one-on-one connections with survivors.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Hope Network provides free, one-on-one support for cancer patients from trained volunteers who are also cancer survivors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer.Net">
      <data key="d0">Cancer.Net</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer.Net is a comprehensive resource for patients and families affected by cancer, providing detailed information about various cancer types including anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer.Net provides information about cancer types, treatment options, and support resources for patients and healthcare professionals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CancerCare">
      <data key="d0">CancerCare</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">CancerCare offers free professional support services to anyone affected by cancer, including financial assistance and educational resources.&lt;SEP&gt;CancerCare offers free support services for cancer patients, including counseling, financial assistance, and educational resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Support Community">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Support Community</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cancer Support Community provides a network of support, education, and resources for patients facing cancer and their families.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Support Community provides support, education, and hope for people affected by cancer and their loved ones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Cancer Alliance">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Cancer Alliance</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Colorectal Cancer Alliance is dedicated to ending colorectal cancer through awareness, support, and research funding.&lt;SEP&gt;The Colorectal Cancer Alliance is dedicated to preventing colorectal cancer and providing support to patients and their families.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fight Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Fight Colorectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Fight Colorectal Cancer advocates for patients and engages in efforts to improve the quality of life for those impacted by the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Fight Colorectal Cancer is a patient advocacy organization that focuses on promoting action and awareness for colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Cancers Alliance">
      <data key="d0">HPV Cancers Alliance</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The HPV Cancers Alliance works to inform the public about the role of HPV in cancer and to enhance prevention outreach.'&lt;SEP&gt;The HPV Cancers Alliance works to raise awareness and support for cancers caused by the human papillomavirus, including anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Love Your Buns">
      <data key="d0">Love Your Buns</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Love Your Buns is an awareness campaign aimed at educating people about anal cancer and promoting early detection and prevention.&lt;SEP&gt;Love Your Buns is an initiative aimed at encouraging awareness about anal cancer and promoting screening and prevention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Radiation">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic radiation is a specific type of radiation therapy targeting cancers within the pelvic area, often used in the treatment of anal and colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interview with Doctors">
      <data key="d0">Interview with Doctors</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Interviewing doctors is a crucial step for patients to gain insight into potential treatments and the qualifications of their healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Survivorship">
      <data key="d0">Survivorship</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Survivorship refers to the phase after cancer treatment, focusing on follow-up care, quality of life, and ongoing health monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Coalition for Cancer Survivorship">
      <data key="d0">National Coalition for Cancer Survivorship</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Coalition for Cancer Survivorship advocates for the needs and rights of cancer survivors and promotes quality of life issues for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PAN Foundation">
      <data key="d0">PAN Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The PAN Foundation provides financial assistance to patients who cannot afford their medications and healthcare costs related to cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Anchor Study">
      <data key="d0">The Anchor Study</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Anchor Study is a clinical trial aimed at assessing treatments and outcomes for anal cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trials Database">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trials Database</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Clinical Trials Database maintains information on clinical studies worldwide, providing details about cancer research trials and eligibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="abdominoperineal resection (APR)">
      <data key="d0">abdominoperineal resection (APR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">APR is a surgical procedure that may be necessary for treating anal cancer, involving the removal of the anus and rectum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="clinical trial">
      <data key="d0">clinical trial</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical trials are research studies that evaluate investigational treatments to establish their effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that evaluate the safety and efficacy of new treatments in patients, including cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="systemic therapy">
      <data key="d0">systemic therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic therapy involves cancer treatments carried through the bloodstream to target cancer cells across the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy involves cancer treatments delivered through the bloodstream, affecting cancer cells throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="anal canal">
      <data key="d0">anal canal</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The anal canal is the final section of the large intestine, connecting the rectum to the anus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="anal sphincter">
      <data key="d0">anal sphincter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The anal sphincter is a muscular structure that controls the opening and closing of the anus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="anus">
      <data key="d0">anus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The anus is the opening at the end of the digestive tract through which stool exits the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="colostomy">
      <data key="d0">colostomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A colostomy is a surgical procedure that creates an opening for stool to leave the body when the anus is removed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="groin">
      <data key="d0">groin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The groin is the area of the body where the thigh meets the abdomen, and it can be involved in the spread of cancers such as anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="human papillomavirus (HPV)">
      <data key="d0">human papillomavirus (HPV)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV is a sexually transmitted virus that can lead to abnormal cell growth and is a major cause of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="infertility">
      <data key="d0">infertility</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Infertility refers to the inability to conceive children, which can be a concern for patients undergoing treatments for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="inguinal lymph nodes">
      <data key="d0">inguinal lymph nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The inguinal lymph nodes are located in the groin area and may be affected by cancers, including anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT)">
      <data key="d0">intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IMRT is an advanced type of radiation therapy that targets tumors with precision, often used in anal cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="libido">
      <data key="d0">libido</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Libido is the term used to describe sexual desire, which can be affected by cancer treatments and surgeries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="perianal region">
      <data key="d0">perianal region</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The perianal region includes the skin surrounding the anus, which can be affected by anal cancer and its treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="vaginal stenosis">
      <data key="d0">vaginal stenosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaginal stenosis is a condition marked by narrowing and shortening of the vagina, which can result from cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients are comprehensive resources provided by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN), designed to assist patients in navigating their cancer care and treatment options. These guidelines deliver expert recommendations based on the latest research and practices from leading cancer centers, offering trusted information tailored for patient understanding.

The guidelines encompass detailed guidance on various aspects of cancer treatment, including chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and side effect management. They address specific cancer types such as colorectal cancer, breast cancer, cervical cancer, thyroid cancer, kidney cancer, skin cancer, and anal cancer. Additionally, the guidelines provide information regarding symptoms and side effects, promoting better management and patient outcomes.

NCCN Guidelines for Patients also emphasize the importance of informed decision-making, empowering patients to understand their treatment options, communicate effectively with their healthcare teams, and advocate for their care. They include support for understanding diagnosis, treatment protocols, and survivorship.

Overall, the NCCN Guidelines for Patients serve as a valuable resource for individuals diagnosed with cancer, offering evidence-based recommendations and resources intended to improve their knowledge and experience throughout their treatment journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation Therapy is a specialized medical treatment that employs high-energy radiation, such as x-rays or gamma rays, to target, kill, or shrink cancer cells. It is primarily utilized when surgical interventions are unfeasible or have not achieved desired results. This treatment modality is commonly integrated into comprehensive cancer treatment plans and can be used in conjunction with other therapies, including chemotherapy and surgery, to enhance its effectiveness.

Radiation Therapy is particularly versatile, as it can effectively treat a variety of cancers, including but not limited to prostate cancer, anal cancer, basal cell skin cancer, and primary central nervous system (CNS) lymphoma. It may be employed pre- or post-surgery to reduce the risk of recurrence and to address any remaining cancerous cells. Advanced techniques, such as intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT), enhance the targeting capabilities to minimize damage to surrounding healthy tissues.

In specific cases, radiation therapy is also used as a treatment option for advanced thyroid cancer and squamous cell skin cancer. Its administration is typically managed by a radiation oncologist, and treatment regimens usually involve multiple sessions over several weeks to effectively deliver high doses of radiation to the affected areas.

Despite its efficacy, radiation therapy can have side effects, which may include cognitive impairments or vision problems, depending on the treatment area. Overall, Radiation Therapy remains a crucial component in the fight against cancer, particularly in cases where other treatment options are limited.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy is a medical procedure that involves the removal of a small sample of tissue or fluid from the body for examination and diagnostic purposes. It is crucial in confirming the presence of cancer and determining its type and characteristics. The procedure can be performed using various techniques, depending on the type of tissue being sampled and the specific medical context.

Biopsies are commonly used to diagnose a range of cancers, including but not limited to squamous cell carcinoma, breast cancer, glioma, inflammatory breast cancer (IBC), ovarian cancer, oropharyngeal cancer, thyroid nodules, prostate cancer, esophageal cancer, kidney cancer, and lymphomas such as chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) and small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL). During the biopsy, tissue samples may be taken directly from tumors or affected areas and subsequently examined under a microscope to identify cancerous cells or assess cellular abnormalities.

The procedure can involve invasive elements, such as extracting prostate tissue or sampling a tumor, which may carry certain risks. Additionally, biopsies are essential not just for diagnosis but also for informing treatment decisions, staging cancer, and assessing the overall health of a patient. In various contexts, biopsies serve as a vital diagnostic tool to guide clinicians in the management of cancer and other medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A CT scan, or computed tomography scan, is a sophisticated medical imaging procedure that combines X-ray technology with computer processing to produce detailed cross-sectional images of the body's internal structures. This imaging technique captures X-ray images from various angles, allowing for the generation of comprehensive views that are crucial for the diagnosis and assessment of a wide range of health conditions, particularly in the context of cancer detection and monitoring.

CT scans are instrumental in diagnosing various cancers, including ovarian tumors and rectal cancer, by providing critical information about the spread of the disease to adjacent tissues or organs. They serve multiple purposes, from evaluating lymph nodes and assessing the extent of Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) before treatment, to identifying potential metastasis in other regions of the body. Additionally, CT scans are often utilized in scenarios where other imaging modalities, such as MRI, may be unsuitable.

In particular, the procedure is recognized for its ability to evaluate specific health issues, including checking for bleeding in the brain or signs of leukemic invasion outside the bone marrow. CT scans also play a vital role in treatment planning for cancer therapy, enabling healthcare providers to map out necessary interventions based on thorough visual assessments of the affected areas.

Overall, CT scans are a critical tool in modern medical diagnostics, providing vital information that aids in the detection, evaluation, and management of various health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Tests encompass a range of examinations performed on blood samples that are essential for assessing overall health and diagnosing various conditions. These tests are particularly significant in the context of cancer, as they can reveal important biomarkers associated with different types of cancer, including but not limited to ovarian, pancreatic, prostate, rectal, and bladder cancers. Blood Tests are utilized to diagnose and monitor blood-related cancers, such as acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) and chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), with specific emphasis on evaluating cell counts and genetic markers to determine treatment effectiveness.

These laboratory tests serve as valuable diagnostic tools that analyze various components of the blood, which include red blood cells, white blood cells, and chemical levels. By doing so, Blood Tests provide critical insights into an individual's health status and help in planning appropriate treatment strategies, especially for conditions like acute myeloid leukemia (AML). They also play a pivotal role in monitoring the response to cancer therapies and assessing kidney function, as well as other health indicators.

In addition, Blood Tests are frequently employed to screen for signs of disease and to gather pertinent data that informs treatment planning. They offer a comprehensive evaluation of hormonal levels relevant to conditions like thyroid cancer, and their results can guide health practitioners in identifying the presence of diseases and tailoring patient care accordingly. Overall, Blood Tests are integral medical procedures that contribute significantly to the detection, diagnosis, and management of a wide range of health conditions, notably cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging Tests are essential medical procedures that create visual representations of the interior of the body for clinical analysis and intervention. They encompass various techniques, including MRI, CT, and PET scans, which are vital in diagnosing and monitoring diseases, particularly cancers. These diagnostic tools visualize internal structures to identify abnormalities, including tumors, and to assist in the detection and assessment of various types of cancer such as ovarian, kidney, prostate, breast, liver, and brain cancers.

Imaging Tests are particularly crucial for staging cancer, planning treatment, and evaluating the spread of disease within the body. For instance, they help visualize the reproductive organs when checking for signs of ovarian cancer, assess the extent of kidney and prostate cancer, and monitor for recurrence in patients previously treated for cancers such as anal or throat cancer. Specific tests, like mammograms, are dedicated to visualizing breast tissue to detect cancerous changes, while ultrasound and scope procedures are used to investigate symptoms associated with cancer.

Overall, Imaging Tests play a critical role in the detection, diagnosis, and management of cancer by providing necessary visual information about the internal condition of the body, thus aiding healthcare providers in making informed decisions regarding patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) is a form of cancer that originates in squamous cells, which are flat cells that line various organs and structures in the body. It is most commonly associated with the skin, where it can develop from damaged or inflamed skin, often due to risk factors such as chronic wounds and ultraviolet (UV) exposure. However, SCC is not limited to the skin and can also arise in several other anatomical locations.

In terms of its classification, squamous cell carcinoma can be categorized into local, regional, and metastatic stages, indicating the extent of the disease. It is prevalent in various forms including:

1. **Skin** - SCC primarily manifests as a common type of skin cancer and is characterized by lesions. 
2. **Lungs** - This cancer can develop in the lungs as a subtype of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), originating from the flat cells lining the bronchi, often linked to smoking.
3. **Mouth** - SCC is the most common type of mouth cancer, forming in the surface lining of the mouth.
4. **Throat** - It also represents the most common type of throat cancer, affecting the mucus membranes lining the mouth, nose, and throat.
5. **Nasopharynx** - SCC is the most prevalent type of nasopharyngeal cancer, arising from squamous cells in that area.
6. **Cervix** - It is identified as the most common type of cervical cancer, resulting from abnormal growth of squamous cells lining the cervix.
7. **Esophagus** - This carcinoma type can also develop in the esophagus, particularly more frequently in regions such as Eastern Europe, Asia, and Africa, originating from the flat, thin cells of the esophageal lining.
8. **Anal Canal** - SCC is significant in discussions of anal cancer, as it is the most common type that originates in the squamous cells of the anal canal.

In summary, Squamous Cell Carcinoma is a diverse group of cancers that primarily arise from squamous cells in various tissues, with significant implications for public health and cancer treatment across multiple organ systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI">
      <data key="d0">MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is a sophisticated and non-invasive medical imaging technique that utilizes strong magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of internal structures within the body. This procedure is particularly effective for visualizing soft tissues, making it invaluable in the diagnosis and assessment of various health conditions, including cancer.

MRI scans are commonly employed to evaluate abnormalities in the breast, providing detailed images that help health professionals assess potential health issues. In cancer diagnosis, MRI is pivotal for identifying primary Central Nervous System (CNS) lymphoma and staging rectal and anal cancers, as it allows for a thorough evaluation of disease extent without the use of ionizing radiation.

Moreover, MRI is widely used in assessing the presence and spread of cancer across different organs, including providing detailed images of the thyroid and neck area. When other imaging methods, such as CT scans or ultrasounds, yield inconclusive results, MRI serves as a reliable alternative for further investigation.

Overall, MRI stands out as an essential tool in modern diagnostic radiology, offering critical insights that aid in the management and monitoring of various medical conditions, especially cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped structures that play a critical role in the body's immune system and are essential components of the lymphatic system. They function as filters for lymph fluid, capturing harmful substances, including cancer cells and pathogens. These small glands are distributed throughout the body and contain immune cells that are vital in the body's defense against infections and diseases, including various types of cancer.

In the context of cancer, lymph nodes are particularly important for staging and diagnosis, as their involvement often indicates the spread of cancer from a primary tumor to other regions. For example, they can be affected by stomach cancer, colon cancer, head and neck cancers, breast cancer, and metastatic prostate cancer. The assessment of lymph nodes is routinely conducted during procedures such as radical cystectomy, where they may be removed to check for cancer metastasis.

As part of the immune response, lymph nodes can swell due to the accumulation of abnormal cells, underscoring their role in detecting and fighting cancer. Overall, lymph nodes are crucial for understanding cancer progression and are integral to both the immune response and cancer treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic Therapy encompasses a range of treatments designed to target cancer cells throughout the entire body. This approach is often utilized in conjunction with other therapies and is crucial for the management of various types of cancers, including advanced cases such as cervical cancer and inflammatory breast cancer. It typically involves the administration of drugs that travel through the bloodstream to affect all parts of the body, including chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy.

As a systemic treatment, it is designed not only to manage localized cancer but also to address metastatic conditions, making it a vital component of comprehensive cancer care. Systemic therapy can be delivered intravenously and may be employed in different stages of treatment, whether before or after surgeries, to reduce the cancer burden. It is important to note that while systemic therapy aims to eliminate cancer cells, it can also impact normal cells, potentially leading to side effects.

In summary, Systemic Therapy is an essential cancer treatment strategy that affects the whole body, utilizing various methods like chemotherapy and immunotherapy to combat cancerous cells and manage various cancer types effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy is a prominent treatment method employed in the fight against cancer, involving the administration of cytotoxic drugs designed to kill or inhibit the growth of cancer cells. It operates primarily as a systemic therapy, targeting fast-dividing cancer cells throughout the body and is typically administered in cycles followed by rest days to allow the body to recover. Various drug regimens are utilized, and chemotherapy can be delivered either intravenously or orally, and in some cases, through direct methods such as intravesical delivery for bladder cancer treatment.

This treatment is widely used for a range of cancers, including acute myeloid leukemia (AML), gliomas, bladder cancer, ovarian cancer, prostate cancer, and primary central nervous system lymphoma (CNS lymphoma). In specific types of cancer, such as advanced-stage prostate cancer, chemotherapy is aimed at targeting rapidly dividing cells, while in other cases, it may be utilized to shrink tumors prior to surgical intervention to improve outcomes.

While chemotherapy is an effective approach to combatting cancer, it comes with a variety of significant side effects, impacting both cancerous and healthy cells, which may lead to various health concerns and complications, including effects on fertility. The choice of chemotherapy is often influenced by the type and stage of cancer and is frequently used in conjunction with other treatment modalities such as surgery and radiation therapy to enhance treatment effectiveness. Additionally, some chemotherapy regimens may incorporate platinum-based drugs, as they have shown efficacy against certain types of cancer. Overall, chemotherapy remains a cornerstone of cancer treatment, continually evolving with advancements in medicine to improve patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical trials are research studies designed to assess the safety and effectiveness of new treatments, therapies, drugs, or devices in human subjects, with a significant focus on various medical conditions, particularly cancers. These trials serve as a vital mechanism for evaluating new therapeutic approaches for diseases, including but not limited to cervical cancer, bladder cancer, advanced prostate cancer, primary CNS lymphoma, gliomas, chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), thyroid cancer, rectal cancer, pancreatic cancer, and lung cancer.

They involve patient participation and run through distinct phases to ensure rigorous testing of a treatment's efficacy and safety. Clinical trials not only aim to advance medical knowledge and treatment options but also provide patients with access to innovative therapies that are not yet widely available. Importantly, they are crucial in evaluating various new medical interventions, ensuring that new methods of treatment improve care outcomes for cancer patients. Ultimately, clinical trials play an essential role in the continuous advancement of cancer care, contributing to the development of new therapies and improving patient outcomes across multiple cancer types and stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow is a soft, sponge-like tissue located in the center of bones, where the production of new blood cells takes place. This tissue is responsible for generating blood stem cells, which differentiate into various types of blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets. It plays a crucial role in the process of hematopoiesis, the formation of blood cells from stem cells.

In addition to blood cell production, bone marrow is significant in the context of various blood cancers. It can be a site of origin for conditions like leukemia, and its examination through aspiration or biopsy is essential for diagnosing blood-related disorders. For example, it is vital in identifying genetic abnormalities associated with diseases like chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) and acute myeloid leukemia (AML). The health of bone marrow is also linked to the immune system, and impairment in its function may increase the risk of developing lymphomas.

Overall, bone marrow serves as a critical component of the hematopoietic system, with its role extending to the immune response and the potential involvement in different types of blood cancers. Its examination is crucial for effective diagnosis and treatment strategies within hematology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Care Team is a collaborative group of healthcare professionals dedicated to diagnosing, treating, and providing comprehensive support to patients, particularly those with cancer. This multidisciplinary team encompasses various specialists, including oncologists, nurses, and professionals from fields such as pulmonology, radiology, and thoracic surgery. They work together to create and implement tailored treatment plans for a range of conditions, including but not limited to chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), primary central nervous system lymphoma, breast cancer, lung diseases, bladder cancer, and acute myeloid leukemia (AML).

In cancer treatment, the Care Team plays a vital role in managing patient care from diagnosis through treatment and recovery, ensuring that patients receive informed assistance and continuous support during medical procedures. They assess patients' conditions through imaging, clinical evaluations, and various tests, closely monitoring health measurements such as blood components and organ function. The Care Team emphasizes effective communication, both among its members and with patients, to facilitate a collaborative approach to patient management.

Overall, the Care Team is instrumental in guiding cancer patients through their treatment journeys, providing a supportive environment that addresses both medical and emotional needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate Cancer is a malignant disease affecting the prostate gland, which is a small walnut-sized organ responsible for producing seminal fluid in men. This type of cancer is characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells within the prostate, leading to various health issues, including problems with urine flow and sexual function. Prostate cancer is notably prevalent among men and is influenced by genetic risk factors, including germline mutations.

Diagnosis and monitoring of prostate cancer often involve a range of medical procedures, including biopsies and various imaging tests. The disease can be categorized into early-stage and advanced-stage conditions, with its aggressiveness varying significantly. While prostate cancer generally grows slowly, more aggressive forms may develop, and in some cases, the cancer can metastasize to other areas of the body, including lymph nodes and bones.

In addition to traditional staging classifications that help determine the extent of the disease, prostate cancer is also assessed through biomarkers such as PSA levels and Gleason scores. These indicators play a vital role in informing treatment options tailored to different risk groups. Overall, prostate cancer remains a significant concern in men's health, necessitating ongoing research and discussion regarding its diagnosis, symptoms, and treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical cancer is a malignant growth that originates from the cervix, commonly linked to persistent infection with human papillomavirus (HPV). It encompasses various types, including squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, and neuroendocrine carcinoma. As a significant cancer affecting the female reproductive system, cervical cancer often requires the surgical removal of lymph nodes during treatment procedures.

Diagnosis is typically achieved through cervical biopsy and cone biopsy, with the disease staged using the FIGO system, which helps determine the extent of spread and guides treatment options. The cancer may metastasize to nearby lymph nodes and other organs, such as the liver, lungs, and bones.

The treatment for cervical cancer involves a combination of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT), chemotherapy, brachytherapy, and surgical interventions, tailored according to the specific stage of the disease. The management approach may vary significantly based on the cancer's progression and the patient's health status. Overall, cervical cancer necessitates careful monitoring and a multifaceted treatment regimen to achieve the best possible outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
