    <edge source="Direct Laryngoscopy" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Direct laryngoscopy often involves taking a biopsy to analyze tissue, specifically looking for tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic examination, tissue analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Direct Laryngoscopy" target="Tissue Sample">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">During direct laryngoscopy, a tissue sample can be collected for further pathological evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedures, tissue analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophagoscopy" target="Bronchoscopy">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both procedures are endoscopic examinations aimed at detecting abnormalities in different areas of the digestive and respiratory systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Both procedures involve examining critical parts of the respiratory and digestive systems respectively, often used in cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical procedures, cancer diagnosis&lt;SEP&gt;medical procedures, diagnostic techniques</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bronchoscopy" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bronchoscopy may be accompanied by a biopsy procedure to confirm the diagnosis of potential lung cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer diagnosis, sample collection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Panendoscopy" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">During a panendoscopy, biopsies may be taken from multiple areas to assess for cancer presence or abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d6">comprehensive examination, multi-site biopsy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)" target="Biopsy Procedure">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FNA is a specific type of biopsy procedure utilized to obtain tissue samples for analysis, especially when assessing abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d6">sample collection, diagnostic procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)" target="Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FNA can suggest the presence of follicular thyroid carcinoma but cannot confirm its diagnosis, requiring additional histological evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)" target="Inguinal Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FNA can be used to obtain tissue samples from enlarged inguinal lymph nodes when there is suspicion of metastasis from anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, cancer evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Deficient Mismatch Repair (dMMR)" target="MSI-H/xMMR Mutation">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MSI-H/xMMR mutations are a result of deficient mismatch repair mechanisms, which leads to instability in microsatellites.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic instability, cancer mechanism</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer (Oropharyngeal Cancer)" target="NCCN Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines provide essential information and recommendations for patients diagnosed with throat cancer regarding treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d6">guidelines, patient resources</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer (Oropharyngeal Cancer)" target="Tumor p16 Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor p16 testing is critical for diagnosing and staging oropharyngeal cancers as it relates to HPV status.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnosis, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer (Oropharyngeal Cancer)" target="Dental Health">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients with throat cancer should monitor their dental health, as treatment can negatively impact the health of teeth and gums.</data>
      <data key="d6">healthcare impact, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy" target="PDL1">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PDL1 is a key target for checkpoint inhibitor therapies, as its expression affects how well these treatments work against cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker, immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy" target="Tumor Mutational Burden High (TMB-H)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High TMB levels can indicate a favorable response to checkpoint inhibitor therapy in cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment response, genomic feature</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy" target="PD-1">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Checkpoint inhibitor therapy often targets PD-1 to enhance the anti-tumor immune response in patients with specific cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">immunotherapy, treatment strategy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Exam" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A thorough throat exam may lead to the decision of performing a biopsy to ascertain the presence of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic process, assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)" target="Bone Marrow">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ALL affects the bone marrow's ability to produce healthy blood cells due to the dominance of lymphoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d6">blood production, disease impact</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)" target="Treatment for ALL">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Treatment for ALL is specifically designed to target and eliminate the lymphoblasts resulting from this cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer therapy, targeted treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)" target="B-ALL">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-ALL is a subtype of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia characterized by its origin in B-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)" target="T-ALL">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-ALL is another subtype of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia that originates in T-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)" target="Adult Cancer Center">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adult Cancer Centers provide treatment and care specifically for patients diagnosed with Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment center, medical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)" target="Blood Tests">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blood tests are essential in diagnosing and monitoring Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia and its treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedures, monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)" target="Burkitt Leukemia">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Burkitt leukemia is described as a rare subtype of ALL, indicating they share similar cellular characteristics but differ in treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)" target="T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma represents a specific manifestation of ALL characterized by T-cell involvement, distinct from B-cell presentations.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Scores" target="Stage 0 to Stage 4">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The stages from 0 to 4 utilize TNM Scores to quantify cancer progression and severity in clinical practice for treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, treatment framework</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Tumors" target="Locoregional Disease">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Locoregional Disease describes the spread of throat tumors to nearby tissues and lymph nodes, indicating advanced but localized cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer spread, regional involvement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="NCCN Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines assist those affected by Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia in making informed treatment choices based on expert recommendations.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines provide important recommendations to patients diagnosed with Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia to help them navigate treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer care, treatment guidance&lt;SEP&gt;cancer care, treatment guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="B-cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell ALL is a subtype of the wider category of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia that affects B-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, specific type</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="T-cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-cell ALL represents another subtype of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia affecting T-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, specific type</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="Pediatric">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pediatric refers to the population cohort affected by Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, particularly individuals aged 18 and under.</data>
      <data key="d6">age demographic, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="AYAs">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AYAs represent a specific demographic within the patient population for Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, often treated in specialized centers.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient demographic, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="B-cell Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia primarily affects B-cell lymphocytes, leading to their uncontrolled growth and proliferation.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtype, lymphocyte impact</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="T-cell Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-cell Lymphocytes can also develop into T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, representing another variant of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtype, lymphocyte impact</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="Lymphoblasts">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymphoblasts are the immature cells that proliferate excessively in Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease mechanism, cellular growth</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="Bone Marrow">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia originates in the bone marrow, affecting blood cell production.</data>
      <data key="d6">blood production, cancer origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="Pediatric Cancer Centers">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pediatric cancer centers specialize in treating Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia in children and adolescents.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, specialized care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-cell ALL" target="B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma occurs when B-cell lymphoblasts proliferate primarily in the lymph nodes in the context of B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">lymphoma, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T-cell ALL" target="T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma develops when T-cell lymphoblasts grow predominantly in the thymus in cases of T-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">lymphoma, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AYAs" target="Pediatric Cancer Center">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adolescents and Young Adults (AYAs) may receive treatment at pediatric centers if their cancers align with pediatric-specific protocols.</data>
      <data key="d6">age-group distinction, treatment centers</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-cell Lymphocytes" target="Antibodies">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell lymphocytes are responsible for producing antibodies that play a critical role in the immune response against infections.</data>
      <data key="d6">immune response, lymphocyte function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Burkitt Lymphoma" target="B-cell Lymphoblasts">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell Lymphoblasts can lead to Burkitt Lymphoma, a variant of B-cell ALL that requires different treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer progression, treatment variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Burkitt Lymphoma" target="Burkitt Leukemia">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Burkitt lymphoma and Burkitt leukemia are related as they both arise from B-cell origins but differ in their development and treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">non-Hodgkin lymphoma, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Progenitor Cells" target="Bone Marrow">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bone marrow contains progenitor cells that develop into various types of blood cells, including lymphocytes that can lead to conditions like ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Progenitor cells are generated in the bone marrow and are crucial in the development of normal and abnormal blood cells, including lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d6">blood formation, cellular differentiation&lt;SEP&gt;cell differentiation, blood formation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pediatric Cancer Centers" target="Adolescents and Young Adults (AYAs)">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AYAs may be treated at pediatric cancer centers depending on the type of cancer and its progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">age demographic, treatment pathways</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN®" target="ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN® provides guidelines and information relevant to the treatment and understanding of ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer care, cancer guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN®" target="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN® provides guidelines for the treatment and management of rectal cancer in patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer care, treatment guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN®" target="Patient Advocacy Groups">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patient advocacy groups often reference guidelines provided by NCCN® to inform their support strategies for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">resource sharing, support frameworks</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="B-ALL">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-ALL is a subtype of ALL, indicating specific characteristics and treatment options related to B lymphocyte involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;B-ALL is a subtype of ALL, involving specific treatment strategies and patient considerations.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, treatment considerations&lt;SEP&gt;subtype, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="T-ALL">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-ALL is a less common subtype of ALL that requires distinct treatment approaches and patient management.&lt;SEP&gt;T-ALL is another subtype of ALL, highlighting the diversity in the disease and its impact on treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, treatment focus&lt;SEP&gt;subtype, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials are often the preferred treatment option for specific types of ALL, seeking to develop new therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials offer new treatment options for patients with ALL, focusing on innovative therapies and approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">research studies, treatment development&lt;SEP&gt;research, treatment innovation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="Fertility Tests">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fertility tests are relevant for ALL patients, particularly children, who may face fertility issues due to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">impact of treatment, reproductive health</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="NCCN Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients include information specifically tailored for understanding and treating ALL, enhancing patient knowledge.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient education, treatment guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="Fertility Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fertility testing is important for patients with ALL to understand potential reproductive impacts of their treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">health assessment, treatment impact</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="Steroids">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Steroids are a crucial part of the treatment regimen for patients with ALL, helping to manage the disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment protocol, medication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="Multiagent Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Multiagent chemotherapy is a standard treatment strategy for ALL, aiming to improve patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment method, cancer care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="Burkitt Leukemia">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Burkitt leukemia is essentially the same as a rare subtype of B-ALL, treated differently from standard B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, treatment distinction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma (B-LL)">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-LL occurs when B-cell lymphoblasts grow mainly in lymph nodes instead of bone marrow, exhibiting a relationship with B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease correlation, localization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T-ALL" target="T Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d4">19.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T lymphocytes are primarily involved in the development of T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, emphasizing their role in this cancer type.&lt;SEP&gt;T-ALL affects T lymphocytes, causing them to proliferate abnormally, which is characteristic of a type of leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer etiology, specific cell role&lt;SEP&gt;cancer subtype, immune dysfunction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T-ALL" target="T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma (T-LL)">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-LL happens when T-cell lymphoblasts grow primarily in the thymus, which relates directly to T-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease correlation, localization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Polyp">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Rectal cancer is often initiated from polyps that develop on the rectal lining, indicating a direct relationship in cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer often begins as polyps on the rectal lining, indicating a direct link between the two.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer development, medical condition&lt;SEP&gt;cancer progression, medical condition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="MRI">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI is utilized to visualize the extent of rectal cancer, thus playing a crucial role in diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scanning is essential for detecting metastasis of rectal cancer to other organs, illustrating its role in comprehensive cancer assessment.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, cancer assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Polypectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Polypectomy is performed to remove polyps that could become cancerous, thereby intervening in the progression of rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, cancer prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection (ESD)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ESD is a treatment for early rectal cancers, showcasing its importance in managing rectal cancer effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;ESD is used for the treatment of superficial rectal cancers that have not invaded deeply into tissues, presenting a less invasive option.</data>
      <data key="d6">minimally invasive technique, cancer treatment&lt;SEP&gt;treatment method, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Transanal Local Excision">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transanal local excision allows for the removal of certain low-risk rectal tumors; it shows a surgical option to treat early rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Transanal local excision allows for the removal of rectal cancer tumors, particularly in low-risk T1 cases, demonstrating its surgical relevance.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical method, cancer removal&lt;SEP&gt;surgical removal, rectal surgery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="MRI of the Pelvis">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI of the pelvis is crucial for staging rectal cancer, providing detailed imaging that assists in treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="CT of the Chest and Abdomen">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans are essential in evaluating whether rectal cancer has metastasized to other organs, thus influencing treatment direction.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, cancer assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Endorectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endorectal ultrasound provides essential information for assessing the depth and extent of rectal cancer before surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, cancer evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polypectomy" target="Polyp">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Polypectomy is performed to remove polyps which, if cancerous, can prevent further development of rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, cancer prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polypectomy" target="Colon Polyps">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Polypectomy is the procedure used to remove Colon Polyps, thus preventing potential progression to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgery, cancer prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polypectomy" target="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Polypectomy is a crucial procedure used to prevent colorectal cancer by removing polyps before they can develop into cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">preventative procedure, cancer prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polypectomy" target="Invasive Cancer">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Invasive cancer can arise from polyps if they are not identified and removed in a timely manner through procedures like polypectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment importance, cancer development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mismatch Repair Testing" target="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mismatch Repair Testing is vital for evaluating colorectal cancer risk and guiding treatment decisions based on genetic markers.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic tool, personalized medicine</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mismatch Repair Testing" target="ctDNA">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mismatch Repair Testing may provide insights into the genetic features of tumors, which can be analyzed further with ctDNA testing in advanced cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic tool, personalized medicine</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ctDNA Testing" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ctDNA testing complements imaging tests by providing additional insights into possible cancer recurrence after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">monitoring techniques, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyperplastic Polyps" target="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hyperplastic polyps, while generally benign, can be monitored during colonoscopy to prevent potential development into colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk assessment, cancer prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Polyps" target="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory polyps are important to identify during colonoscopy as they indicate inflammatory processes but do not typically progress to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic relevance, cancer screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)" target="Prostate Cancer">
